var MATH_PROBLEM = {"m1":{"ask":"Find the number <i>x<\/i> that makes 3<i>x<\/i> + 7 equal 22.","idea":"Undo what was done to <i>x<\/i>, in reverse order. <i>x<\/i> was multiplied by 3 and then 7 was added, so first subtract 7, then divide by 3. Whatever you do to one side, do to the other so the equation stays balanced.","steps":["Subtract 7 from both sides: 3<i>x<\/i> + 7 − 7 = 22 − 7, so 3<i>x<\/i> = 15. This removes the +7 that was added last.","Divide both sides by 3: <i>x<\/i> = 15 ÷ 3 = 5. This undoes the multiplication."],"check":"3(5) + 7 = 15 + 7 = 22 ✓","wrong":{"3":"Doesn't work: 3(3) + 7 = 16, not 22.","7":"7 is the number added to 3<i>x<\/i>, not the value of <i>x<\/i>. Check: 3(7) + 7 = 28.","29/3":"Comes from adding 7 instead of subtracting it (3<i>x<\/i> = 29)."}},"m2":{"ask":"How steep is the line through (0, 4) and (2, 10)?","idea":"Slope measures how much <i>y<\/i> changes for each 1-unit step in <i>x<\/i>: slope = (change in <i>y<\/i>) ÷ (change in <i>x<\/i>). Subtract the coordinates in the same order on the top and bottom.","steps":["Change in <i>y<\/i>: 10 − 4 = 6.","Change in <i>x<\/i>: 2 − 0 = 2.","Slope = 6 ÷ 2 = 3. For every 1 step right, the line rises 3."],"check":"Start at (0, 4) and go right 2 while rising 3 each step: 4 + 6 = 10, so you reach (2, 10) ✓","wrong":{"2":"That's the change in <i>x<\/i>, not the slope.","6":"That's the change in <i>y<\/i>; you still need to divide by the change in <i>x<\/i>.","4":"That's where the line crosses the <i>y<\/i>-axis (the <i>y<\/i>-intercept), not its slope."}},"m3":{"ask":"Write a rule for the total cost after <i>m<\/i> months.","idea":"A charge that repeats every month gets multiplied by the number of months. A one-time charge is added just once. So the pattern is: (monthly amount) × (months) + (one-time fee).","steps":["The $15 is paid every month, so after <i>m<\/i> months it's 15<i>m<\/i>.","The $25 sign-up fee is paid once, so add it a single time: 15<i>m<\/i> + 25.","So C = 15<i>m<\/i> + 25."],"check":"After 2 months: $25 + $15 + $15 = $55, and 15(2) + 25 = 55 ✓","wrong":{"<i>C<\/i> = 25<i>m<\/i> + 15":"Swaps the two amounts: it charges the $25 fee every month.","<i>C<\/i> = 40<i>m<\/i>":"Adds the fee and the monthly price and charges $40 every month, but the fee is paid only once.","<i>C<\/i> = 15(<i>m<\/i> + 25)":"Multiplies the fee by $15. Expanded, it's 15<i>m<\/i> + 375."}},"m4":{"ask":"Two numbers add to 10 and differ by 4. What is the larger one, <i>x<\/i>?","idea":"When one equation has +<i>y<\/i> and the other has −<i>y<\/i>, adding the equations makes <i>y<\/i> cancel. This is called elimination, and it leaves one equation with only <i>x<\/i>.","steps":["Add the left sides and the right sides: (<i>x<\/i> + <i>y<\/i>) + (<i>x<\/i> − <i>y<\/i>) = 10 + 4.","The <i>y<\/i>'s cancel: 2<i>x<\/i> = 14.","Divide by 2: <i>x<\/i> = 7."],"check":"<i>y<\/i> = 10 − 7 = 3. Then 7 + 3 = 10 ✓ and 7 − 3 = 4 ✓","wrong":{"3":"That's the value of <i>y<\/i>, not <i>x<\/i>.","14":"That's 2<i>x<\/i>; divide by 2 to get <i>x<\/i>.","6":"Doesn't work: then <i>y<\/i> = 4, and 6 − 4 = 2, not 4."}},"m5":{"ask":"Find <i>x<\/i> so that 4(2<i>x<\/i> − 1) and 3(<i>x<\/i> + 2) are equal.","idea":"First clear the parentheses by distributing: multiply the number outside by <em>every<\/em> term inside. Then gather the <i>x<\/i> terms on one side and the plain numbers on the other.","steps":["Distribute: 4(2<i>x<\/i> − 1) = 8<i>x<\/i> − 4 and 3(<i>x<\/i> + 2) = 3<i>x<\/i> + 6. So 8<i>x<\/i> − 4 = 3<i>x<\/i> + 6.","Subtract 3<i>x<\/i> from both sides: 5<i>x<\/i> − 4 = 6.","Add 4 to both sides: 5<i>x<\/i> = 10, so <i>x<\/i> = 2."],"check":"Left: 4(2·2 − 1) = 4(3) = 12. Right: 3(2 + 2) = 12 ✓","wrong":{"1":"Doesn't work: left side 4(1) = 4, right side 3(3) = 9.","5/2":"Doesn't work: the left side is 4(4) = 16 but the right side is 3(4.5) = 13.5.","10/11":"Comes from adding the <i>x<\/i> terms instead of subtracting (11<i>x<\/i> = 10)."}},"m6":{"ask":"For which <i>k<\/i> do these two lines never meet?","idea":"Two lines never meet when they're parallel: same slope, different <i>y<\/i>-intercept. For equations like <i>A<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>B<\/i><i>y<\/i> = <i>C<\/i>, that means the <i>x<\/i> and <i>y<\/i> parts match (after scaling) but the right sides don't.","steps":["Compare the <i>x<\/i> coefficients: 4 is 2 × 2, so scale the first equation by 2: 4<i>x<\/i> + 6<i>y<\/i> = 12.","For the lines to be parallel, the <i>y<\/i> parts must also match: <i>k<\/i> = 6.","Now the second equation is 4<i>x<\/i> + 6<i>y<\/i> = 5. The left sides are the same but 12 ≠ 5, so no (<i>x<\/i>, <i>y<\/i>) can satisfy both."],"check":"With <i>k<\/i> = 6, both lines have slope −2/3 but different intercepts (2 and 5/6), so they're parallel ✓","wrong":{"3":"Copies the 3 from the first equation without scaling by 2. With <i>k<\/i> = 3 the slopes differ, so the lines cross once.","12":"Scales twice. With <i>k<\/i> = 12 the slopes differ, so there's one solution.","−6":"Gives slope +2/3, not −2/3, so the lines cross."}},"m23":{"ask":"Find <i>x<\/i> when one-fourth of <i>x<\/i>, minus 2, equals 3.","idea":"Undo operations in reverse order: the 2 was subtracted last, so add it back first. Then undo the division by 4 by multiplying by 4.","steps":["Add 2 to both sides: <i>x<\/i>/4 = 5.","Multiply both sides by 4: <i>x<\/i> = 20."],"check":"20 ÷ 4 − 2 = 5 − 2 = 3 ✓","wrong":{"4":"That's the denominator. Check: 4 ÷ 4 − 2 = −1.","12":"Doesn't work: 12 ÷ 4 − 2 = 1, not 3.","24":"Doesn't work: 24 ÷ 4 − 2 = 4, not 3."}},"m24":{"ask":"What does the 2.5 in the plant equation mean in real life?","idea":"In <i>y<\/i> = <i>m<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>b<\/i>, the number multiplied by <i>x<\/i> (the slope) is how much <i>y<\/i> changes each time <i>x<\/i> goes up by 1. The number added on (<i>b<\/i>) is the value when <i>x<\/i> = 0, the starting point.","steps":["Identify the parts: 2.5 multiplies <i>x<\/i> (weeks), and 40 is added on.","So each additional week adds 2.5 centimeters to the height.","The 40 is the height at week 0, when the plant was first measured."],"check":"Week 0: 40 cm. Week 1: 42.5 cm. Week 2: 45 cm. The height goes up 2.5 cm each week ✓","wrong":{"The plant was 2.5 centimeters tall when it was first measured.":"The starting height is the constant 40 (the value when <i>x<\/i> = 0), not 2.5.","The plant grows about 40 centimeters each week.":"Mixes up the parts: 40 is the starting height, not the weekly growth.","The plant will be 40 centimeters tall after 2.5 weeks.":"After 2.5 weeks the model gives 2.5(2.5) + 40 = 46.25 cm, and 40 is the height at week 0."}},"m25":{"ask":"What is the largest whole number <i>x<\/i> that keeps 3(<i>x<\/i> − 4) at least as big as 5<i>x<\/i> + 2?","idea":"Solve an inequality like an equation, with one extra rule: if you multiply or divide both sides by a negative number, flip the inequality sign. Moving terms so <i>x<\/i> ends up with a positive coefficient avoids that.","steps":["Distribute: 3<i>x<\/i> − 12 ≥ 5<i>x<\/i> + 2.","Subtract 3<i>x<\/i> and 2 from both sides: −14 ≥ 2<i>x<\/i>.","Divide by 2 (positive, so no flip): −7 ≥ <i>x<\/i>, which means <i>x<\/i> ≤ −7.","The greatest integer that is ≤ −7 is −7 itself."],"check":"<i>x<\/i> = −7: 3(−11) = −33 and 5(−7) + 2 = −33, so −33 ≥ −33 ✓. <i>x<\/i> = −6: −30 ≥ −28 is false.","wrong":{"−8":"It satisfies the inequality, but it isn't the greatest: −7 is larger and also works.","7":"Loses the negative sign. Check: 3(3) = 9 is not ≥ 37.","8":"Loses the sign and rounds the wrong way. Check: 12 is not ≥ 42."}},"m26":{"ask":"A line crosses another at a right angle and goes through (4, 1). Where does it cross the <i>y<\/i>-axis?","idea":"Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals: flip the fraction and change its sign. Once you know the slope and one point, you can find the <i>y<\/i>-intercept <i>b<\/i> from <i>y<\/i> = <i>m<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>b<\/i>.","steps":["Find the given line's slope by solving for <i>y<\/i>: −5<i>y<\/i> = −2<i>x<\/i> + 10, so <i>y<\/i> = (2/5)<i>x<\/i> − 2. Its slope is 2/5.","Perpendicular slope: flip and change sign: −5/2.","Use the point (4, 1): 1 = (−5/2)(4) + <i>b<\/i> = −10 + <i>b<\/i>.","So <i>b<\/i> = 11."],"check":"Line ℓ: <i>y<\/i> = −(5/2)<i>x<\/i> + 11. At <i>x<\/i> = 4: −10 + 11 = 1 ✓. Slopes: (2/5)(−5/2) = −1, so they're perpendicular ✓","wrong":{"−9":"Uses slope +5/2 (flipped but no sign change): 1 = 10 + <i>b<\/i>.","1":"That's the <i>y<\/i>-coordinate of the given point, not the intercept.","9":"Doesn't work: with <i>b<\/i> = 9, the line gives −10 + 9 = −1 at <i>x<\/i> = 4, not 1."}},"m41":{"ask":"Find <i>x<\/i> so that 5<i>x<\/i> minus 8 equals 17.","idea":"Undo the operations in reverse order: 8 was subtracted last, so add 8 back first, then divide by 5.","steps":["Add 8 to both sides: 5<i>x<\/i> = 25.","Divide both sides by 5: <i>x<\/i> = 5."],"check":"5(5) − 8 = 25 − 8 = 17 ✓","wrong":{"1.8":"Comes from subtracting 8 instead of adding it (5<i>x<\/i> = 9).","9":"Divides only part of the equation. Check: 5(9) − 8 = 37.","25":"That's 5<i>x<\/i>; divide by 5 to get <i>x<\/i>."}},"m42":{"ask":"How much does a 7-mile taxi ride cost?","idea":"Total cost = the one-time base fee + (price per mile × number of miles). The per-mile price repeats; the base fee doesn't.","steps":["Mileage charge: $2 × 7 = $14.","Add the $3 base fee once: $14 + $3 = $17."],"check":"As a rule: C = 2<i>m<\/i> + 3, and 2(7) + 3 = 17 ✓","wrong":{"12":"Too low: the mileage alone is already $14.","14":"Forgets the $3 base fee.","35":"Charges $5 per mile, adding the base fee to every mile."}},"m43":{"ask":"Which point is on the line <i>y<\/i> = 3<i>x<\/i> − 2?","idea":"A point (<i>x<\/i>, <i>y<\/i>) is on a line if its coordinates make the equation true. Plug in the <i>x<\/i>-value, compute 3<i>x<\/i> − 2, and see whether you get the point's <i>y<\/i>-value.","steps":["Try (1, 1): 3(1) − 2 = 1. That matches <i>y<\/i> = 1.","So (1, 1) is on the line."],"check":"The others fail: (2, 5) gives 3(2) − 2 = 4 ≠ 5; (0, 2) gives −2 ≠ 2; (3, 6) gives 7 ≠ 6.","wrong":{"(2, 5)":"At <i>x<\/i> = 2 the line's <i>y<\/i> is 4, not 5.","(0, 2)":"The line crosses the <i>y<\/i>-axis at −2, not 2 (sign mistake).","(3, 6)":"At <i>x<\/i> = 3 the line's <i>y<\/i> is 7, not 6."}},"m44":{"ask":"The question asks for <i>x<\/i> + 3, not <i>x<\/i>.","idea":"Sometimes you can find what's asked without solving for <i>x<\/i>. Here the expression <i>x<\/i> + 3 appears as a group, so treat it as one thing.","steps":["2 times (<i>x<\/i> + 3) is 14.","Divide both sides by 2: <i>x<\/i> + 3 = 7. That's already the answer."],"check":"<i>x<\/i> = 4, and 2(4 + 3) = 14 ✓. So <i>x<\/i> + 3 = 7.","wrong":{"4":"That's <i>x<\/i>, but the question asks for <i>x<\/i> + 3.","11":"Doesn't work: if <i>x<\/i> + 3 were 11, then 2(<i>x<\/i> + 3) would be 22, not 14.","14":"That's 2(<i>x<\/i> + 3); divide by 2."}},"m45":{"ask":"When will Ari and Bea have the same amount of money?","idea":"Write an expression for each person's money after <i>w<\/i> weeks, then set the two expressions equal. Saving adds each week; spending subtracts.","steps":["Ari: 120 + 15<i>w<\/i>. Bea: 200 − 5<i>w<\/i>.","Set them equal: 120 + 15<i>w<\/i> = 200 − 5<i>w<\/i>.","Add 5<i>w<\/i> to both sides: 120 + 20<i>w<\/i> = 200.","Subtract 120: 20<i>w<\/i> = 80, so <i>w<\/i> = 4."],"check":"After 4 weeks: Ari 120 + 60 = $180, Bea 200 − 20 = $180 ✓","wrong":{"5":"After 5 weeks Ari has $195 and Bea $175.","8":"After 8 weeks Ari has $240 and Bea has $160, so Ari has already passed her.","16":"Divides the $80 gap by $5, ignoring Ari's saving."}},"m46":{"ask":"Where does the line cross the <i>y<\/i>-axis?","idea":"Every point on the <i>y<\/i>-axis has <i>x<\/i> = 0. So set <i>x<\/i> = 0 and solve for <i>y<\/i>.","steps":["Put <i>x<\/i> = 0: 4(0) + 2<i>y<\/i> = 12, so 2<i>y<\/i> = 12.","Divide by 2: <i>y<\/i> = 6. The point is (0, 6)."],"check":"4(0) + 2(6) = 12 ✓","wrong":{"(0, 3)":"Divides 12 by 4, which is the <i>x<\/i>-coefficient.","(3, 0)":"That's where the line crosses the <i>x<\/i>-axis (set <i>y<\/i> = 0), not the <i>y<\/i>-axis.","(0, 12)":"Forgets to divide by the 2 in front of <i>y<\/i>."}},"m47":{"ask":"Find the <i>x<\/i> that makes 3<i>x<\/i> + 4 and 4<i>x<\/i> − 5 equal.","idea":"Collect the <i>x<\/i> terms on one side and the numbers on the other. Subtracting the smaller <i>x<\/i> term keeps the <i>x<\/i> positive.","steps":["Subtract 3<i>x<\/i> from both sides: 4 = <i>x<\/i> − 5.","Add 5 to both sides: 9 = <i>x<\/i>."],"check":"3(9) + 4 = 31 and 4(9) − 5 = 31 ✓","wrong":{"−9":"A sign error. Check: 3(−9) + 4 = −23 but 4(−9) − 5 = −41.","1":"Subtracts 5 from 4 instead of adding. Check: 7 ≠ −1.","−1":"Subtracts the numbers the wrong way. Check: 1 ≠ −9."}},"m48":{"ask":"The school bought 35 items for $460. How many were $20 hoodies?","idea":"Two unknowns (hoodies and t-shirts) need two facts: one about the count of items and one about the money. Use the count to write t-shirts in terms of hoodies, then use the money equation.","steps":["Let <i>h<\/i> = hoodies. Then t-shirts = 35 − <i>h<\/i>.","Money: 20<i>h<\/i> + 8(35 − <i>h<\/i>) = 460.","Distribute: 20<i>h<\/i> + 280 − 8<i>h<\/i> = 460, so 12<i>h<\/i> + 280 = 460.","12<i>h<\/i> = 180, so <i>h<\/i> = 15."],"check":"15 hoodies + 20 t-shirts = 35 items; 15($20) + 20($8) = $300 + $160 = $460 ✓","wrong":{"20":"That's the number of t-shirts.","23":"Divides $460 by $20 as if every item were a hoodie.","12":"That's the price difference ($20 − $8), not a count."}},"m49":{"ask":"How many $3 snacks can the student buy after a $12 ticket, without going over $50?","idea":"“≤ 50” means the total can't be more than $50. Solve like an equation, then remember you can only buy whole snacks, so round <em>down<\/em>.","steps":["Subtract 12: 3<i>n<\/i> ≤ 38.","Divide by 3: <i>n<\/i> ≤ 12.67.","Whole snacks only, and you can't exceed the limit, so the greatest is 12."],"check":"12 snacks: 12 + 36 = $48 ≤ $50 ✓. 13 snacks: 12 + 39 = $51, too much.","wrong":{"13":"Rounds up: 13 snacks would cost $51.","16":"Ignores the ticket: 50 ÷ 3 ≈ 16.7.","21":"Adds the ticket price instead of subtracting it."}},"m50":{"ask":"Where do the line and the parabola meet? Add the <i>x<\/i>-values of those points.","idea":"At an intersection point, both equations give the same <i>y<\/i>. So set the two right sides equal, which gives one equation in <i>x<\/i>. Get 0 on one side and factor.","steps":["Set equal: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 2 = 2<i>x<\/i> + 1.","Move everything to one side: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 2<i>x<\/i> − 3 = 0.","Factor: find two numbers that multiply to −3 and add to −2: −3 and 1. So (<i>x<\/i> − 3)(<i>x<\/i> + 1) = 0.","<i>x<\/i> = 3 or <i>x<\/i> = −1. Their sum is 2."],"check":"<i>x<\/i> = 3: line gives 7, parabola gives 9 − 2 = 7 ✓. <i>x<\/i> = −1: line gives −1, parabola 1 − 2 = −1 ✓","wrong":{"−2":"Sign error on the sum. Shortcut: the sum of the solutions of <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + <i>b<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>c<\/i> = 0 is −<i>b<\/i> = 2.","3":"That's only one of the two <i>x<\/i>-values.","4":"That's the difference of the two <i>x<\/i>-values, 3 − (−1), not their sum."}},"m51":{"ask":"Where does the line with slope 3/4 through (8, 2) cross the <i>x<\/i>-axis?","idea":"The <i>x<\/i>-axis is where <i>y<\/i> = 0. Use the slope to move from the known point down to <i>y<\/i> = 0: slope 3/4 means <i>y<\/i> changes 3 for every 4 that <i>x<\/i> changes.","steps":["Write the line: <i>y<\/i> − 2 = (3/4)(<i>x<\/i> − 8).","Set <i>y<\/i> = 0: −2 = (3/4)(<i>x<\/i> − 8).","Multiply both sides by 4/3: <i>x<\/i> − 8 = −8/3.","Add 8 (= 24/3): <i>x<\/i> = 16/3."],"check":"From (16/3, 0) to (8, 2): <i>y<\/i> rises 2 while <i>x<\/i> increases 8 − 16/3 = 8/3. Slope = 2 ÷ (8/3) = 3/4 ✓","wrong":{"−8/3":"That's <i>x<\/i> − 8, not <i>x<\/i>. Add 8 back.","8":"That's the <i>x<\/i>-coordinate of the given point, where <i>y<\/i> = 2, not 0.","32/3":"Moves in the wrong direction: adds 8/3 instead of subtracting."}},"m52":{"ask":"For what <i>a<\/i> and <i>b<\/i> is the equation true for every <i>x<\/i>? Then add them.","idea":"An equation has infinitely many solutions when both sides are exactly the same expression. So the <i>x<\/i> parts must match and the constant parts must match.","steps":["Expand the right side: 2(3<i>x<\/i> + <i>b<\/i>) = 6<i>x<\/i> + 2<i>b<\/i>.","Match the <i>x<\/i> terms: <i>a<\/i><i>x<\/i> = 6<i>x<\/i>, so <i>a<\/i> = 6.","Match the constants: 6 = 2<i>b<\/i>, so <i>b<\/i> = 3.","<i>a<\/i> + <i>b<\/i> = 9."],"check":"6<i>x<\/i> + 6 = 2(3<i>x<\/i> + 3) = 6<i>x<\/i> + 6, identical for every <i>x<\/i> ✓","wrong":{"6":"That's <i>a<\/i> alone.","12":"Uses <i>b<\/i> = 6, forgetting to divide 6 by 2.","15":"Too big: matching the two sides forces <i>a<\/i> = 6 and <i>b<\/i> = 3."}},"m53":{"ask":"How many texts in total gave a $52.50 bill?","idea":"Only texts <em>beyond<\/em> 300 cost extra. Find how much of the bill is the extra charge, turn that into a number of extra texts, then add the 300 free texts back.","steps":["Extra charge: $52.50 − $40 = $12.50.","Extra texts: $12.50 ÷ $0.10 = 125.","Those are texts over 300, so the total is 300 + 125 = 425."],"check":"425 texts → 125 over 300 → 125 × $0.10 = $12.50, plus $40 = $52.50 ✓","wrong":{"125":"That's only the texts over 300. Add the first 300.","300":"That's the free allowance; the extra $12.50 paid for more.","525":"Divides the whole $52.50 by $0.10, as if every text cost extra and the $40 didn't exist."}},"m54":{"ask":"Find 2<i>x<\/i>, where <i>x<\/i> and <i>y<\/i> satisfy both equations.","idea":"The <i>y<\/i> terms are −2<i>y<\/i> and +2<i>y<\/i>, so adding the equations eliminates <i>y<\/i> immediately.","steps":["Add: (3<i>x<\/i> − 2<i>y<\/i>) + (<i>x<\/i> + 2<i>y<\/i>) = 7 + 9, so 4<i>x<\/i> = 16.","<i>x<\/i> = 4, so 2<i>x<\/i> = 8."],"check":"<i>x<\/i> = 4 gives 2<i>y<\/i> = 9 − 4 = 5, <i>y<\/i> = 2.5. First equation: 12 − 5 = 7 ✓","wrong":{"4":"That's <i>x<\/i>; the question asks for 2<i>x<\/i>.","16":"That's 4<i>x<\/i>.","2.5":"That's the value of <i>y<\/i>."}},"m55":{"ask":"A line goes through (−2, 5) and (4, −7). What is its output at <i>x<\/i> = 1?","idea":"A linear function is <i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) = <i>m<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>b<\/i>. Find the slope from the two points, then use one point to find <i>b<\/i>, then plug in <i>x<\/i> = 1.","steps":["Slope: (−7 − 5) ÷ (4 − (−2)) = −12 ÷ 6 = −2.","Use (−2, 5): 5 = −2(−2) + <i>b<\/i> = 4 + <i>b<\/i>, so <i>b<\/i> = 1.","<i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) = −2<i>x<\/i> + 1, so <i>f<\/i>(1) = −2 + 1 = −1."],"check":"Shortcut: <i>x<\/i> = 1 is halfway between −2 and 4, so <i>f<\/i>(1) is halfway between 5 and −7: (5 + (−7)) ÷ 2 = −1 ✓","wrong":{"−3":"That's <i>f<\/i>(2), one step too far.","1":"That's <i>b<\/i>, the value at <i>x<\/i> = 0.","3":"That's <i>f<\/i>(−1)."}},"m56":{"ask":"How many more adult tickets than student tickets were sold?","idea":"Write one equation for the number of tickets and one for the money. Solve for adult tickets, find student tickets, and then subtract, because the question asks for the difference.","steps":["Let <i>a<\/i> = adult tickets, so students = 180 − <i>a<\/i>.","Money: 12<i>a<\/i> + 7(180 − <i>a<\/i>) = 1,760, so 5<i>a<\/i> + 1,260 = 1,760.","5<i>a<\/i> = 500, so <i>a<\/i> = 100 adult and 80 student tickets.","Difference: 100 − 80 = 20."],"check":"100($12) + 80($7) = $1,200 + $560 = $1,760 ✓","wrong":{"80":"That's the number of student tickets.","100":"That's the number of adult tickets, not the difference.","180":"That's the total number of tickets."}},"m118":{"ask":"Find <i>x<\/i> when 4<i>x<\/i> equals 2<i>x<\/i> + 10.","idea":"Get all the <i>x<\/i> terms on one side. Subtracting 2<i>x<\/i> from both sides keeps the equation balanced and leaves <i>x<\/i> on the left only.","steps":["Subtract 2<i>x<\/i> from both sides: 4<i>x<\/i> − 2<i>x<\/i> = 10, so 2<i>x<\/i> = 10.","Divide by 2: <i>x<\/i> = 5."],"check":"4(5) = 20 and 2(5) + 10 = 20 ✓","wrong":{"2":"Doesn't work: 4(2) = 8 but 2(2) + 10 = 14.","10":"That's 2<i>x<\/i>; divide by 2.","20":"That's 4<i>x<\/i> when <i>x<\/i> = 5, not <i>x<\/i> itself."}},"m119":{"ask":"Write a rule for the water left in the pool after <i>m<\/i> minutes.","idea":"Start with the amount at time 0, then subtract the amount lost each minute times the number of minutes. Draining means subtracting.","steps":["Starting amount: 500 gallons.","Each minute 12 gallons leave, so after <i>m<\/i> minutes, 12<i>m<\/i> gallons are gone.","Remaining: <i>g<\/i> = 500 − 12<i>m<\/i>."],"check":"After 10 minutes: 500 − 120 = 380 gallons, which makes sense for a draining pool ✓","wrong":{"<i>g<\/i> = 500 + 12<i>m<\/i>":"Adds water, as if the pool were filling.","<i>g<\/i> = 12<i>m<\/i> − 500":"Gives −500 gallons at the start.","<i>g<\/i> = 512<i>m<\/i>":"Adds the two numbers together, which has no meaning here."}},"m120":{"ask":"Find <i>y<\/i> when both equations are true.","idea":"The second equation already says what <i>x<\/i> equals in terms of <i>y<\/i>. Substitute <i>y<\/i> + 4 in place of <i>x<\/i> in the first equation, leaving an equation with only <i>y<\/i>.","steps":["Replace <i>x<\/i> with (<i>y<\/i> + 4): 2(<i>y<\/i> + 4) + 3<i>y<\/i> = 18.","Distribute: 2<i>y<\/i> + 8 + 3<i>y<\/i> = 18, so 5<i>y<\/i> + 8 = 18.","5<i>y<\/i> = 10, so <i>y<\/i> = 2."],"check":"<i>x<\/i> = 2 + 4 = 6. Then 2(6) + 3(2) = 12 + 6 = 18 ✓","wrong":{"2.8":"Forgets to multiply the 4 by 2: 5<i>y<\/i> + 4 = 18 gives <i>y<\/i> = 2.8.","4":"That's the number added in <i>x<\/i> = <i>y<\/i> + 4, not <i>y<\/i>.","6":"That's the value of <i>x<\/i>."}},"m133":{"ask":"Find <i>x<\/i> when twice (<i>x<\/i> + 5) is 24.","idea":"Undo the outside operation first: divide both sides by 2 to remove the multiplication, then subtract 5.","steps":["Divide by 2: <i>x<\/i> + 5 = 12.","Subtract 5: <i>x<\/i> = 7."],"check":"2(7 + 5) = 2(12) = 24 ✓","wrong":{"2":"Doesn't work: 2(2 + 5) = 14.","12":"That's <i>x<\/i> + 5, not <i>x<\/i>.","17":"Adds 5 instead of subtracting it."}},"m134":{"ask":"How long until the battery drops from 100% to 60%?","idea":"The battery loses charge at a steady rate: 4 percentage points every 10 minutes. Find the total drop needed, then count how many 10-minute blocks that takes.","steps":["Total drop: 100 − 60 = 40 percentage points.","Blocks needed: 40 ÷ 4 = 10 blocks of 10 minutes.","Time: 10 × 10 = 100 minutes."],"check":"After 100 minutes: 100 − 4 × 10 = 60% ✓","wrong":{"40":"That's the drop in percentage points, not minutes.","60":"That's the target battery level, not the time.","160":"Not a valid time: 160 minutes would drain 64 points, leaving 36%."}},"m135":{"ask":"Find <i>x<\/i> + <i>y<\/i> when both equations are true.","idea":"Solve the first equation for <i>y<\/i> (it has a lone −<i>y<\/i>), substitute into the second, and solve for <i>x<\/i>. Then find <i>y<\/i> and add.","steps":["From 3<i>x<\/i> − <i>y<\/i> = 11: <i>y<\/i> = 3<i>x<\/i> − 11.","Substitute: <i>x<\/i> + 2(3<i>x<\/i> − 11) = 20, so 7<i>x<\/i> − 22 = 20 and 7<i>x<\/i> = 42.","<i>x<\/i> = 6, and <i>y<\/i> = 3(6) − 11 = 7.","<i>x<\/i> + <i>y<\/i> = 13."],"check":"3(6) − 7 = 11 ✓ and 6 + 2(7) = 20 ✓","wrong":{"6":"That's <i>x<\/i> alone.","7":"That's <i>y<\/i> alone.","19":"Too big: <i>x<\/i> = 6 and <i>y<\/i> = 7 add to 13."}},"m136":{"ask":"In a group of 12 that paid $84, how many were children?","idea":"Two unknowns need two facts: the number of people (12) and the money ($84). Write adults in terms of children, then use the money equation.","steps":["Let <i>c<\/i> = children, so adults = 12 − <i>c<\/i>.","Money: 9(12 − <i>c<\/i>) + 5<i>c<\/i> = 84.","Distribute: 108 − 9<i>c<\/i> + 5<i>c<\/i> = 84, so 108 − 4<i>c<\/i> = 84.","−4<i>c<\/i> = −24, so <i>c<\/i> = 6."],"check":"6 children and 6 adults: 6($9) + 6($5) = $54 + $30 = $84 ✓","wrong":{"4":"Doesn't work: 4 children and 8 adults pay $20 + $72 = $92.","8":"Doesn't work: 8 children and 4 adults pay $40 + $36 = $76.","9":"That's the adult price, not a number of people."}},"mx1":{"ask":"The table shows a line's values. At what input <i>k<\/i> does the output reach −21?","idea":"A linear function changes by the same amount for each step in <i>x<\/i>. Find that rate (the slope) from the table, build the rule <i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) = <i>m<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>b<\/i>, then solve <i>f<\/i>(<i>k<\/i>) = −21. The table's <i>x<\/i>-values aren't 1 apart, so divide by the change in <i>x<\/i>.","steps":["Slope: from <i>x<\/i> = −2 to <i>x<\/i> = 1, <i>f<\/i> goes from 13 to 7: (7 − 13) ÷ (1 − (−2)) = −6 ÷ 3 = −2.","Use (1, 7): 7 = −2(1) + <i>b<\/i>, so <i>b<\/i> = 9, and <i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) = −2<i>x<\/i> + 9.","Set −2<i>k<\/i> + 9 = −21: −2<i>k<\/i> = −30, so <i>k<\/i> = 15."],"check":"<i>f<\/i>(15) = −30 + 9 = −21 ✓. Also, from (7, −5), the output must drop 16 more; at −2 per step that's 8 more steps: 7 + 8 = 15 ✓","wrong":{"−6":"That's the change in <i>f<\/i> between the first two columns, not an input.","6":"Doesn't work: <i>f<\/i>(6) = −12 + 9 = −3, not −21.","13.5":"Doesn't work: <i>f<\/i>(13.5) = −27 + 9 = −18, not −21."}},"mx2":{"ask":"Line <i>m<\/i> is at a right angle to the graphed line ℓ and goes through (6, −2). Where does <i>m<\/i> cross the <i>y<\/i>-axis?","idea":"Perpendicular lines have negative reciprocal slopes: flip the fraction and change its sign. Read ℓ's slope from two clear points on the graph, then use the point (6, −2) to find <i>m<\/i>'s <i>y<\/i>-intercept.","steps":["From the graph, ℓ rises 6 (from −3 to 3) while running 4, so its slope is 6 ÷ 4 = 3/2.","Line <i>m<\/i>'s slope: flip and change sign: −2/3.","Use (6, −2): −2 = (−2/3)(6) + <i>b<\/i> = −4 + <i>b<\/i>, so <i>b<\/i> = 2."],"check":"Slopes multiply to (3/2)(−2/3) = −1, so the lines are perpendicular ✓. At <i>x<\/i> = 6, <i>m<\/i> gives −4 + 2 = −2 ✓","wrong":{"−3":"That's where line ℓ crosses the <i>y<\/i>-axis, not line <i>m<\/i>.","−2":"That's the <i>y<\/i>-coordinate of the given point.","10":"Doesn't work: a line with slope −2/3 and intercept 10 is at −4 + 10 = 6 when <i>x<\/i> = 6, not −2."}},"mx3":{"ask":"For which <i>a<\/i> are the two lines parallel (so they never meet)?","idea":"A system has no solution when the lines are parallel and different. For <i>A<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>B<\/i><i>y<\/i> = <i>C<\/i> equations, that happens when the <i>x<\/i>- and <i>y<\/i>-coefficients are in the same ratio but the constants are not.","steps":["The <i>y<\/i>-coefficients are 3 and −6, a ratio of 3/(−6) = −1/2.","The <i>x<\/i>-coefficients must have the same ratio: <i>a<\/i>/4 = −1/2, so <i>a<\/i> = −2.","Check the constants: 7/5 is not −1/2, so the lines are different, not the same line."],"check":"With <i>a<\/i> = −2: both lines have slope 2/3, with <i>y<\/i>-intercepts 7/3 and −5/6. Parallel, so no solution ✓","wrong":{"2":"Gives slope −2/3 for the first line and 2/3 for the second; they cross.","−8":"Uses the ratio the wrong way round (4/<i>a<\/i> = −1/2).","8":"Gives the first line slope −8/3 and the second 2/3, so they cross once."}},"mx4":{"ask":"For which <i>k<\/i> are the two equations the same line?","idea":"Infinitely many solutions means the equations describe the same line: one is an exact multiple of the other, including the constant on the right.","steps":["Compare the <i>x<\/i>-coefficients and constants: 12 = 4 × 3 and 32 = 4 × 8. So the second equation must be 4 times the first.","4 times the first: 12<i>x<\/i> − 20<i>y<\/i> = 32.","So <i>k<\/i> = −20."],"check":"With <i>k<\/i> = −20, dividing the second equation by 4 gives exactly 3<i>x<\/i> − 5<i>y<\/i> = 8 ✓","wrong":{"20":"Drops the negative sign: 4 × (−5) = −20.","−5":"Copies the coefficient without multiplying by 4.","32":"That's the constant on the right side, not <i>k<\/i>."}},"mx5":{"ask":"What's the most total data Nadia can use while keeping her bill at $100 or less?","idea":"Only data beyond the first 5 GB costs extra. Let <i>g<\/i> be the total data. The extra is <i>g<\/i> − 5, so the bill is 40 + 12(<i>g<\/i> − 5). “At most” means ≤.","steps":["Inequality: 40 + 12(<i>g<\/i> − 5) ≤ 100.","Subtract 40: 12(<i>g<\/i> − 5) ≤ 60.","Divide by 12: <i>g<\/i> − 5 ≤ 5.","Add 5: <i>g<\/i> ≤ 10 GB total."],"check":"10 GB: 5 extra GB × $12 = $60, plus $40 = $100 ✓","wrong":{"5":"That's the extra data only; add the 5 GB included in the plan.","8":"Doesn't use the full budget: 8 GB costs 40 + 36 = $76, and more data still fits.","13":"Too much: 13 GB costs 40 + 96 = $136."}},"mx6":{"ask":"Find 11<i>x<\/i>, where <i>x<\/i> and <i>y<\/i> satisfy both equations.","idea":"Look for a way to cancel <i>y<\/i> directly. The <i>y<\/i>-terms are +2<i>y<\/i> and −4<i>y<\/i>. Doubling the first equation makes +4<i>y<\/i>, which cancels with −4<i>y<\/i> when you add. The question asks for 11<i>x<\/i>, which is exactly what's left.","steps":["Double the first equation: 6<i>x<\/i> + 4<i>y<\/i> = 24.","Add the second: (6<i>x<\/i> + 4<i>y<\/i>) + (5<i>x<\/i> − 4<i>y<\/i>) = 24 + 9.","11<i>x<\/i> = 33. That's the answer; no need to find <i>x<\/i>."],"check":"<i>x<\/i> = 3, then 2<i>y<\/i> = 12 − 9 = 3, so <i>y<\/i> = 1.5. Second equation: 15 − 6 = 9 ✓","wrong":{"3":"That's <i>x<\/i>; the question asks for 11<i>x<\/i>.","21":"Adds the equations without doubling the first: 8<i>x<\/i> − 2<i>y<\/i> = 21, which doesn't eliminate <i>y<\/i>.","11":"That's the coefficient of <i>x<\/i>, not the value of 11<i>x<\/i>."}},"mx7":{"ask":"Find where the two lines cross, then add the coordinates.","idea":"At the crossing point, both equations give the same <i>y<\/i>. Set 2<i>x<\/i> − 1 equal to −<i>x<\/i> + 5 to find <i>x<\/i>, then substitute to find <i>y<\/i>. You can use the graph to check.","steps":["Set equal: 2<i>x<\/i> − 1 = −<i>x<\/i> + 5.","Add <i>x<\/i> and add 1: 3<i>x<\/i> = 6, so <i>x<\/i> = 2.","<i>y<\/i> = 2(2) − 1 = 3.","<i>x<\/i> + <i>y<\/i> = 5."],"check":"Second line: −2 + 5 = 3 ✓, matching the crossing point (2, 3) on the graph.","wrong":{"3":"That's <i>y<\/i> alone.","2":"That's <i>x<\/i> alone.","6":"That's 3<i>x<\/i>, before dividing by 3."}},"mx8":{"ask":"On the line through (−2, 5) and (4, −7), where is <i>y<\/i> = −15?","idea":"Find the line's equation from the two points (slope, then <i>y<\/i>-intercept), then set <i>y<\/i> = −15 and solve for <i>x<\/i>.","steps":["Slope: (−7 − 5) ÷ (4 − (−2)) = −12 ÷ 6 = −2.","Use (−2, 5): 5 = −2(−2) + <i>b<\/i>, so <i>b<\/i> = 1, and <i>y<\/i> = −2<i>x<\/i> + 1.","Set −15 = −2<i>x<\/i> + 1: −2<i>x<\/i> = −16, so <i>x<\/i> = 8."],"check":"<i>y<\/i> = −2(8) + 1 = −15 ✓","wrong":{"−8":"Sign error when dividing by −2.","7":"Doesn't work: −2(7) + 1 = −13, not −15.","10":"Doesn't work: −2(10) + 1 = −19."}},"mx9":{"ask":"How many whole numbers <i>x<\/i> make 2<i>x<\/i> − 3 less than 7 units away from 0?","idea":"|stuff| &lt; 7 means “stuff” is between −7 and 7. So write it as a double inequality and solve the middle. The endpoints are not included because the sign is &lt;, not ≤.","steps":["Rewrite: −7 &lt; 2<i>x<\/i> − 3 &lt; 7.","Add 3 to all three parts: −4 &lt; 2<i>x<\/i> &lt; 10.","Divide by 2: −2 &lt; <i>x<\/i> &lt; 5.","Integers strictly between −2 and 5: −1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4. That's 6."],"check":"<i>x<\/i> = −2 gives |−7| = 7, which is not &lt; 7, so −2 is excluded; <i>x<\/i> = 5 gives |7| = 7, also excluded ✓","wrong":{"7":"Includes one endpoint, but |2<i>x<\/i> − 3| = 7 there, which isn't less than 7.","5":"Misses one integer; count carefully: −1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4.","4":"Only counts from 1 to 4, forgetting −1 and 0."}},"mx10":{"ask":"When the two tanks hold equal amounts, how many liters are in each? (The question wants liters, not minutes.)","idea":"Write each tank's amount after <i>t<\/i> minutes: draining subtracts, filling adds. Set them equal to find the time, then substitute back to find the amount.","steps":["Tank A: 400 − 12<i>t<\/i>. Tank B: 150 + 8<i>t<\/i>.","Set equal: 400 − 12<i>t<\/i> = 150 + 8<i>t<\/i>, so 250 = 20<i>t<\/i> and <i>t<\/i> = 12.5 minutes.","Amount: 400 − 12(12.5) = 400 − 150 = 250 liters."],"check":"Tank B: 150 + 8(12.5) = 150 + 100 = 250 ✓","wrong":{"12.5":"That's the time in minutes, not the amount in liters.","150":"That's Tank B's starting amount.","275":"Averages the starting amounts ((400 + 150) ÷ 2), but the tanks change at different rates."}},"mx11":{"ask":"How much of the 30% solution goes into 18 liters of a 20% mix?","idea":"In a mixture, the amount of salt adds up. Salt in a solution = (percent as a decimal) × (liters). Let <i>y<\/i> be the liters of 30% solution; the rest, 18 − <i>y<\/i>, is the 12% solution.","steps":["Salt equation: 0.12(18 − <i>y<\/i>) + 0.30<i>y<\/i> = 0.20(18).","Simplify: 2.16 − 0.12<i>y<\/i> + 0.30<i>y<\/i> = 3.6, so 2.16 + 0.18<i>y<\/i> = 3.6.","0.18<i>y<\/i> = 1.44, so <i>y<\/i> = 8 liters."],"check":"8 L of 30% has 2.4 L salt; 10 L of 12% has 1.2 L salt. Total 3.6 L in 18 L = 20% ✓","wrong":{"10":"That's the amount of the 12% solution.","6":"Doesn't work: 6 L of 30% and 12 L of 12% give 3.24 L of salt, only 18%.","9":"Splits the mix evenly, but 20% is closer to 12% than to 30%, so less than half should be the 30% solution."}},"mx12":{"ask":"Which equation fits all three pairs in the table?","idea":"Find the slope from two pairs, then the <i>y<\/i>-intercept from one pair. The <i>x<\/i>-values aren't evenly spaced by 1, so divide the change in <i>y<\/i> by the change in <i>x<\/i>. Always check the third pair.","steps":["Slope: (19 − 11) ÷ (7 − 3) = 8 ÷ 4 = 2.","Use (3, 11): 11 = 2(3) + <i>b<\/i>, so <i>b<\/i> = 5.","Equation: <i>y<\/i> = 2<i>x<\/i> + 5."],"check":"(15, 35): 2(15) + 5 = 35 ✓","wrong":{"<i>y<\/i> = 2<i>x<\/i> + 11":"Uses 11 as the intercept, but 11 is the <i>y<\/i>-value at <i>x<\/i> = 3, not at <i>x<\/i> = 0.","<i>y<\/i> = 4<i>x<\/i> − 1":"Works for (3, 11) only: at <i>x<\/i> = 7 it gives 27, not 19.","<i>y<\/i> = <i>x<\/i> + 8":"Works for (3, 11) only: at <i>x<\/i> = 7 it gives 15, not 19."}},"mx13":{"ask":"Find <i>x<\/i> from two equations with fractions.","idea":"Clear fractions first by multiplying each equation by its denominator. Then the equations become <i>x<\/i> + <i>y<\/i> = 18 and <i>x<\/i> − <i>y<\/i> = 6, and adding them cancels <i>y<\/i>.","steps":["Multiply the first by 2: <i>x<\/i> + <i>y<\/i> = 18. Multiply the second by 3: <i>x<\/i> − <i>y<\/i> = 6.","Add: 2<i>x<\/i> = 24, so <i>x<\/i> = 12."],"check":"<i>y<\/i> = 6. Then (12 + 6)/2 = 9 ✓ and (12 − 6)/3 = 2 ✓","wrong":{"6":"That's <i>y<\/i>, or <i>x<\/i> − <i>y<\/i>.","18":"That's <i>x<\/i> + <i>y<\/i>.","15":"Doesn't work: then <i>y<\/i> = 3, and (15 − 3)/3 = 4, not 2."}},"mx14":{"ask":"A line parallel to 4<i>x<\/i> − 3<i>y<\/i> = 12 goes through (3, 5). Where does it cross the <i>x<\/i>-axis?","idea":"Parallel lines have the same slope. Find the slope of the given line, write line <i>j<\/i> through (3, 5), and set <i>y<\/i> = 0 for the <i>x<\/i>-axis.","steps":["Solve for <i>y<\/i>: −3<i>y<\/i> = −4<i>x<\/i> + 12, so <i>y<\/i> = (4/3)<i>x<\/i> − 4. Slope 4/3.","Line <i>j<\/i>: <i>y<\/i> − 5 = (4/3)(<i>x<\/i> − 3).","Set <i>y<\/i> = 0: −5 = (4/3)(<i>x<\/i> − 3), so <i>x<\/i> − 3 = −15/4.","<i>x<\/i> = 3 − 15/4 = −3/4."],"check":"From (−3/4, 0) to (3, 5): rise 5, run 15/4. Slope = 5 ÷ (15/4) = 4/3 ✓","wrong":{"3/4":"Sign error at the end: 3 − 15/4 is negative.","3":"That's where the original line 4<i>x<\/i> − 3<i>y<\/i> = 12 crosses the <i>x<\/i>-axis, not line <i>j<\/i>.","−15/4":"That's <i>x<\/i> − 3; add 3 back."}},"mx15":{"ask":"<i>x<\/i> = −1 makes the equation true. What must <i>c<\/i> be?","idea":"If a value is a solution, substituting it makes both sides equal. Plug in <i>x<\/i> = −1, and the only unknown left is <i>c<\/i>.","steps":["Left side: 2(−1 − <i>c<\/i>) + 5 = −2 − 2<i>c<\/i> + 5 = 3 − 2<i>c<\/i>.","Right side: 3(−1) − 4 = −7.","3 − 2<i>c<\/i> = −7, so −2<i>c<\/i> = −10 and <i>c<\/i> = 5."],"check":"2(−1 − 5) + 5 = −12 + 5 = −7 ✓","wrong":{"−5":"Sign error dividing by −2.","2":"Doesn't work: 2(−3) + 5 = −1, not −7.","−2":"Doesn't work: 2(1) + 5 = 7, not −7."}},"mx16":{"ask":"Which point is in the region where both inequalities are true?","idea":"Test each point in both inequalities. Pay attention to the symbols: ≤ allows equality, but &gt; does not. A point must pass both tests.","steps":["Test (0, 4): 4 ≤ −0 + 4 → 4 ≤ 4, true. 4 &gt; 2(0) − 5 → 4 &gt; −5, true.","Both are true, so (0, 4) is in the solution region."],"check":"It lies on the boundary of the first inequality, which is allowed because of ≤ ✓","wrong":{"(3, 1)":"1 ≤ 1 is true, but 1 &gt; 2(3) − 5 = 1 is false: equality isn't allowed with &gt;.","(4, 0)":"0 ≤ 0 is true, but 0 &gt; 3 is false.","(−2, 7)":"7 ≤ 6 is false."}},"m7":{"ask":"What does the function give when you put in 4?","idea":"<i>f<\/i>(4) means “replace every <i>x<\/i> with 4.” Follow the order of operations: square first, then multiply, then subtract.","steps":["Replace <i>x<\/i> with 4: <i>f<\/i>(4) = 4<sup>2<\/sup> − 3(4).","Square: 4<sup>2<\/sup> = 16. Multiply: 3(4) = 12.","Subtract: 16 − 12 = 4."],"check":"Same result by factoring: <i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) = <i>x<\/i>(<i>x<\/i> − 3), and 4(4 − 3) = 4 ✓","wrong":{"7":"Doesn't work out: 16 − 12 = 4, not 7.","16":"That's only 4<sup>2<\/sup>; you still subtract 3(4).","28":"Adds 12 instead of subtracting it."}},"m27":{"ask":"Multiply out 3<i>x<\/i>(<i>x<\/i> + 4).","idea":"Distributing means multiplying the outside term by <em>each<\/em> term inside the parentheses. When you multiply <i>x<\/i> by <i>x<\/i>, you get <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","steps":["3<i>x<\/i> times <i>x<\/i> = 3<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","3<i>x<\/i> times 4 = 12<i>x<\/i>.","Add them: 3<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 12<i>x<\/i>."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 2: 3(2)(2 + 4) = 36, and 3(4) + 12(2) = 12 + 24 = 36 ✓","wrong":{"3<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 4":"Multiplies 3<i>x<\/i> by <i>x<\/i> but forgets to multiply it by 4.","3<i>x<\/i> + 12":"That's 3(<i>x<\/i> + 4): it multiplies by 3 but drops the <i>x<\/i> outside the parentheses.","4<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 12<i>x<\/i>":"Adds 3<i>x<\/i> and <i>x<\/i> to get 4<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> instead of multiplying."}},"m8":{"ask":"Solve <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 5<i>x<\/i> + 6 = 0 and add the two solutions.","idea":"Factor by finding two numbers that multiply to the constant (6) and add to the middle coefficient (−5). Each factor set to zero gives a solution. Shortcut: for <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + <i>b<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>c<\/i> = 0, the solutions always add to −<i>b<\/i>.","steps":["Two numbers that multiply to 6 and add to −5: −2 and −3.","Factor: (<i>x<\/i> − 2)(<i>x<\/i> − 3) = 0, so <i>x<\/i> = 2 or <i>x<\/i> = 3.","Sum: 2 + 3 = 5."],"check":"Shortcut: −<i>b<\/i> = −(−5) = 5 ✓","wrong":{"−5":"That's the middle coefficient; the sum is its opposite.","1":"That's the difference 3 − 2, not the sum.","6":"That's the product of the solutions, not the sum."}},"m9":{"ask":"Multiply out (<i>x<\/i> + 3)<sup>2<\/sup>.","idea":"Squaring means multiplying the expression by itself: (<i>x<\/i> + 3)(<i>x<\/i> + 3). Multiply every term by every term. The result always has a middle term: (<i>a<\/i> + <i>b<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>a<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 2<i>ab<\/i> + <i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","steps":["Write it out: (<i>x<\/i> + 3)(<i>x<\/i> + 3).","Multiply: <i>x<\/i>·<i>x<\/i> = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>, <i>x<\/i>·3 = 3<i>x<\/i>, 3·<i>x<\/i> = 3<i>x<\/i>, 3·3 = 9.","Combine: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 6<i>x<\/i> + 9."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 1: (1 + 3)<sup>2<\/sup> = 16, and 1 + 6 + 9 = 16 ✓","wrong":{"<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 9":"Squares each term and forgets the middle term 6<i>x<\/i>. Check <i>x<\/i> = 1: gives 10, not 16.","<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 3<i>x<\/i> + 9":"Counts the middle term only once; there are two 3<i>x<\/i> terms.","<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 6<i>x<\/i> + 6":"Doubles the 3 instead of squaring it: 3<sup>2<\/sup> = 9."}},"m28":{"ask":"<i>x<\/i> is negative and <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 49. What is <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>?","idea":"A squared number is 49 if the number is 7 or −7, since both give 49. The condition <i>x<\/i> &lt; 0 picks −7. Cubing a negative number stays negative.","steps":["<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 49 means <i>x<\/i> = 7 or <i>x<\/i> = −7.","<i>x<\/i> &lt; 0, so <i>x<\/i> = −7.","(−7)<sup>3<\/sup> = (−7)(−7)(−7) = 49 × (−7) = −343."],"check":"(−7)<sup>2<\/sup> = 49 ✓ and −7 &lt; 0 ✓","wrong":{"−49":"That's −<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>, not <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>.","49":"That's <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","343":"That's 7<sup>3<\/sup>, using the positive value; but <i>x<\/i> must be negative."}},"m10":{"ask":"What is the smallest value this function ever reaches?","idea":"A squared quantity is never negative; its smallest value is 0. In <i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) = 2(<i>x<\/i> − 1)<sup>2<\/sup> + 5, the squared part is 0 when <i>x<\/i> = 1, so the smallest output is 2(0) + 5. This is vertex form: <i>a<\/i>(<i>x<\/i> − <i>h<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> + <i>k<\/i> has vertex (<i>h<\/i>, <i>k<\/i>), and with <i>a<\/i> &gt; 0 the minimum is <i>k<\/i>.","steps":["The squared term (<i>x<\/i> − 1)<sup>2<\/sup> is at least 0.","It equals 0 when <i>x<\/i> = 1.","Then <i>f<\/i>(1) = 2(0) + 5 = 5, and no output can be smaller."],"check":"<i>f<\/i>(0) = 7 and <i>f<\/i>(2) = 7, both bigger than 5 ✓","wrong":{"1":"That's where the minimum happens (<i>x<\/i> = 1), not the minimum value.","2":"That's the stretch factor <i>a<\/i>.","7":"That's <i>f<\/i>(0) or <i>f<\/i>(2), not the lowest value."}},"m11":{"ask":"Which rule gives the population after <i>t<\/i> hours if it doubles every 3 hours?","idea":"Exponential growth multiplies by the same factor each period. Here the factor is 2 and the period is 3 hours. After <i>t<\/i> hours, the number of 3-hour periods is <i>t<\/i>/3, so you multiply by 2 that many times.","steps":["Starting amount: 800.","Number of doublings after <i>t<\/i> hours: <i>t<\/i> ÷ 3.","Population: 800 · 2<sup><i>t<\/i>/3<\/sup>."],"check":"<i>t<\/i> = 3: 800 · 2<sup>1<\/sup> = 1,600 (one doubling) ✓. <i>t<\/i> = 6: 800 · 2<sup>2<\/sup> = 3,200 ✓","wrong":{"800 · 2<sup>3<i>t<\/i><\/sup>":"Doubles 3 times every hour. At <i>t<\/i> = 3 it gives 800 · 512.","800 · 3<sup><i>t<\/i>/2<\/sup>":"Swaps the roles of 2 and 3; the population would triple, not double.","800 + 2<i>t<\/i>/3":"Adds instead of multiplying, which is linear growth, not doubling."}},"m29":{"ask":"For which <i>c<\/i> does the parabola touch the <i>x<\/i>-axis at exactly one point?","idea":"The discriminant, <i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4<i>a<\/i><i>c<\/i>, tells how many real zeros a quadratic has: positive means two, zero means one, negative means none. Set it equal to 0.","steps":["Here <i>a<\/i> = 1, <i>b<\/i> = −6 and the constant is <i>c<\/i>.","Discriminant: (−6)<sup>2<\/sup> − 4(1)(<i>c<\/i>) = 36 − 4<i>c<\/i>.","Set to 0: 36 − 4<i>c<\/i> = 0, so <i>c<\/i> = 9."],"check":"<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 6<i>x<\/i> + 9 = (<i>x<\/i> − 3)<sup>2<\/sup>, which is 0 only at <i>x<\/i> = 3 ✓","wrong":{"3":"That's where the single zero is (<i>x<\/i> = 3), not <i>c<\/i>.","6":"That's the size of the middle coefficient.","36":"That's <i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>; divide by 4."}},"m30":{"ask":"Where is the turning point (vertex) of the parabola, left to right?","idea":"A parabola is symmetric, so its vertex sits exactly halfway between its two <i>x<\/i>-intercepts. In factored form (<i>x<\/i> − <i>r<\/i>)(<i>x<\/i> − <i>s<\/i>), the intercepts are <i>r<\/i> and <i>s<\/i>. Watch the signs.","steps":["Zeros: <i>x<\/i> − 3 = 0 gives 3; <i>x<\/i> + 5 = 0 gives −5.","Halfway: (3 + (−5)) ÷ 2 = −2 ÷ 2 = −1."],"check":"Expanded: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 2<i>x<\/i> − 15. Vertex <i>x<\/i> = −<i>b<\/i>/(2<i>a<\/i>) = −2/2 = −1 ✓","wrong":{"−5":"That's one of the zeros, not the midpoint.","1":"Reads the zeros with flipped signs (−3 and 5).","3":"That's the other zero."}},"m31":{"ask":"Find <i>x<\/i> when 4<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup> equals 8<sup><i>x<\/i> − 1<\/sup>.","idea":"To compare exponents, rewrite both sides with the same base. Both 4 and 8 are powers of 2: 4 = 2<sup>2<\/sup> and 8 = 2<sup>3<\/sup>. A power of a power multiplies the exponents. Once the bases match, the exponents must be equal.","steps":["4<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup> = (2<sup>2<\/sup>)<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup> = 2<sup>2x<\/sup>.","8<sup><i>x<\/i> − 1<\/sup> = (2<sup>3<\/sup>)<sup><i>x<\/i> − 1<\/sup> = 2<sup>3x − 3<\/sup>.","Set exponents equal: 2<i>x<\/i> = 3<i>x<\/i> − 3, so <i>x<\/i> = 3."],"check":"4<sup>3<\/sup> = 64 and 8<sup>2<\/sup> = 64 ✓","wrong":{"1":"Doesn't work: 4<sup>1<\/sup> = 4 but 8<sup>0<\/sup> = 1.","2":"Doesn't work: 4<sup>2<\/sup> = 16 but 8<sup>1<\/sup> = 8.","6":"Doesn't work: 4<sup>6<\/sup> = 4,096 but 8<sup>5<\/sup> = 32,768."}},"m32":{"ask":"How high does the ball go at its highest point?","idea":"With a negative <i>t<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> coefficient, the parabola opens down, so its highest point is the vertex. The vertex happens at <i>t<\/i> = −<i>b<\/i> ÷ (2<i>a<\/i>). Then plug that time in to get the height.","steps":["<i>a<\/i> = −16, <i>b<\/i> = 64: <i>t<\/i> = −64 ÷ (2 × −16) = −64 ÷ −32 = 2 seconds.","Height: <i>h<\/i>(2) = −16(4) + 64(2) + 5 = −64 + 128 + 5 = 69 feet."],"check":"<i>h<\/i>(1) = 53 and <i>h<\/i>(3) = 53, both lower and equal on either side of 2 ✓","wrong":{"64":"That's the coefficient <i>b<\/i>, not a height.","80":"Too high: the most the ball ever reaches is the vertex height, <i>h<\/i>(2) = 69.","101":"Too high: the most the ball ever reaches is the vertex height, <i>h<\/i>(2) = 69."}},"m57":{"ask":"What is <i>g<\/i>(−3)?","idea":"Substitute −3 for <i>x<\/i>, and square it <em>with<\/em> its negative sign: (−3)<sup>2<\/sup> = 9, not −9. Order of operations: square, then multiply by 2, then add 1.","steps":["(−3)<sup>2<\/sup> = 9.","2(9) = 18.","18 + 1 = 19."],"check":"<i>g<\/i>(3) is also 19, as expected, because squaring removes the sign ✓","wrong":{"−17":"Squares −3 as −9: 2(−9) + 1.","13":"Doesn't work: (−3)<sup>2<\/sup> = 9, so 2(9) + 1 = 19.","37":"Squares 2 × (−3) = −6 to get 36, then adds 1: that's (2<i>x<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> + 1, not 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 1."}},"m58":{"ask":"Factor <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 16.","idea":"<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 16 is a <strong>difference of squares<\/strong>: something squared minus something squared. The pattern is <i>a<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − <i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = (<i>a<\/i> − <i>b<\/i>)(<i>a<\/i> + <i>b<\/i>). Here 16 = 4<sup>2<\/sup>.","steps":["Recognize <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4<sup>2<\/sup>.","Apply the pattern: (<i>x<\/i> − 4)(<i>x<\/i> + 4)."],"check":"Expand: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 4<i>x<\/i> − 4<i>x<\/i> − 16 = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 16 ✓","wrong":{"(<i>x<\/i> − 4)<sup>2<\/sup>":"Expands to <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 8<i>x<\/i> + 16, which has a middle term and +16.","(<i>x<\/i> − 8)(<i>x<\/i> + 8)":"Expands to <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 64; 8 is half of 16, not its square root.","(<i>x<\/i> + 4)<sup>2<\/sup>":"Expands to <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 8<i>x<\/i> + 16."}},"m59":{"ask":"Simplify <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> times <i>x<\/i><sup>4<\/sup>.","idea":"<i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> means three <i>x<\/i>'s multiplied together, and <i>x<\/i><sup>4<\/sup> means four. Multiplied together, that's seven <i>x<\/i>'s. So when multiplying powers of the same base, <em>add<\/em> the exponents.","steps":["<i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> · <i>x<\/i><sup>4<\/sup> = (<i>x<\/i>·<i>x<\/i>·<i>x<\/i>)(<i>x<\/i>·<i>x<\/i>·<i>x<\/i>·<i>x<\/i>).","That's 7 factors of <i>x<\/i>: <i>x<\/i><sup>7<\/sup>."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 2: 8 × 16 = 128 = 2<sup>7<\/sup> ✓","wrong":{"<i>x<\/i><sup>12<\/sup>":"Multiplies the exponents; that rule is for a power of a power, like (<i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>)<sup>4<\/sup>.","2<i>x<\/i><sup>7<\/sup>":"There's no 2: you're multiplying the powers, not adding them.","<i>x<\/i>":"Subtracts exponents, which is the rule for dividing."}},"m60":{"ask":"Which input makes the output 12?","idea":"“<i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) = 12” means the <em>output<\/em> is 12, so set 5<i>x<\/i> − 3 = 12 and solve for the input <i>x<\/i>. It's different from <i>f<\/i>(12), which means the input is 12.","steps":["Set 5<i>x<\/i> − 3 = 12.","Add 3: 5<i>x<\/i> = 15.","Divide by 5: <i>x<\/i> = 3."],"check":"<i>f<\/i>(3) = 15 − 3 = 12 ✓","wrong":{"9":"Doesn't work: <i>f<\/i>(9) = 45 − 3 = 42, not 12.","57":"That's <i>f<\/i>(12), which puts 12 in as the input.","1.8":"Subtracts 3 instead of adding: 5<i>x<\/i> = 9."}},"m61":{"ask":"Solve <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 2<i>x<\/i> − 15 = 0.","idea":"Factor by finding two numbers that multiply to −15 and add to +2. Then each factor equals 0 gives a solution, with the opposite sign of the number in the factor.","steps":["Numbers that multiply to −15 and add to 2: 5 and −3.","Factor: (<i>x<\/i> + 5)(<i>x<\/i> − 3) = 0.","<i>x<\/i> + 5 = 0 gives <i>x<\/i> = −5; <i>x<\/i> − 3 = 0 gives <i>x<\/i> = 3."],"check":"(−5)<sup>2<\/sup> + 2(−5) − 15 = 25 − 10 − 15 = 0 ✓; 9 + 6 − 15 = 0 ✓","wrong":{"<i>x<\/i> = 5 and <i>x<\/i> = −3":"Uses the numbers from the factors without flipping their signs.","<i>x<\/i> = −15 and <i>x<\/i> = 1":"These multiply to −15 but add to −14, not 2.","<i>x<\/i> = 15 and <i>x<\/i> = −1":"These multiply to −15 but add to 14, not 2."}},"m62":{"ask":"Which rule shows 2,000 fish losing 10% each year?","idea":"Losing 10% each year means keeping 90% each year. Keeping 90% means multiplying by 0.9, once per year. After <i>t<\/i> years, that's multiplying by 0.9 a total of <i>t<\/i> times: (0.9)<sup><i>t<\/i><\/sup>.","steps":["Each year: new = old × (1 − 0.10) = old × 0.9.","After <i>t<\/i> years: 2,000(0.9)<sup><i>t<\/i><\/sup>."],"check":"After 1 year: 2,000 × 0.9 = 1,800, which is 10% fewer ✓","wrong":{"2,000(0.1)<sup><i>t<\/i><\/sup>":"Keeps only 10% each year, which means losing 90%.","2,000(1.1)<sup><i>t<\/i><\/sup>":"Grows by 10% each year.","2,000 − 0.1<i>t<\/i>":"Subtracts a tiny fixed amount; it's not a percent decrease."}},"m63":{"ask":"Find <i>x<\/i> when (<i>x<\/i> + 2) divided by (<i>x<\/i> − 1) equals 4.","idea":"Clear the fraction by multiplying both sides by the denominator (<i>x<\/i> − 1). Then solve the resulting linear equation. Check that your answer doesn't make the denominator 0.","steps":["Multiply both sides by (<i>x<\/i> − 1): <i>x<\/i> + 2 = 4(<i>x<\/i> − 1) = 4<i>x<\/i> − 4.","Subtract <i>x<\/i> and add 4: 6 = 3<i>x<\/i>.","<i>x<\/i> = 2."],"check":"(2 + 2)/(2 − 1) = 4/1 = 4 ✓, and the denominator isn't 0.","wrong":{"−2":"Makes the top 0, so the fraction equals 0, not 4.","1":"Makes the denominator 0, so the expression is undefined.","6":"That's 3<i>x<\/i>, before dividing by 3."}},"m64":{"ask":"How low does the parabola go? Find the <i>y<\/i>-value of its vertex.","idea":"For <i>y<\/i> = <i>a<\/i><i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + <i>b<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>c<\/i>, the vertex is at <i>x<\/i> = −<i>b<\/i> ÷ (2<i>a<\/i>). The <i>y<\/i>-value there, <i>k<\/i>, is found by plugging that <i>x<\/i> into the equation.","steps":["<i>a<\/i> = 1, <i>b<\/i> = −8: <i>h<\/i> = 8 ÷ 2 = 4.","<i>k<\/i> = 4<sup>2<\/sup> − 8(4) + 7 = 16 − 32 + 7 = −9."],"check":"Completing the square: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 8<i>x<\/i> + 7 = (<i>x<\/i> − 4)<sup>2<\/sup> − 9 ✓","wrong":{"4":"That's <i>h<\/i>, the <i>x<\/i>-coordinate of the vertex.","7":"That's the <i>y<\/i>-intercept (the value at <i>x<\/i> = 0).","−16":"Forgets the +7: 16 − 32 = −16."}},"m65":{"ask":"Solve the equation, take the larger solution, and square it.","idea":"Factor: find two numbers that multiply to 12 and add to −7. Then pick the larger root and follow the last instruction: square it.","steps":["Numbers: −3 and −4 (product 12, sum −7). Factor: (<i>x<\/i> − 3)(<i>x<\/i> − 4) = 0.","Solutions: 3 and 4. The larger is <i>r<\/i> = 4.","<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 16."],"check":"4<sup>2<\/sup> − 7(4) + 12 = 16 − 28 + 12 = 0 ✓","wrong":{"9":"Squares the smaller solution, 3.","12":"That's the constant term, which equals the product of the solutions.","49":"Squares 7, the sum of the solutions."}},"m66":{"ask":"Multiply out (2<i>x<\/i> + 3)(<i>x<\/i> − 4). What is the <i>x<\/i>-coefficient?","idea":"Multiply every term in the first parentheses by every term in the second. The middle term <i>b<\/i><i>x<\/i> comes from two products, the “outer” and “inner” ones, which you then combine.","steps":["2<i>x<\/i> · <i>x<\/i> = 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> and 2<i>x<\/i> · (−4) = −8<i>x<\/i>.","3 · <i>x<\/i> = 3<i>x<\/i> and 3 · (−4) = −12.","Combine the <i>x<\/i> terms: −8<i>x<\/i> + 3<i>x<\/i> = −5<i>x<\/i>. So <i>b<\/i> = −5."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 1: (5)(−3) = −15, and 2 − 5 − 12 = −15 ✓","wrong":{"−8":"Uses only the outer product, forgetting +3<i>x<\/i>.","3":"Uses only the inner product, forgetting −8<i>x<\/i>.","11":"Adds 8 and 3 without the negative sign."}},"m67":{"ask":"What is $1,000 worth after 3 years of 5% interest compounded once a year?","idea":"“Compounded” means each year's interest is added to the balance, and next year's interest is earned on that bigger balance. So you multiply by 1.05 each year: 1,000(1.05)<sup>3<\/sup>. Simple interest (5% of $1,000 each year) is smaller.","steps":["Year 1: 1,000 × 1.05 = 1,050.","Year 2: 1,050 × 1.05 = 1,102.50.","Year 3: 1,102.50 × 1.05 = 1,157.63, about $1,158."],"check":"1,000(1.05)<sup>3<\/sup> = 1,000 × 1.157625 = 1,157.63 ✓","wrong":{"$1,150":"That's simple interest: $50 each year, without earning interest on interest.","$1,500":"Uses 50% instead of 5% per year.","$1,050":"That's the value after only 1 year."}},"m68":{"ask":"For which positive <i>k<\/i> does the equation have exactly one solution?","idea":"A quadratic has exactly one real solution when its discriminant, <i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4<i>ac<\/i>, equals 0. That's also when the quadratic is a perfect square.","steps":["Here <i>a<\/i> = 1, <i>b<\/i> = <i>k<\/i>, <i>c<\/i> = 25.","Discriminant: <i>k<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4(1)(25) = <i>k<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 100 = 0.","<i>k<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 100, so <i>k<\/i> = 10 (the positive value)."],"check":"<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 10<i>x<\/i> + 25 = (<i>x<\/i> + 5)<sup>2<\/sup>, which is 0 only at <i>x<\/i> = −5 ✓","wrong":{"5":"That's the square root of 25, but the middle coefficient is 2 × 5.","25":"Copies the constant term.","50":"Uses 2 × 25 instead of 2 × √25."}},"m69":{"ask":"Solve √(<i>x<\/i> + 7) = <i>x<\/i> − 5.","idea":"Square both sides to remove the square root, then solve the quadratic. Squaring can create a false solution, because a square root is never negative. So check every answer in the original equation.","steps":["Square both sides: <i>x<\/i> + 7 = (<i>x<\/i> − 5)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 10<i>x<\/i> + 25.","Rearrange: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 11<i>x<\/i> + 18 = 0, so (<i>x<\/i> − 2)(<i>x<\/i> − 9) = 0.","Candidates: <i>x<\/i> = 2 and <i>x<\/i> = 9.","Check <i>x<\/i> = 2: √9 = 3, but 2 − 5 = −3. Fails. Check <i>x<\/i> = 9: √16 = 4 and 9 − 5 = 4. Works."],"check":"Only <i>x<\/i> = 9 satisfies the original equation ✓","wrong":{"2":"It solves the squared equation but not the original: √9 is 3, not −3.","2 and 9":"Keeps the false solution 2 without checking.","11":"That's the sum of the candidates, not a solution. Check: √18 ≠ 6."}},"m70":{"ask":"For which <i>x<\/i> does 3 · 2<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup> equal 96?","idea":"Isolate the power first by dividing by 3. Then write the result as a power of 2 and match exponents.","steps":["Divide by 3: 2<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup> = 32.","32 = 2<sup>5<\/sup>, since 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 32.","So <i>x<\/i> = 5."],"check":"3 · 2<sup>5<\/sup> = 3 × 32 = 96 ✓","wrong":{"4":"Doesn't work: 3 · 2<sup>4<\/sup> = 48.","16":"Far too big: 2<sup>16<\/sup> is 65,536.","32":"That's 2<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup>, not <i>x<\/i>."}},"m71":{"ask":"How many of the function's zeros lie between −2 and 3?","idea":"In factored form, each factor (<i>x<\/i> − <i>r<\/i>) gives a zero at <i>x<\/i> = <i>r<\/i> (flip the sign). The graph crosses the <i>x<\/i>-axis at each zero. Then count only those inside the interval −2 &lt; <i>x<\/i> &lt; 3.","steps":["Zeros: <i>x<\/i> = 2, <i>x<\/i> = −1 and <i>x<\/i> = 5.","Inside −2 &lt; <i>x<\/i> &lt; 3: 2 yes, −1 yes, 5 no.","So there are 2."],"check":"<i>p<\/i>(2) = 0 and <i>p<\/i>(−1) = 0 ✓; 5 is outside the interval.","wrong":{"1":"Misses one: both −1 and 2 are between −2 and 3.","3":"Counts all three zeros, but 5 is outside the interval.","0":"There are zeros in the interval: −1 and 2 are both between −2 and 3."}},"m115":{"ask":"What is the output when the input is 0?","idea":"<i>f<\/i>(0) means replace <i>x<\/i> with 0. Anything times 0 is 0, so only the constant is left. That's also where the graph crosses the <i>y<\/i>-axis.","steps":["<i>f<\/i>(0) = 3(0) − 4.","= 0 − 4 = −4."],"check":"The graph of <i>y<\/i> = 3<i>x<\/i> − 4 crosses the <i>y<\/i>-axis at −4 ✓","wrong":{"0":"The 3<i>x<\/i> part is 0, but the −4 remains.","3":"That's the slope.","4":"Drops the negative sign."}},"m116":{"ask":"Simplify (2<i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>)<sup>2<\/sup>.","idea":"Squaring a product squares <em>each<\/em> factor: the 2 and the <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>. A power of a power multiplies the exponents: (<i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup>6<\/sup>.","steps":["Square the number: 2<sup>2<\/sup> = 4.","Square the power: (<i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup>3·2<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup>6<\/sup>.","Result: 4<i>x<\/i><sup>6<\/sup>."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 1: (2)<sup>2<\/sup> = 4, and 4(1) = 4 ✓. Try <i>x<\/i> = 2: (16)<sup>2<\/sup> = 256 and 4(64) = 256 ✓","wrong":{"2<i>x<\/i><sup>6<\/sup>":"Forgets to square the 2.","4<i>x<\/i><sup>5<\/sup>":"Adds the exponents (3 + 2) instead of multiplying.","2<i>x<\/i><sup>9<\/sup>":"Cubes the exponent and forgets to square the 2."}},"m117":{"ask":"Where is the lowest point of the parabola, left to right?","idea":"Because <i>a<\/i> is positive, the parabola opens upward, so its minimum is at the vertex. A parabola is symmetric, so the vertex is halfway between its <i>x<\/i>-intercepts.","steps":["Zeros from the factors: <i>x<\/i> = 2 and <i>x<\/i> = −6.","Halfway: (2 + (−6)) ÷ 2 = −4 ÷ 2 = −2."],"check":"Expanded: <i>a<\/i>(<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 4<i>x<\/i> − 12); vertex at <i>x<\/i> = −4/2 = −2 ✓","wrong":{"−6":"That's one zero, not the midpoint.","2":"That's the other zero.","6":"Reads a zero with the wrong sign."}},"m137":{"ask":"Find <i>f<\/i>(0) and <i>f<\/i>(2), then add them.","idea":"Evaluate the function at each input separately, then add the two outputs. Don't add the inputs first.","steps":["<i>f<\/i>(0) = 3(0) − 4 = −4.","<i>f<\/i>(2) = 3(2) − 4 = 2.","Sum: −4 + 2 = −2."],"check":"Note <i>f<\/i>(0 + 2) = <i>f<\/i>(2) = 2, which is different: adding the inputs first gives the wrong answer.","wrong":{"−6":"Uses <i>f<\/i>(2) = −2 by mistake. Check: 3(2) − 4 = 2.","2":"That's <i>f<\/i>(2) alone, or <i>f<\/i>(0 + 2).","6":"Drops both negative signs."}},"m138":{"ask":"Which equation crosses the <i>x<\/i>-axis at <i>x<\/i> = −2 and <i>x<\/i> = 5?","idea":"A factor (<i>x<\/i> − <i>r<\/i>) makes the expression 0 when <i>x<\/i> = <i>r<\/i>. So an intercept at 5 needs (<i>x<\/i> − 5), and an intercept at −2 needs (<i>x<\/i> − (−2)) = (<i>x<\/i> + 2). The signs in the factors are opposite to the intercepts.","steps":["Intercept at −2 → factor (<i>x<\/i> + 2).","Intercept at 5 → factor (<i>x<\/i> − 5).","Equation: <i>y<\/i> = (<i>x<\/i> + 2)(<i>x<\/i> − 5)."],"check":"<i>x<\/i> = −2: (0)(−7) = 0 ✓. <i>x<\/i> = 5: (7)(0) = 0 ✓","wrong":{"<i>y<\/i> = (<i>x<\/i> − 2)(<i>x<\/i> + 5)":"Crosses at 2 and −5; the signs are flipped.","<i>y<\/i> = (<i>x<\/i> + 2)(<i>x<\/i> + 5)":"Crosses at −2 and −5.","<i>y<\/i> = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 10":"Never crosses the <i>x<\/i>-axis; it's always at least 10."}},"m139":{"ask":"Find <i>x<\/i> when 2<sup>3x<\/sup> = 64.","idea":"Write 64 as a power of 2. When both sides have the same base, the exponents must be equal.","steps":["64 = 2<sup>6<\/sup> (2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64).","So 3<i>x<\/i> = 6.","<i>x<\/i> = 2."],"check":"2<sup>3·2<\/sup> = 2<sup>6<\/sup> = 64 ✓","wrong":{"3":"Doesn't work: 2<sup>9<\/sup> = 512.","6":"That's 3<i>x<\/i>, the exponent; divide by 3.","32":"That's 64 ÷ 2, which isn't how exponents work."}},"m140":{"ask":"Find the stretch factor <i>a<\/i> for the parabola with vertex (2, 1) through (0, 5).","idea":"The vertex form <i>y<\/i> = <i>a<\/i>(<i>x<\/i> − 2)<sup>2<\/sup> + 1 already uses the vertex. The only unknown is <i>a<\/i>, so plug in the other point (0, 5) and solve.","steps":["Substitute <i>x<\/i> = 0, <i>y<\/i> = 5: 5 = <i>a<\/i>(0 − 2)<sup>2<\/sup> + 1.","(−2)<sup>2<\/sup> = 4: 5 = 4<i>a<\/i> + 1.","4<i>a<\/i> = 4, so <i>a<\/i> = 1."],"check":"<i>y<\/i> = (<i>x<\/i> − 2)<sup>2<\/sup> + 1 at <i>x<\/i> = 0: 4 + 1 = 5 ✓","wrong":{"2":"That's the vertex's <i>x<\/i>-coordinate.","4":"That's (0 − 2)<sup>2<\/sup>, or 4<i>a<\/i>; divide by 4.","5":"That's the <i>y<\/i>-value of the given point."}},"mx17":{"ask":"Read the parabola's vertex and one more point from the graph, find <i>a<\/i>, <i>h<\/i> and <i>k<\/i>, then add them.","idea":"In vertex form <i>a<\/i>(<i>x<\/i> − <i>h<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> + <i>k<\/i>, the vertex is (<i>h<\/i>, <i>k<\/i>), so you can read <i>h<\/i> and <i>k<\/i> straight from the graph. To find <i>a<\/i>, plug in any other point on the graph, like an <i>x<\/i>-intercept.","steps":["The vertex is (3, −4), so <i>h<\/i> = 3 and <i>k<\/i> = −4.","The graph crosses the <i>x<\/i>-axis at (1, 0). Substitute: 0 = <i>a<\/i>(1 − 3)<sup>2<\/sup> − 4 = 4<i>a<\/i> − 4.","4<i>a<\/i> = 4, so <i>a<\/i> = 1.","<i>a<\/i> + <i>h<\/i> + <i>k<\/i> = 1 + 3 − 4 = 0."],"check":"The other intercept (5, 0): 1(5 − 3)<sup>2<\/sup> − 4 = 0 ✓","wrong":{"8":"Uses <i>k<\/i> = +4 instead of −4: 1 + 3 + 4.","−2":"Doesn't match the graph: with vertex (3, −4) and <i>a<\/i> = 1, the sum is 1 + 3 − 4 = 0.","4":"Leaves out <i>k<\/i> = −4: 1 + 3 = 4."}},"mx18":{"ask":"For which <i>c<\/i> do the parabola and the line touch at exactly one point?","idea":"Where the graphs meet, their <i>y<\/i>-values are equal. Setting the equations equal gives a quadratic; the number of intersection points equals its number of real solutions. Exactly one solution means the discriminant <i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4<i>ac<\/i> is 0.","steps":["Set equal: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 6<i>x<\/i> + <i>c<\/i> = 2<i>x<\/i>.","Rearrange: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 8<i>x<\/i> + <i>c<\/i> = 0.","Discriminant: (−8)<sup>2<\/sup> − 4(1)(<i>c<\/i>) = 64 − 4<i>c<\/i> = 0.","<i>c<\/i> = 16."],"check":"<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 8<i>x<\/i> + 16 = (<i>x<\/i> − 4)<sup>2<\/sup>, which is 0 only at <i>x<\/i> = 4 ✓","wrong":{"9":"Uses the original −6 instead of combining to −8: 36 − 4<i>c<\/i> = 0.","8":"That's the size of the middle coefficient, not <i>c<\/i>.","64":"That's <i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>; divide by 4."}},"mx19":{"ask":"The line crosses the circle twice. Add the <i>x<\/i>-values of those two points.","idea":"Substitute the line into the circle's equation to get one equation in <i>x<\/i>. For a quadratic <i>a<\/i><i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + <i>b<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>c<\/i> = 0, the solutions add to −<i>b<\/i>/<i>a<\/i>, so you don't even need to solve it.","steps":["Replace <i>y<\/i> with <i>x<\/i> + 1: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + (<i>x<\/i> + 1)<sup>2<\/sup> = 25.","Expand: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 2<i>x<\/i> + 1 = 25, so 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 2<i>x<\/i> − 24 = 0.","Divide by 2: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + <i>x<\/i> − 12 = 0.","Sum of solutions: −<i>b<\/i>/<i>a<\/i> = −1."],"check":"Factor: (<i>x<\/i> + 4)(<i>x<\/i> − 3) = 0, so <i>x<\/i> = −4 and 3, and −4 + 3 = −1 ✓","wrong":{"1":"Forgets the minus sign in −<i>b<\/i>/<i>a<\/i>.","7":"That's the difference 3 − (−4), not the sum.","−12":"That's the product of the solutions (<i>c<\/i>/<i>a<\/i>), not the sum."}},"mx20":{"ask":"Which equation matches the table of values?","idea":"Check how the values change. If they drop by the same <em>amount<\/em> each year, the model is linear. If they drop by the same <em>percent<\/em> (the same ratio), it's exponential: <i>V<\/i> = (start) × (ratio)<sup><i>t<\/i><\/sup>. Keeping 85% each year means losing 15%.","steps":["Differences: 800 − 680 = 120, but 680 − 578 = 102. Not constant, so not linear.","Ratios: 680 ÷ 800 = 0.85 and 578 ÷ 680 = 0.85. Constant, so exponential with factor 0.85.","Starting value (at <i>t<\/i> = 0): 800. So <i>V<\/i> = 800(0.85)<sup><i>t<\/i><\/sup>."],"check":"<i>t<\/i> = 3: 800 × 0.85<sup>3<\/sup> = 800 × 0.614125 = 491.30 ✓","wrong":{"<i>V<\/i> = 800 − 120<i>t<\/i>":"Assumes a constant drop of $120, which only fits the first year; at <i>t<\/i> = 2 it gives 560, not 578.","<i>V<\/i> = 800(0.15)<sup><i>t<\/i><\/sup>":"Uses the percent lost (15%) as the multiplier instead of the percent kept (85%). At <i>t<\/i> = 1 it gives 120.","<i>V<\/i> = 680(0.85)<sup><i>t<\/i><\/sup>":"Starts at the year-1 value; at <i>t<\/i> = 0 it gives 680, not 800."}},"mx21":{"ask":"Solve an equation where both sides share the factor (<i>x<\/i> − 4), given that <i>x<\/i> isn't 4.","idea":"Because <i>x<\/i> ≠ 4, the factor (<i>x<\/i> − 4) isn't zero, so you can safely divide both sides by it. (Dividing by something that might be zero is what you must avoid.)","steps":["Divide both sides by (<i>x<\/i> − 4): <i>x<\/i> + 2 = 2<i>x<\/i> − 5.","Subtract <i>x<\/i> and add 5: 7 = <i>x<\/i>."],"check":"Left: (3)(9) = 27. Right: (3)(9) = 27 ✓","wrong":{"4":"Excluded by the problem (<i>x<\/i> ≠ 4), even though it makes both sides 0.","−2":"Makes the left side 0 but the right side (−6)(−9) = 54.","3":"Doesn't work: left (−1)(5) = −5, right (−1)(1) = −1."}},"mx22":{"ask":"<i>g<\/i> is <i>f<\/i> shifted. What is <i>g<\/i>(5)?","idea":"<i>g<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) = <i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i> − 2) means: to find <i>g<\/i> at an input, subtract 2 from the input and use <i>f<\/i>. So <i>g<\/i>(5) = <i>f<\/i>(3).","steps":["<i>g<\/i>(5) = <i>f<\/i>(5 − 2) = <i>f<\/i>(3).","<i>f<\/i>(3) = 3 · 2<sup>3<\/sup> = 3 × 8 = 24."],"check":"Shifting right by 2 means <i>g<\/i> reaches at <i>x<\/i> = 5 what <i>f<\/i> reached at <i>x<\/i> = 3 ✓","wrong":{"96":"That's <i>f<\/i>(5), without the shift.","12":"That's <i>f<\/i>(2), subtracting too much.","48":"That's <i>f<\/i>(4), shifting by only 1."}},"mx23":{"ask":"Solve an equation with fractions whose denominators contain <i>x<\/i>.","idea":"Notice <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4 = (<i>x<\/i> − 2)(<i>x<\/i> + 2), a difference of squares, so it's the common denominator. Multiply everything by it to clear the fractions. Then reject any answer that makes a denominator zero (<i>x<\/i> = 2 or −2).","steps":["Multiply each term by (<i>x<\/i> − 2)(<i>x<\/i> + 2): 5(<i>x<\/i> + 2) − 3(<i>x<\/i> − 2) = 16.","Expand: 5<i>x<\/i> + 10 − 3<i>x<\/i> + 6 = 16, so 2<i>x<\/i> + 16 = 16.","2<i>x<\/i> = 0, so <i>x<\/i> = 0. It doesn't make any denominator zero, so it's allowed."],"check":"5/(−2) − 3/2 = −2.5 − 1.5 = −4, and 16/(0 − 4) = −4 ✓","wrong":{"2":"Makes <i>x<\/i> − 2 = 0, so two of the fractions are undefined.","−2":"Makes <i>x<\/i> + 2 = 0, so two of the fractions are undefined.","8":"Doesn't work: the left side is 5/6 − 3/10 = 8/15, but the right side is 16/60 = 4/15."}},"mx24":{"ask":"Find all the zeros of the cubic and give the largest.","idea":"Knowing one factor, (<i>x<\/i> − 1), lets you divide the cubic to get a quadratic, which you can factor. A factor (<i>x<\/i> − <i>r<\/i>) gives the zero <i>x<\/i> = <i>r<\/i>.","steps":["Divide <i>p<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) by (<i>x<\/i> − 1): <i>p<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) = (<i>x<\/i> − 1)(<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − <i>x<\/i> − 6).","Factor the quadratic: two numbers that multiply to −6 and add to −1 are −3 and 2: (<i>x<\/i> − 3)(<i>x<\/i> + 2).","Zeros: 1, 3 and −2. The greatest is 3."],"check":"<i>p<\/i>(3) = 27 − 18 − 15 + 6 = 0 ✓","wrong":{"1":"That's the zero you were given, not the greatest.","6":"That's the constant term. Check: <i>p<\/i>(6) = 216 − 72 − 30 + 6 = 120.","−2":"That's the smallest zero."}},"mx25":{"ask":"How many times does the graph reach a height of 9?","idea":"The solutions of <i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) = 9 are the <i>x<\/i>-values where the graph is at height 9. Picture a horizontal line at <i>y<\/i> = 9 and count how many times it touches the graph.","steps":["The parabola opens downward with its highest point at (2, 9).","The line <i>y<\/i> = 9 touches the graph only at the top, the vertex.","So there is exactly one solution, <i>x<\/i> = 2."],"check":"Any height below 9 would be hit twice, and any height above 9 never ✓","wrong":{"2":"That's true for heights below the maximum, but 9 is the maximum itself.","0":"The graph does reach 9, at its vertex.","3":"A parabola can meet a horizontal line at most twice."}},"mx26":{"ask":"Find <i>x<\/i> in an equation with powers of 9, 3 and 27.","idea":"Rewrite everything as a power of the same base, 3: 9 = 3<sup>2<\/sup> and 27 = 3<sup>3<\/sup>. Then use the exponent rules: a power of a power multiplies exponents, and multiplying powers adds exponents. Finally, set the exponents equal.","steps":["9<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup> = (3<sup>2<\/sup>)<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup> = 3<sup>2x<\/sup>. And 27<sup>3<\/sup> = (3<sup>3<\/sup>)<sup>3<\/sup> = 3<sup>9<\/sup>.","Left side: 3<sup>2x<\/sup> · 3<sup>x + 2<\/sup> = 3<sup>3x + 2<\/sup>.","Set exponents equal: 3<i>x<\/i> + 2 = 9, so 3<i>x<\/i> = 7 and <i>x<\/i> = 7/3."],"check":"3<i>x<\/i> + 2 = 7 + 2 = 9 ✓","wrong":{"3":"Gives 3<sup>11<\/sup> on the left, not 3<sup>9<\/sup>.","7":"That's 3<i>x<\/i>; divide by 3.","1/3":"Gives 3<sup>3<\/sup> on the left; it uses 27 instead of 27<sup>3<\/sup>."}},"mx27":{"ask":"Use three points on the parabola to find <i>a<\/i>.","idea":"Each known value gives an equation. <i>f<\/i>(0) gives <i>c<\/i> right away because the other terms vanish. The equations for <i>f<\/i>(1) and <i>f<\/i>(−1) then have only <i>a<\/i> and <i>b<\/i>, and adding them cancels <i>b<\/i>.","steps":["<i>f<\/i>(0) = <i>c<\/i> = 3.","<i>f<\/i>(1) = <i>a<\/i> + <i>b<\/i> + 3 = 6, so <i>a<\/i> + <i>b<\/i> = 3.","<i>f<\/i>(−1) = <i>a<\/i> − <i>b<\/i> + 3 = 4, so <i>a<\/i> − <i>b<\/i> = 1.","Add: 2<i>a<\/i> = 4, so <i>a<\/i> = 2 (and <i>b<\/i> = 1)."],"check":"<i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) = 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + <i>x<\/i> + 3: <i>f<\/i>(1) = 6 ✓, <i>f<\/i>(−1) = 4 ✓","wrong":{"1":"That's <i>b<\/i>.","3":"That's <i>c<\/i>.","4":"That's 2<i>a<\/i>; divide by 2."}},"mx28":{"ask":"What is the highest value this function reaches?","idea":"The <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> coefficient is negative, so the parabola opens downward and its highest point is the vertex. Find the vertex's <i>x<\/i> with −<i>b<\/i> ÷ (2<i>a<\/i>), then plug it in: the maximum is the <em>output<\/em> there.","steps":["<i>x<\/i> = −12 ÷ (2 × −3) = −12 ÷ −6 = 2.","<i>f<\/i>(2) = −3(4) + 12(2) − 5 = −12 + 24 − 5 = 7."],"check":"<i>f<\/i>(1) = 4 and <i>f<\/i>(3) = 4, both lower than 7 ✓","wrong":{"2":"That's where the maximum occurs, not the maximum value.","−5":"That's <i>f<\/i>(0), the <i>y<\/i>-intercept.","17":"Too high: the vertex value, <i>f<\/i>(2) = 7, is the largest the function gets."}},"mx29":{"ask":"The population follows a pattern. What will it be in year 4?","idea":"Check whether the growth is a constant amount (linear) or a constant percent (exponential). Here the increases get bigger (400, then 432), so test ratios instead. A constant ratio means you keep multiplying by it.","steps":["Ratios: 5,400 ÷ 5,000 = 1.08 and 5,832 ÷ 5,400 = 1.08. Growth is 8% per year.","Year 3: 5,832 × 1.08 = 6,298.56.","Year 4: 6,298.56 × 1.08 = 6,802.44, about 6,802."],"check":"5,000 × 1.08<sup>4<\/sup> = 5,000 × 1.36049 ≈ 6,802 ✓","wrong":{"6,600":"Adds 400 each year (linear), but the increases are growing.","6,264":"Adds 432 each year, the second increase, which is still linear.","7,200":"Too high: 5,000 × 1.08<sup>4<\/sup> ≈ 6,802."}},"mx30":{"ask":"Simplify the product of two fractions.","idea":"Factor every numerator and denominator. Then any factor that appears on both the top and bottom cancels (it divides to 1). The restrictions <i>x<\/i> ≠ 3, −3 and 2 tell you these factors are never zero.","steps":["<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 5<i>x<\/i> + 6 = (<i>x<\/i> − 2)(<i>x<\/i> − 3).","<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 9 = (<i>x<\/i> − 3)(<i>x<\/i> + 3).","The product is [(<i>x<\/i> − 2)(<i>x<\/i> − 3) · (<i>x<\/i> + 3)] ÷ [(<i>x<\/i> − 3)(<i>x<\/i> + 3) · (<i>x<\/i> − 2)].","Every factor cancels, leaving 1."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 4: (16 − 20 + 6)/(16 − 9) · (7/2) = (2/7)(7/2) = 1 ✓","wrong":{"<i>x<\/i> − 3":"Cancels (<i>x<\/i> − 3) from the bottom but leaves it on the top; it appears on both.","(<i>x<\/i> − 3)/(<i>x<\/i> + 3)":"That's the first fraction simplified, before multiplying by the second.","<i>x<\/i> + 3":"Leaves (<i>x<\/i> + 3) uncanceled; it appears on both top and bottom."}},"mx31":{"ask":"For which <i>k<\/i> does the equation have two different real solutions?","idea":"Two distinct real solutions means the discriminant is positive: <i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4<i>ac<\/i> &gt; 0. Here that gives <i>k<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> &gt; 36. A square is bigger than 36 when the number is bigger than 6 <em>or<\/em> less than −6.","steps":["Discriminant: <i>k<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4(1)(9) = <i>k<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 36.","Need <i>k<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 36 &gt; 0, so <i>k<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> &gt; 36.","That's true when <i>k<\/i> &gt; 6 or <i>k<\/i> &lt; −6."],"check":"<i>k<\/i> = −7: 49 − 36 = 13 &gt; 0 ✓. <i>k<\/i> = 0: −36 &lt; 0, no real solutions.","wrong":{"−6 &lt; <i>k<\/i> &lt; 6":"That's where the discriminant is negative: no real solutions.","<i>k<\/i> &gt; 6 only":"Forgets negative values: <i>k<\/i> = −7 also works, since (−7)<sup>2<\/sup> = 49.","<i>k<\/i> = 6 or <i>k<\/i> = −6":"Makes the discriminant exactly 0, which gives one solution, not two."}},"mx32":{"ask":"When does the ball reach its highest point? (The question asks for a time, not a height.)","idea":"The height formula is a downward parabola (negative <i>t<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> coefficient), so the highest point is at the vertex. The vertex time is <i>t<\/i> = −<i>b<\/i> ÷ (2<i>a<\/i>).","steps":["<i>a<\/i> = −4.9 and <i>b<\/i> = 29.4.","<i>t<\/i> = −29.4 ÷ (2 × −4.9) = −29.4 ÷ −9.8 = 3 seconds."],"check":"<i>h<\/i>(2) = <i>h<\/i>(4) = 41.2, equal heights on either side of 3 ✓","wrong":{"6":"Doubles the vertex time; around then the ball is back near launch height.","46.1":"That's the maximum height in meters, <i>h<\/i>(3), not the time.","2":"That's the launch height in meters, not a time."}},"m12":{"ask":"What does a $40 shirt cost after 25% off?","idea":"“25% off” means you pay the remaining 75%. You can find the discount and subtract it, or multiply by 0.75 directly. Make sure you give the price paid, not the discount.","steps":["Discount: 25% of $40 = 0.25 × 40 = $10.","Sale price: $40 − $10 = $30.","Or in one step: 40 × 0.75 = 30."],"check":"$30 is 75% of $40 ✓","wrong":{"$10":"That's the discount, not the sale price.","$15":"Doesn't match: $15 off $40 is a 37.5% discount, not 25%.","$35":"Subtracts only $5, not 25% of $40."}},"m13":{"ask":"Find the average of 4, 8, 10 and 14.","idea":"The mean is the total divided by how many numbers there are. It's the value each number would have if the total were shared out equally.","steps":["Total: 4 + 8 + 10 + 14 = 36.","Count: 4 numbers.","Mean: 36 ÷ 4 = 9."],"check":"4 × 9 = 36 ✓","wrong":{"8":"Too small: 4 × 8 = 32, not 36.","10":"Too big: 4 × 10 = 40.","12":"Divides by 3 instead of 4."}},"m33":{"ask":"If 2 cups of milk need 3 cups of flour, how much flour goes with 8 cups of milk?","idea":"A ratio stays the same when you scale a recipe: multiply both ingredients by the same factor. Find the factor from the milk, then apply it to the flour.","steps":["Scale factor: 8 cups of milk ÷ 2 cups = 4.","Flour: 3 cups × 4 = 12 cups."],"check":"12 : 8 simplifies to 3 : 2 ✓","wrong":{"10":"Doesn't keep the ratio: 10 : 8 simplifies to 5 : 4, not 3 : 2.","16":"Doubles the milk amount instead of using the ratio.","24":"Multiplies 3 by 8 instead of by the scale factor 4."}},"m14":{"ask":"At the same speed, how far does the car go in 4 hours?","idea":"Find the unit rate first: miles per 1 hour (the speed). Then multiply by the number of hours. Distance = speed × time.","steps":["Speed: 150 miles ÷ 2.5 hours = 60 miles per hour.","Distance: 60 × 4 = 240 miles."],"check":"Proportion: 150/2.5 = 240/4, since both equal 60 ✓","wrong":{"200":"Doesn't match the rate: 200 ÷ 4 = 50 miles per hour, not 60.","225":"Doesn't match the rate: 225 ÷ 4 = 56.25 miles per hour.","375":"Multiplies 150 by 2.5 instead of dividing to find the speed."}},"m15":{"ask":"How many of 200 students chose science?","idea":"The percents of all the groups must add to 100%. Find science's share first, then take that percent of 200.","steps":["Science: 100% − 30% − 45% = 25%.","25% of 200 = 0.25 × 200 = 50."],"check":"Math 60 + English 90 + Science 50 = 200 ✓","wrong":{"25":"That's the percent, not the number of students.","60":"That's the number who chose math (30% of 200).","90":"That's the number who chose English (45% of 200)."}},"m16":{"ask":"What happens to the mean and median when the outlier 20 is removed?","idea":"An outlier is a value far from the others. The mean uses every value, so an outlier pulls it strongly. The median is just the middle value, so an extreme value barely affects it. Compute both before and after to be sure.","steps":["Before: mean = (3 + 5 + 5 + 7 + 20) ÷ 5 = 40 ÷ 5 = 8. Median = 5 (the middle of 5 values).","After removing 20: mean = 20 ÷ 4 = 5. Median = (5 + 5) ÷ 2 = 5 (average of the two middle values).","The mean drops from 8 to 5; the median stays 5."],"check":"Ordered data after: 3, 5, 5, 7. The middle two are both 5 ✓","wrong":{"Both the mean and the median decrease.":"The median is 5 both before and after.","The mean stays the same, and the median decreases.":"Reverses them: the mean drops from 8 to 5.","Both the mean and the median stay the same.":"The mean changes from 8 to 5."}},"m17":{"ask":"Up 20%, then down 20%: where does the price end up?","idea":"Percent changes multiply. +20% means × 1.20, and −20% means × 0.80. The decrease is taken from the <em>larger<\/em> price, so it removes more than the increase added.","steps":["Multiply the factors: 1.20 × 0.80 = 0.96.","The final price is 96% of the original, which is 4% lower."],"check":"Start at $100: up to $120, then down 20% of $120 = $24, to $96 ✓","wrong":{"the same":"Assumes the changes cancel, but 20% of the higher price is a bigger amount.","4% higher":"The factor 0.96 is less than 1, so the price is lower.","40% lower":"Adds the percents as if both were decreases."}},"m34":{"ask":"The club grew 25% to reach 200. How many members were there to start?","idea":"The 25% increase is based on the <em>original<\/em> number, which you don't know yet. After a 25% increase, the new total is 125% of the original. So divide by 1.25; don't take 25% off 200.","steps":["New = original × 1.25, so 200 = original × 1.25.","Original = 200 ÷ 1.25 = 160."],"check":"25% of 160 is 40, and 160 + 40 = 200 ✓","wrong":{"150":"Subtracts 25% of 200, but the 25% was of the original number.","175":"Subtracts only 25 members. Check: 175 × 1.25 = 218.75.","250":"Adds 25% instead of undoing it."}},"m35":{"ask":"Among 10th graders only, what fraction joined Robotics?","idea":"“If one of the 10th-grade students is selected” limits the choice to 10th graders. So the denominator is the 10th-grade total, not everyone surveyed.","steps":["The group: 10th graders, total 28.","In that group, Robotics: 18.","Probability: 18/28 = 9/14."],"check":"9/14 ≈ 0.64, and 18 out of 28 is a bit under two-thirds ✓","wrong":{"3/10":"That's 18/60, using everyone surveyed as the denominator.","3/8":"Doesn't match the table: no count in the 10th-grade column gives 3/8.","3/5":"That's 18/30, the fraction of Robotics members who are 10th graders, which is the reverse question."}},"m36":{"ask":"Removing one number changed the mean from 14 to 13. What was removed?","idea":"Means are easiest to work with through totals: total = mean × count. Compare the total before and after; the difference is the number that was removed.","steps":["Original total: 10 × 14 = 140.","New total: 9 × 13 = 117.","Removed number: 140 − 117 = 23."],"check":"(140 − 23) ÷ 9 = 117 ÷ 9 = 13 ✓","wrong":{"1":"That's the change in the mean, not the removed number.","13":"That's the new mean.","14":"That's the original mean. Removing a number equal to the mean wouldn't change the mean."}},"m72":{"ask":"If 5 notebooks cost $12, what do 15 cost?","idea":"At a constant price per notebook, the cost scales with the number of notebooks. 15 is 3 times 5, so the cost is 3 times $12.","steps":["Scale factor: 15 ÷ 5 = 3.","Cost: 3 × $12 = $36."],"check":"Unit price: $12 ÷ 5 = $2.40; 15 × $2.40 = $36 ✓","wrong":{"$24":"Doubles the cost, but 15 notebooks is 3 times as many.","$45":"Doesn't match the rate: $45 ÷ 15 = $3 each, not $2.40.","$60":"Multiplies $12 by 5 instead of by 3."}},"m73":{"ask":"Find the middle value of 7, 2, 9, 4, 11.","idea":"The median is the middle value <em>after putting the data in order<\/em>. With 5 values, it's the 3rd one.","steps":["Order: 2, 4, 7, 9, 11.","The middle (3rd) value is 7."],"check":"Two values are below 7 and two are above ✓","wrong":{"6.6":"That's the mean: 33 ÷ 5.","9":"That's the middle value of the list <em>before<\/em> sorting.","4":"That's the 2nd value in order, not the middle."}},"m74":{"ask":"12 out of 40 is what percent?","idea":"A percent is a part out of 100. Divide the part by the whole to get a decimal, then multiply by 100.","steps":["12 ÷ 40 = 0.30.","0.30 × 100 = 30%."],"check":"30% of 40 = 12 ✓","wrong":{"12%":"Uses the count as the percent; it's out of 40, not 100.","28%":"That's 40 − 12 = 28, the number of non-red marbles, treated as a percent.","48%":"Doesn't match: 48% of 40 is 19.2."}},"m75":{"ask":"Convert 3.5 inches on the map to miles.","idea":"A map scale is a rate: every inch stands for 25 miles. So multiply the number of inches by 25.","steps":["3.5 × 25 = 87.5 miles."],"check":"3 inches = 75 miles and half an inch = 12.5 miles; 75 + 12.5 = 87.5 ✓","wrong":{"28.5":"Adds 3.5 and 25 instead of multiplying.","75":"Uses only 3 inches and drops the half.","100":"Rounds 3.5 up to 4 inches."}},"m76":{"ask":"What is the total price of an $80 jacket with 6% tax?","idea":"Tax is a percent of the price, added on top. Total = price + tax, or price × 1.06.","steps":["Tax: 6% of $80 = 0.06 × 80 = $4.80.","Total: $80 + $4.80 = $84.80."],"check":"80 × 1.06 = 84.80 ✓","wrong":{"$80.48":"Uses 0.6% instead of 6%.","$86.00":"Adds $6 as if the tax were a flat $6.","$4.80":"That's only the tax."}},"m77":{"ask":"Use the line of best fit to predict the score for 6 hours of study.","idea":"A line of best fit turns an <i>x<\/i>-value into a predicted <i>y<\/i>-value. Substitute the hours for <i>x<\/i>.","steps":["Substitute <i>x<\/i> = 6: 4.5(6) + 62.","4.5 × 6 = 27; 27 + 62 = 89."],"check":"The model predicts 4.5 more points per extra hour, starting at 62: 62 + 6(4.5) = 89 ✓","wrong":{"62":"That's the prediction for 0 hours.","67.5":"Adds 4.5 once instead of 6 times.","99":"Doesn't match: 4.5(6) + 62 = 89."}},"m78":{"ask":"Find the average age when the table gives how many members have each age.","idea":"Each age counts as many times as there are members with that age. Multiply each age by its count to get the total age, then divide by the total number of members.","steps":["Totals: 14 × 5 = 70, 15 × 8 = 120, 16 × 7 = 112.","Sum: 70 + 120 + 112 = 302.","Members: 5 + 8 + 7 = 20. Mean: 302 ÷ 20 = 15.1."],"check":"Most members are 15 or 16, so a mean a little above 15 makes sense ✓","wrong":{"15.0":"Averages 14, 15 and 16 as if each age had the same number of members.","15.3":"Doesn't match: 15.3 × 20 = 306, but the total is 302.","15.5":"Doesn't match: 15.5 × 20 = 310."}},"m79":{"ask":"By what percent did the value fall from $24,000 to $18,000?","idea":"Percent change always compares the change to the <em>original<\/em> amount: (change ÷ original) × 100.","steps":["Change: 24,000 − 18,000 = 6,000.","Divide by the original: 6,000 ÷ 24,000 = 0.25.","That's a 25% decrease."],"check":"25% of $24,000 is $6,000, and 24,000 − 6,000 = 18,000 ✓","wrong":{"33.3%":"Divides by the new value, 18,000, instead of the original.","6%":"Uses the $6,000 drop as if it were a percent.","75%":"That's the percent of the value that remains (18,000 ÷ 24,000)."}},"m80":{"ask":"Scale the sample's result up to the whole town.","idea":"A random sample represents the population it came from. The fraction of the sample who support the park estimates the fraction of all voters, so multiply that fraction by the population.","steps":["Sample fraction: 140 ÷ 250 = 0.56 (56%).","Estimate: 0.56 × 12,000 = 6,720 voters."],"check":"Proportion: 140/250 = 6,720/12,000, both equal 0.56 ✓","wrong":{"5,280":"That's the estimate for voters who do <em>not<\/em> support the park (44%).","7,000":"Doesn't match the sample: 7,000 ÷ 12,000 ≈ 58%, not 56%.","8,400":"Uses 70%, perhaps from 140 ÷ 200. The sample size is 250."}},"m81":{"ask":"How much water turns 15 L of 20% salt solution into a 12% solution?","idea":"Adding pure water doesn't change the amount of salt, only the total volume. Find the salt, then find what total volume makes that salt 12%, then subtract the starting volume.","steps":["Salt: 20% of 15 L = 3 L.","New total: 3 ÷ 0.12 = 25 L.","Water added: 25 − 15 = 10 L."],"check":"3 L of salt in 25 L is 3 ÷ 25 = 12% ✓","wrong":{"8":"Too little: 3 L of salt in 23 L is about 13%.","12":"Too much: 3 L of salt in 27 L is about 11%.","25":"That's the new total volume, not the water added."}},"m82":{"ask":"Combine two groups of different sizes into one average.","idea":"When groups have different sizes, you can't just average their means; the bigger group counts more. Use totals: total points ÷ total students.","steps":["Group totals: 12 × 80 = 960 and 8 × 90 = 720.","All points: 960 + 720 = 1,680.","Mean: 1,680 ÷ 20 = 84."],"check":"84 is closer to 80 than to 90, as it should be, since the 80-average group is bigger ✓","wrong":{"85":"Averages 80 and 90 as if the groups were the same size.","86":"Leans toward the smaller group; it should lean toward 80.","88":"Leans strongly toward the smaller group."}},"m83":{"ask":"Among the tea drinkers only, what fraction prefer the evening?","idea":"“Of the people who prefer tea” limits the group to tea drinkers. So the denominator is the tea total, 120, not all 200 people.","steps":["The group: tea drinkers, 120.","Tea in the evening: 80.","Fraction: 80/120 = 2/3."],"check":"2/3 of 120 = 80 ✓","wrong":{"2/5":"That's 80/200, using everyone as the denominator.","4/5":"That's 80/100, using all evening drinkers as the denominator.","1/3":"That's the fraction of tea drinkers who prefer the morning (40/120)."}},"m84":{"ask":"What can we conclude from this sample and its margin of error?","idea":"A random sample tells you about the population it was drawn from, here one university. The margin of error gives a range of likely values: result ± margin. It doesn't give an exact value, and it doesn't extend to other groups.","steps":["Range: 62% − 5% = 57% to 62% + 5% = 67%.","Population: students at <em>that<\/em> university.","So between 57% and 67% of the university's students likely prefer digital textbooks."],"check":"This conclusion uses both the right range and the right population ✓","wrong":{"Exactly 62% of all students at the university prefer digital textbooks.":"A sample gives an estimate, not an exact value; the margin of error shows the uncertainty.","Between 57% and 67% of all college students in the country likely prefer digital textbooks.":"The sample came from one university, so it can't be generalized to the whole country.","At least 67% of the students surveyed prefer digital textbooks.":"Exactly 62% of those surveyed did; the margin of error applies to the whole university, not the sample."}},"m85":{"ask":"Two increases in a row, 10% then 20%. What's the total increase?","idea":"Percent changes multiply, they don't add. The second increase applies to the already-higher price, so the total is more than 10% + 20%.","steps":["Factors: 1.10 and 1.20.","Multiply: 1.10 × 1.20 = 1.32.","1.32 means 32% more than the original."],"check":"Start at $100: up to $110, then 20% of $110 = $22, to $132 ✓","wrong":{"30%":"Adds the percents; the 20% was applied to the higher price.","2%":"Multiplies 10% × 20% = 0.02.","130%":"The new price is 132% of the original, which is an increase of 32%, not 130%."}},"m86":{"ask":"Convert 18 kilometers per hour into meters per second.","idea":"Convert each unit separately by multiplying by fractions equal to 1: 1,000 meters per kilometer, and 1 hour per 3,600 seconds. Units cancel like numbers.","steps":["18 km/h × (1,000 m / 1 km) = 18,000 meters per hour.","18,000 m/h × (1 h / 3,600 s) = 5 meters per second."],"check":"5 m/s × 3,600 s = 18,000 m = 18 km in one hour ✓","wrong":{"0.3":"Far too slow: 0.3 meters per second is only about 1 kilometer per hour.","64.8":"Multiplies by 3.6 instead of dividing by 3.6.","300":"Converts hours to minutes only (18,000 ÷ 60 = 300 meters per minute)."}},"m111":{"ask":"If you pull out one marble without looking, how likely is it to be blue?","idea":"Probability = (number of outcomes you want) ÷ (total number of equally likely outcomes). Every marble is equally likely, so count blue marbles and all marbles.","steps":["Total marbles: 3 + 5 + 2 = 10.","Blue marbles: 5.","Probability: 5/10 = 1/2."],"check":"Half of the 10 marbles are blue ✓","wrong":{"1/5":"That's 2/10, the probability of green.","3/10":"That's the probability of red.","5/8":"Leaves the green marbles out of the total. There are 10 marbles, not 8."}},"m112":{"ask":"What share of all items sold over the two days came from Monday?","idea":"“Percent of the two-day total” means Monday's amount divided by the <em>total<\/em> for both days, not divided by Tuesday's amount.","steps":["Total: 120 + 150 = 270.","Monday's share: 120 ÷ 270 ≈ 0.444.","As a percent: about 44.4%."],"check":"Monday sold fewer than Tuesday, so its share should be under 50% ✓","wrong":{"25%":"Doesn't match: 25% of 270 is 67.5, not 120.","55.6%":"That's Tuesday's share (150 ÷ 270).","80%":"Compares Monday with Tuesday (120 ÷ 150) instead of with the total."}},"m113":{"ask":"List all 10 scores in order. What is the middle score?","idea":"The median is the middle value of the ordered list. With an even number of values (10), there are two middle values, the 5th and 6th, and the median is their average.","steps":["Ordered: 6, 6, 6, 6, 6, 7, 7, 7, 10, 10.","5th value: 6. 6th value: 7.","Median: (6 + 7) ÷ 2 = 6.5."],"check":"5 scores are at or below 6.5 and 5 are above ✓","wrong":{"6":"That's the 5th value only; with 10 values you average the 5th and 6th.","7":"That's the 6th value only.","7.1":"That's the mean: 71 ÷ 10."}},"m114":{"ask":"Tea to coffee is 3 : 7, and coffee has 200 more. How many people in total?","idea":"Think of the ratio as equal-sized parts: 3 parts tea, 7 parts coffee. The difference between them is 7 − 3 = 4 parts, and that difference is 200 people. That tells you the size of one part.","steps":["Difference: 7 − 3 = 4 parts = 200 people, so 1 part = 50 people.","Total: 3 + 7 = 10 parts = 10 × 50 = 500."],"check":"Tea 150, coffee 350: 350 − 150 = 200 ✓ and 150 : 350 = 3 : 7 ✓","wrong":{"350":"That's the number who prefer coffee.","700":"Treats one part as 100 people, but 4 parts must equal 200.","1,000":"Treats one part as 100 people, doubling everything."}},"m129":{"ask":"Write 20 out of 50 as a simplified fraction.","idea":"“What fraction” means part over whole: 20/50. Simplify by dividing the top and bottom by their greatest common factor, 10.","steps":["Fraction: 20/50.","Divide top and bottom by 10: 2/5."],"check":"2/5 of 50 = 20 ✓","wrong":{"1/5":"That would be 10 students out of 50.","1/2":"That would be 25 students out of 50.","5/2":"Flips the fraction: whole over part, which is more than 1."}},"m130":{"ask":"What does the worker earn for 46 hours, with overtime after 40?","idea":"Split the hours into two groups: the first 40 at the regular rate, and the extra hours at 1.5 times that rate. Pay each group separately, then add.","steps":["Regular: 40 × $18 = $720.","Overtime rate: 1.5 × $18 = $27. Overtime hours: 46 − 40 = 6. Overtime pay: 6 × $27 = $162.","Total: $720 + $162 = $882."],"check":"More than 46 × $18 = $828, as it should be with overtime ✓","wrong":{"$828":"Pays all 46 hours at the regular rate.","$858":"Too low: overtime is 6 hours at $27, which is $162, plus $720 regular pay.","$1,242":"Pays all 46 hours at the overtime rate of $27."}},"m131":{"ask":"If 3 cups of rice serve 8 people, how much for 20 people?","idea":"Find the amount per person (the unit rate), then multiply by the number of people. Or scale: 20 people is 2.5 times 8 people.","steps":["Per person: 3 ÷ 8 = 0.375 cup.","For 20 people: 0.375 × 20 = 7.5 cups."],"check":"Scale factor: 20 ÷ 8 = 2.5, and 3 × 2.5 = 7.5 ✓","wrong":{"6":"Doubles the recipe (16 people), which isn't enough for 20.","9":"Triples the recipe (24 people), which is more than needed.","12":"Too much: 12 cups would serve 32 people at this rate."}},"m132":{"ask":"9 soccer players are 25% of the 60% who play a sport. How many students in all?","idea":"A percent of a percent multiplies: soccer players are 25% of 60% of the school. First find what percent of the whole school plays soccer, then work backward from 9 students.","steps":["Soccer share of the school: 0.25 × 0.60 = 0.15, or 15%.","15% of the school is 9 students: 0.15 × total = 9.","Total: 9 ÷ 0.15 = 60 students."],"check":"60 students → 36 play a sport → 25% of 36 = 9 play soccer ✓","wrong":{"36":"That's the number who play a sport, not the whole school.","150":"Doesn't work: 15% of 150 is 22.5, not 9.","225":"Doesn't work: 15% of 225 is about 34, not 9."}},"mx33":{"ask":"How far is this student's actual score from what the line predicts?","idea":"A residual measures the gap between reality and the model: residual = actual − predicted. A positive residual means the point is above the line (the student did better than predicted).","steps":["Predicted: 0.8(20) + 12 = 16 + 12 = 28.","Residual: 31 − 28 = 3."],"check":"The student scored 3 points more than predicted, so the point is 3 units above the line ✓","wrong":{"−3":"Subtracts in the wrong order: predicted − actual.","28":"That's the predicted score.","31":"That's the actual score."}},"mx34":{"ask":"Read the bar graph. What percent of all members are in 11th grade?","idea":"A percent of a group is (part ÷ whole) × 100. Read every bar to get the whole: the total of all grades.","steps":["Bars: 9th 12, 10th 18, 11th 24, 12th 16.","Total: 12 + 18 + 24 + 16 = 70.","11th grade: 24 ÷ 70 ≈ 0.343, about 34%."],"check":"24 is the tallest bar and about a third of 70 ✓","wrong":{"24%":"Uses the count, 24, as if it were a percent.","30%":"Doesn't match: 30% of 70 is 21, not 24.","48%":"Divides by 50 instead of 70, perhaps leaving out a bar."}},"mx35":{"ask":"Among students who prefer online classes, what is the chance of picking an 11th grader?","idea":"“If a student who prefers online classes is chosen” means the group is only the online students. So the denominator is the online total (105), not all 11th graders or all 200 students.","steps":["The group: prefer online, 105 students.","11th graders in that group: 60.","Probability: 60 ÷ 105 ≈ 0.571, about 0.57."],"check":"60 is a bit more than half of 105 ✓","wrong":{"0.60":"That's 60/100, using all 11th graders as the group; it answers a different question.","0.30":"That's 60/200, using everyone surveyed.","0.45":"That's 45/100, the fraction of 10th graders who prefer online."}},"mx36":{"ask":"After a 25% increase, what percent decrease brings the price back?","idea":"The decrease is measured from the <em>new, larger<\/em> price, so the same dollar amount is a smaller percent of it. Try a simple number like 100 to see this clearly.","steps":["Start at 100. After +25%: 125.","To get back to 100, drop 25.","25 out of 125 = 0.20 = 20%."],"check":"125 × 0.80 = 100 ✓","wrong":{"25%":"25% off 125 is 93.75, below the original price.","80%":"That's the percent that remains (100 ÷ 125), not the decrease.","12.5%":"125 × 0.875 = 109.375, still above the original price."}},"mx37":{"ask":"Removing the largest number drops the mean from 42 to 40. What was that number?","idea":"Use totals: total = mean × count. The difference between the two totals is the number that was removed.","steps":["Total of 10 numbers: 10 × 42 = 420.","Total of the remaining 9: 9 × 40 = 360.","Removed: 420 − 360 = 60."],"check":"(420 − 60) ÷ 9 = 360 ÷ 9 = 40 ✓","wrong":{"42":"That's the original mean. Removing it wouldn't change the mean.","20":"Doesn't match: removing 20 would raise the mean to 400 ÷ 9 ≈ 44.4.","38":"Removing a number below the mean would raise the mean, not lower it."}},"mx38":{"ask":"What does 350 km of driving cost in fuel?","idea":"Chain the rates. First find the liters needed (liters per 100 km × number of hundreds of km), then multiply by the price per liter. Keep track of units at each step.","steps":["350 km is 3.5 hundreds of kilometers.","Fuel: 3.5 × 6.5 = 22.75 liters.","Cost: 22.75 × $1.40 = $31.85."],"check":"About 23 liters at about $1.40 is about $32 ✓","wrong":{"$22.75":"That's the number of liters, not the cost.","$49.00":"Doesn't match: that would buy 35 liters, but the trip uses 22.75 liters.","$3.19":"Treats 350 km as 0.35 hundreds of kilometers. It's 3.5."}},"mx39":{"ask":"On average, how many degrees per hour did the temperature rise from hour 2 to hour 6?","idea":"Average rate of change = (change in output) ÷ (change in input), like a slope between two points on the graph. It averages over the whole interval, even if the rise isn't steady.","steps":["Read the graph: 18°C at hour 2 and 30°C at hour 6.","Change in temperature: 30 − 18 = 12 degrees.","Change in time: 6 − 2 = 4 hours. Rate: 12 ÷ 4 = 3°C per hour."],"check":"Rising 3° per hour for 4 hours from 18° gives 30° ✓","wrong":{"12":"That's the total change; divide by the 4 hours.","6":"Divides by 2 hours instead of 4.","4":"That's the number of hours."}},"mx40":{"ask":"What does this study allow us to conclude, and about whom?","idea":"Ask two questions. Were subjects <strong>randomly assigned<\/strong> to groups? If yes, a cause-and-effect conclusion is supported. Were they <strong>randomly selected<\/strong> from a larger population? If not (volunteers), the result applies only to people like them.","steps":["Random assignment: yes, so green tea likely <em>caused<\/em> the better sleep.","Random selection: no, they were volunteers, so the result applies to adults similar to them.","Conclusion: green tea likely causes better sleep among adults similar to these volunteers."],"check":"This choice claims cause (allowed) and limits who it applies to (required) ✓","wrong":{"Drinking green tea causes better sleep for all adults everywhere.":"Volunteers aren't a random sample of all adults, so the result can't be generalized that far.","No conclusion about cause and effect is possible from this study.":"Random assignment is exactly what makes a cause-and-effect conclusion possible.","People who sleep better choose to drink green tea.":"People didn't choose; the researchers assigned the drinks at random."}},"m18":{"ask":"How long is the longest side of a right triangle with legs 6 and 8?","idea":"In a right triangle, the two legs and the hypotenuse (the longest side, across from the right angle) always satisfy <i>a<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + <i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = <i>c<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>. That's the Pythagorean theorem.","steps":["Square the legs: 6<sup>2<\/sup> = 36 and 8<sup>2<\/sup> = 64.","Add: 36 + 64 = 100 = <i>c<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","Take the square root: <i>c<\/i> = 10."],"check":"6-8-10 is the 3-4-5 triangle doubled ✓","wrong":{"12":"Doesn't satisfy the theorem: 12<sup>2<\/sup> = 144, not 100.","14":"Adds the legs, 6 + 8. The hypotenuse is always shorter than the two legs combined.","48":"That's 6 × 8, twice the triangle's area."}},"m37":{"ask":"How far is it around a 12-by-5 rectangle?","idea":"Perimeter is the distance around the outside: add all four sides. A rectangle has two lengths and two widths. (Area, length × width, measures the space inside.)","steps":["Two lengths: 2 × 12 = 24.","Two widths: 2 × 5 = 10.","Perimeter: 24 + 10 = 34 units."],"check":"12 + 5 + 12 + 5 = 34 ✓","wrong":{"17":"Adds one length and one width; that's only half the way around.","60":"That's the area, 12 × 5.","120":"Doubles the area."}},"m19":{"ask":"A circle has area 49π. How far is it around?","idea":"Area and circumference both depend on the radius <i>r<\/i>. Use the area to find <i>r<\/i> (area = π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>), then use <i>r<\/i> in circumference = 2π<i>r<\/i>.","steps":["π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 49π, so <i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 49 and <i>r<\/i> = 7.","Circumference: 2π(7) = 14π."],"check":"Area with <i>r<\/i> = 7: π(49) = 49π ✓","wrong":{"7π":"Uses π<i>r<\/i>, forgetting the 2 in 2π<i>r<\/i>.","49π":"That's the area again.","98π":"Doubles the area instead of finding the radius."}},"m20":{"ask":"Two angles add to 180°, and one is 3 times the other. Find the smaller one.","idea":"“Supplementary” means the angles add to 180°. Call the smaller angle <i>x<\/i>; the larger is 3<i>x<\/i>. Write one equation for their sum.","steps":["<i>x<\/i> + 3<i>x<\/i> = 180.","4<i>x<\/i> = 180, so <i>x<\/i> = 45°."],"check":"45° and 135° add to 180°, and 135 = 3 × 45 ✓","wrong":{"30°":"Doesn't work: 30° + 90° = 120°, not 180°.","60°":"Doesn't work: 60° and 3 × 60° = 180° add to 240°, not 180°.","135°":"That's the larger angle."}},"m38":{"ask":"How much space is inside a cylinder with radius 3 and height 10?","idea":"A cylinder's volume is the area of its circular base times its height: <i>V<\/i> = π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup><i>h<\/i>. Square the radius before multiplying.","steps":["Base area: π(3<sup>2<\/sup>) = 9π.","Volume: 9π × 10 = 90π cubic inches."],"check":"Units: square inches × inches = cubic inches ✓","wrong":{"30π":"Uses π<i>r<\/i><i>h<\/i>, forgetting to square the radius; it matches ⅓ of the volume.","60π":"That's 2π<i>r<\/i><i>h<\/i>, the area of the curved side, not the volume.","300π":"Squares the height instead of the radius, or multiplies by 10 twice."}},"m21":{"ask":"In a right triangle, sin <i>A<\/i> = 3/5. What is cos <i>A<\/i>?","idea":"sin = opposite/hypotenuse, so you can picture a triangle with the side opposite <i>A<\/i> = 3 and hypotenuse = 5. Find the missing side with the Pythagorean theorem, then cos = adjacent/hypotenuse.","steps":["Opposite = 3, hypotenuse = 5.","Adjacent: √(5<sup>2<\/sup> − 3<sup>2<\/sup>) = √16 = 4. (A 3-4-5 triangle.)","cos <i>A<\/i> = adjacent/hypotenuse = 4/5."],"check":"sin<sup>2<\/sup> + cos<sup>2<\/sup> = 9/25 + 16/25 = 1 ✓","wrong":{"3/4":"That's tan <i>A<\/i> = opposite/adjacent.","5/4":"Hypotenuse over adjacent, which is greater than 1. Cosine can never be more than 1.","5/3":"Hypotenuse over opposite, which is also greater than 1."}},"m22":{"ask":"Read the center and radius from the circle's equation.","idea":"A circle's equation (<i>x<\/i> − <i>h<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> + (<i>y<\/i> − <i>k<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> has center (<i>h<\/i>, <i>k<\/i>) and radius <i>r<\/i>. Because the form has minus signs, the center's coordinates are the <em>opposites<\/em> of the numbers you see. The right side is <i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>, not <i>r<\/i>.","steps":["(<i>x<\/i> − 2) means <i>h<\/i> = 2.","(<i>y<\/i> + 3) = (<i>y<\/i> − (−3)) means <i>k<\/i> = −3.","<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 16, so <i>r<\/i> = 4. Center (2, −3), radius 4."],"check":"The point (6, −3) is 4 units right of the center: (4)<sup>2<\/sup> + 0 = 16 ✓","wrong":{"Center (−2, 3), radius 4":"Copies the signs from the equation instead of taking their opposites.","Center (2, −3), radius 16":"Uses <i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> as the radius; take the square root.","Center (−2, 3), radius 16":"Makes both mistakes: sign flip and <i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>."}},"m39":{"ask":"Every side of the bigger triangle is 5/2 times as long. What is its area?","idea":"When lengths are scaled by a factor <i>k<\/i>, areas are scaled by <i>k<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>, because area uses two lengths multiplied together (like base × height).","steps":["Scale factor for lengths: <i>k<\/i> = 5/2.","Scale factor for area: (5/2)<sup>2<\/sup> = 25/4.","Area: 12 × 25/4 = 75."],"check":"A square with side 2 (area 4) scaled by 5/2 has side 5 (area 25): 4 × 25/4 = 25 ✓","wrong":{"30":"Multiplies by 5/2 instead of (5/2)<sup>2<\/sup>.","48":"Multiplies by 4, which isn't the right factor.","300":"Multiplies by 25 without dividing by 4."}},"m40":{"ask":"How long is an 80° arc on a circle with radius 9?","idea":"An arc is a fraction of the whole circle's circumference. The fraction is (central angle ÷ 360°).","steps":["Full circumference: 2π(9) = 18π.","Fraction: 80/360 = 2/9.","Arc length: (2/9) × 18π = 4π cm."],"check":"80° is a bit less than a quarter of the circle, and 4π is a bit less than 18π ÷ 4 = 4.5π ✓","wrong":{"2π":"Uses the radius 9 as if it were the diameter.","8π":"Doubles the fraction or uses the wrong circumference.","18π":"That's the whole circumference."}},"m87":{"ask":"A triangle has angles 35° and 65°. What is the third?","idea":"The three angles of any triangle add to 180°. Subtract the two you know.","steps":["35 + 65 = 100.","180 − 100 = 80°."],"check":"35 + 65 + 80 = 180 ✓","wrong":{"70°":"Doesn't work: 35 + 65 + 70 = 170.","90°":"Assumes a right angle, but nothing says it's a right triangle; 35 + 65 + 90 = 190.","100°":"That's the sum of the two given angles."}},"m88":{"ask":"A square has area 81. How far is it around?","idea":"A square's area is side × side, so the side is the square root of the area. The perimeter is 4 sides.","steps":["Side: √81 = 9 cm.","Perimeter: 4 × 9 = 36 cm."],"check":"9 × 9 = 81 ✓","wrong":{"9 cm":"That's one side, not the perimeter.","18 cm":"Adds only two sides.","81 cm":"Uses the area as the perimeter."}},"m89":{"ask":"How much space is inside a 4 × 3 × 5 box?","idea":"The volume of a rectangular box is length × width × height, in cubic units. (Surface area, which adds up the areas of all six faces, measures something different.)","steps":["4 × 3 = 12 (the base area).","12 × 5 = 60 cubic inches."],"check":"Units: inches × inches × inches = cubic inches ✓","wrong":{"12":"That's just the base area; multiply by the height too.","47":"Doesn't match: the volume is 4 × 3 × 5 = 60.","94":"That's the surface area: 2(12 + 20 + 15)."}},"m90":{"ask":"How long is the segment from (1, 2) to (7, 10)?","idea":"Draw a right triangle: the segment is the hypotenuse, and the legs are the horizontal and vertical changes. Then use the Pythagorean theorem. (That's what the distance formula does.)","steps":["Horizontal change: 7 − 1 = 6. Vertical change: 10 − 2 = 8.","Distance: √(6<sup>2<\/sup> + 8<sup>2<\/sup>) = √(36 + 64) = √100 = 10."],"check":"A 6-8-10 right triangle, the 3-4-5 triangle doubled ✓","wrong":{"8":"That's only the vertical change.","14":"Adds the changes, 6 + 8; that's the distance along the grid, not straight across.","100":"That's the distance squared; take the square root."}},"m91":{"ask":"Find the point exactly halfway between (−4, 6) and (8, −2).","idea":"The midpoint's coordinates are the averages of the endpoints' coordinates: average the <i>x<\/i>'s, and separately average the <i>y<\/i>'s.","steps":["<i>x<\/i>: (−4 + 8) ÷ 2 = 4 ÷ 2 = 2.","<i>y<\/i>: (6 + (−2)) ÷ 2 = 4 ÷ 2 = 2.","Midpoint: (2, 2)."],"check":"From (−4, 6) to (2, 2) is +6, −4, and from (2, 2) to (8, −2) is also +6, −4 ✓","wrong":{"(4, 4)":"Adds the coordinates but forgets to divide by 2.","(6, −4)":"That's half the <em>difference<\/em> between the endpoints, not the midpoint.","(12, −8)":"That's the difference between the endpoints (8 − (−4), −2 − 6)."}},"m92":{"ask":"Find the area of a right triangle with one leg 5 and hypotenuse 13.","idea":"The area of a right triangle is ½ × leg × leg, because the legs are perpendicular (a base and a height). The hypotenuse isn't a height, so first find the missing leg with the Pythagorean theorem.","steps":["Other leg: √(13<sup>2<\/sup> − 5<sup>2<\/sup>) = √(169 − 25) = √144 = 12.","Area: ½ × 5 × 12 = 30."],"check":"5-12-13 is a well-known right triangle ✓","wrong":{"32.5":"Uses the hypotenuse as the height: ½ × 5 × 13.","60":"Forgets the ½: 5 × 12.","65":"Multiplies 5 × 13 without the ½; the hypotenuse isn't a height."}},"m93":{"ask":"Convert 3π/4 radians to degrees.","idea":"Radians and degrees are two units for angles. The key fact is π radians = 180°. So replace π with 180°.","steps":["3π/4 = (3/4) × π.","(3/4) × 180° = 135°."],"check":"π/4 = 45°, and 3 × 45° = 135° ✓","wrong":{"45°":"That's π/4 radians; multiply by 3.","240°":"That's 4π/3 radians, with the fraction flipped.","270°":"That's 3π/2 radians."}},"m94":{"ask":"Find the radius from an expanded circle equation.","idea":"Rewrite the equation in the form (<i>x<\/i> − <i>h<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> + (<i>y<\/i> − <i>k<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> by <strong>completing the square<\/strong>: for <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + <i>b<\/i><i>x<\/i>, add (<i>b<\/i>/2)<sup>2<\/sup>. Whatever you add on the left, add on the right too.","steps":["Group and move the constant: (<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 6<i>x<\/i>) + (<i>y<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 4<i>y<\/i>) = 12.","Complete the squares: add (−6/2)<sup>2<\/sup> = 9 and (4/2)<sup>2<\/sup> = 4 to both sides.","(<i>x<\/i> − 3)<sup>2<\/sup> + (<i>y<\/i> + 2)<sup>2<\/sup> = 12 + 9 + 4 = 25.","<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 25, so <i>r<\/i> = 5."],"check":"The center is (3, −2); the point (8, −2) is 5 units away, and it satisfies the original equation: 64 + 4 − 48 − 8 − 12 = 0 ✓","wrong":{"12":"Uses the constant from the original equation without completing the squares.","25":"That's <i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>; take the square root.","√12":"Forgets to add the 9 and 4 to the right side."}},"m95":{"ask":"In a right triangle, tan <i>P<\/i> = 12/5. What is sin <i>Q<\/i>?","idea":"Build the triangle from tan <i>P<\/i> = opposite/adjacent = 12/5, find the hypotenuse, then read sin <i>Q<\/i> from the angle <i>Q<\/i>'s point of view. Key fact: the side opposite <i>Q<\/i> is the side adjacent to <i>P<\/i>.","steps":["From <i>P<\/i>: opposite = 12, adjacent = 5.","Hypotenuse: √(12<sup>2<\/sup> + 5<sup>2<\/sup>) = √169 = 13.","From <i>Q<\/i>: the opposite side is 5 (the side next to <i>P<\/i>). sin <i>Q<\/i> = 5/13."],"check":"<i>P<\/i> and <i>Q<\/i> add to 90°, so sin <i>Q<\/i> = cos <i>P<\/i> = 5/13 ✓","wrong":{"5/12":"That's tan <i>Q<\/i> (opposite/adjacent from <i>Q<\/i>).","12/13":"That's sin <i>P<\/i>, from the wrong angle.","13/12":"Greater than 1; a sine can never be more than 1."}},"m96":{"ask":"How much space is inside a cone with radius 6 and height 8?","idea":"A cone holds exactly one-third of a cylinder with the same base and height: <i>V<\/i> = ⅓π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup><i>h<\/i>.","steps":["Base area: π(6<sup>2<\/sup>) = 36π.","Cylinder would be 36π × 8 = 288π.","Cone: ⅓ × 288π = 96π."],"check":"Units: cubic units, and 96π is one-third of the matching cylinder ✓","wrong":{"288π":"Forgets the ⅓; that's the cylinder's volume.","48π":"Divides by 6 instead of 3.","120π":"Doesn't match the formula: ⅓ × 36π × 8 = 96π."}},"m97":{"ask":"Two same-side interior angles are (5<i>x<\/i> − 20)° and (3<i>x<\/i> + 40)°. Find <i>x<\/i>.","idea":"When a line crosses two parallel lines, the two interior angles on the same side add to 180° (they're supplementary). They are not equal; alternate interior angles are the equal ones.","steps":["Add them to 180: (5<i>x<\/i> − 20) + (3<i>x<\/i> + 40) = 180.","Combine: 8<i>x<\/i> + 20 = 180.","8<i>x<\/i> = 160, so <i>x<\/i> = 20."],"check":"Angles: 5(20) − 20 = 80° and 3(20) + 40 = 100°, which add to 180° ✓","wrong":{"30":"Sets the angles equal; same-side interior angles add to 180° instead.","25":"Doesn't work: the angles would be 105° and 115°, which add to 220°.","45":"Doesn't work: the angles would be 205° and 175°, which is impossible here."}},"m98":{"ask":"Find the area of a regular hexagon with side 4.","idea":"A regular hexagon splits into 6 identical equilateral triangles, each with side equal to the hexagon's side. The area of an equilateral triangle with side <i>s<\/i> is (√3/4)<i>s<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","steps":["One triangle: (√3/4)(4<sup>2<\/sup>) = (√3/4)(16) = 4√3.","Six triangles: 6 × 4√3 = 24√3."],"check":"Each triangle has base 4 and height 2√3: ½ × 4 × 2√3 = 4√3 ✓","wrong":{"24":"Leaves out the √3 from the triangle area.","48√3":"Uses 12 triangles, or doubles each triangle's area.","12√3":"Uses 3 triangles, or halves the area."}},"m99":{"ask":"A sector of a radius-10 circle has area 25π. What is its angle?","idea":"A sector is a fraction of the whole circle. The same fraction applies to area and to angle: sector area ÷ circle area = central angle ÷ 360°.","steps":["Full circle: π(10<sup>2<\/sup>) = 100π.","Fraction: 25π ÷ 100π = 1/4.","Angle: 1/4 × 360° = 90°."],"check":"A 90° sector is a quarter circle: 100π ÷ 4 = 25π ✓","wrong":{"45°":"Uses a fraction of 1/8, or divides by 2 too many times.","25°":"Uses the area number 25 as the angle.","180°":"That's half the circle, which would have area 50π."}},"m100":{"ask":"The triangles are similar with AB = 6 matching DE = 9. What is the larger perimeter?","idea":"In similar figures, every length is scaled by the same factor <i>k<\/i>, and a perimeter is a sum of lengths, so it scales by <i>k<\/i> too. (Only area scales by <i>k<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.)","steps":["Scale factor: 9 ÷ 6 = 1.5.","Perimeter: 20 × 1.5 = 30."],"check":"Each side grows by half again, so the perimeter does too: 20 + 10 = 30 ✓","wrong":{"23":"Adds 3 (the difference 9 − 6) instead of scaling.","45":"Scales by 1.5<sup>2<\/sup> = 2.25, which is the rule for area, not perimeter.","40/3":"Scales down by 6/9 instead of up."}},"m101":{"ask":"A rectangle's area is 45 and its width is 5. How long is it?","idea":"Area = length × width. If you know the area and one side, divide to find the other side.","steps":["length × 5 = 45.","length = 45 ÷ 5 = 9 units."],"check":"9 × 5 = 45 ✓","wrong":{"15":"Doesn't work: 15 × 5 = 75.","40":"Subtracts 5 from 45 instead of dividing.","225":"Multiplies 45 × 5 instead of dividing."}},"m102":{"ask":"How far is it around a circle with radius 5?","idea":"Circumference (the distance around) = 2π<i>r<\/i>, or π times the diameter. Don't confuse it with area, π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","steps":["2π(5) = 10π."],"check":"The diameter is 10, and π × 10 = 10π ✓","wrong":{"5π":"Uses π<i>r<\/i>, forgetting the 2.","25π":"That's the area, π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","100π":"Squares the diameter: π(10)<sup>2<\/sup>."}},"m103":{"ask":"Find the distance around a triangle drawn on the grid.","idea":"Two sides lie along grid lines, so their lengths are easy to count. The third side is slanted; it's the hypotenuse of a right triangle, so use the Pythagorean theorem.","steps":["AB: from (0, 0) to (6, 0) = 6. BC: from (6, 0) to (6, 8) = 8.","AC: √(6<sup>2<\/sup> + 8<sup>2<\/sup>) = √100 = 10.","Perimeter: 6 + 8 + 10 = 24."],"check":"6-8-10 is the 3-4-5 triangle doubled ✓","wrong":{"14":"Adds only the two legs, leaving out the slanted side.","30":"Doesn't match: 6 + 8 + 10 = 24.","48":"That's 6 × 8, twice the area."}},"m104":{"ask":"A cube holds 64 cubic inches. How long is each edge?","idea":"A cube's volume is edge × edge × edge = edge<sup>3<\/sup>. So the edge is the <em>cube root<\/em> of the volume: the number that, used three times as a factor, gives 64.","steps":["Find <i>e<\/i> with <i>e<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> = 64.","4 × 4 × 4 = 64, so <i>e<\/i> = 4 inches."],"check":"4<sup>3<\/sup> = 64 ✓","wrong":{"8":"That's the square root of 64; 8<sup>3<\/sup> = 512.","16":"That's 64 ÷ 4.","32":"That's 64 ÷ 2."}},"m105":{"ask":"Find the area of a 45° slice of a circle with radius 8.","idea":"A sector is a fraction of the whole circle: (central angle ÷ 360°) of the full area.","steps":["Full area: π(8<sup>2<\/sup>) = 64π.","Fraction: 45/360 = 1/8.","Sector: 64π ÷ 8 = 8π."],"check":"Eight 45° sectors make a full circle: 8 × 8π = 64π ✓","wrong":{"4π":"That's 1/16 of the circle; a 45° sector is 1/8.","16π":"Uses 1/4 of the circle (a 90° sector).","64π":"That's the whole circle."}},"m106":{"ask":"The angles are 2<i>x<\/i>, 3<i>x<\/i> and 4<i>x<\/i>. What is the biggest angle?","idea":"All three angles of a triangle add to 180°. Add the expressions, solve for <i>x<\/i>, then find the specific angle asked for: the largest, 4<i>x<\/i>.","steps":["2<i>x<\/i> + 3<i>x<\/i> + 4<i>x<\/i> = 180, so 9<i>x<\/i> = 180.","<i>x<\/i> = 20.","Largest angle: 4<i>x<\/i> = 80°."],"check":"40° + 60° + 80° = 180° ✓","wrong":{"20°":"That's <i>x<\/i>, not an angle of the triangle.","40°":"That's 2<i>x<\/i>, the smallest angle.","60°":"That's 3<i>x<\/i>, the middle angle."}},"m107":{"ask":"A cylinder holds 96. How much does a cone with the same radius and height hold?","idea":"A cone holds exactly one-third of the cylinder with the same base and height. Compare the formulas: cone ⅓π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup><i>h<\/i>, cylinder π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup><i>h<\/i>.","steps":["Cone = ⅓ × cylinder.","⅓ × 96 = 32 cubic centimeters."],"check":"Three of these cones would fill the cylinder: 3 × 32 = 96 ✓","wrong":{"24":"Uses ¼ instead of ⅓.","48":"Uses ½ instead of ⅓.","288":"Multiplies by 3 instead of dividing."}},"m108":{"ask":"The right angle is at <i>B<\/i>. What is sin <i>A<\/i>?","idea":"sin = opposite ÷ hypotenuse, from the angle's point of view. The hypotenuse is across from the right angle (so it's <i>AC<\/i>). The side opposite <i>A<\/i> is the one that doesn't touch <i>A<\/i>: <i>BC<\/i>.","steps":["Hypotenuse <i>AC<\/i>: √(7<sup>2<\/sup> + 24<sup>2<\/sup>) = √(49 + 576) = √625 = 25.","Opposite <i>A<\/i>: <i>BC<\/i> = 24.","sin <i>A<\/i> = 24/25."],"check":"7-24-25 is a Pythagorean triple ✓","wrong":{"7/24":"That's tan <i>C<\/i> (or 1/tan <i>A<\/i>), not a sine.","7/25":"That's cos <i>A<\/i>: 7 is the side next to <i>A<\/i>.","25/24":"Greater than 1; a sine can't be more than 1."}},"m109":{"ask":"A square's corners touch a circle of radius 6. What is the square's area?","idea":"When a square is inscribed in a circle, its diagonal goes straight across through the center, so the diagonal equals the circle's diameter. For a square, area = diagonal<sup>2<\/sup> ÷ 2 (because the diagonal is side × √2).","steps":["Diagonal = diameter = 2 × 6 = 12.","Area = 12<sup>2<\/sup> ÷ 2 = 144 ÷ 2 = 72."],"check":"Side = 12 ÷ √2 = 6√2, and (6√2)<sup>2<\/sup> = 36 × 2 = 72 ✓","wrong":{"36":"Uses the radius 6 as the side length.","144":"Uses the diameter 12 as the side length. The side is shorter than the diagonal.","36π":"That's the circle's area, not the square's."}},"m110":{"ask":"The diameter's endpoints are (2, −1) and (2, 7). Write the circle's equation.","idea":"The center is the midpoint of a diameter, and the radius is half the diameter. Then use (<i>x<\/i> − <i>h<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> + (<i>y<\/i> − <i>k<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>; the right side is the radius <em>squared<\/em>.","steps":["Center: ((2 + 2)/2, (−1 + 7)/2) = (2, 3).","Diameter: from −1 to 7 is 8, so the radius is 4 and <i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 16.","Equation: (<i>x<\/i> − 2)<sup>2<\/sup> + (<i>y<\/i> − 3)<sup>2<\/sup> = 16."],"check":"(2, 7): 0 + 16 = 16 ✓ and (2, −1): 0 + 16 = 16 ✓","wrong":{"(<i>x<\/i> − 2)<sup>2<\/sup> + (<i>y<\/i> − 3)<sup>2<\/sup> = 4":"Uses the radius 4 instead of its square, 16.","(<i>x<\/i> − 2)<sup>2<\/sup> + (<i>y<\/i> + 3)<sup>2<\/sup> = 16":"Puts the center at (2, −3); the sign is flipped.","(<i>x<\/i> − 2)<sup>2<\/sup> + (<i>y<\/i> − 3)<sup>2<\/sup> = 64":"Squares the diameter instead of the radius."}},"m121":{"ask":"A square's perimeter is 36. What is its area?","idea":"Perimeter is 4 equal sides, so one side is the perimeter ÷ 4. Area is side × side.","steps":["Side: 36 ÷ 4 = 9 cm.","Area: 9 × 9 = 81 square cm."],"check":"Perimeter: 4 × 9 = 36 ✓","wrong":{"9":"That's one side, not the area.","18":"That's two sides added, 9 + 9.","144":"Divides by 3 instead of 4 (a square has 4 sides), giving side 12."}},"m122":{"ask":"Find the area of a triangle with base 12 and height 5.","idea":"A triangle is half of a rectangle with the same base and height, so its area is ½ × base × height.","steps":["½ × 12 × 5 = ½ × 60 = 30 square units."],"check":"The matching rectangle has area 60, and the triangle is half ✓","wrong":{"17":"Adds the base and height.","35":"Doesn't match: ½ × 12 × 5 = 30.","60":"Forgets the ½."}},"m123":{"ask":"Find the perimeter of a triangle with corners (0, 0), (9, 0) and (9, 12).","idea":"Two sides run along grid lines (easy to count). The slanted side is a hypotenuse, found with the Pythagorean theorem. Then add all three.","steps":["Horizontal side: 9. Vertical side: 12.","Slanted side: √(9<sup>2<\/sup> + 12<sup>2<\/sup>) = √(81 + 144) = √225 = 15.","Perimeter: 9 + 12 + 15 = 36."],"check":"9-12-15 is the 3-4-5 triangle times 3 ✓","wrong":{"21":"Adds only the two legs.","27":"Uses 6 for the slanted side instead of 15.","54":"That's the area, ½ × 9 × 12."}},"m124":{"ask":"Angles A and B add to 90°. Find <i>x<\/i>.","idea":"“Complementary” means the angles add to 90° (“supplementary” is 180°). Add the expressions, set them equal to 90, and solve.","steps":["(2<i>x<\/i> + 10) + 3<i>x<\/i> = 90.","5<i>x<\/i> + 10 = 90, so 5<i>x<\/i> = 80.","<i>x<\/i> = 16."],"check":"A = 42° and B = 48°, which add to 90° ✓","wrong":{"20":"Doesn't work: the angles would be 50° and 60°, which add to 110°.","30":"Doesn't work: the angles would add to 160°.","34":"Uses 180° (supplementary) instead of 90°."}},"m125":{"ask":"A box with a 4-by-4 base holds 112. How tall is it?","idea":"Volume of a prism = base area × height. With a square base of side 4, the base area is 4 × 4. Divide the volume by the base area.","steps":["Base area: 4 × 4 = 16.","Height: 112 ÷ 16 = 7."],"check":"4 × 4 × 7 = 112 ✓","wrong":{"4":"That's the base side length.","28":"Divides by only one side (112 ÷ 4).","112":"That's the volume."}},"m126":{"ask":"A circle has area 36π. What is the area of a 120° slice?","idea":"A sector is the fraction (angle ÷ 360°) of the whole circle's area. Here you're already given the full area.","steps":["Fraction: 120/360 = 1/3.","Sector: 36π ÷ 3 = 12π."],"check":"Three 120° slices make a full circle: 3 × 12π = 36π ✓","wrong":{"6π":"Uses 1/6 of the circle (a 60° slice).","18π":"Uses 1/2 of the circle (a 180° slice).","36π":"That's the whole circle."}},"m127":{"ask":"The right angle is at <i>B<\/i>, with <i>AB<\/i> = 6 and <i>BC<\/i> = 8. What is sin <i>A<\/i>?","idea":"sin = opposite ÷ hypotenuse. The hypotenuse is across from the right angle at <i>B<\/i>, so it's <i>AC<\/i>. The side opposite <i>A<\/i> is the side that doesn't touch <i>A<\/i>: <i>BC<\/i>.","steps":["Hypotenuse: √(6<sup>2<\/sup> + 8<sup>2<\/sup>) = √100 = 10.","Opposite <i>A<\/i>: <i>BC<\/i> = 8.","sin <i>A<\/i> = 8/10 = 4/5."],"check":"6-8-10 is the 3-4-5 triangle doubled ✓","wrong":{"3/5":"That's cos <i>A<\/i>: 6 is the side next to <i>A<\/i>.","3/4":"That's 6/8, adjacent over opposite from <i>A<\/i> (the reciprocal of tan <i>A<\/i>).","5/4":"Greater than 1; a sine can't be more than 1."}},"m128":{"ask":"A sphere's volume is 288π. What is its radius?","idea":"Set the formula equal to the volume and undo the operations: divide out π and 4/3, then take the cube root.","steps":["(4/3)π<i>r<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> = 288π. Divide by π: (4/3)<i>r<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> = 288.","Multiply by 3/4: <i>r<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> = 216.","Cube root: <i>r<\/i> = 6, since 6 × 6 × 6 = 216."],"check":"(4/3)π(216) = 288π ✓","wrong":{"8":"Doesn't work: (4/3)π(512) ≈ 683π.","12":"Doesn't work: 12<sup>3<\/sup> = 1,728.","72":"Divides 288 by 4 and stops before taking the cube root."}},"mx41":{"ask":"<i>DE<\/i> is parallel to <i>BC<\/i>. Use the smaller triangle to find <i>BC<\/i>.","idea":"A line parallel to one side of a triangle cuts off a smaller triangle that is similar to the big one (same angles). Matching sides are in the same ratio. Careful: compare <i>AD<\/i> with the <em>whole<\/em> side <i>AB<\/i>, not with <i>DB<\/i>.","steps":["Small triangle <i>ADE<\/i>, big triangle <i>ABC<\/i>.","<i>AB<\/i> = <i>AD<\/i> + <i>DB<\/i> = 6 + 4 = 10.","Scale factor: <i>AB<\/i>/<i>AD<\/i> = 10/6.","<i>BC<\/i> = <i>DE<\/i> × 10/6 = 9 × 10/6 = 15."],"check":"Ratios match: <i>DE<\/i>/<i>BC<\/i> = 9/15 = 3/5 and <i>AD<\/i>/<i>AB<\/i> = 6/10 = 3/5 ✓","wrong":{"13.5":"Uses <i>AD<\/i>/<i>DB<\/i> = 6/4 as the scale factor. Compare with the whole side <i>AB<\/i> = 10.","6":"Scales down instead of up; <i>BC<\/i> must be longer than <i>DE<\/i>.","10":"That's the length of <i>AB<\/i>, not <i>BC<\/i>."}},"mx42":{"ask":"The central angle is 80°. What is the angle at <i>C<\/i> on the circle?","idea":"An inscribed angle (vertex on the circle) is half the central angle (vertex at the center) that cuts off the same arc.","steps":["Both angles open onto arc <i>AB<\/i>.","Inscribed angle = ½ × central angle = ½ × 80° = 40°."],"check":"The central angle is always twice the inscribed angle on the same arc: 2 × 40 = 80 ✓","wrong":{"80°":"That's the central angle; the inscribed angle is half of it.","160°":"Doubles the central angle instead of halving it.","100°":"That's 180° − 80°, which doesn't apply here."}},"mx43":{"ask":"Find the circle's center, then its distance from (0, 0).","idea":"Complete the square to put the equation in center-radius form. Then use the distance formula (the Pythagorean theorem) between the center and the origin. The question asks for that distance, not the radius.","steps":["Group: (<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 8<i>x<\/i>) + (<i>y<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 6<i>y<\/i>) = 11.","Add (−8/2)<sup>2<\/sup> = 16 and (6/2)<sup>2<\/sup> = 9 to both sides: (<i>x<\/i> − 4)<sup>2<\/sup> + (<i>y<\/i> + 3)<sup>2<\/sup> = 36.","Center <i>C<\/i> = (4, −3).","Distance to the origin: √(4<sup>2<\/sup> + 3<sup>2<\/sup>) = √25 = 5."],"check":"A 3-4-5 right triangle from (0, 0) to (4, −3) ✓","wrong":{"6":"That's the radius, not the distance from the center to the origin.","7":"Adds 4 + 3; the straight-line distance uses the Pythagorean theorem.","1":"Subtracts 4 − 3."}},"mx44":{"ask":"In a right triangle, cos <i>A<\/i> = 5/13. What is tan <i>A<\/i>?","idea":"cos = adjacent/hypotenuse, so picture adjacent = 5 and hypotenuse = 13. Find the missing opposite side with the Pythagorean theorem, then tan = opposite/adjacent.","steps":["Adjacent 5, hypotenuse 13.","Opposite: √(13<sup>2<\/sup> − 5<sup>2<\/sup>) = √(169 − 25) = √144 = 12.","tan <i>A<\/i> = 12/5."],"check":"5-12-13 is a Pythagorean triple ✓","wrong":{"5/12":"That's adjacent over opposite, flipped.","12/13":"That's sin <i>A<\/i>.","13/5":"That's hypotenuse over adjacent."}},"mx45":{"ask":"Find the area left after cutting a half-circle out of a 12-by-8 rectangle.","idea":"Area of a shape with a piece removed = whole area − removed area. A semicircle is half a circle, and its radius is half its diameter.","steps":["Rectangle: 12 × 8 = 96.","Semicircle: diameter 8, so radius 4. Area = ½π(4<sup>2<\/sup>) = ½ × 16π = 8π.","Shaded: 96 − 8π."],"check":"8π ≈ 25, so the shaded area is about 71, a bit under three-quarters of the rectangle ✓","wrong":{"96 − 16π":"Removes a full circle instead of half.","96 − 4π":"Removes a quarter circle (¼π × 16 = 4π) instead of half.","96 − 32π":"Uses the diameter 8 as the radius, then halves."}},"mx46":{"ask":"The cone is 3 times as tall as the cylinder, with the same radius. Compare their volumes.","idea":"A cone holds ⅓ as much as a cylinder of the same height. But this cone is 3 times as tall, which triples its volume. The ⅓ and the 3 cancel.","steps":["Cylinder: π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup><i>h<\/i>.","Cone: ⅓π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>(3<i>h<\/i>) = π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup><i>h<\/i>.","They're equal: the ratio is 1 : 1."],"check":"Try <i>r<\/i> = 1, <i>h<\/i> = 1: cylinder π, cone ⅓π(3) = π ✓","wrong":{"1 : 3":"Uses the cone formula but forgets the cone is 3 times as tall.","3 : 1":"Triples the height but forgets the ⅓.","1 : 9":"Divides by 3 twice."}},"mx47":{"ask":"An arc of length 5π is on a circle with radius 6. What is its angle?","idea":"An arc is a fraction of the full circumference. Find the circumference, then the fraction the arc takes up, and apply that fraction to 360°.","steps":["Circumference: 2π(6) = 12π.","Fraction: 5π ÷ 12π = 5/12.","Angle: 5/12 × 360° = 150°."],"check":"150° is a bit less than half the circle, and 5π is a bit less than half of 12π ✓","wrong":{"300°":"Uses the radius as the diameter (circumference 6π).","75°":"Halves the correct angle.","30°":"Uses 1/12 instead of 5/12."}},"mx48":{"ask":"A similar triangle has perimeter 75. How long is its longest side?","idea":"Similar triangles scale every length by the same factor, including the perimeter. Find the scale factor from the perimeters, then apply it to the longest side (13).","steps":["Original perimeter: 5 + 12 + 13 = 30.","Scale factor: 75 ÷ 30 = 2.5.","Longest side: 13 × 2.5 = 32.5."],"check":"New sides 12.5, 30 and 32.5 add to 75 ✓","wrong":{"13":"That's the original longest side, before scaling.","30":"That's the original perimeter (or the new middle side).","26":"Scales by 2 instead of 2.5."}},"a1":{"ask":"What number plus 12 equals 30?","idea":"To get <i>x<\/i> alone, undo the +12 by subtracting 12. Do the same thing to both sides so the equation stays balanced, like a scale.","steps":["Subtract 12 from both sides: <i>x<\/i> + 12 − 12 = 30 − 12.","<i>x<\/i> = 18."],"check":"18 + 12 = 30 ✓","wrong":{"−18":"Subtracts in the wrong order (12 − 30). Check: −18 + 12 = −6.","2.5":"Divides 30 by 12, but nothing was multiplied.","42":"Adds 12 instead of subtracting it."}},"a2":{"ask":"4 times what number is 28?","idea":"4<i>x<\/i> means 4 × <i>x<\/i>. To undo multiplying by 4, divide both sides by 4.","steps":["Divide both sides by 4: <i>x<\/i> = 28 ÷ 4.","<i>x<\/i> = 7."],"check":"4 × 7 = 28 ✓","wrong":{"24":"Subtracts 4 instead of dividing.","32":"Adds 4 instead of dividing.","112":"Multiplies by 4 instead of dividing."}},"a3":{"ask":"What number divided by 3 gives 9?","idea":"<i>x<\/i>/3 means <i>x<\/i> ÷ 3. The opposite of dividing by 3 is multiplying by 3, so multiply both sides by 3.","steps":["Multiply both sides by 3: <i>x<\/i> = 9 × 3.","<i>x<\/i> = 27."],"check":"27 ÷ 3 = 9 ✓","wrong":{"3":"Divides 9 by 3 instead of multiplying. Check: 3 ÷ 3 = 1.","6":"Subtracts 3. Check: 6 ÷ 3 = 2.","12":"Adds 3. Check: 12 ÷ 3 = 4."}},"a4":{"ask":"Find the <i>x<\/i> that makes 3<i>x<\/i> − 7 and 2<i>x<\/i> + 5 equal.","idea":"Collect the <i>x<\/i> terms on one side and the plain numbers on the other. Subtract the smaller <i>x<\/i> term (2<i>x<\/i>) from both sides so <i>x<\/i> stays positive.","steps":["Subtract 2<i>x<\/i> from both sides: <i>x<\/i> − 7 = 5.","Add 7 to both sides: <i>x<\/i> = 12."],"check":"3(12) − 7 = 29 and 2(12) + 5 = 29 ✓","wrong":{"−12":"A sign slip. Check: 3(−12) − 7 = −43 but 2(−12) + 5 = −19.","−2":"Subtracts 5 − 7 instead of adding. Check: −13 ≠ 1.","2":"Doesn't work: 3(2) − 7 = −1 but 2(2) + 5 = 9."}},"a5":{"ask":"Which values of <i>x<\/i> make 2<i>x<\/i> + 5 greater than 17?","idea":"Solve an inequality like an equation, keeping the &gt; sign. The sign only flips if you multiply or divide by a negative number, and here you divide by 2, which is positive.","steps":["Subtract 5: 2<i>x<\/i> &gt; 12.","Divide by 2: <i>x<\/i> &gt; 6."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 7: 2(7) + 5 = 19 &gt; 17 ✓. Try <i>x<\/i> = 6: 17 is not &gt; 17, so 6 itself doesn't work.","wrong":{"<i>x<\/i> &gt; 12":"Stops before dividing by 2.","<i>x<\/i> &lt; 6":"Flips the sign, but you only flip when dividing by a negative.","<i>x<\/i> &gt; 11":"Adds 5 and then divides: 2<i>x<\/i> &gt; 22. The 5 must be subtracted."}},"a6":{"ask":"5 times (<i>x<\/i> − 2) is 20. Find <i>x<\/i>.","idea":"Undo the outside first: the parentheses are multiplied by 5, so divide both sides by 5. Then undo the −2 by adding 2.","steps":["Divide by 5: <i>x<\/i> − 2 = 4.","Add 2: <i>x<\/i> = 6."],"check":"5(6 − 2) = 5(4) = 20 ✓","wrong":{"2":"That's the number subtracted inside the parentheses. Check: 5(0) = 0.","4":"That's <i>x<\/i> − 2; add 2.","22":"Doesn't work: 5(22 − 2) = 100, not 20."}},"a7":{"ask":"Which values of <i>x<\/i> make −3<i>x<\/i> + 4 at most 19?","idea":"Solve like an equation, with one extra rule: when you multiply or divide both sides by a <em>negative<\/em> number, flip the inequality sign. Here you'll divide by −3.","steps":["Subtract 4: −3<i>x<\/i> ≤ 15.","Divide by −3 and flip the sign: <i>x<\/i> ≥ −5."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 0 (which is ≥ −5): 4 ≤ 19 ✓. Try <i>x<\/i> = −6: 22 ≤ 19 is false ✓","wrong":{"<i>x<\/i> ≥ 5":"Loses the negative sign: 15 ÷ (−3) = −5.","<i>x<\/i> ≤ −5":"Forgets to flip the sign after dividing by −3.","<i>x<\/i> ≤ 5":"Makes both mistakes: drops the sign and doesn't flip."}},"a8":{"ask":"Two fractions are equal. Find <i>x<\/i>.","idea":"When one fraction equals another, cross-multiply: multiply each numerator by the other fraction's denominator. That removes the fractions.","steps":["Cross-multiply: 2(<i>x<\/i> + 4) = 3(<i>x<\/i> − 2).","Distribute: 2<i>x<\/i> + 8 = 3<i>x<\/i> − 6.","Subtract 2<i>x<\/i> and add 6: 14 = <i>x<\/i>."],"check":"(14 + 4)/3 = 6 and (14 − 2)/2 = 6 ✓","wrong":{"−14":"A sign slip. Check: (−10)/3 ≠ (−16)/2.","2":"Makes the right side 0 but the left side 2.","10":"Doesn't work: 14/3 ≠ 8/2."}},"a9":{"ask":"Rearrange the area formula so <i>h<\/i> is by itself.","idea":"Treat the letters like numbers and undo operations the same way. <i>h<\/i> is multiplied by ½ and by <i>b<\/i>, so multiply by 2 and divide by <i>b<\/i>.","steps":["Multiply both sides by 2: 2<i>A<\/i> = <i>b<\/i><i>h<\/i>.","Divide both sides by <i>b<\/i>: <i>h<\/i> = 2<i>A<\/i>/<i>b<\/i>."],"check":"Try <i>b<\/i> = 4, <i>h<\/i> = 3: <i>A<\/i> = 6, and 2(6)/4 = 3 ✓","wrong":{"<i>h<\/i> = <i>Ab<\/i>/2":"Multiplies by <i>b<\/i> and divides by 2, the opposite of what's needed.","<i>h<\/i> = <i>A<\/i>/(2<i>b<\/i>)":"Divides by 2 instead of multiplying.","<i>h<\/i> = 2<i>Ab<\/i>":"Multiplies by <i>b<\/i> instead of dividing."}},"a10":{"ask":"With a $45 limit, how many 5-cent texts can Mia send?","idea":"“At most” means ≤. The $30 is paid once; the $0.05 is paid per text. Write the total, keep it ≤ 45, and solve.","steps":["30 + 0.05<i>t<\/i> ≤ 45.","Subtract 30: 0.05<i>t<\/i> ≤ 15.","Divide by 0.05: <i>t<\/i> ≤ 300."],"check":"300 texts: 30 + 15 = $45 ✓","wrong":{"150":"Uses 10 cents per text.","900":"Ignores the $30 monthly charge: 45 ÷ 0.05.","1,500":"Divides by 0.01 instead of 0.05."}},"a11":{"ask":"How steep is the line <i>y<\/i> = −4<i>x<\/i> + 9?","idea":"In slope-intercept form <i>y<\/i> = <i>m<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>b<\/i>, the slope is <i>m<\/i>, the number multiplied by <i>x<\/i>, including its sign. <i>b<\/i> is where the line crosses the <i>y<\/i>-axis.","steps":["Match to <i>y<\/i> = <i>m<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>b<\/i>: <i>m<\/i> = −4, <i>b<\/i> = 9.","The slope is −4."],"check":"From <i>x<\/i> = 0 to <i>x<\/i> = 1, <i>y<\/i> goes from 9 to 5, a change of −4 ✓","wrong":{"−9":"Uses the intercept with a negative sign.","4":"Drops the negative sign; the line goes down.","9":"That's the <i>y<\/i>-intercept."}},"a12":{"ask":"What is the output when the input is 5?","idea":"<i>f<\/i>(5) means replace every <i>x<\/i> with 5 and calculate. Multiply first, then subtract.","steps":["<i>f<\/i>(5) = 2(5) − 1.","= 10 − 1 = 9."],"check":"Each step up in <i>x<\/i> adds 2: <i>f<\/i>(4) = 7, <i>f<\/i>(5) = 9 ✓","wrong":{"3":"Doesn't work: 2(5) − 1 = 9.","10":"Forgets to subtract 1.","11":"Adds 1 instead of subtracting."}},"a13":{"ask":"Where does the line cross the <i>y<\/i>-axis?","idea":"Every point on the <i>y<\/i>-axis has <i>x<\/i> = 0. Put <i>x<\/i> = 0 into the equation to find <i>y<\/i>. In <i>y<\/i> = <i>m<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>b<\/i>, that point is (0, <i>b<\/i>).","steps":["<i>x<\/i> = 0: <i>y<\/i> = 3(0) − 8 = −8.","The point is (0, −8)."],"check":"(0, −8) has <i>x<\/i> = 0, so it's on the <i>y<\/i>-axis ✓","wrong":{"(−8, 0)":"Swaps the coordinates; that point is on the <i>x<\/i>-axis.","(0, 3)":"Uses the slope as the intercept.","(0, 8)":"Drops the negative sign."}},"a14":{"ask":"How steep is the line through (1, 3) and (4, 12)?","idea":"Slope = rise ÷ run = (change in <i>y<\/i>) ÷ (change in <i>x<\/i>). Subtract in the same order on the top and the bottom.","steps":["Rise: 12 − 3 = 9.","Run: 4 − 1 = 3.","Slope: 9 ÷ 3 = 3."],"check":"From (1, 3), go right 3 and up 9: you reach (4, 12) ✓","wrong":{"−3":"Subtracts in different orders on the top and bottom.","1/3":"Divides run by rise, which is upside down.","9":"That's only the rise; divide by the run."}},"a15":{"ask":"Write the line with slope 5 that crosses the <i>y<\/i>-axis at 2.","idea":"Slope-intercept form is <i>y<\/i> = <i>m<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>b<\/i>: <i>m<\/i> is the slope and <i>b<\/i> is the <i>y<\/i>-intercept. Just fill them in.","steps":["<i>m<\/i> = 5 and <i>b<\/i> = 2.","<i>y<\/i> = 5<i>x<\/i> + 2."],"check":"At <i>x<\/i> = 0, <i>y<\/i> = 2 ✓","wrong":{"<i>y<\/i> = 5<i>x<\/i> − 2":"Crosses at −2, not 2.","<i>y<\/i> = 2<i>x<\/i> − 5":"Swaps the slope and intercept, and flips a sign.","<i>y<\/i> = 2<i>x<\/i> + 5":"Swaps the slope and the intercept."}},"a16":{"ask":"Which line never meets <i>y<\/i> = 2<i>x<\/i> + 7?","idea":"Parallel lines have the same slope (same steepness) but different <i>y<\/i>-intercepts. Look for slope 2.","steps":["The given slope is 2.","Only <i>y<\/i> = 2<i>x<\/i> − 3 has slope 2, and its intercept is different (−3)."],"check":"Both rise 2 for every 1 to the right, so they stay the same distance apart ✓","wrong":{"<i>y<\/i> = ½<i>x<\/i> + 7":"Slope ½, so it isn't parallel. (It even shares the point (0, 7).)","<i>y<\/i> = −2<i>x<\/i> + 7":"Slope −2: it goes down, so it crosses the given line.","<i>y<\/i> = 7<i>x<\/i> + 2":"Slope 7, which is steeper."}},"a17":{"ask":"Write the equation of the line through (2, 5) and (6, −3).","idea":"Find the slope from the two points, then find <i>b<\/i> by putting one point into <i>y<\/i> = <i>m<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>b<\/i>.","steps":["Slope: (−3 − 5) ÷ (6 − 2) = −8 ÷ 4 = −2.","Use (2, 5): 5 = −2(2) + <i>b<\/i> = −4 + <i>b<\/i>, so <i>b<\/i> = 9.","<i>y<\/i> = −2<i>x<\/i> + 9."],"check":"(6, −3): −12 + 9 = −3 ✓","wrong":{"<i>y<\/i> = −2<i>x<\/i> + 1":"Right slope, wrong intercept. Check (2, 5): −4 + 1 = −3.","<i>y<\/i> = 2<i>x<\/i> + 1":"Wrong sign on the slope; the line goes down from (2, 5) to (6, −3).","<i>y<\/i> = −½<i>x<\/i> + 6":"Divides run by rise for the slope. Check (6, −3): −3 + 6 = 3."}},"a18":{"ask":"Find the line through (0, 0) at a right angle to <i>y<\/i> = (3/4)<i>x<\/i> − 1.","idea":"Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals: flip the fraction and change the sign. A line through the origin has <i>b<\/i> = 0.","steps":["Given slope: 3/4.","Flip and change sign: −4/3.","Through the origin: <i>y<\/i> = −(4/3)<i>x<\/i>."],"check":"(3/4) × (−4/3) = −1, the sign of perpendicular slopes ✓","wrong":{"<i>y<\/i> = (3/4)<i>x<\/i>":"Same slope, so it's parallel, not perpendicular.","<i>y<\/i> = −(3/4)<i>x<\/i>":"Changes the sign but doesn't flip the fraction.","<i>y<\/i> = (4/3)<i>x<\/i>":"Flips the fraction but doesn't change the sign."}},"a19":{"ask":"What do the numbers in C = 2.5<i>m<\/i> + 4 mean?","idea":"The number multiplied by the variable is the rate: the cost added for each mile. The number added on is the starting fee, the cost at 0 miles.","steps":["2.5 multiplies <i>m<\/i> (miles), so each mile adds $2.50.","4 is added once, so it's a $4 starting fee."],"check":"A 1-mile ride costs 2.5 + 4 = $6.50, and each extra mile adds $2.50 ✓","wrong":{"Each mile adds $4 to the cost.":"$4 is the starting fee, charged once.","The ride costs $2.50 before any miles are driven.":"At 0 miles, C = 4, so the starting cost is $4.","A 4-mile ride costs $2.50.":"A 4-mile ride costs 2.5(4) + 4 = $14."}},"a20":{"ask":"Which input makes the output 0?","idea":"Set the function equal to 0 and solve. The answer is where the line crosses the <i>x<\/i>-axis (the <i>x<\/i>-intercept).","steps":["−3<i>x<\/i> + 12 = 0.","Subtract 12: −3<i>x<\/i> = −12.","Divide by −3: <i>x<\/i> = 4."],"check":"<i>f<\/i>(4) = −12 + 12 = 0 ✓","wrong":{"−4":"Sign error when dividing by −3.","12":"That's the <i>y<\/i>-intercept, <i>f<\/i>(0).","36":"Multiplies 12 by 3 instead of dividing."}},"a21":{"ask":"Find <i>y<\/i> when <i>y<\/i> is twice <i>x<\/i> and they add to 9.","idea":"Substitution: the first equation says <i>y<\/i> = 2<i>x<\/i>, so replace <i>y<\/i> with 2<i>x<\/i> in the second equation. That leaves one equation with only <i>x<\/i>.","steps":["<i>x<\/i> + 2<i>x<\/i> = 9, so 3<i>x<\/i> = 9 and <i>x<\/i> = 3.","<i>y<\/i> = 2(3) = 6."],"check":"3 + 6 = 9 ✓ and 6 = 2 × 3 ✓","wrong":{"3":"That's <i>x<\/i>; the question asks for <i>y<\/i>.","9":"That's the sum <i>x<\/i> + <i>y<\/i>.","18":"That's 2 × 9, not a solution."}},"a22":{"ask":"Does (2, 5) make both equations true?","idea":"A solution to a system must make <em>every<\/em> equation true. Substitute <i>x<\/i> = 2 and <i>y<\/i> = 5 into each equation and check.","steps":["First: 2 + 3 = 5 ✓.","Second: 2(2) + 1 = 5 ✓.","Both work, so (2, 5) is a solution."],"check":"It's the point where the two lines cross ✓","wrong":{"No, it satisfies only the second":"It satisfies the first as well: 2 + 3 = 5.","No, it satisfies neither":"It satisfies both.","No, it satisfies only the first":"It satisfies the second as well: 2(2) + 1 = 5."}},"a23":{"ask":"Two numbers add to 12 and differ by 4. Find <i>y<\/i>.","idea":"Adding the equations cancels <i>y<\/i> (because +<i>y<\/i> and −<i>y<\/i> cancel). Find <i>x<\/i>, then use it to find <i>y<\/i>.","steps":["Add: 2<i>x<\/i> = 16, so <i>x<\/i> = 8.","<i>y<\/i> = 12 − 8 = 4."],"check":"8 + 4 = 12 ✓ and 8 − 4 = 4 ✓","wrong":{"8":"That's <i>x<\/i>.","12":"That's the sum.","16":"That's 2<i>x<\/i>."}},"a24":{"ask":"Solve the system and give <i>x<\/i>.","idea":"The second equation says <i>x<\/i> = <i>y<\/i> + 4. Substitute (<i>y<\/i> + 4) for <i>x<\/i> in the first equation, solve for <i>y<\/i>, then find <i>x<\/i>.","steps":["2(<i>y<\/i> + 4) + 3<i>y<\/i> = 13, so 5<i>y<\/i> + 8 = 13.","5<i>y<\/i> = 5, so <i>y<\/i> = 1.","<i>x<\/i> = 1 + 4 = 5."],"check":"2(5) + 3(1) = 13 ✓","wrong":{"1":"That's <i>y<\/i>.","4":"That's the number added in <i>x<\/i> = <i>y<\/i> + 4.","9":"Doesn't work: then <i>y<\/i> = 5, and 2(9) + 3(5) = 33."}},"a25":{"ask":"A family bought 8 tickets for $64. How many were adult tickets?","idea":"Two unknowns need two facts: the number of tickets and the money. Write child tickets as 8 − <i>a<\/i>, then use the money equation.","steps":["Let <i>a<\/i> = adult tickets; child = 8 − <i>a<\/i>.","10<i>a<\/i> + 6(8 − <i>a<\/i>) = 64, so 4<i>a<\/i> + 48 = 64.","4<i>a<\/i> = 16, so <i>a<\/i> = 4."],"check":"4 adults ($40) + 4 children ($24) = $64 ✓","wrong":{"2":"Doesn't work: 2 adults and 6 children cost $56.","6":"Doesn't work: 6 adults and 2 children cost $72.","8":"All adults would cost $80."}},"a26":{"ask":"Solve the system and give <i>y<\/i>.","idea":"The <i>y<\/i> terms are +2<i>y<\/i> and −2<i>y<\/i>, so adding the equations cancels <i>y<\/i>. Solve for <i>x<\/i>, then substitute back to find <i>y<\/i>.","steps":["Add: 8<i>x<\/i> = 24, so <i>x<\/i> = 3.","3(3) + 2<i>y<\/i> = 16, so 2<i>y<\/i> = 7.","<i>y<\/i> = 3.5."],"check":"Second equation: 5(3) − 2(3.5) = 15 − 7 = 8 ✓","wrong":{"3":"That's <i>x<\/i>.","4":"Doesn't work: 3(3) + 2(4) = 17, not 16.","7":"That's 2<i>y<\/i>; divide by 2."}},"a27":{"ask":"How many points do these two lines share?","idea":"Put both equations in <i>y<\/i> = <i>m<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>b<\/i> form. Different slopes: one solution. Same slope, different intercept: none (parallel). Same slope and intercept: they're the same line, so infinitely many.","steps":["Solve the second for <i>y<\/i>: −2<i>y<\/i> = −6<i>x<\/i> − 4, so <i>y<\/i> = 3<i>x<\/i> + 2.","That's exactly the first equation.","Same line, so infinitely many solutions."],"check":"Every point on <i>y<\/i> = 3<i>x<\/i> + 2, like (0, 2) and (1, 5), satisfies both ✓","wrong":{"None":"That would need the same slope with different intercepts; here the intercepts match too.","Exactly one":"That needs different slopes; both slopes are 3.","Exactly two":"Two different straight lines can never meet exactly twice."}},"a28":{"ask":"How many points do <i>y<\/i> = 4<i>x<\/i> + 1 and <i>y<\/i> = 4<i>x<\/i> − 3 share?","idea":"Both lines have slope 4 but different <i>y<\/i>-intercepts, so they're parallel: they never meet. No meeting point means no solution.","steps":["Slopes: 4 and 4 (the same).","Intercepts: 1 and −3 (different).","Parallel lines, so no solution."],"check":"Setting them equal gives 1 = −3, which is never true ✓","wrong":{"Two":"Two different straight lines can't meet exactly twice.","One":"That needs different slopes.","Infinitely many":"That needs the same line; the intercepts differ."}},"a29":{"ask":"40 drinks sold for $164. How many were large?","idea":"Two unknowns, two facts: the number of drinks and the money. Write small drinks as 40 − <i>L<\/i> and use the money equation.","steps":["3(40 − <i>L<\/i>) + 5<i>L<\/i> = 164.","120 + 2<i>L<\/i> = 164, so 2<i>L<\/i> = 44.","<i>L<\/i> = 22."],"check":"22 large ($110) + 18 small ($54) = $164 ✓","wrong":{"18":"That's the number of small drinks.","20":"Assumes an even split; that brings in only $160.","24":"Doesn't work: 24 large and 16 small bring in $168."}},"a30":{"ask":"Solve the system and give <i>x<\/i> + <i>y<\/i>.","idea":"Solve the first equation for <i>y<\/i> (it has a lone −<i>y<\/i>), substitute into the second, and solve. Then add <i>x<\/i> and <i>y<\/i>.","steps":["<i>y<\/i> = 4<i>x<\/i> − 10.","2<i>x<\/i> + 3(4<i>x<\/i> − 10) = 19, so 14<i>x<\/i> − 30 = 19, 14<i>x<\/i> = 49 and <i>x<\/i> = 3.5.","<i>y<\/i> = 4(3.5) − 10 = 4.","<i>x<\/i> + <i>y<\/i> = 7.5."],"check":"2(3.5) + 3(4) = 7 + 12 = 19 ✓","wrong":{"3.5":"That's <i>x<\/i> alone.","4":"That's <i>y<\/i> alone.","29":"Adds the right sides of the equations (10 + 19)."}},"a31":{"ask":"What is 3 to the fourth power?","idea":"An exponent tells you how many times to multiply the base by itself: 3<sup>4<\/sup> = 3 × 3 × 3 × 3. It's not 3 × 4.","steps":["3 × 3 = 9.","9 × 3 = 27.","27 × 3 = 81."],"check":"3<sup>2<\/sup> = 9, and 9<sup>2<\/sup> = 81 ✓","wrong":{"7":"Adds 3 + 4.","12":"Multiplies 3 × 4.","64":"That's 4<sup>3<\/sup>, with the base and exponent swapped."}},"a32":{"ask":"What positive number times itself gives 49?","idea":"The square root asks: which positive number, multiplied by itself, gives this? It undoes squaring.","steps":["7 × 7 = 49.","So √49 = 7."],"check":"7<sup>2<\/sup> = 49 ✓","wrong":{"14":"That's 7 × 2; a square root isn't half.","24.5":"Divides 49 by 2.","2401":"That's 49<sup>2<\/sup>, squaring instead of taking the root."}},"a33":{"ask":"Simplify <i>x<\/i><sup>5<\/sup> times <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>.","idea":"<i>x<\/i><sup>5<\/sup> is five <i>x<\/i>'s multiplied, and <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> is three more. Together that's eight <i>x<\/i>'s. So when multiplying powers of the same base, add the exponents.","steps":["5 + 3 = 8.","<i>x<\/i><sup>5<\/sup> · <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup>8<\/sup>."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 2: 32 × 8 = 256 = 2<sup>8<\/sup> ✓","wrong":{"<i>x<\/i><sup>15<\/sup>":"Multiplies the exponents; that's for a power of a power.","<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>":"Subtracts the exponents; that's for dividing.","2<i>x<\/i><sup>8<\/sup>":"There's no 2: the powers are multiplied, not added."}},"a34":{"ask":"Simplify (2<i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>)<sup>2<\/sup>.","idea":"Squaring something means multiplying it by itself, so <em>both<\/em> parts get squared: the 2 and the <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>. A power of a power multiplies exponents.","steps":["2<sup>2<\/sup> = 4.","(<i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup>3·2<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup>6<\/sup>.","Result: 4<i>x<\/i><sup>6<\/sup>."],"check":"(2<i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>)(2<i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>) = 4<i>x<\/i><sup>6<\/sup> ✓","wrong":{"4<i>x<\/i><sup>5<\/sup>":"Adds the exponents (3 + 2) instead of multiplying.","2<i>x<\/i><sup>6<\/sup>":"Forgets to square the 2.","2<i>x<\/i><sup>9<\/sup>":"Cubes the power and forgets the 2."}},"a35":{"ask":"What is 5 to the power 0?","idea":"Any nonzero number to the power 0 equals 1. One way to see it: 5<sup>3<\/sup> ÷ 5<sup>3<\/sup> = 1, and by the subtraction rule it's also 5<sup>3−3<\/sup> = 5<sup>0<\/sup>.","steps":["5<sup>0<\/sup> = 1."],"check":"Pattern: 5<sup>2<\/sup> = 25, 5<sup>1<\/sup> = 5, 5<sup>0<\/sup> = 1 (divide by 5 each time) ✓","wrong":{"−5":"A zero exponent doesn't make a number negative.","0":"The exponent is 0, but the value is 1.","5":"That's 5<sup>1<\/sup>."}},"a36":{"ask":"What is 2 to the power −3?","idea":"A negative exponent means “one over”: 2<sup>−3<\/sup> = 1 ÷ 2<sup>3<\/sup>. It does <em>not<\/em> make the number negative.","steps":["2<sup>3<\/sup> = 8.","2<sup>−3<\/sup> = 1/8."],"check":"Pattern: 2<sup>0<\/sup> = 1, 2<sup>−1<\/sup> = 1/2, 2<sup>−2<\/sup> = 1/4, 2<sup>−3<\/sup> = 1/8 ✓","wrong":{"−8":"Treats the negative exponent as a negative number.","−6":"Multiplies 2 × (−3).","1/6":"Uses 2 × 3 instead of 2<sup>3<\/sup> in the denominator."}},"a37":{"ask":"Simplify √72.","idea":"Look for the largest perfect square that divides 72. Then √(square × other) = √square × √other.","steps":["72 = 36 × 2, and 36 is a perfect square.","√72 = √36 × √2 = 6√2."],"check":"(6√2)<sup>2<\/sup> = 36 × 2 = 72 ✓","wrong":{"2√6":"(2√6)<sup>2<\/sup> = 24, not 72.","8√3":"(8√3)<sup>2<\/sup> = 192, not 72.","36√2":"Forgets to take the square root of 36."}},"a38":{"ask":"Simplify <i>x<\/i><sup>7<\/sup> divided by <i>x<\/i><sup>−2<\/sup>.","idea":"When dividing powers of the same base, subtract the exponents: top minus bottom. Subtracting a negative is the same as adding.","steps":["7 − (−2) = 7 + 2 = 9.","<i>x<\/i><sup>7<\/sup> ÷ <i>x<\/i><sup>−2<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup>9<\/sup>."],"check":"Dividing by <i>x<\/i><sup>−2<\/sup> is the same as multiplying by <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>: <i>x<\/i><sup>7<\/sup> · <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup>9<\/sup> ✓","wrong":{"<i>x<\/i><sup>−5<\/sup>":"Adds 7 + (−2) as if multiplying.","<i>x<\/i><sup>5<\/sup>":"Subtracts 2 instead of subtracting −2.","<i>x<\/i><sup>−14<\/sup>":"Multiplies the exponents."}},"a39":{"ask":"What is 27 to the power 2/3?","idea":"In a fraction exponent, the bottom is the root and the top is the power. 27<sup>2/3<\/sup> means: take the cube root of 27, then square it.","steps":["Cube root of 27: 3 (since 3 × 3 × 3 = 27).","Square it: 3<sup>2<\/sup> = 9."],"check":"9<sup>3<\/sup> = 729 and 27<sup>2<\/sup> = 729, so 9 is correct ✓","wrong":{"3":"Takes the cube root but forgets to square.","18":"Multiplies 27 × 2/3.","81":"Too big: the cube root of 27 is 3, and 3 squared is 9."}},"a40":{"ask":"Multiply two numbers in scientific notation.","idea":"Multiply the front numbers together, and multiply the powers of 10 by adding their exponents. Then make sure the front number is between 1 and 10.","steps":["3 × 2 = 6.","10<sup>4<\/sup> × 10<sup>3<\/sup> = 10<sup>4+3<\/sup> = 10<sup>7<\/sup>.","Result: 6 × 10<sup>7<\/sup>."],"check":"30,000 × 2,000 = 60,000,000 = 6 × 10<sup>7<\/sup> ✓","wrong":{"5 × 10<sup>7<\/sup>":"Adds 3 + 2 instead of multiplying.","6 × 10<sup>1<\/sup>":"Subtracts the exponents.","6 × 10<sup>12<\/sup>":"Multiplies the exponents instead of adding."}},"a41":{"ask":"Add the two expressions.","idea":"“Like terms” have the same variable part. Combine <i>x<\/i>-terms with <i>x<\/i>-terms and plain numbers with plain numbers. With addition, the parentheses can simply be dropped.","steps":["<i>x<\/i>-terms: 3<i>x<\/i> + <i>x<\/i> = 4<i>x<\/i>.","Numbers: 2 + (−5) = −3.","Result: 4<i>x<\/i> − 3."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 1: (5) + (−4) = 1, and 4 − 3 = 1 ✓","wrong":{"2<i>x<\/i> − 3":"Subtracts <i>x<\/i> instead of adding it.","3<i>x<\/i> − 3":"Forgets the <i>x<\/i> in the second group.","4<i>x<\/i> + 7":"Adds 5 instead of subtracting it."}},"a42":{"ask":"Subtract the second expression from the first.","idea":"The minus sign in front of the parentheses applies to <em>every<\/em> term inside. So −(3<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4<i>x<\/i>) becomes −3<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 4<i>x<\/i>. Then combine like terms.","steps":["Distribute the minus: 5<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 2<i>x<\/i> − 3<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 4<i>x<\/i>.","<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> terms: 5 − 3 = 2. <i>x<\/i> terms: 2 + 4 = 6.","Result: 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 6<i>x<\/i>."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 1: 7 − (−1) = 8, and 2 + 6 = 8 ✓","wrong":{"2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 2<i>x<\/i>":"Subtracts only the first term; −(−4<i>x<\/i>) is +4<i>x<\/i>.","8<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 2<i>x<\/i>":"Adds the <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> terms instead of subtracting.","2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 2<i>x<\/i>":"Leaves out the −4<i>x<\/i> term entirely."}},"a43":{"ask":"Multiply out 2<i>x<\/i>(<i>x<\/i> + 5).","idea":"Distribute: multiply the outside term by each term inside. <i>x<\/i> times <i>x<\/i> is <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","steps":["2<i>x<\/i> · <i>x<\/i> = 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","2<i>x<\/i> · 5 = 10<i>x<\/i>.","Result: 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 10<i>x<\/i>."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 1: 2(6) = 12, and 2 + 10 = 12 ✓","wrong":{"2<i>x<\/i> + 10<i>x<\/i>":"Multiplies 2 × <i>x<\/i> instead of 2<i>x<\/i> × <i>x<\/i>; it should be 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 5":"Forgets to multiply the 5 by 2<i>x<\/i>.","<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 10<i>x<\/i>":"Drops the 2 in 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>."}},"a44":{"ask":"Multiply (<i>x<\/i> + 3)(<i>x<\/i> + 4).","idea":"Multiply each term in the first parentheses by each term in the second (sometimes called FOIL: First, Outer, Inner, Last). Then combine the two middle terms.","steps":["First: <i>x<\/i> · <i>x<\/i> = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","Outer + inner: 4<i>x<\/i> + 3<i>x<\/i> = 7<i>x<\/i>.","Last: 3 · 4 = 12.","Result: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 7<i>x<\/i> + 12."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 1: (4)(5) = 20, and 1 + 7 + 12 = 20 ✓","wrong":{"<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 12<i>x<\/i> + 7":"Swaps the sum and product: the middle term is 3 + 4 and the last is 3 × 4.","<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 12":"Forgets the middle terms.","<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 7<i>x<\/i> + 7":"Adds 3 + 4 for the last term instead of multiplying."}},"a45":{"ask":"Write <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 8<i>x<\/i> + 15 as a product of two parentheses.","idea":"For <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + <i>b<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>c<\/i>, find two numbers that multiply to <i>c<\/i> (15) and add to <i>b<\/i> (8). Those numbers go in the parentheses.","steps":["Pairs that multiply to 15: 1 and 15, 3 and 5.","3 + 5 = 8 ✓.","(<i>x<\/i> + 3)(<i>x<\/i> + 5)."],"check":"Expand: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 5<i>x<\/i> + 3<i>x<\/i> + 15 = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 8<i>x<\/i> + 15 ✓","wrong":{"(<i>x<\/i> + 1)(<i>x<\/i> + 15)":"Multiplies to 15 but adds to 16.","(<i>x<\/i> − 3)(<i>x<\/i> − 5)":"Gives −8<i>x<\/i>, not +8<i>x<\/i>.","(<i>x<\/i> + 2)(<i>x<\/i> + 6)":"Adds to 8 but multiplies to 12."}},"a46":{"ask":"Factor out everything the two terms share.","idea":"Find the greatest common factor (GCF) of the numbers and of the variables. The GCF of 6 and 9 is 3, and both terms have at least one <i>x<\/i>. “Completely” means take out the <em>largest<\/em> common factor.","steps":["GCF: 3<i>x<\/i>.","6<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> ÷ 3<i>x<\/i> = 2<i>x<\/i>, and 9<i>x<\/i> ÷ 3<i>x<\/i> = 3.","Result: 3<i>x<\/i>(2<i>x<\/i> + 3)."],"check":"3<i>x<\/i>(2<i>x<\/i>) + 3<i>x<\/i>(3) = 6<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 9<i>x<\/i> ✓","wrong":{"3<i>x<\/i>(2<i>x<\/i> + 9)":"Doesn't divide the 9 by 3.","<i>x<\/i>(6<i>x<\/i> + 9)":"Correct but not complete: 3 still divides 6 and 9.","3(2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 3<i>x<\/i>)":"Correct but not complete: <i>x<\/i> still divides both terms."}},"a47":{"ask":"Factor <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 49.","idea":"A <strong>difference of squares<\/strong> factors as <i>a<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − <i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = (<i>a<\/i> − <i>b<\/i>)(<i>a<\/i> + <i>b<\/i>). Here 49 = 7<sup>2<\/sup>.","steps":["<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 7<sup>2<\/sup>.","(<i>x<\/i> − 7)(<i>x<\/i> + 7)."],"check":"Expand: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 7<i>x<\/i> − 7<i>x<\/i> − 49 = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 49 ✓","wrong":{"(<i>x<\/i> − 7)<sup>2<\/sup>":"Expands to <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 14<i>x<\/i> + 49.","(<i>x<\/i> + 7)<sup>2<\/sup>":"Expands to <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 14<i>x<\/i> + 49.","(<i>x<\/i> − 49)(<i>x<\/i> + 1)":"Expands to <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 48<i>x<\/i> − 49."}},"a48":{"ask":"Factor <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − <i>x<\/i> − 12.","idea":"Find two numbers that multiply to −12 and add to −1 (the middle coefficient). A negative product means the numbers have opposite signs.","steps":["Pairs for −12 with opposite signs: −4 and 3 add to −1 ✓.","(<i>x<\/i> − 4)(<i>x<\/i> + 3)."],"check":"Expand: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 3<i>x<\/i> − 4<i>x<\/i> − 12 = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − <i>x<\/i> − 12 ✓","wrong":{"(<i>x<\/i> − 6)(<i>x<\/i> + 2)":"Multiplies to −12 but adds to −4.","(<i>x<\/i> − 12)(<i>x<\/i> + 1)":"Multiplies to −12 but adds to −11.","(<i>x<\/i> + 4)(<i>x<\/i> − 3)":"Adds to +1; the signs are flipped."}},"a49":{"ask":"Multiply out (2<i>x<\/i> − 3)<sup>2<\/sup>.","idea":"Squaring means multiplying the expression by itself: (2<i>x<\/i> − 3)(2<i>x<\/i> − 3). There's always a middle term: (<i>a<\/i> − <i>b<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>a<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 2<i>ab<\/i> + <i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","steps":["(2<i>x<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> = 4<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","Middle: 2 × (2<i>x<\/i>)(−3) = −12<i>x<\/i>.","(−3)<sup>2<\/sup> = +9.","Result: 4<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 12<i>x<\/i> + 9."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 1: (−1)<sup>2<\/sup> = 1, and 4 − 12 + 9 = 1 ✓","wrong":{"4<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 9":"Forgets the middle term.","4<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 6<i>x<\/i> + 9":"Counts the middle term only once.","4<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 12<i>x<\/i> − 9":"(−3) × (−3) is +9, not −9."}},"a50":{"ask":"Factor 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 7<i>x<\/i> + 3.","idea":"With a number in front of <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>, try pairs of factors and check the middle term by expanding. The first terms must multiply to 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> and the last terms to 3.","steps":["Try (2<i>x<\/i> + 1)(<i>x<\/i> + 3): outer 6<i>x<\/i> + inner <i>x<\/i> = 7<i>x<\/i> ✓.","So 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 7<i>x<\/i> + 3 = (2<i>x<\/i> + 1)(<i>x<\/i> + 3)."],"check":"Expand: 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 6<i>x<\/i> + <i>x<\/i> + 3 ✓","wrong":{"(2<i>x<\/i> + 7)(<i>x<\/i> + 3)":"Expands to 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 13<i>x<\/i> + 21.","(2<i>x<\/i> + 3)(<i>x<\/i> + 1)":"Expands to 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 5<i>x<\/i> + 3; the middle term is 5<i>x<\/i>.","(<i>x<\/i> + 1)(<i>x<\/i> + 3)":"Expands to <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 4<i>x<\/i> + 3; it loses the 2 in front."}},"a51":{"ask":"Which numbers squared give 36?","idea":"Both a positive and a negative number square to the same result, because a negative times a negative is positive. So <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 36 has two solutions.","steps":["6 × 6 = 36 and (−6) × (−6) = 36.","<i>x<\/i> = 6 or <i>x<\/i> = −6."],"check":"Both check ✓","wrong":{"6 only":"Misses −6, which also squares to 36.","18":"Divides 36 by 2 instead of taking the square root.","18 and −18":"18<sup>2<\/sup> = 324, not 36."}},"a52":{"ask":"When is (<i>x<\/i> − 2)(<i>x<\/i> + 5) equal to 0?","idea":"If a product is 0, at least one factor must be 0 (the zero product property). Set each factor equal to 0. Each solution has the <em>opposite<\/em> sign of the number in its factor.","steps":["<i>x<\/i> − 2 = 0 gives <i>x<\/i> = 2.","<i>x<\/i> + 5 = 0 gives <i>x<\/i> = −5."],"check":"<i>x<\/i> = 2: (0)(7) = 0 ✓. <i>x<\/i> = −5: (−7)(0) = 0 ✓","wrong":{"−2 and 5":"Copies the signs from the factors instead of flipping them.","2 and 5":"Only the first is right: <i>x<\/i> = 5 gives (3)(10) = 30.","−2 and −5":"Only −5 is right: <i>x<\/i> = −2 gives (−4)(3) = −12."}},"a53":{"ask":"Find the turning point of the parabola.","idea":"In vertex form <i>y<\/i> = (<i>x<\/i> − <i>h<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> + <i>k<\/i>, the vertex is (<i>h<\/i>, <i>k<\/i>). Because of the minus sign in the form, <i>h<\/i> is the opposite of the number you see; <i>k<\/i> is read as is.","steps":["(<i>x<\/i> − 3) means <i>h<\/i> = 3.","+1 means <i>k<\/i> = 1.","Vertex: (3, 1)."],"check":"At <i>x<\/i> = 3, the squared part is 0, so <i>y<\/i> = 1 is the lowest value ✓","wrong":{"(3, −1)":"Flips the sign of <i>k<\/i>; <i>k<\/i> is read as is.","(−3, 1)":"Doesn't flip the sign of <i>h<\/i>.","(1, 3)":"Swaps the coordinates."}},"a54":{"ask":"Solve <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 6<i>x<\/i> + 8 = 0.","idea":"Factor by finding two numbers that multiply to 8 and add to 6. Then set each factor to 0; the solutions have the opposite signs.","steps":["2 and 4 multiply to 8 and add to 6.","(<i>x<\/i> + 2)(<i>x<\/i> + 4) = 0.","<i>x<\/i> = −2 or <i>x<\/i> = −4."],"check":"(−2)<sup>2<\/sup> + 6(−2) + 8 = 4 − 12 + 8 = 0 ✓","wrong":{"2 and 4":"Forgets to flip the signs. Check: 4 + 12 + 8 = 24.","−2 and 4":"Only −2 works: 16 + 24 + 8 = 48 for <i>x<\/i> = 4.","−8 and 1":"Doesn't work: 64 − 48 + 8 = 24 for <i>x<\/i> = −8."}},"a55":{"ask":"Find the vertical line that splits the parabola into mirror halves.","idea":"The axis of symmetry passes through the vertex. For <i>y<\/i> = <i>a<\/i><i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + <i>b<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>c<\/i>, it's the line <i>x<\/i> = −<i>b<\/i> ÷ (2<i>a<\/i>).","steps":["<i>a<\/i> = 1, <i>b<\/i> = −10.","<i>x<\/i> = −(−10) ÷ 2 = 5."],"check":"Points on either side match: <i>x<\/i> = 4 and <i>x<\/i> = 6 both give <i>y<\/i> = −21 ✓","wrong":{"<i>x<\/i> = 3":"That's the constant <i>c<\/i>.","<i>x<\/i> = −5":"Forgets the minus sign in −<i>b<\/i>/(2<i>a<\/i>).","<i>x<\/i> = 10":"Forgets to divide by 2<i>a<\/i>."}},"a56":{"ask":"Solve 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 50.","idea":"Get <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> alone by dividing by 2, then take the square root. Remember both the positive and negative roots.","steps":["Divide by 2: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 25.","<i>x<\/i> = 5 or <i>x<\/i> = −5."],"check":"2(25) = 50 for both ✓","wrong":{"25 and −25":"Stops at <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 25 and forgets the square root.","5 only":"Misses the negative solution.","12.5 and −12.5":"Divides by 4 instead of taking a square root."}},"a57":{"ask":"Solve a quadratic that doesn't factor nicely.","idea":"When factoring doesn't work, use the quadratic formula: <i>x<\/i> = (−<i>b<\/i> ± √(<i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4<i>ac<\/i>)) ÷ (2<i>a<\/i>). Then simplify the square root.","steps":["<i>a<\/i> = 1, <i>b<\/i> = −4, <i>c<\/i> = −1.","<i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4<i>ac<\/i> = 16 + 4 = 20.","<i>x<\/i> = (4 ± √20) ÷ 2 = (4 ± 2√5) ÷ 2 = 2 ± √5."],"check":"Sum of solutions = 4 = −<i>b<\/i>/<i>a<\/i> ✓","wrong":{"2 ± √3":"Uses 16 − 4 = 12 under the root; with <i>c<\/i> = −1, it's 16 + 4.","4 ± √5":"Divides only part of the numerator by 2.","−2 ± √5":"Forgets the minus in −<i>b<\/i>."}},"a58":{"ask":"How many real solutions does 3<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 2<i>x<\/i> + 5 = 0 have?","idea":"The discriminant <i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4<i>ac<\/i> counts real solutions: positive → two, zero → one, negative → none (you can't take the square root of a negative number).","steps":["<i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4<i>ac<\/i> = 4 − 4(3)(5) = 4 − 60 = −56.","Negative, so no real solutions."],"check":"The parabola opens up with its lowest point above the <i>x<\/i>-axis, so it never crosses ✓","wrong":{"One":"That needs a discriminant of exactly 0.","Two":"That needs a positive discriminant.","Infinitely many":"A quadratic equation has at most two solutions."}},"a59":{"ask":"When does the ball reach the ground (height 0)?","idea":"“Hits the ground” means <i>h<\/i> = 0. Set the formula equal to 0, simplify by dividing by −16, and factor. A time can't be negative.","steps":["−16<i>t<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 32<i>t<\/i> + 48 = 0. Divide by −16: <i>t<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 2<i>t<\/i> − 3 = 0.","Factor: (<i>t<\/i> − 3)(<i>t<\/i> + 1) = 0.","<i>t<\/i> = 3 or <i>t<\/i> = −1. Time can't be negative, so <i>t<\/i> = 3 seconds."],"check":"<i>h<\/i>(3) = −144 + 96 + 48 = 0 ✓","wrong":{"48 seconds":"48 feet is the starting height, not a time.","4 seconds":"Doesn't work: <i>h<\/i>(4) = −256 + 128 + 48 = −80.","1 second":"That's when the ball is at its highest (64 feet), not when it lands."}},"a60":{"ask":"How high does the ball go?","idea":"The parabola opens downward (negative <i>t<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> term), so its maximum is at the vertex, <i>t<\/i> = −<i>b<\/i> ÷ (2<i>a<\/i>). The maximum height is the output there.","steps":["<i>t<\/i> = −20 ÷ (2 × −5) = 2 seconds.","<i>h<\/i>(2) = −5(4) + 20(2) + 1 = −20 + 40 + 1 = 21 meters."],"check":"<i>h<\/i>(1) = <i>h<\/i>(3) = 16, lower on both sides ✓","wrong":{"2 meters":"2 is the time in seconds, not the height.","41 meters":"Adds the −20 instead of subtracting.","20 meters":"Forgets the +1."}},"a61":{"ask":"What is <i>f<\/i>(3) for <i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) = 3 · 2<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup>?","idea":"Order of operations: do the exponent first (2<sup>3<\/sup>), then multiply by 3.","steps":["2<sup>3<\/sup> = 8.","3 × 8 = 24."],"check":"<i>f<\/i>(0) = 3, <i>f<\/i>(1) = 6, <i>f<\/i>(2) = 12, <i>f<\/i>(3) = 24: doubling each time ✓","wrong":{"18":"Multiplies 3 × 2 × 3 instead of using the exponent.","48":"That's <i>f<\/i>(4).","216":"Multiplies 3 × 2 first and then cubes: 6<sup>3<\/sup>."}},"a62":{"ask":"Which equation grows by the same percent each step?","idea":"Exponential functions have the variable in the <em>exponent<\/em>: <i>y<\/i> = <i>a<\/i> · <i>b<\/i><sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup>. If the base <i>b<\/i> is greater than 1, it's growth; between 0 and 1, it's decay.","steps":["<i>y<\/i> = 5(1.08)<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup> has <i>x<\/i> in the exponent and base 1.08 &gt; 1.","So it grows by 8% each time <i>x<\/i> increases by 1."],"check":"<i>x<\/i> = 0 → 5, <i>x<\/i> = 1 → 5.4, <i>x<\/i> = 2 → 5.832: each is 1.08 times the last ✓","wrong":{"<i>y<\/i> = 5<i>x<\/i> + 1.08":"Linear: it adds 5 each step instead of multiplying.","<i>y<\/i> = 5(0.92)<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup>":"Exponential <em>decay<\/em>: the base 0.92 is less than 1.","<i>y<\/i> = 1.08<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>":"Quadratic: <i>x<\/i> is in the base, not the exponent."}},"a63":{"ask":"What is $2,000 worth after 2 years of 5% growth?","idea":"Growing by 5% means multiplying by 1.05 each year. The second year's growth is 5% of the <em>new<\/em> amount, so it's a bit more than the first year's.","steps":["Year 1: 2,000 × 1.05 = 2,100.","Year 2: 2,100 × 1.05 = 2,205."],"check":"2,000 × 1.05<sup>2<\/sup> = 2,000 × 1.1025 = 2,205 ✓","wrong":{"$2,100":"That's only 1 year.","$2,200":"Adds $100 each year (5% of the original), which is simple, not compound, growth.","$4,410":"Doubles the 2-year value."}},"a64":{"ask":"Write the car's value after <i>t<\/i> years if it loses 15% each year.","idea":"Losing 15% means keeping 85%, so multiply by 0.85 once per year. After <i>t<\/i> years, that's (0.85)<sup><i>t<\/i><\/sup>.","steps":["Each year: value × 0.85.","After <i>t<\/i> years: 20,000(0.85)<sup><i>t<\/i><\/sup>."],"check":"After 1 year: 20,000 × 0.85 = 17,000, which is 15% less ✓","wrong":{"20,000(1.15)<sup><i>t<\/i><\/sup>":"Grows by 15% each year.","20,000(0.15)<sup><i>t<\/i><\/sup>":"Keeps only 15% each year, losing 85%.","20,000 − 0.15<i>t<\/i>":"Subtracts a tiny fixed amount; it's not a percent loss."}},"a65":{"ask":"Starting at 300 and doubling every 5 years, what is the population after 15 years?","idea":"Count the doubling periods first: 15 years ÷ 5 years = 3 doublings. Then double the starting value that many times.","steps":["Doublings: 15 ÷ 5 = 3.","300 → 600 → 1,200 → 2,400."],"check":"300 × 2<sup>3<\/sup> = 300 × 8 = 2,400 ✓","wrong":{"900":"Triples instead of doubling three times (300 × 3).","1,800":"Multiplies by 6 (3 × 2) instead of 2<sup>3<\/sup> = 8.","4,500":"Multiplies 300 by 15."}},"a66":{"ask":"Which <i>x<\/i> makes 2 · 3<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup> equal 162?","idea":"Isolate the power by dividing by 2. Then write the result as a power of 3 and match the exponents.","steps":["Divide by 2: 3<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup> = 81.","81 = 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 = 3<sup>4<\/sup>.","<i>x<\/i> = 4."],"check":"2 · 3<sup>4<\/sup> = 2 × 81 = 162 ✓","wrong":{"3":"Doesn't work: 2 · 27 = 54.","27":"That's 3<sup>3<\/sup>, not the exponent.","81":"That's 3<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup>, not <i>x<\/i>."}},"a67":{"ask":"80 g loses half its mass every 6 hours. How much is left after 24 hours?","idea":"Each 6-hour period halves the amount (this is called a half-life). Count the periods, then halve that many times. It never reaches 0 this way.","steps":["Periods: 24 ÷ 6 = 4.","80 → 40 → 20 → 10 → 5 grams."],"check":"80 × (1/2)<sup>4<\/sup> = 80 ÷ 16 = 5 ✓","wrong":{"0 g":"Halving never reaches exactly 0.","10 g":"Stops after 3 halvings (18 hours).","20 g":"Stops after 2 halvings (12 hours)."}},"a68":{"ask":"Which equation fits the table?","idea":"Look at how <i>y<\/i> changes as <i>x<\/i> goes up by 1. Adding the same amount means linear; multiplying by the same number means exponential. The value at <i>x<\/i> = 0 is the starting value.","steps":["5 → 15 → 45 → 135: each is 3 times the one before, so it's exponential with base 3.","At <i>x<\/i> = 0, <i>y<\/i> = 5, so the starting value is 5.","<i>y<\/i> = 5 · 3<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup>."],"check":"<i>x<\/i> = 3: 5 × 27 = 135 ✓","wrong":{"<i>y<\/i> = 3 · 5<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup>":"Swaps the start and the base: at <i>x<\/i> = 0 it gives 3.","<i>y<\/i> = 5 + 10<i>x<\/i>":"Linear: it fits only the first two points (at <i>x<\/i> = 2 it gives 25).","<i>y<\/i> = 15<i>x<\/i>":"Gives 0 at <i>x<\/i> = 0, not 5."}},"a69":{"ask":"Compare adding $100 a year with growing 8% a year for 10 years.","idea":"Option A is linear (adds the same amount), and Option B is exponential (multiplies by the same factor). Compute each after 10 years and subtract.","steps":["A: 1,000 + 10 × 100 = $2,000.","B: 1,000 × 1.08<sup>10<\/sup> ≈ $2,158.92.","B is worth more, by about $159."],"check":"In year 1, B earns $80 and A earns $100, but B's yearly gain keeps growing and passes A's ✓","wrong":{"Option B, by about $800":"Too big: B is about $2,159 and A is $2,000.","They are worth the same":"They differ by about $159.","Option A, by about $159":"The right difference, but B is the larger one."}},"a70":{"ask":"Multiplying by 0.75 each step is a decrease of what percent?","idea":"Multiplying by 0.75 keeps 75% of the value. The percent decrease is the part lost: 100% − 75%.","steps":["Kept: 75%.","Lost: 100% − 75% = 25%."],"check":"<i>g<\/i>(0) = 400, <i>g<\/i>(1) = 300: a drop of 100, which is 25% of 400 ✓","wrong":{"0.75%":"Reads the decimal 0.75 as a percent.","75%":"That's the percent kept, not lost.","400%":"That's the starting value, not a percent."}},"a71":{"ask":"Find the average of 4, 7, 9, 10 and 15.","idea":"Mean = total ÷ number of values.","steps":["Total: 4 + 7 + 9 + 10 + 15 = 45.","5 values: 45 ÷ 5 = 9."],"check":"5 × 9 = 45 ✓","wrong":{"10":"Too big: 5 × 10 = 50.","11":"Too big: 5 × 11 = 55.","45":"That's the total; divide by 5."}},"a72":{"ask":"How spread out are the data from smallest to largest?","idea":"Range = largest value − smallest value. Find both first; the list isn't in order.","steps":["Largest: 20. Smallest: 3.","Range: 20 − 3 = 17."],"check":"All values lie between 3 and 20 ✓","wrong":{"8":"That's 20 − 12, not using the smallest value.","9.6":"That's the mean: 48 ÷ 5.","20":"That's the largest value."}},"a73":{"ask":"Find the middle value of six numbers.","idea":"Put the values in order first. With an even number of values, there are two middle values, and the median is their average.","steps":["Order: 5, 7, 9, 12, 15, 20.","Middle two: 9 and 12.","Median: (9 + 12) ÷ 2 = 10.5."],"check":"Three values are below 10.5 and three above ✓","wrong":{"9":"Takes only one of the two middle values.","11.3":"That's the mean: 68 ÷ 6.","12":"Takes only the other middle value."}},"a74":{"ask":"Every score goes up 5. What happens to the mean?","idea":"Adding the same amount to every value adds that amount to the total for each value, so the mean goes up by exactly that amount.","steps":["New mean = 78 + 5 = 83."],"check":"With 3 students: 70, 78, 86 (mean 78) become 75, 83, 91 (mean 83) ✓","wrong":{"73":"Subtracts 5 instead of adding.","78":"The mean changes, because every score went up.","390":"Multiplies 78 × 5."}},"a75":{"ask":"Practice goes up and errors go down. What kind of relationship is that?","idea":"Correlation describes the direction of a trend. Both variables rising is positive; one rising while the other falls is negative. A correlation alone never proves that one causes the other.","steps":["As practice increases, errors decrease.","Opposite directions means negative correlation."],"check":"A scatterplot would slope downward from left to right ✓","wrong":{"No correlation":"There is a clear trend.","Proof that practice causes fewer errors":"A scatterplot shows a relationship, but it can't prove cause.","Positive correlation":"Positive means both go up together."}},"a76":{"ask":"Use the line to predict the score for 10 hours of study.","idea":"Substitute the <i>x<\/i>-value into the line of best fit to get the predicted <i>y<\/i>.","steps":["2.4(10) + 50 = 24 + 50 = 74."],"check":"Each hour adds 2.4 points to a starting prediction of 50 ✓","wrong":{"24":"Forgets to add 50.","50":"That's the prediction for 0 hours.","76":"Doesn't match: 2.4(10) + 50 = 74."}},"a77":{"ask":"The mean of 5 numbers is 20. Find the missing fifth number.","idea":"Use the total: total = mean × count. Subtract the numbers you know from the total.","steps":["Total: 5 × 20 = 100.","Known: 18 + 22 + 25 + 15 = 80.","Missing: 100 − 80 = 20."],"check":"(80 + 20) ÷ 5 = 20 ✓","wrong":{"18":"Doesn't work: the total would be 98 and the mean 19.6.","80":"That's the sum of the four known numbers.","100":"That's the total of all five."}},"a78":{"ask":"Which measure best describes a typical value when one value is far from the rest?","idea":"The mean uses every value, so one extreme value (an outlier) can pull it far away from the typical values. The median, the middle value, isn't affected much by outliers.","steps":["Mean: 45 ÷ 5 = 9, which is bigger than four of the five values.","Median: 4, right among the typical values.","So the median is the better description."],"check":"Without the 30, the values are 2, 4, 4 and 5, close to 4 ✓","wrong":{"The range, because it shows the spread":"Range measures spread, not a typical value.","The mean, because it uses every value":"Using every value is exactly why the outlier distorts it.","The mean, because 30 is the largest value":"A large outlier is a reason <em>not<\/em> to use the mean."}},"a79":{"ask":"Among band members only, what fraction play a sport?","idea":"“What fraction of the band members” sets the group: band members. Count all band members for the denominator, then how many of them play a sport.","steps":["Band members: 15 (sport) + 10 (no sport) = 25.","Sport players in the band: 15.","Fraction: 15/25 = 3/5."],"check":"3/5 of 25 = 15 ✓","wrong":{"1/4":"That's 15/60, using the whole class.","2/5":"That's the band members who don't play a sport, 10/25.","3/7":"That's 15/35, using the non-sport students as the group."}},"a80":{"ask":"Which set is more spread out?","idea":"Standard deviation measures how far the values typically are from the mean. Both sets have mean 50, so compare how far their values sit from 50.","steps":["Set A: 48, 50, 52 are within 2 of 50.","Set B: 30, 50, 70 are up to 20 away from 50.","Set B has the larger standard deviation."],"check":"Same center, but B's values are 10 times as far out ✓","wrong":{"Set A, because it has the larger smallest value":"The smallest value doesn't measure spread.","They are the same, because both have a mean of 50":"Equal means don't mean equal spread.","Set A, because its values are closer to 50":"Being closer to the mean means <em>less<\/em> spread."}},"g1":{"ask":"What angle adds to 65° to make 180°?","idea":"Supplementary angles add to 180° (together they make a straight line). Complementary angles add to 90°. To find a supplement, subtract from 180°.","steps":["180 − 65 = 115°."],"check":"65 + 115 = 180 ✓","wrong":{"25°":"That's the complement: it adds to 90°, not 180°.","295°":"Subtracts from 360° instead of 180°.","65°":"That's the same angle; supplementary angles add to 180°."}},"g2":{"ask":"When two lines cross, what is the angle directly across from a 72° angle?","idea":"When two lines cross, the angles directly across from each other (vertical angles) are always equal. Angles side by side add to 180°.","steps":["The vertical angle equals the given angle: 72°."],"check":"The angle next to it is 180 − 72 = 108°, and its opposite is also 108°, so all four add to 360° ✓","wrong":{"18°":"That's 90 − 72, the complement.","108°":"That's the angle <em>next to<\/em> 72°, not across from it.","144°":"Doubles the angle."}},"g3":{"ask":"What angle adds to 34° to make 90°?","idea":"Complementary angles add to 90° (together they make a right angle). Subtract from 90°.","steps":["90 − 34 = 56°."],"check":"34 + 56 = 90 ✓","wrong":{"66°":"Doesn't work: 34 + 66 = 100.","34°":"That's the same angle.","146°":"That's the supplement (adds to 180°)."}},"g4":{"ask":"Corresponding angles on parallel lines are (3<i>x<\/i> + 10)° and (5<i>x<\/i> − 30)°. Find <i>x<\/i>.","idea":"When a line crosses two parallel lines, corresponding angles (same position at each crossing) are equal. Set the expressions equal and solve.","steps":["3<i>x<\/i> + 10 = 5<i>x<\/i> − 30.","Subtract 3<i>x<\/i> and add 30: 40 = 2<i>x<\/i>.","<i>x<\/i> = 20."],"check":"Both angles are 70°: 3(20) + 10 = 70 and 5(20) − 30 = 70 ✓","wrong":{"25":"Doesn't work: the angles would be 85° and 95°.","40":"That's 2<i>x<\/i>; divide by 2.","50":"Doesn't work: the angles would be 160° and 220°."}},"g5":{"ask":"Three angles on a straight line are <i>x<\/i>, 2<i>x<\/i> and 3<i>x<\/i>. Find the largest.","idea":"Angles that together form a straight line add to 180°. Add the expressions, solve for <i>x<\/i>, then find the angle asked for.","steps":["<i>x<\/i> + 2<i>x<\/i> + 3<i>x<\/i> = 180, so 6<i>x<\/i> = 180.","<i>x<\/i> = 30.","Largest: 3<i>x<\/i> = 90°."],"check":"30 + 60 + 90 = 180 ✓","wrong":{"180°":"That's the total of all three angles.","30°":"That's <i>x<\/i>, the smallest angle.","60°":"That's 2<i>x<\/i>, the middle angle."}},"g6":{"ask":"Alternate interior angles are (4<i>x<\/i> − 12)° and (2<i>x<\/i> + 38)°. How big is each?","idea":"Alternate interior angles (on opposite sides of the crossing line, between the parallels) are equal. Solve for <i>x<\/i>, then substitute to get the angle, which is what's asked.","steps":["4<i>x<\/i> − 12 = 2<i>x<\/i> + 38, so 2<i>x<\/i> = 50 and <i>x<\/i> = 25.","Angle: 4(25) − 12 = 88°."],"check":"2(25) + 38 = 88 ✓","wrong":{"50°":"That's 2<i>x<\/i>, not an angle measure.","25°":"That's <i>x<\/i>; substitute it to get the angle.","92°":"That's the supplement, 180 − 88."}},"g7":{"ask":"Same-side interior angles are (2<i>x<\/i> + 20)° and (3<i>x<\/i> − 40)°. Find <i>x<\/i>.","idea":"Same-side interior angles (between the parallel lines, on the same side of the crossing line) add to 180°. They are <em>not<\/em> equal.","steps":["(2<i>x<\/i> + 20) + (3<i>x<\/i> − 40) = 180.","5<i>x<\/i> − 20 = 180, so 5<i>x<\/i> = 200.","<i>x<\/i> = 40."],"check":"Angles: 100° and 80°, which add to 180° ✓","wrong":{"32":"Doesn't work: the angles would be 84° and 56°, adding to 140°.","60":"Sets the angles equal; they should add to 180°.","100":"That's one of the angles (2<i>x<\/i> + 20), not <i>x<\/i>."}},"g8":{"ask":"A bisector splits ∠<i>ABC<\/i> into two equal parts. How big is the whole angle?","idea":"To bisect means to cut into two equal halves. Set the two halves equal, solve, then add both halves for the whole angle.","steps":["3<i>x<\/i> + 4 = 5<i>x<\/i> − 12, so 16 = 2<i>x<\/i> and <i>x<\/i> = 8.","Each half: 3(8) + 4 = 28°.","Whole angle: 2 × 28 = 56°."],"check":"5(8) − 12 = 28 ✓","wrong":{"112°":"Doubles the whole angle.","28°":"That's only one half.","8°":"That's <i>x<\/i>."}},"g9":{"ask":"Two angles add to 90°, and one is 12° more than twice the other. Find the larger one.","idea":"Translate the words: if the smaller angle is <i>x<\/i>, the larger is 2<i>x<\/i> + 12. Complementary means they add to 90°.","steps":["<i>x<\/i> + (2<i>x<\/i> + 12) = 90, so 3<i>x<\/i> + 12 = 90.","3<i>x<\/i> = 78, so <i>x<\/i> = 26.","Larger: 2(26) + 12 = 64°."],"check":"26 + 64 = 90 ✓","wrong":{"78°":"That's 3<i>x<\/i>, not an angle.","56°":"Doesn't work: 90 − 56 = 34, and 2(34) + 12 = 80, not 56.","26°":"That's the smaller angle."}},"g10":{"ask":"Four angles around a point. Find the largest.","idea":"Angles all the way around a point add to 360°, a full turn.","steps":["<i>x<\/i> + (<i>x<\/i> + 20) + 2<i>x<\/i> + (2<i>x<\/i> − 20) = 6<i>x<\/i> = 360.","<i>x<\/i> = 60.","Angles: 60°, 80°, 120° and 100°. The largest is 120°."],"check":"60 + 80 + 120 + 100 = 360 ✓","wrong":{"100°":"That's 2<i>x<\/i> − 20, the second largest.","60°":"That's <i>x<\/i>.","90°":"Uses 180° for the total. Angles all the way around a point add to 360°."}},"g11":{"ask":"Find the third angle of a triangle with angles 50° and 60°.","idea":"The three angles of every triangle add to 180°.","steps":["180 − 50 − 60 = 70°."],"check":"50 + 60 + 70 = 180 ✓","wrong":{"80°":"Doesn't work: 50 + 60 + 80 = 190.","110°":"That's 50 + 60.","90°":"Assumes a right triangle."}},"g12":{"ask":"An isosceles triangle's top angle is 40°. How big is each base angle?","idea":"An isosceles triangle has two equal sides, and the angles opposite them (the base angles) are equal. All three angles add to 180°.","steps":["The base angles share 180 − 40 = 140°.","Each: 140 ÷ 2 = 70°."],"check":"40 + 70 + 70 = 180 ✓","wrong":{"50°":"Doesn't work: 40 + 50 + 50 = 140.","140°":"That's both base angles together.","40°":"Doesn't work: 40 + 40 + 40 = 120."}},"g13":{"ask":"Find an exterior angle from the two interior angles that aren't next to it.","idea":"An exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two “remote” interior angles (the two not touching it). That's because the exterior angle and its neighbor add to 180°, and so do all three interior angles.","steps":["45 + 70 = 115°."],"check":"The third interior angle is 65°, and 65 + 115 = 180 on the straight line ✓","wrong":{"25°":"That's 70 − 45.","245°":"Subtracts from 360°.","65°":"That's the interior angle next to the exterior angle."}},"g14":{"ask":"Sides 5 and 9: which third side makes a real triangle?","idea":"The triangle inequality: any two sides must add to more than the third. So the third side must be longer than the difference of the other two and shorter than their sum.","steps":["Greater than 9 − 5 = 4.","Less than 9 + 5 = 14.","Only 7 is between 4 and 14."],"check":"5 + 7 = 12 &gt; 9 ✓, 5 + 9 &gt; 7 ✓, 7 + 9 &gt; 5 ✓","wrong":{"3":"Too short: 5 + 3 = 8 is less than 9, so the sides can't meet.","14":"Equal to 5 + 9, so the sides would lie flat in a line.","15":"Too long: longer than 5 + 9."}},"g15":{"ask":"The angles are in the ratio 2 : 3 : 4. Find the largest.","idea":"A ratio 2 : 3 : 4 means the angles are 2, 3 and 4 equal “parts.” Total parts: 9, which must equal 180°.","steps":["One part: 180 ÷ 9 = 20°.","Largest: 4 × 20 = 80°."],"check":"40 + 60 + 80 = 180 ✓","wrong":{"40°":"That's the smallest angle, 2 parts.","60°":"That's the middle angle, 3 parts.","90°":"Doesn't fit the ratio: the angles would not add to 180°."}},"g16":{"ask":"Base angles are (2<i>x<\/i> + 10)° each and the top angle is <i>x<\/i>°. Find a base angle.","idea":"An isosceles triangle has two equal base angles. All three angles add to 180°, so count the base angle twice.","steps":["2(2<i>x<\/i> + 10) + <i>x<\/i> = 180, so 5<i>x<\/i> + 20 = 180.","5<i>x<\/i> = 160, so <i>x<\/i> = 32.","Base angle: 2(32) + 10 = 74°."],"check":"74 + 74 + 32 = 180 ✓","wrong":{"84°":"Doesn't work: a base angle of 84° means <i>x<\/i> = 37, and 84 + 84 + 37 = 205, not 180.","32°":"That's the top angle, <i>x<\/i>.","64°":"That's 2<i>x<\/i>; add 10."}},"g17":{"ask":"An exterior angle equals the sum of the two far interior angles. Find <i>x<\/i>.","idea":"Exterior angle = sum of the two remote interior angles. Write that as an equation.","steps":["5<i>x<\/i> − 10 = 2<i>x<\/i> + (<i>x<\/i> + 30) = 3<i>x<\/i> + 30.","2<i>x<\/i> = 40, so <i>x<\/i> = 20."],"check":"Exterior 90°, remote angles 40° and 50°, and 40 + 50 = 90 ✓","wrong":{"10":"Doesn't work: the exterior angle would be 40° but the remote angles add to 60°.","40":"That's 2<i>x<\/i>.","90":"That's the exterior angle's measure, not <i>x<\/i>."}},"g18":{"ask":"Which three lengths can form a triangle?","idea":"Triangle inequality: the two shorter sides must add to <em>more<\/em> than the longest side. Otherwise they can't meet to close the triangle.","steps":["4, 6, 9: 4 + 6 = 10 &gt; 9 ✓."],"check":"The others fail: 1 + 5 = 6 &lt; 7, 2 + 3 = 5 &lt; 6, 3 + 4 = 7 &lt; 8.","wrong":{"1, 5, 7":"1 + 5 = 6, which is less than 7.","2, 3, 6":"2 + 3 = 5, which is less than 6.","3, 4, 8":"3 + 4 = 7, which is less than 8."}},"g19":{"ask":"A midsegment is half the third side. Find <i>x<\/i>.","idea":"A midsegment connects the midpoints of two sides; it's parallel to the third side and half as long. So the third side is twice the midsegment.","steps":["2(2<i>x<\/i> + 3) = 5<i>x<\/i> − 4.","4<i>x<\/i> + 6 = 5<i>x<\/i> − 4.","<i>x<\/i> = 10."],"check":"Midsegment 23, third side 46 = 2 × 23 ✓","wrong":{"7":"Doesn't work: midsegment 17, third side 31, not twice 17.","23":"That's the midsegment's length.","46":"That's the third side's length."}},"g20":{"ask":"Angles (<i>x<\/i> + 15)°, (2<i>x<\/i> − 5)° and (3<i>x<\/i> + 20)°. Find the largest.","idea":"Add the three expressions and set the sum to 180°. Find <i>x<\/i>, then compute each angle to see which is largest.","steps":["6<i>x<\/i> + 30 = 180, so <i>x<\/i> = 25.","Angles: 40°, 45°, 95°.","Largest: 95°."],"check":"40 + 45 + 95 = 180 ✓","wrong":{"25°":"That's <i>x<\/i>.","45°":"That's the middle angle.","40°":"That's the smallest angle."}},"g21":{"ask":"Similar triangles with scale factor 3. The small side is 4; find the matching large side.","idea":"Similar figures have the same shape; every length in the larger figure is the scale factor times the matching length in the smaller one.","steps":["4 × 3 = 12."],"check":"12 ÷ 4 = 3, the scale factor ✓","wrong":{"4/3":"Divides by the scale factor instead of multiplying.","7":"Adds 3 instead of multiplying.","36":"Multiplies by 3 twice (that's the area factor 9 applied to 4)."}},"g22":{"ask":"Which rule proves triangles congruent using three pairs of equal sides?","idea":"Congruent means exactly the same size and shape. SSS (side-side-side): if all three sides match, the triangles are congruent. Angle-only rules (AA, AAA) only show the same shape (similarity), and SSA doesn't guarantee a match.","steps":["Three pairs of equal sides → SSS."],"check":"Three fixed side lengths can only make one triangle ✓","wrong":{"SSA":"Two sides and a non-included angle don't always determine one triangle.","AA":"Angles alone prove similarity, not congruence.","AAA":"Same angles can come in different sizes, so only similarity."}},"g23":{"ask":"A 3-by-5 rectangle is scaled so the short side is 6. How long is the long side?","idea":"Similar shapes are scaled by <em>multiplying<\/em> every side by the same factor, not by adding the same amount.","steps":["Scale factor: 6 ÷ 3 = 2.","Long side: 5 × 2 = 10."],"check":"Ratios match: 3/5 = 6/10 ✓","wrong":{"8":"Adds 3 to the long side; similar figures multiply, not add.","15":"Multiplies by 3 instead of 2.","30":"Multiplies 5 by 6."}},"g24":{"ask":"A 6-8-10 triangle is scaled so its shortest side is 9. Find the longest side.","idea":"Find the scale factor from matching sides (shortest to shortest), then multiply the longest side by it.","steps":["Scale factor: 9 ÷ 6 = 1.5.","Longest: 10 × 1.5 = 15."],"check":"New sides 9, 12, 15 keep the 3 : 4 : 5 ratio ✓","wrong":{"12":"That's the new middle side (8 × 1.5).","13":"Adds 3 to 10 instead of multiplying.","20":"Doubles the side instead of multiplying by 1.5."}},"g25":{"ask":"Use a person's shadow to find a tree's height.","idea":"At the same moment, the sun makes similar triangles, so height ÷ shadow is the same for the person and the tree. Set up a proportion.","steps":["6/4 = <i>h<\/i>/18.","<i>h<\/i> = 18 × 6/4 = 18 × 1.5 = 27 feet."],"check":"27/18 = 1.5 = 6/4 ✓","wrong":{"20 ft":"Adds 2 (the height-shadow difference) instead of scaling.","12 ft":"Flips the ratio: 18 × 4/6.","108 ft":"Multiplies 18 × 6 without dividing by 4."}},"g26":{"ask":"Similar figures with scale factor 2. The small area is 15; find the large area.","idea":"Lengths scale by <i>k<\/i>, but areas scale by <i>k<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>, because area multiplies two lengths.","steps":["Area factor: 2<sup>2<\/sup> = 4.","15 × 4 = 60."],"check":"A 1-by-1 square (area 1) doubled is 2-by-2 (area 4) ✓","wrong":{"30":"Multiplies by 2 instead of 4.","45":"Multiplies by 3.","120":"Multiplies by 8, the volume factor."}},"g27":{"ask":"Matching sides of similar triangles are in the same ratio. Find <i>x<\/i>.","idea":"In similar triangles, matching sides have equal ratios. Write a proportion with the known pair and the pair containing <i>x<\/i>, then cross-multiply.","steps":["8/12 = (<i>x<\/i> + 2)/(2<i>x<\/i> − 1).","Cross-multiply: 8(2<i>x<\/i> − 1) = 12(<i>x<\/i> + 2), so 16<i>x<\/i> − 8 = 12<i>x<\/i> + 24.","4<i>x<\/i> = 32, so <i>x<\/i> = 8."],"check":"<i>BC<\/i> = 10 and <i>EF<\/i> = 15, and 10/15 = 8/12 ✓","wrong":{"4":"Doesn't work: 6/7 ≠ 8/12.","10":"Doesn't work: 12/19 ≠ 8/12.","15":"Doesn't work: 17/29 ≠ 8/12."}},"g28":{"ask":"Similar solids with scale factor 3. The small volume is 10; find the large volume.","idea":"Lengths scale by <i>k<\/i>, areas by <i>k<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>, and volumes by <i>k<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>, because volume multiplies three lengths.","steps":["Volume factor: 3<sup>3<\/sup> = 27.","10 × 27 = 270."],"check":"A 1-unit cube becomes 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 cubes ✓","wrong":{"30":"Uses the length factor 3.","90":"Uses the area factor 9.","1,000":"Cubes the volume (10<sup>3<\/sup>) instead of the scale factor."}},"g29":{"ask":"<i>DE<\/i> is parallel to <i>BC<\/i>. Find <i>EC<\/i>.","idea":"A line parallel to one side of a triangle divides the other two sides in the same ratio. So <i>AD<\/i> : <i>DB<\/i> = <i>AE<\/i> : <i>EC<\/i>.","steps":["4/6 = 6/<i>EC<\/i>.","Cross-multiply: 4 × <i>EC<\/i> = 36, so <i>EC<\/i> = 9."],"check":"Both ratios equal 2/3 ✓","wrong":{"4":"Copies <i>AD<\/i>.","8":"Adds 2 (the difference 6 − 4) instead of keeping the ratio.","12":"Doubles <i>AE<\/i>."}},"g30":{"ask":"Which fact is enough to prove two triangles similar?","idea":"AA similarity: if two angles of one triangle equal two angles of another, the third angles must match too (all add to 180°), so the triangles have the same shape.","steps":["Two pairs of equal angles → AA → similar."],"check":"Same angles force the same shape, just possibly a different size ✓","wrong":{"Both triangles are right triangles":"That gives only one pair of equal angles (the right angles).","The triangles have equal perimeters":"Triangles with the same perimeter can have very different shapes.","One pair of corresponding sides is equal":"One side says nothing about the shape."}},"g31":{"ask":"A right triangle has legs 5 and 12. How long is the hypotenuse?","idea":"Pythagorean theorem: leg<sup>2<\/sup> + leg<sup>2<\/sup> = hypotenuse<sup>2<\/sup>. The hypotenuse is the longest side, across from the right angle.","steps":["5<sup>2<\/sup> + 12<sup>2<\/sup> = 25 + 144 = 169.","√169 = 13."],"check":"5-12-13 is a well-known right triangle ✓","wrong":{"7":"Subtracts the legs.","17":"Adds the legs; the hypotenuse is shorter than the two legs combined.","60":"Multiplies the legs."}},"g32":{"ask":"Find sin <i>A<\/i> from the three sides.","idea":"SOH-CAH-TOA: <strong>S<\/strong>ine = <strong>O<\/strong>pposite ÷ <strong>H<\/strong>ypotenuse, <strong>C<\/strong>osine = <strong>A<\/strong>djacent ÷ <strong>H<\/strong>ypotenuse, <strong>T<\/strong>angent = <strong>O<\/strong>pposite ÷ <strong>A<\/strong>djacent.","steps":["sin <i>A<\/i> = opposite ÷ hypotenuse = 3/5."],"check":"A sine is always between 0 and 1 for these angles, and 3/5 = 0.6 ✓","wrong":{"3/4":"That's tan <i>A<\/i> (opposite ÷ adjacent).","4/5":"That's cos <i>A<\/i> (adjacent ÷ hypotenuse).","5/3":"Upside down, and greater than 1."}},"g33":{"ask":"Hypotenuse 10, one leg 6. Find the other leg.","idea":"Pythagorean theorem: leg<sup>2<\/sup> + leg<sup>2<\/sup> = hypotenuse<sup>2<\/sup>. When the hypotenuse is known, <em>subtract<\/em>: missing leg<sup>2<\/sup> = hypotenuse<sup>2<\/sup> − known leg<sup>2<\/sup>.","steps":["<i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 10<sup>2<\/sup> − 6<sup>2<\/sup> = 100 − 36 = 64.","<i>b<\/i> = 8."],"check":"6-8-10 is the 3-4-5 triangle doubled ✓","wrong":{"4":"Subtracts the lengths instead of their squares.","11.7":"Adds the squares (as if 10 were a leg): √136.","16":"Adds the lengths."}},"g34":{"ask":"In a 30°-60°-90° triangle, the short leg is 5. Find the hypotenuse.","idea":"A 30-60-90 triangle is half of an equilateral triangle, so its sides always follow the pattern <i>x<\/i> (short leg), <i>x<\/i>√3 (long leg), 2<i>x<\/i> (hypotenuse).","steps":["Hypotenuse = 2 × short leg = 2 × 5 = 10."],"check":"5<sup>2<\/sup> + (5√3)<sup>2<\/sup> = 25 + 75 = 100 = 10<sup>2<\/sup> ✓","wrong":{"5√2":"That's the 45-45-90 pattern.","15":"Triples the short leg.","5√3":"That's the longer leg."}},"g35":{"ask":"In a 45°-45°-90° triangle, each leg is 7. Find the hypotenuse.","idea":"A 45-45-90 triangle is half of a square, so its sides follow the pattern <i>x<\/i>, <i>x<\/i>, <i>x<\/i>√2.","steps":["Hypotenuse = 7√2."],"check":"7<sup>2<\/sup> + 7<sup>2<\/sup> = 98 = (7√2)<sup>2<\/sup> ✓","wrong":{"7√3":"That's from the 30-60-90 pattern.","49":"Squares the leg.","14":"Doubles the leg; that's the 30-60-90 hypotenuse rule."}},"g36":{"ask":"How high up the wall does a 13-foot ladder reach, with its foot 5 feet out?","idea":"The wall, the ground and the ladder form a right triangle. The ladder is the hypotenuse (it's across from the right angle at the wall's base).","steps":["<i>h<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 13<sup>2<\/sup> − 5<sup>2<\/sup> = 169 − 25 = 144.","<i>h<\/i> = 12 feet."],"check":"5-12-13 is a right triangle ✓","wrong":{"13.9 ft":"Adds the squares, treating the ladder as a leg.","8 ft":"Subtracts the lengths instead of their squares.","18 ft":"Adds the lengths."}},"g37":{"ask":"From 40 m away, the top of a tree is at a 35° angle up. How tall is the tree?","idea":"You know the side <em>adjacent<\/em> to the angle (40 m along the ground) and want the side <em>opposite<\/em> (the height). tan = opposite ÷ adjacent connects them.","steps":["tan 35° = height ÷ 40.","height = 40 × 0.7002 ≈ 28.0 m."],"check":"A 35° angle is less than 45°, so the height should be less than the 40 m distance ✓","wrong":{"57.1 m":"Divides 40 by tan 35° instead of multiplying.","22.9 m":"Uses sine (≈ 0.574) instead of tangent.","32.8 m":"Uses cos 35° (≈ 0.819) instead of tan 35°."}},"g38":{"ask":"sin <i>x<\/i>° equals cos 30°. Find <i>x<\/i>.","idea":"In a right triangle, the two acute angles add to 90°, and the sine of one equals the cosine of the other. So sin <i>x<\/i>° = cos(90° − <i>x<\/i>°).","steps":["sin <i>x<\/i>° = cos 30° means <i>x<\/i> + 30 = 90.","<i>x<\/i> = 60."],"check":"sin 60° = cos 30° = √3/2 ≈ 0.866 ✓","wrong":{"30":"sin 30° = 0.5, but cos 30° ≈ 0.866.","45":"sin 45° ≈ 0.707, not 0.866.","90":"sin 90° = 1, and <i>x<\/i> must be less than 90."}},"g39":{"ask":"A 30-60-90 triangle has hypotenuse 12. Find the long leg.","idea":"30-60-90 pattern: short leg <i>x<\/i>, long leg <i>x<\/i>√3, hypotenuse 2<i>x<\/i>. Find <i>x<\/i> from the hypotenuse first.","steps":["2<i>x<\/i> = 12, so the short leg <i>x<\/i> = 6.","Long leg: 6√3."],"check":"6<sup>2<\/sup> + (6√3)<sup>2<\/sup> = 36 + 108 = 144 = 12<sup>2<\/sup> ✓","wrong":{"6√2":"Uses the 45-45-90 pattern.","6":"That's the short leg.","12√3":"Multiplies the hypotenuse by √3 instead of the short leg."}},"g40":{"ask":"A 20-foot ramp rises at 10°. How high does it rise?","idea":"The ramp is the hypotenuse, and the rise is <em>opposite<\/em> the angle. sin = opposite ÷ hypotenuse connects them.","steps":["sin 10° = rise ÷ 20.","rise = 20 × 0.1736 ≈ 3.5 feet."],"check":"A gentle 10° slope should rise only a small part of its length ✓","wrong":{"19.7 ft":"Uses cosine, which gives the horizontal distance.","0.2 ft":"That's just sin 10°; multiply by 20.","115.2 ft":"Divides 20 by sin 10°."}},"g41":{"ask":"How far is (6, 8) from the origin?","idea":"The straight-line distance is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs are the horizontal and vertical changes. Use the Pythagorean theorem.","steps":["Horizontal change 6, vertical change 8.","Distance: √(6<sup>2<\/sup> + 8<sup>2<\/sup>) = √100 = 10."],"check":"6-8-10 is the 3-4-5 triangle doubled ✓","wrong":{"14":"Adds the changes; that's walking along the grid, not straight across.","48":"Multiplies the changes.","100":"That's the distance squared; take the square root."}},"g42":{"ask":"Find the point halfway between (2, 4) and (8, 10).","idea":"The midpoint averages the coordinates: add the two <i>x<\/i>'s and divide by 2, then do the same for the <i>y<\/i>'s.","steps":["<i>x<\/i>: (2 + 8) ÷ 2 = 5.","<i>y<\/i>: (4 + 10) ÷ 2 = 7.","Midpoint: (5, 7)."],"check":"From (2, 4) to (5, 7) is +3, +3, and from (5, 7) to (8, 10) is also +3, +3 ✓","wrong":{"(10, 14)":"Adds the coordinates but forgets to divide by 2.","(3, 3)":"That's half the change, not the midpoint.","(6, 6)":"That's the full change from one endpoint to the other."}},"g43":{"ask":"How steep is the line through (0, 1) and (2, 7)?","idea":"Slope = rise ÷ run = (change in <i>y<\/i>) ÷ (change in <i>x<\/i>).","steps":["Rise: 7 − 1 = 6. Run: 2 − 0 = 2.","Slope: 6 ÷ 2 = 3."],"check":"From (0, 1), go right 2 and up 6 to reach (2, 7) ✓","wrong":{"1/3":"Run over rise, upside down.","4":"Doesn't match: 6 ÷ 2 = 3.","6":"That's only the rise."}},"g44":{"ask":"Find the distance around the rectangle.","idea":"Read the side lengths from the coordinates: the width is the change in <i>x<\/i> and the height is the change in <i>y<\/i>. Perimeter adds all four sides.","steps":["Width: 4 − 0 = 4. Height: 3 − 0 = 3.","Perimeter: 2(4 + 3) = 14."],"check":"4 + 3 + 4 + 3 = 14 ✓","wrong":{"7":"Adds only two sides.","12":"That's the area, 4 × 3.","24":"Doubles the area."}},"g45":{"ask":"The midpoint is (3, 5) and one end is (1, 2). Find the other end.","idea":"The midpoint is halfway, so the second half of the segment repeats the first half. Find the change from <i>P<\/i> to the midpoint, then add it again.","steps":["From (1, 2) to (3, 5): <i>x<\/i> goes up 2 and <i>y<\/i> up 3.","Add again: (3 + 2, 5 + 3) = (5, 8)."],"check":"Midpoint of (1, 2) and (5, 8): (3, 5) ✓","wrong":{"(2, 3.5)":"That's the midpoint of <i>P<\/i> and (3, 5), not the other end.","(4, 7)":"Adds 1 and 2 instead of the actual changes.","(7, 12)":"Doubles the midpoint without subtracting <i>P<\/i>."}},"g46":{"ask":"How do the lines <i>y<\/i> = 3<i>x<\/i> + 1 and <i>y<\/i> = −(1/3)<i>x<\/i> + 4 relate?","idea":"Compare slopes. Equal slopes: parallel. Slopes that multiply to −1 (negative reciprocals): perpendicular, meeting at a right angle.","steps":["Slopes: 3 and −1/3.","3 × (−1/3) = −1.","So the lines are perpendicular."],"check":"One is the other flipped with the sign changed ✓","wrong":{"The same line":"The slopes and intercepts are both different.","Neither parallel nor perpendicular":"The slopes multiply to −1, so they are perpendicular.","Parallel":"Parallel lines need the same slope."}},"g47":{"ask":"Find the area of the triangle with corners (1, 1), (5, 1) and (1, 4).","idea":"Two sides run along grid lines and meet at (1, 1) at a right angle. For a right triangle, the legs are the base and height: area = ½ × base × height.","steps":["Base: from (1, 1) to (5, 1) is 4.","Height: from (1, 1) to (1, 4) is 3.","Area: ½ × 4 × 3 = 6."],"check":"It's half of a 4-by-3 rectangle (area 12) ✓","wrong":{"7":"Adds the legs.","12":"Forgets the ½.","20":"Doesn't match: ½ × 4 × 3 = 6."}},"g48":{"ask":"Find the point one-third of the way from (0, 0) to (9, 12).","idea":"Going a fraction of the way means taking that fraction of each change: one-third of the change in <i>x<\/i> and one-third of the change in <i>y<\/i>.","steps":["Change: 9 in <i>x<\/i> and 12 in <i>y<\/i>.","One-third: 3 and 4.","From (0, 0): (3, 4)."],"check":"(3, 4) to (9, 12) is (6, 8), twice as far, so (3, 4) is one-third of the way ✓","wrong":{"(4.5, 6)":"That's the midpoint, halfway.","(1, 2)":"Doesn't match: one-third of 9 is 3 and of 12 is 4.","(6, 8)":"That's two-thirds of the way."}},"g49":{"ask":"Which corner of the triangle is a right angle?","idea":"Two sides meet at a right angle when their slopes multiply to −1. Find the slopes of the sides and check which pair meets at 90°.","steps":["Slope <i>AB<\/i>: 4/3. Slope <i>BC<\/i>: (1 − 4)/(7 − 3) = −3/4.","(4/3)(−3/4) = −1, so <i>AB<\/i> ⊥ <i>BC<\/i>.","They meet at <i>B<\/i>."],"check":"<i>AB<\/i> = 5, <i>BC<\/i> = 5, <i>AC<\/i> = √50, and 25 + 25 = 50 ✓","wrong":{"Angle C":"Slopes <i>BC<\/i> (−3/4) and <i>AC<\/i> (1/7) don't multiply to −1.","Angle A":"Slopes <i>AB<\/i> (4/3) and <i>AC<\/i> (1/7) don't multiply to −1.","None of the angles":"<i>AB<\/i> and <i>BC<\/i> are perpendicular."}},"g50":{"ask":"A line through (2, −1) is perpendicular to 2<i>x<\/i> + <i>y<\/i> = 5. Where does it cross the <i>y<\/i>-axis?","idea":"Get the given line's slope by solving for <i>y<\/i>. The perpendicular slope is its negative reciprocal. Then use the point to find <i>b<\/i>.","steps":["<i>y<\/i> = −2<i>x<\/i> + 5, so the slope is −2.","Perpendicular slope: 1/2.","−1 = ½(2) + <i>b<\/i> = 1 + <i>b<\/i>, so <i>b<\/i> = −2."],"check":"<i>y<\/i> = ½<i>x<\/i> − 2 at <i>x<\/i> = 2: 1 − 2 = −1 ✓","wrong":{"−1/2":"Doesn't work: at <i>x<\/i> = 2 the line would be at ½, not −1.","0":"Doesn't work: at <i>x<\/i> = 2 the line would be at 1, not −1.","2":"Sign error. Check: 1 + 2 = 3, not −1."}},"g51":{"ask":"Find the area of a trapezoid with bases 6 and 10 and height 4.","idea":"A trapezoid's area is the average of the two parallel sides (bases) times the height: ½(<i>b<\/i><sub>1<\/sub> + <i>b<\/i><sub>2<\/sub>) × <i>h<\/i>.","steps":["Average base: (6 + 10) ÷ 2 = 8.","Area: 8 × 4 = 32."],"check":"It's between a 6-by-4 rectangle (24) and a 10-by-4 rectangle (40) ✓","wrong":{"20":"Adds 6 + 10 + 4.","40":"Uses only the longer base: 10 × 4.","64":"Forgets the ½: 16 × 4."}},"g52":{"ask":"What do the inside angles of a six-sided figure add up to?","idea":"Any polygon with <i>n<\/i> sides can be split into <i>n<\/i> − 2 triangles from one corner, and each triangle has 180°. So the sum is (<i>n<\/i> − 2) × 180°.","steps":["<i>n<\/i> = 6: (6 − 2) × 180 = 4 × 180 = 720°."],"check":"A regular hexagon has six 120° angles: 6 × 120 = 720 ✓","wrong":{"1,080°":"That's an octagon (8 sides).","540°":"That's a pentagon (5 sides).","360°":"That's a quadrilateral, or the exterior angles of any polygon."}},"g53":{"ask":"How big is each inside angle of a regular 8-sided figure?","idea":"First find the total with (<i>n<\/i> − 2) × 180°. A regular polygon's angles are all equal, so divide by <i>n<\/i>.","steps":["Total: (8 − 2) × 180 = 1,080°.","Each: 1,080 ÷ 8 = 135°."],"check":"Each exterior angle is 180 − 135 = 45°, and 8 × 45 = 360 ✓","wrong":{"45°":"That's each exterior angle.","120°":"That's a regular hexagon's angle.","1,080°":"That's the total of all eight angles."}},"g54":{"ask":"Find the area of a parallelogram with base 12, height 7 and slanted sides 9.","idea":"A parallelogram can be rearranged into a rectangle with the same base and height, so its area is base × height. The height is the <em>perpendicular<\/em> distance, not the slanted side.","steps":["Area: 12 × 7 = 84."],"check":"The slanted side (9) is longer than the height (7), as it must be ✓","wrong":{"42":"Uses ½, which is the triangle formula.","63":"Multiplies height by the slanted side.","108":"Multiplies the base by the slanted side."}},"g55":{"ask":"Find the area of a rhombus with diagonals 10 and 16.","idea":"A rhombus's diagonals cross at right angles and split it into 4 right triangles. Together they make half of a rectangle measuring <i>d<\/i><sub>1<\/sub> by <i>d<\/i><sub>2<\/sub>, so area = ½ × <i>d<\/i><sub>1<\/sub> × <i>d<\/i><sub>2<\/sub>.","steps":["½ × 10 × 16 = 80."],"check":"Four triangles, each ½ × 5 × 8 = 20: 4 × 20 = 80 ✓","wrong":{"26":"Adds the diagonals.","40":"Divides by 4 instead of 2.","160":"Forgets the ½."}},"g56":{"ask":"Each inside angle is 150°. How many sides does the regular polygon have?","idea":"Each exterior angle is 180° minus the interior angle, and the exterior angles of any polygon add to 360°. So sides = 360 ÷ exterior angle.","steps":["Exterior angle: 180 − 150 = 30°.","Sides: 360 ÷ 30 = 12."],"check":"Interior total: (12 − 2) × 180 = 1,800, and 1,800 ÷ 12 = 150 ✓","wrong":{"10":"A regular 10-gon has 144° angles.","15":"A regular 15-gon has 156° angles.","150":"That's the angle measure, not a number of sides."}},"g57":{"ask":"The length is twice the width and the perimeter is 30. Find the area.","idea":"Let the width be <i>w<\/i>; then the length is 2<i>w<\/i>. Use the perimeter to find <i>w<\/i>, then multiply length × width.","steps":["Perimeter: 2(<i>w<\/i> + 2<i>w<\/i>) = 6<i>w<\/i> = 30, so <i>w<\/i> = 5.","Length: 10.","Area: 10 × 5 = 50."],"check":"Perimeter: 2(10 + 5) = 30 ✓","wrong":{"25":"Uses a 5-by-5 square.","30":"That's the perimeter.","200":"Far too big: a 10-by-5 rectangle has area 50."}},"g58":{"ask":"Find the area of a rectangle with a half-circle attached.","idea":"Split the figure into simple shapes and add their areas. The semicircle sits on the 6-unit side, so its diameter is 6 and its radius is 3. A semicircle is half a circle.","steps":["Rectangle: 10 × 6 = 60.","Semicircle: ½ × π × 3<sup>2<\/sup> = 4.5π.","Total: 60 + 4.5π."],"check":"4.5π ≈ 14, so the total is about 74 ✓","wrong":{"60 + 3π":"Uses the circumference-style value π<i>r<\/i> instead of area.","60 + 18π":"Uses the diameter 6 as the radius (½π × 36).","60 + 9π":"Uses a full circle instead of half."}},"g59":{"ask":"Find the area of an equilateral triangle with side 10.","idea":"The height of an equilateral triangle splits it into two 30-60-90 triangles, so height = (side/2)√3. Area = ½ × base × height, which simplifies to (√3/4) × side<sup>2<\/sup>.","steps":["Height: 5√3.","Area: ½ × 10 × 5√3 = 25√3."],"check":"Formula: (√3/4) × 100 = 25√3 ✓","wrong":{"100":"Squares the side, which is a square's area.","50":"Uses ½ × 10 × 10, treating a side as the height.","50√3":"Forgets the ½."}},"g60":{"ask":"A square's diagonal is 10. Find its area.","idea":"A square's diagonal is side × √2 (a 45-45-90 triangle). So side = 10/√2, and area = side<sup>2<\/sup> = diagonal<sup>2<\/sup> ÷ 2.","steps":["Area = 10<sup>2<\/sup> ÷ 2 = 100 ÷ 2 = 50."],"check":"Side = 10/√2 ≈ 7.07, and 7.07<sup>2<\/sup> ≈ 50 ✓","wrong":{"25":"Divides by 4 instead of 2.","100":"Uses the diagonal as the side.","10√2":"Multiplies the diagonal by √2 instead of dividing."}},"g61":{"ask":"Find the area of a circle with radius 6.","idea":"Area = π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>: square the radius, then multiply by π.","steps":["6<sup>2<\/sup> = 36.","Area: 36π."],"check":"Units: square units ✓","wrong":{"12π":"That's the circumference, 2π<i>r<\/i>.","6π":"Uses π<i>r<\/i> without squaring.","72π":"Doubles the area."}},"g62":{"ask":"How far is it around a circle with diameter 10?","idea":"Circumference = π × diameter (or 2π × radius).","steps":["π × 10 = 10π."],"check":"Radius 5: 2π(5) = 10π ✓","wrong":{"20π":"Uses 2π × diameter, doubling twice.","25π":"That's the area, π(5)<sup>2<\/sup>.","5π":"Uses the radius in π × diameter."}},"g63":{"ask":"What fraction of a full circle is a 90° angle?","idea":"A full circle is 360°. The fraction is the angle ÷ 360.","steps":["90 ÷ 360 = 1/4."],"check":"Four 90° angles make a full circle ✓","wrong":{"1/90":"Uses 1 ÷ 90.","1/3":"That's 120°.","1/2":"That's 180°."}},"g64":{"ask":"An inscribed angle cuts off a 100° arc. How big is the angle?","idea":"An inscribed angle (its vertex on the circle) is half of the arc it cuts off.","steps":["100 ÷ 2 = 50°."],"check":"A central angle on the same arc would be 100°, twice as big ✓","wrong":{"80°":"That's 180 − 100.","100°":"That would be a central angle, with its vertex at the center.","200°":"Doubles instead of halving."}},"g65":{"ask":"Find the length of a 120° arc on a circle with radius 9.","idea":"An arc is a fraction of the circumference: (angle ÷ 360) × 2π<i>r<\/i>.","steps":["Circumference: 2π(9) = 18π.","Fraction: 120/360 = 1/3.","Arc: 18π ÷ 3 = 6π."],"check":"Three 120° arcs make the full 18π ✓","wrong":{"27π":"That's the sector's area, 1/3 of 81π.","18π":"That's the whole circumference.","3π":"Uses the radius as the diameter."}},"g66":{"ask":"Write the circle with center (2, −3) and radius 4.","idea":"Circle equation: (<i>x<\/i> − <i>h<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> + (<i>y<\/i> − <i>k<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>. Subtracting a negative gives a plus, and the right side is the radius <em>squared<\/em>.","steps":["<i>h<\/i> = 2: (<i>x<\/i> − 2)<sup>2<\/sup>.","<i>k<\/i> = −3: (<i>y<\/i> − (−3))<sup>2<\/sup> = (<i>y<\/i> + 3)<sup>2<\/sup>.","<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 16."],"check":"The point (6, −3) is 4 units right of the center: 16 + 0 = 16 ✓","wrong":{"(x − 2)² + (y + 3)² = 4":"Uses the radius instead of its square.","(x + 2)² + (y + 3)² = 16":"Center would be (−2, −3); the <i>x<\/i> sign is flipped.","(x + 2)² + (y − 3)² = 16":"Center would be (−2, 3); both signs are flipped."}},"g67":{"ask":"Find the area of a 72° slice of a circle with radius 10.","idea":"A sector is a fraction of the circle's area: (angle ÷ 360) × π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","steps":["Full area: π(10<sup>2<\/sup>) = 100π.","Fraction: 72/360 = 1/5.","Sector: 100π ÷ 5 = 20π."],"check":"Five 72° slices make the whole circle: 5 × 20π = 100π ✓","wrong":{"10π":"Uses 1/10 of the circle.","40π":"Uses 2/5 of the circle.","4π":"That's the arc length, 1/5 of 20π."}},"g68":{"ask":"Find the length of a tangent from a point 13 units from the center of a radius-5 circle.","idea":"A tangent line touches the circle at one point and is perpendicular to the radius there. So the radius, the tangent and the line to the center make a right triangle, with the 13-unit line as the hypotenuse.","steps":["<i>t<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 5<sup>2<\/sup> = 13<sup>2<\/sup>.","<i>t<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 169 − 25 = 144.","<i>t<\/i> = 12."],"check":"5-12-13 is a right triangle ✓","wrong":{"8":"Subtracts the lengths instead of their squares.","13.9":"Adds the squares, treating 13 as a leg.","18":"Adds the lengths."}},"g69":{"ask":"Find the center of the circle from its expanded equation.","idea":"Complete the square for <i>x<\/i> and for <i>y<\/i> to rewrite it as (<i>x<\/i> − <i>h<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> + (<i>y<\/i> − <i>k<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>. The center's coordinates are the opposites of the numbers inside the parentheses.","steps":["(<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 6<i>x<\/i> + 9) + (<i>y<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4<i>y<\/i> + 4) = 12 + 9 + 4.","(<i>x<\/i> + 3)<sup>2<\/sup> + (<i>y<\/i> − 2)<sup>2<\/sup> = 25.","Center (−3, 2), radius 5."],"check":"(−3, 2) with radius 5 contains (2, 2): 25 + 0 = 25, and 4 + 4 + 12 − 8 − 12 = 0 ✓","wrong":{"(3, −2)":"Copies the signs without flipping them.","(6, −4)":"Uses the coefficients 6 and −4 directly; halve them and flip the signs.","(−6, 4)":"Flips the signs but forgets to halve."}},"g70":{"ask":"In a four-sided figure inside a circle, opposite angles add to 180°. Find ∠<i>A<\/i>.","idea":"When a quadrilateral's corners all lie on a circle, its opposite angles are supplementary (add to 180°). ∠<i>A<\/i> and ∠<i>C<\/i> are opposite.","steps":["(3<i>x<\/i> + 10) + (2<i>x<\/i> + 20) = 180, so 5<i>x<\/i> + 30 = 180.","<i>x<\/i> = 30.","∠<i>A<\/i> = 3(30) + 10 = 100°."],"check":"∠<i>C<\/i> = 80°, and 100 + 80 = 180 ✓","wrong":{"30°":"That's <i>x<\/i>.","80°":"That's ∠<i>C<\/i>.","90°":"Doesn't work: ∠<i>A<\/i> = 90° needs <i>x<\/i> = 80/3, and then ∠<i>A<\/i> + ∠<i>C<\/i> is about 163°, not 180°."}},"g71":{"ask":"How much space is inside a 3 × 4 × 5 box?","idea":"Volume of a rectangular prism = length × width × height.","steps":["3 × 4 × 5 = 60."],"check":"Units: cubic units ✓","wrong":{"12":"That's only the base area, 3 × 4.","47":"Doesn't match: 3 × 4 × 5 = 60.","94":"That's the surface area, 2(12 + 15 + 20)."}},"g72":{"ask":"Find the volume of a cube with side 4.","idea":"A cube's volume is side × side × side = side<sup>3<\/sup>.","steps":["4<sup>3<\/sup> = 4 × 4 × 4 = 64."],"check":"16 unit cubes per layer, 4 layers: 64 ✓","wrong":{"12":"Multiplies 4 × 3 instead of cubing.","16":"That's the area of one face.","96":"That's the surface area, 6 × 16."}},"g73":{"ask":"Find the volume of a cylinder with radius 3 and height 7.","idea":"Cylinder volume = base area × height = π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup><i>h<\/i>. Square the radius first.","steps":["π(3<sup>2<\/sup>) = 9π.","9π × 7 = 63π."],"check":"Units: cubic units ✓","wrong":{"21π":"Uses π<i>r<\/i><i>h<\/i>, forgetting to square the radius.","42π":"That's 2π<i>r<\/i><i>h<\/i>, the curved side's area.","147π":"Squares the height instead of the radius."}},"g74":{"ask":"Find the volume of a cone with radius 6 and height 10.","idea":"A cone holds one-third of the matching cylinder: <i>V<\/i> = ⅓π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup><i>h<\/i>.","steps":["π(6<sup>2<\/sup>)(10) = 360π for the cylinder.","Cone: 360π ÷ 3 = 120π."],"check":"⅓ × 36π × 10 = 120π ✓","wrong":{"360π":"Forgets the ⅓; that's the cylinder.","60π":"Uses π<i>r<\/i><i>h<\/i> without squaring the radius.","20π":"Uses ⅓π<i>r<\/i><i>h<\/i> without squaring the radius."}},"g75":{"ask":"Find the total outside area of a cube with side 5.","idea":"Surface area adds up the areas of all the faces. A cube has 6 identical square faces.","steps":["One face: 5 × 5 = 25.","Six faces: 6 × 25 = 150."],"check":"Units: square units, unlike volume ✓","wrong":{"25":"That's only one face.","100":"Counts only 4 faces.","125":"That's the volume."}},"g76":{"ask":"Find the volume of a sphere with radius 3.","idea":"Use <i>V<\/i> = (4/3)π<i>r<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>: cube the radius first, then multiply by 4/3.","steps":["3<sup>3<\/sup> = 27.","(4/3) × 27 = 36.","<i>V<\/i> = 36π."],"check":"Units: cubic units ✓","wrong":{"27π":"Forgets the 4/3.","108π":"Multiplies by 4 but forgets to divide by 3.","12π":"Uses <i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> instead of <i>r<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>."}},"g77":{"ask":"A cylinder has volume 200π and radius 5. How tall is it?","idea":"Put the known values into <i>V<\/i> = π<i>r<\/i><sup>2<\/sup><i>h<\/i> and solve for <i>h<\/i>.","steps":["π(25)<i>h<\/i> = 200π.","25<i>h<\/i> = 200, so <i>h<\/i> = 8."],"check":"π × 25 × 8 = 200π ✓","wrong":{"4":"Divides by 50 (2 × 25) instead of 25.","10":"Divides 200 by 20.","40":"Divides by 5 instead of 5<sup>2<\/sup>."}},"g78":{"ask":"Find the total outside area of a cylinder with radius 4 and height 6.","idea":"A cylinder's surface is two circles (top and bottom) plus the curved side. Unrolled, the side is a rectangle: height × circumference.","steps":["Two circles: 2 × π(4<sup>2<\/sup>) = 32π.","Curved side: 2π(4)(6) = 48π.","Total: 80π."],"check":"Units: square units ✓","wrong":{"48π":"Only the curved side; add the top and bottom.","64π":"Uses only one circle (16π + 48π).","96π":"Doesn't match: 32π + 48π = 80π."}},"g79":{"ask":"Find the volume of a pyramid with a 6-by-6 base and height 10.","idea":"A pyramid holds one-third of the prism with the same base and height: <i>V<\/i> = ⅓ × base area × height.","steps":["Base area: 6 × 6 = 36.","⅓ × 36 × 10 = 120."],"check":"The matching box is 360, and 360 ÷ 3 = 120 ✓","wrong":{"60":"Multiplies the side by the height. The base area is 6 × 6 = 36.","180":"Uses ½ instead of ⅓.","360":"Forgets the ⅓."}},"g80":{"ask":"How many liters fit in a 2 m × 3 m × 1.5 m tank?","idea":"Find the volume in cubic meters, then convert: each cubic meter holds 1,000 liters.","steps":["Volume: 2 × 3 × 1.5 = 9 m<sup>3<\/sup>.","Liters: 9 × 1,000 = 9,000."],"check":"Each 1 m × 1 m × 1 m cube holds 1,000 L, and 9 of them fit ✓","wrong":{"6.5":"Adds the dimensions.","900":"Multiplies by 100 instead of 1,000.","90,000":"Multiplies by 10,000 instead of 1,000."}},"b1":{"ask":"Move the graph of <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> up 3 units. What's the new rule?","idea":"Changes <em>outside<\/em> the function move the graph up or down: adding 3 to the output lifts every point 3 units. Changes <em>inside<\/em>, next to <i>x<\/i>, move it left or right.","steps":["Up 3 means add 3 to every output.","<i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 3."],"check":"The vertex moves from (0, 0) to (0, 3) ✓","wrong":{"f(x) = (x + 3)²":"Adding inside the parentheses moves the graph 3 <em>left<\/em>.","f(x) = (x − 3)²":"Moves the graph 3 <em>right<\/em>.","f(x) = 3x²":"Stretches the graph vertically; its vertex stays at (0, 0)."}},"b2":{"ask":"Put 3 into <i>g<\/i>, then put that result into <i>f<\/i>.","idea":"<i>f<\/i>(<i>g<\/i>(3)) is a composition: work from the inside out. The output of <i>g<\/i> becomes the input of <i>f<\/i>.","steps":["<i>g<\/i>(3) = 3<sup>2<\/sup> = 9.","<i>f<\/i>(9) = 2(9) + 1 = 19."],"check":"The order matters: <i>g<\/i>(<i>f<\/i>(3)) = <i>g<\/i>(7) = 49, a different answer ✓","wrong":{"7":"That's <i>f<\/i>(3), skipping <i>g<\/i>.","13":"Doesn't match: <i>f<\/i>(9) = 19.","49":"That's <i>g<\/i>(<i>f<\/i>(3)), the reverse order."}},"b3":{"ask":"Which inputs are allowed in √(<i>x<\/i> − 4)?","idea":"The domain is every input that gives a real output. A square root of a negative number isn't a real number, so the expression under the root must be 0 or more.","steps":["<i>x<\/i> − 4 ≥ 0.","<i>x<\/i> ≥ 4."],"check":"<i>x<\/i> = 4 gives √0 = 0, which is fine; <i>x<\/i> = 3 gives √(−1), not real ✓","wrong":{"x ≥ −4":"Sign error: <i>x<\/i> − 4 ≥ 0 gives <i>x<\/i> ≥ 4.","x > 4":"Leaves out 4, but √0 = 0 is allowed.","All real numbers":"Inputs below 4 put a negative under the root."}},"b4":{"ask":"Find the function that undoes <i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) = 3<i>x<\/i> − 6.","idea":"An inverse reverses the function: it undoes the steps in reverse order. <i>f<\/i> multiplies by 3, then subtracts 6. So the inverse adds 6, then divides by 3. Formally: swap <i>x<\/i> and <i>y<\/i>, then solve for <i>y<\/i>.","steps":["<i>y<\/i> = 3<i>x<\/i> − 6. Swap: <i>x<\/i> = 3<i>y<\/i> − 6.","Add 6: <i>x<\/i> + 6 = 3<i>y<\/i>.","Divide by 3: <i>y<\/i> = (<i>x<\/i> + 6)/3."],"check":"<i>f<\/i>(2) = 0, and the inverse at 0 gives (0 + 6)/3 = 2 ✓","wrong":{"f⁻¹(x) = x/3 − 6":"Undoes the steps in the wrong order: add 6 <em>before<\/em> dividing.","f⁻¹(x) = 1/(3x − 6)":"That's the reciprocal, 1 over <i>f<\/i>, not the inverse.","f⁻¹(x) = 3x + 6":"Changes the sign but doesn't undo the multiplication."}},"b5":{"ask":"How does <i>y<\/i> = (<i>x<\/i> − 2)<sup>2<\/sup> − 5 move the basic parabola?","idea":"Inside changes (with <i>x<\/i>) move the graph horizontally, in the opposite direction to the sign: (<i>x<\/i> − 2) moves right 2. Outside changes move it vertically, in the same direction: − 5 moves down 5.","steps":["(<i>x<\/i> − 2): right 2.","− 5: down 5.","The vertex moves from (0, 0) to (2, −5)."],"check":"At <i>x<\/i> = 2, <i>y<\/i> = 0 − 5 = −5, the new lowest point ✓","wrong":{"Left 2 and up 5":"Both directions are reversed.","Left 2 and down 5":"The horizontal direction is reversed: (<i>x<\/i> − 2) moves right.","Right 2 and up 5":"The vertical direction is reversed: − 5 moves down."}},"b6":{"ask":"What is the smallest value of |<i>x<\/i> − 1| + 2?","idea":"An absolute value is never negative; its smallest value is 0. So the whole expression is smallest when |<i>x<\/i> − 1| = 0.","steps":["|<i>x<\/i> − 1| = 0 when <i>x<\/i> = 1.","Minimum: 0 + 2 = 2."],"check":"<i>f<\/i>(0) = 3 and <i>f<\/i>(2) = 3, both larger ✓","wrong":{"−1":"An absolute value can't be negative, so <i>f<\/i> is always at least 2.","0":"That's the smallest value of |<i>x<\/i> − 1|; add 2.","1":"That's where the minimum happens (<i>x<\/i> = 1), not its value."}},"b7":{"ask":"Divide <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4 by <i>x<\/i> + 2 and simplify.","idea":"Factor the top: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4 is a difference of squares, (<i>x<\/i> − 2)(<i>x<\/i> + 2). Then cancel the factor shared with the bottom.","steps":["(<i>x<\/i> − 2)(<i>x<\/i> + 2) ÷ (<i>x<\/i> + 2).","Cancel (<i>x<\/i> + 2): <i>x<\/i> − 2."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 3: 5 ÷ 5 = 1, and 3 − 2 = 1 ✓","wrong":{"x² − x − 6":"Doesn't match: try <i>x<\/i> = 3, where the original is 5 ÷ 5 = 1 but this gives 0.","x − 4":"Subtracts the expressions instead of dividing.","x + 2":"Cancels the wrong factor; (<i>x<\/i> + 2) is what gets removed."}},"b8":{"ask":"The inverse of <i>f<\/i> sends <i>a<\/i> to 4. Find <i>a<\/i>.","idea":"An inverse reverses input and output. <i>f<\/i><sup>−1<\/sup>(<i>a<\/i>) = 4 means <i>f<\/i>(4) = <i>a<\/i>. So just evaluate <i>f<\/i> at 4.","steps":["<i>a<\/i> = <i>f<\/i>(4) = 2(4) − 5 = 3."],"check":"<i>f<\/i><sup>−1<\/sup>(<i>x<\/i>) = (<i>x<\/i> + 5)/2, and (3 + 5)/2 = 4 ✓","wrong":{"−3":"Sign error: 8 − 5 = 3.","4.5":"That's <i>f<\/i><sup>−1<\/sup>(4), the reverse question.","13":"Adds 5 instead of subtracting."}},"b9":{"ask":"Which graph is a mirror image across the <i>y<\/i>-axis?","idea":"A function is even if <i>f<\/i>(−<i>x<\/i>) = <i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>): plugging in the opposite input gives the same output. Functions built only from even powers of <i>x<\/i> (and constants) are even, because (−<i>x<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> and (−<i>x<\/i>)<sup>4<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup>4<\/sup>.","steps":["<i>f<\/i>(−<i>x<\/i>) = (−<i>x<\/i>)<sup>4<\/sup> − 3(−<i>x<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup>4<\/sup> − 3<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = <i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>).","So it's even."],"check":"<i>f<\/i>(2) = 16 − 12 = 4 and <i>f<\/i>(−2) = 4 ✓","wrong":{"f(x) = 2x − 1":"<i>f<\/i>(1) = 1 but <i>f<\/i>(−1) = −3.","f(x) = x³ + x":"Odd powers only: <i>f<\/i>(−<i>x<\/i>) = −<i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>), so it's odd, not even.","f(x) = x² + x":"The <i>x<\/i> term breaks the symmetry: <i>f<\/i>(1) = 2 but <i>f<\/i>(−1) = 0."}},"b10":{"ask":"What is the highest value of <i>y<\/i> = −2(<i>x<\/i> + 1)<sup>2<\/sup> + 3?","idea":"In vertex form <i>a<\/i>(<i>x<\/i> − <i>h<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> + <i>k<\/i>, the vertex is (<i>h<\/i>, <i>k<\/i>). A negative <i>a<\/i> means the parabola opens down, so <i>k<\/i> is the maximum.","steps":["<i>a<\/i> = −2, so it opens downward.","Vertex: (−1, 3).","Maximum value: 3."],"check":"The squared term is at least 0, so −2(…)<sup>2<\/sup> is at most 0 and <i>y<\/i> is at most 3 ✓","wrong":{"−2":"That's <i>a<\/i>.","−1":"That's where the maximum happens (the <i>x<\/i>-value).","1":"Uses the wrong sign from (<i>x<\/i> + 1)."}},"b11":{"ask":"What is <i>i<\/i> squared?","idea":"The imaginary unit <i>i<\/i> is defined as the number whose square is −1: <i>i<\/i> = √−1. It lets us take square roots of negative numbers.","steps":["By definition, <i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = −1."],"check":"This is the one fact all complex-number arithmetic uses ✓","wrong":{"−i":"That's <i>i<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>.","1":"That's <i>i<\/i><sup>4<\/sup>.","i":"That's <i>i<\/i><sup>1<\/sup>."}},"b12":{"ask":"Add two complex numbers.","idea":"A complex number has a real part and an imaginary part (the part with <i>i<\/i>). Add real parts to real parts, and imaginary parts to imaginary parts, like combining like terms.","steps":["Real: 3 + 1 = 4.","Imaginary: 2<i>i<\/i> + (−5<i>i<\/i>) = −3<i>i<\/i>.","Sum: 4 − 3<i>i<\/i>."],"check":"Same as combining 3 + 2<i>x<\/i> + 1 − 5<i>x<\/i> = 4 − 3<i>x<\/i> ✓","wrong":{"4 + 7i":"Adds 5<i>i<\/i> instead of subtracting it.","2 − 3i":"Subtracts the real parts.","4 − 3":"Drops the <i>i<\/i>; the imaginary part must keep it."}},"b13":{"ask":"Multiply (2 + <i>i<\/i>)(3 − <i>i<\/i>).","idea":"Multiply like two binomials (every term by every term), then replace <i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> with −1.","steps":["6 − 2<i>i<\/i> + 3<i>i<\/i> − <i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","Combine: 6 + <i>i<\/i> − <i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","<i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = −1, so − <i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = +1: 7 + <i>i<\/i>."],"check":"The only special step is − <i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = +1 ✓","wrong":{"7 − i":"Sign error on the <i>i<\/i> terms: −2<i>i<\/i> + 3<i>i<\/i> = +<i>i<\/i>.","6 − i":"Forgets the <i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> term and misadds the <i>i<\/i> terms.","5 + i":"Treats − <i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> as −1 instead of +1."}},"b14":{"ask":"Solve <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 9 = 0.","idea":"<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = −9 has no real solution, since no real number squares to a negative. Using <i>i<\/i> = √−1: √−9 = √9 · √−1 = 3<i>i<\/i>. Like any square root equation, there are two solutions, ±.","steps":["<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = −9.","<i>x<\/i> = ±√−9 = ±3<i>i<\/i>."],"check":"(3<i>i<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> = 9<i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 9(−1) = −9 ✓","wrong":{"9i and −9i":"Forgets the square root: (9<i>i<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> = −81.","3 and −3":"3<sup>2<\/sup> = +9, which gives 9 + 9 = 18, not 0.","No solutions of any kind":"There are no <em>real<\/em> solutions, but there are two complex ones."}},"b15":{"ask":"How many real solutions does <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 6<i>x<\/i> + 13 = 0 have?","idea":"The discriminant <i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4<i>ac<\/i> tells the number and type of solutions: positive → two real, zero → one real, negative → two complex (non-real) solutions.","steps":["<i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4<i>ac<\/i> = 36 − 52 = −16.","Negative → no real solutions, but two complex ones."],"check":"The solutions are 3 ± 2<i>i<\/i> ✓","wrong":{"Two real solutions":"That needs a positive discriminant.","One real solution":"That needs a discriminant of 0.","No solutions of any kind":"There are two complex solutions, just no real ones."}},"b16":{"ask":"Solve <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4<i>x<\/i> + 13 = 0.","idea":"Use the quadratic formula. When the number under the root is negative, write √(−36) = 6<i>i<\/i>.","steps":["<i>x<\/i> = (4 ± √(16 − 52)) ÷ 2 = (4 ± √(−36)) ÷ 2.","√(−36) = 6<i>i<\/i>: <i>x<\/i> = (4 ± 6<i>i<\/i>) ÷ 2.","<i>x<\/i> = 2 ± 3<i>i<\/i>."],"check":"Sum: (2 + 3<i>i<\/i>) + (2 − 3<i>i<\/i>) = 4 = −<i>b<\/i>/<i>a<\/i> ✓","wrong":{"−2 ± 3i":"Forgets the minus in −<i>b<\/i>.","4 ± 6i":"Forgets to divide by 2.","2 ± 3":"Drops the <i>i<\/i>; the root of a negative is imaginary."}},"b17":{"ask":"Simplify <i>i<\/i> to the 23rd power.","idea":"Powers of <i>i<\/i> cycle every 4: <i>i<\/i>, −1, −<i>i<\/i>, 1, then repeat. Divide the exponent by 4; the remainder tells you where you land.","steps":["23 ÷ 4 = 5 remainder 3.","So <i>i<\/i><sup>23<\/sup> = <i>i<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> = −<i>i<\/i>."],"check":"<i>i<\/i><sup>20<\/sup> = 1 (5 full cycles), then 3 more steps: <i>i<\/i>, −1, −<i>i<\/i> ✓","wrong":{"i":"That's remainder 1.","−1":"That's remainder 2.","1":"That's remainder 0."}},"b18":{"ask":"Square (1 + <i>i<\/i>).","idea":"(<i>a<\/i> + <i>b<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>a<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 2<i>ab<\/i> + <i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>. Then use <i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = −1.","steps":["1<sup>2<\/sup> + 2(1)(<i>i<\/i>) + <i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 1 + 2<i>i<\/i> + <i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","<i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = −1: 1 + 2<i>i<\/i> − 1 = 2<i>i<\/i>."],"check":"(1 + <i>i<\/i>)(1 + <i>i<\/i>) = 1 + <i>i<\/i> + <i>i<\/i> + <i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 2<i>i<\/i> ✓","wrong":{"0":"Forgets the middle term 2<i>i<\/i>.","2":"Treats <i>i<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> as +1.","1 + i²":"Squares each term and forgets the middle term."}},"b19":{"ask":"Rewrite <i>y<\/i> = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 8<i>x<\/i> + 10 in vertex form.","idea":"Complete the square: take half the <i>x<\/i>-coefficient (8 ÷ 2 = 4), square it (16), then add and subtract it. The first three terms become a perfect square.","steps":["<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 8<i>x<\/i> + 16 − 16 + 10.","(<i>x<\/i> + 4)<sup>2<\/sup> − 6."],"check":"Expand: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 8<i>x<\/i> + 16 − 6 = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 8<i>x<\/i> + 10 ✓","wrong":{"y = (x − 4)² − 6":"Wrong sign inside: that expands to <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 8<i>x<\/i> + 10.","y = (x + 4)² + 10":"Adds 16 without subtracting it back.","y = (x + 8)² − 54":"Uses 8 instead of half of 8."}},"b20":{"ask":"Add the two solutions of 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 10<i>x<\/i> + 7 = 0.","idea":"For <i>a<\/i><i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + <i>b<\/i><i>x<\/i> + <i>c<\/i> = 0, the solutions always add to −<i>b<\/i>/<i>a<\/i>. You don't need to find them.","steps":["<i>a<\/i> = 2, <i>b<\/i> = −10.","Sum: −(−10)/2 = 5."],"check":"The quadratic formula gives (10 ± √44)/4; the ± parts cancel when added, leaving 20/4 = 5 ✓","wrong":{"−5":"Forgets the minus sign in −<i>b<\/i>/<i>a<\/i>.","3.5":"That's the product, <i>c<\/i>/<i>a<\/i>.","10":"Forgets to divide by <i>a<\/i>."}},"b21":{"ask":"What is the highest power in the polynomial?","idea":"The degree of a polynomial is its highest exponent. It tells you the most zeros it can have and its end behavior.","steps":["Exponents: 5, 3 and 0 (the constant).","Degree: 5."],"check":"4<i>x<\/i><sup>5<\/sup> is the leading term ✓","wrong":{"3":"That's the middle term's exponent.","4":"That's the leading coefficient.","7":"That's the constant term."}},"b22":{"ask":"What does the graph of <i>y<\/i> = <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> do at the far left and far right?","idea":"End behavior depends on the degree and the leading coefficient. Odd degree: the ends go opposite ways. With a positive leading coefficient, it falls on the left and rises on the right.","steps":["Large positive <i>x<\/i>: <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> is large positive, so it rises on the right.","Large negative <i>x<\/i>: <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> is large negative, so it falls on the left."],"check":"(−10)<sup>3<\/sup> = −1,000 and 10<sup>3<\/sup> = 1,000 ✓","wrong":{"Rises on both ends":"That's even degree with a positive leading coefficient, like <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","Falls on both ends":"That's even degree with a negative leading coefficient.","Rises on the left, falls on the right":"That's odd degree with a <em>negative<\/em> leading coefficient."}},"b23":{"ask":"Where is <i>y<\/i> = <i>x<\/i>(<i>x<\/i> − 3)(<i>x<\/i> + 2) equal to 0?","idea":"A product is 0 when any factor is 0. Each factor gives a zero; the lone <i>x<\/i> is a factor too, giving <i>x<\/i> = 0.","steps":["<i>x<\/i> = 0 → 0.","<i>x<\/i> − 3 = 0 → 3.","<i>x<\/i> + 2 = 0 → −2."],"check":"Degree 3, so up to 3 zeros, and there are exactly 3 ✓","wrong":{"0, −3 and 2":"Copies the signs from the factors without flipping them.","−3 and 2":"Flips the signs and forgets the lone <i>x<\/i>.","3 and −2":"Forgets the zero from the lone <i>x<\/i>."}},"b24":{"ask":"Find the remainder when dividing by (<i>x<\/i> − 2).","idea":"The Remainder Theorem: when a polynomial <i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) is divided by (<i>x<\/i> − <i>c<\/i>), the remainder is <i>f<\/i>(<i>c<\/i>). So just evaluate at <i>x<\/i> = 2.","steps":["<i>f<\/i>(2) = 2<sup>3<\/sup> − 2(2) + 5 = 8 − 4 + 5 = 9."],"check":"Long division gives <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 2<i>x<\/i> + 2 with remainder 9 ✓","wrong":{"1":"That's <i>f<\/i>(−2). Dividing by (<i>x<\/i> − 2) means evaluating at +2.","5":"That's the constant term, <i>f<\/i>(0).","17":"Adds 4 instead of subtracting it."}},"b25":{"ask":"Factor <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> − 8.","idea":"Difference of cubes: <i>a<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> − <i>b<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> = (<i>a<\/i> − <i>b<\/i>)(<i>a<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + <i>ab<\/i> + <i>b<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>). Here <i>a<\/i> = <i>x<\/i> and <i>b<\/i> = 2 (since 8 = 2<sup>3<\/sup>). Memory aid: “same, opposite, always positive.”","steps":["(<i>x<\/i> − 2)(<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 2<i>x<\/i> + 4)."],"check":"Expand: <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> + 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 4<i>x<\/i> − 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4<i>x<\/i> − 8 = <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> − 8 ✓","wrong":{"(x + 2)(x² − 2x + 4)":"That's the sum of cubes, <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> + 8.","(x − 2)³":"Expands to <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> − 6<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 12<i>x<\/i> − 8.","(x − 2)(x² − 2x + 4)":"The middle sign is wrong; this doesn't expand back to <i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> − 8."}},"b26":{"ask":"For which <i>k<\/i> is (<i>x<\/i> + 1) a factor?","idea":"Factor Theorem: (<i>x<\/i> − <i>c<\/i>) is a factor exactly when <i>f<\/i>(<i>c<\/i>) = 0. Here (<i>x<\/i> + 1) = (<i>x<\/i> − (−1)), so set <i>f<\/i>(−1) = 0.","steps":["<i>f<\/i>(−1) = (−1)<sup>3<\/sup> + <i>k<\/i>(−1) + 3 = −1 − <i>k<\/i> + 3.","2 − <i>k<\/i> = 0, so <i>k<\/i> = 2."],"check":"<i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> + 2<i>x<\/i> + 3 at <i>x<\/i> = −1: −1 − 2 + 3 = 0 ✓","wrong":{"−2":"Sign error: 2 − <i>k<\/i> = 0 gives <i>k<\/i> = 2.","3":"Doesn't work: <i>f<\/i>(−1) = −1 − 3 + 3 = −1.","4":"Doesn't work: <i>f<\/i>(−1) = −2."}},"b27":{"ask":"Divide 2<i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> + 3<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 5<i>x<\/i> − 6 by (<i>x<\/i> + 2).","idea":"Synthetic division with −2 (the zero of <i>x<\/i> + 2): bring down, multiply by −2, add, repeat. The numbers you get are the coefficients of the answer, one degree lower.","steps":["Coefficients 2, 3, −5, −6. Bring down 2.","2 × (−2) = −4; 3 + (−4) = −1. Then −1 × (−2) = 2; −5 + 2 = −3.","−3 × (−2) = 6; −6 + 6 = 0 remainder. Answer: 2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − <i>x<\/i> − 3."],"check":"(<i>x<\/i> + 2)(2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − <i>x<\/i> − 3) = 2<i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> + 3<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 5<i>x<\/i> − 6 ✓","wrong":{"2x² + 7x + 9":"Uses +2 instead of −2 in synthetic division.","x² − x − 3":"Drops the leading 2.","2x² − x + 3":"Sign error on the last coefficient: multiplying back gives 2<i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup> + 3<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + <i>x<\/i> + 6."}},"b28":{"ask":"A polynomial has zeros 1, −2 and 3. What is its constant term?","idea":"Each zero <i>r<\/i> gives a factor (<i>x<\/i> − <i>r<\/i>). The constant term is the product of the constants in the factors.","steps":["<i>f<\/i>(<i>x<\/i>) = (<i>x<\/i> − 1)(<i>x<\/i> + 2)(<i>x<\/i> − 3).","Constant: (−1)(2)(−3) = 6."],"check":"<i>f<\/i>(0) = (−1)(2)(−3) = 6 ✓","wrong":{"−6":"Sign error: two negatives multiply to a positive.","0":"Assumes 0 is a zero; it isn't.","2":"Adds the constants instead of multiplying."}},"b29":{"ask":"How many real zeros does <i>x<\/i><sup>4<\/sup> − 5<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 4 have?","idea":"This is a quadratic in disguise: let <i>u<\/i> = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>, and it becomes <i>u<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 5<i>u<\/i> + 4. Factor, then undo the substitution.","steps":["(<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 1)(<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4) = 0.","<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 1 → ±1; <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> = 4 → ±2.","Four real zeros."],"check":"A degree-4 polynomial has at most 4 zeros, and all 4 are real here ✓","wrong":{"0":"There are real zeros, like <i>x<\/i> = 1: 1 − 5 + 4 = 0.","2":"Counts only the positive roots.","5":"A degree-4 polynomial can't have 5 zeros."}},"b30":{"ask":"What does the graph do at <i>x<\/i> = 2?","idea":"A zero's multiplicity is how many times its factor appears. Odd multiplicity: the graph crosses the <i>x<\/i>-axis. Even multiplicity: it touches the axis and turns back.","steps":["(<i>x<\/i> − 2)<sup>2<\/sup>: the zero 2 has multiplicity 2 (even).","So the graph touches the <i>x<\/i>-axis at 2 and turns around."],"check":"Near <i>x<\/i> = 2, (<i>x<\/i> − 2)<sup>2<\/sup> is positive on both sides, so the sign doesn't change ✓","wrong":{"It has a vertical asymptote there":"Polynomials never have asymptotes.","It crosses the x-axis":"That's for odd multiplicity, like at <i>x<\/i> = −1.","It never reaches the x-axis":"<i>f<\/i>(2) = 0, so it does reach it."}},"b31":{"ask":"Simplify (<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 9)/(<i>x<\/i> − 3).","idea":"Factor the top, then cancel any factor that appears on both top and bottom. <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 9 is a difference of squares.","steps":["<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 9 = (<i>x<\/i> − 3)(<i>x<\/i> + 3).","Cancel (<i>x<\/i> − 3): <i>x<\/i> + 3."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 4: 7 ÷ 1 = 7, and 4 + 3 = 7 ✓","wrong":{"x − 9":"Divides term by term; you can only cancel factors.","x − 3":"Cancels the wrong factor.","x² − 3":"Cancels the <i>x<\/i> from the bottom only."}},"b32":{"ask":"When is 5/(<i>x<\/i> − 4) undefined?","idea":"Division by zero is undefined. Find where the denominator equals 0.","steps":["<i>x<\/i> − 4 = 0 → <i>x<\/i> = 4."],"check":"At <i>x<\/i> = 4 the expression is 5/0 ✓","wrong":{"−4":"Sign error: <i>x<\/i> − 4 = 0 at <i>x<\/i> = 4.","0":"At <i>x<\/i> = 0 the value is 5/(−4), which is fine.","5":"At <i>x<\/i> = 5 the value is 5/1 = 5."}},"b33":{"ask":"Add 1/<i>x<\/i> and 1/(2<i>x<\/i>).","idea":"To add fractions, first rewrite them with a common denominator. Never add the denominators. The common denominator here is 2<i>x<\/i>.","steps":["1/<i>x<\/i> = 2/(2<i>x<\/i>).","2/(2<i>x<\/i>) + 1/(2<i>x<\/i>) = 3/(2<i>x<\/i>)."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 1: 1 + 1/2 = 1.5, and 3/2 = 1.5 ✓","wrong":{"1/(3x)":"Adds the denominators; that's not how fractions add.","2/(3x)":"Adds tops and bottoms separately.","2/x":"Doubles 1/<i>x<\/i>; 1/(2<i>x<\/i>) is only half of 1/<i>x<\/i>."}},"b34":{"ask":"Solve 3/(<i>x<\/i> + 1) = 1/2.","idea":"Cross-multiply to clear the fractions: 3 × 2 = 1 × (<i>x<\/i> + 1).","steps":["<i>x<\/i> + 1 = 6.","<i>x<\/i> = 5."],"check":"3/(5 + 1) = 3/6 = 1/2 ✓","wrong":{"1/6":"Doesn't work: 3/(7/6) = 18/7.","6":"That's <i>x<\/i> + 1; subtract 1.","7":"Adds 1 instead of subtracting."}},"b35":{"ask":"Where does <i>y<\/i> = 2/(<i>x<\/i> + 5) have a vertical asymptote?","idea":"A vertical asymptote is a vertical line the graph approaches but never touches. It occurs where the denominator is 0 (and the numerator isn't).","steps":["<i>x<\/i> + 5 = 0 → <i>x<\/i> = −5.","Asymptote: <i>x<\/i> = −5."],"check":"Near <i>x<\/i> = −5, the denominator is tiny and the values blow up ✓","wrong":{"x = 5":"Sign error.","x = 2":"Uses the numerator.","y = 2":"A vertical asymptote has the form <i>x<\/i> = …; also, this graph's horizontal asymptote is <i>y<\/i> = 0."}},"b36":{"ask":"Solve <i>x<\/i>/(<i>x<\/i> − 2) = 3.","idea":"Multiply both sides by the denominator to clear the fraction. Then check that your answer doesn't make the denominator zero.","steps":["<i>x<\/i> = 3(<i>x<\/i> − 2) = 3<i>x<\/i> − 6.","−2<i>x<\/i> = −6, so <i>x<\/i> = 3.","<i>x<\/i> = 3 doesn't make <i>x<\/i> − 2 zero, so it's valid."],"check":"3/(3 − 2) = 3 ✓","wrong":{"−3":"Doesn't work: −3/(−5) = 0.6.","2":"Makes the denominator 0, so it's never allowed.","6":"Doesn't work: 6/4 = 1.5."}},"b37":{"ask":"What horizontal line does the graph approach far to the left and right?","idea":"For very large <i>x<\/i>, only the leading terms matter. When the top and bottom have the same degree, the horizontal asymptote is the ratio of their leading coefficients.","steps":["Top leading coefficient 3, bottom 1 (both degree 1).","Asymptote: <i>y<\/i> = 3/1 = 3."],"check":"At <i>x<\/i> = 1,000: 3,001/998 ≈ 3.007 ✓","wrong":{"y = 0":"That's when the bottom has the higher degree.","y = −1/2":"Uses the constants 1/(−2) instead of the leading coefficients.","x = 2":"That's the vertical asymptote."}},"b38":{"ask":"Multiply the fractions and simplify.","idea":"Factor everything, then cancel factors that appear on both top and bottom. <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4 = (<i>x<\/i> − 2)(<i>x<\/i> + 2).","steps":["[(<i>x<\/i> − 2)(<i>x<\/i> + 2)/(<i>x<\/i> + 3)] · [(<i>x<\/i> + 3)/(<i>x<\/i> − 2)].","Cancel (<i>x<\/i> + 3) and (<i>x<\/i> − 2).","Left: <i>x<\/i> + 2."],"check":"Try <i>x<\/i> = 3: (5/6)(6/1) = 5, and 3 + 2 = 5 ✓","wrong":{"(x² − 4)/(x − 2)":"Cancels (<i>x<\/i> + 3) but stops before factoring <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 4.","1":"Cancels too much; (<i>x<\/i> + 2) remains.","x − 2":"Cancels (<i>x<\/i> + 2) instead of (<i>x<\/i> − 2)."}},"b39":{"ask":"Working together, how long do Ana and Ben take to paint the room?","idea":"Add rates, not times. Ana paints 1/3 of the room per hour and Ben 1/6 per hour. Together they paint the sum per hour; the time is 1 ÷ that rate.","steps":["Combined rate: 1/3 + 1/6 = 2/6 + 1/6 = 1/2 room per hour.","Time: 1 ÷ (1/2) = 2 hours."],"check":"In 2 hours Ana paints 2/3 and Ben 1/3, together 1 room ✓","wrong":{"4.5 hours":"Averages the times; together they must be faster than Ana alone.","3 hours":"That's Ana alone; Ben's help makes it faster.","9 hours":"Adds the times."}},"b40":{"ask":"Solve an equation with fractions.","idea":"Multiply every term by the common denominator <i>x<\/i>(<i>x<\/i> + 2) to clear the fractions. Then check that the answer doesn't make any denominator zero (<i>x<\/i> ≠ 0, <i>x<\/i> ≠ −2).","steps":["(<i>x<\/i> + 2) + <i>x<\/i> = 4.","2<i>x<\/i> + 2 = 4, so <i>x<\/i> = 1.","1 doesn't make any denominator zero."],"check":"1/1 + 1/3 = 4/3, and 4/(1 × 3) = 4/3 ✓","wrong":{"−2":"Makes a denominator zero, so it's never allowed.","0":"Makes a denominator zero, so it's never allowed.","2":"Doesn't work: 1/2 + 1/4 = 3/4 but 4/8 = 1/2."}},"b41":{"ask":"What is 8 to the power 1/3?","idea":"A fraction exponent 1/<i>n<\/i> means the <i>n<\/i>th root. So 8<sup>1/3<\/sup> is the cube root of 8: the number that, multiplied by itself three times, gives 8.","steps":["2 × 2 × 2 = 8.","8<sup>1/3<\/sup> = 2."],"check":"2<sup>3<\/sup> = 8 ✓","wrong":{"8/3":"Multiplies 8 by 1/3 instead of taking a root.","4":"4<sup>3<\/sup> = 64, not 8.","24":"Multiplies 8 by 3."}},"b42":{"ask":"Write <i>x<\/i><sup>1/2<\/sup> using a root sign.","idea":"An exponent of 1/2 means square root, because squaring it gives (<i>x<\/i><sup>1/2<\/sup>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup>1<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i>.","steps":["<i>x<\/i><sup>1/2<\/sup> = √<i>x<\/i>."],"check":"(√<i>x<\/i>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i>, and (<i>x<\/i><sup>1/2<\/sup>)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i> ✓","wrong":{"x/2":"Divides by 2 instead of taking a root.","2x":"Multiplies by 2.","1/x²":"Confuses a fraction exponent with a negative exponent."}},"b43":{"ask":"Simplify √50.","idea":"Find the largest perfect square factor of 50, then take its root outside: √(<i>a<\/i> × <i>b<\/i>) = √<i>a<\/i> × √<i>b<\/i>.","steps":["50 = 25 × 2.","√50 = √25 × √2 = 5√2."],"check":"(5√2)<sup>2<\/sup> = 25 × 2 = 50 ✓","wrong":{"25√2":"Forgets to take the square root of 25.","2√5":"(2√5)<sup>2<\/sup> = 20, not 50.","10√5":"(10√5)<sup>2<\/sup> = 500, not 50."}},"b44":{"ask":"What is 16 to the power 3/4?","idea":"In a fraction exponent, the bottom is the root and the top is the power. It's easiest to take the root first (smaller numbers), then the power.","steps":["Fourth root of 16: 2 (since 2<sup>4<\/sup> = 16).","Cube it: 2<sup>3<\/sup> = 8."],"check":"8<sup>4<\/sup> = 4,096 = 16<sup>3<\/sup> ✓","wrong":{"2":"Takes the fourth root but forgets to cube.","12":"Multiplies 16 × 3/4.","64":"Cubes 4 (the square root of 16) instead of 2."}},"b45":{"ask":"Solve √(<i>x<\/i> + 3) = 5.","idea":"Square both sides to undo the square root, then solve. Check the answer in the original, since squaring can add false solutions.","steps":["Square: <i>x<\/i> + 3 = 25.","<i>x<\/i> = 22."],"check":"√(22 + 3) = √25 = 5 ✓","wrong":{"2":"Subtracts 3 from 5 without squaring first.","8":"Adds 3 to 5 without squaring.","28":"Adds 3 instead of subtracting it."}},"b46":{"ask":"Simplify (<i>x<\/i><sup>2/3<\/sup>)<sup>3<\/sup>.","idea":"A power raised to another power multiplies the exponents: (<i>x<\/i><sup><i>a<\/i><\/sup>)<sup><i>b<\/i><\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup><i>ab<\/i><\/sup>.","steps":["(2/3) × 3 = 2.","Result: <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>."],"check":"Cubing a cube root undoes it: (<i>x<\/i><sup>2/3<\/sup>)<sup>3<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> ✓","wrong":{"x^(2/9)":"Divides the exponent by 3 instead of multiplying.","x^(5/3)":"Adds 1 to the exponent. Raising a power to a power multiplies the exponents.","x⁶":"Multiplies 2 × 3 and ignores the 1/3."}},"b47":{"ask":"Solve √(2<i>x<\/i> + 7) = <i>x<\/i> + 2.","idea":"Square both sides and solve the quadratic. Squaring can create an extra, false solution, because a square root is never negative. Always check each answer in the original.","steps":["2<i>x<\/i> + 7 = (<i>x<\/i> + 2)<sup>2<\/sup> = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 4<i>x<\/i> + 4.","<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 2<i>x<\/i> − 3 = 0, so (<i>x<\/i> + 3)(<i>x<\/i> − 1) = 0: <i>x<\/i> = −3 or 1.","Check −3: √1 = 1, but −3 + 2 = −1. Fails.","Check 1: √9 = 3 and 1 + 2 = 3. Works."],"check":"Only <i>x<\/i> = 1 ✓","wrong":{"−3":"It solves the squared equation but not the original: √1 is 1, not −1.","3":"Doesn't work: √13 ≈ 3.6, but 3 + 2 = 5.","1 and −3":"Keeps the false solution −3 without checking."}},"b48":{"ask":"Remove the square root from the denominator of 6/√3.","idea":"“Rationalizing” means multiplying top and bottom by the root in the denominator, which equals multiplying by 1. Then √3 × √3 = 3.","steps":["(6/√3) × (√3/√3) = 6√3/3.","Simplify: 2√3."],"check":"2√3 ≈ 3.46, and 6 ÷ 1.732 ≈ 3.46 ✓","wrong":{"3√2":"Doesn't match: 3√2 ≈ 4.24.","6√3":"Multiplies the top by √3 but not the bottom.","√3/2":"Flips the fraction: 6/√3 is about 3.46, but √3/2 is about 0.87."}},"b49":{"ask":"What is 32 to the power −2/5?","idea":"Take it in parts. The negative sign means reciprocal (one over). The 5 in the denominator means fifth root, and the 2 means square.","steps":["Fifth root of 32: 2 (since 2<sup>5<\/sup> = 32).","Square: 2<sup>2<\/sup> = 4.","Negative exponent → reciprocal: 1/4."],"check":"32<sup>2/5<\/sup> = 4, so 32<sup>−2/5<\/sup> = 1/4 ✓","wrong":{"−4":"A negative exponent means reciprocal, not negative.","−1/4":"Takes the reciprocal and also adds a negative sign.","4":"Forgets the reciprocal from the negative exponent."}},"b50":{"ask":"Simplify √(18<i>x<\/i><sup>4<\/sup>).","idea":"Split the root: √18 × √(<i>x<\/i><sup>4<\/sup>). Pull out perfect squares: 18 = 9 × 2, and <i>x<\/i><sup>4<\/sup> = (<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>)<sup>2<\/sup>.","steps":["√18 = √9 × √2 = 3√2.","√(<i>x<\/i><sup>4<\/sup>) = <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","Result: 3<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>√2."],"check":"(3<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>√2)<sup>2<\/sup> = 9<i>x<\/i><sup>4<\/sup> × 2 = 18<i>x<\/i><sup>4<\/sup> ✓","wrong":{"3x√2":"Takes √(<i>x<\/i><sup>4<\/sup>) as <i>x<\/i>; it's <i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>.","9x²√2":"Forgets to take the square root of 9.","3x²√6":"Splits 18 as 3 × 6 incorrectly; 3 isn't a perfect square."}},"b51":{"ask":"2 to what power equals 8?","idea":"A logarithm is an exponent. log<sub>2<\/sub> 8 asks: “2 raised to what power gives 8?”","steps":["2<sup>3<\/sup> = 8.","log<sub>2<\/sub> 8 = 3."],"check":"2 × 2 × 2 = 8 ✓","wrong":{"1/3":"Flips the relationship.","4":"2<sup>4<\/sup> = 16, not 8.","16":"Multiplies 2 × 8."}},"b52":{"ask":"10 to what power equals 1,000?","idea":"“log” with no base written means base 10. log 1,000 asks: “10 to what power is 1,000?” Count the zeros.","steps":["10<sup>3<\/sup> = 1,000.","log 1,000 = 3."],"check":"1,000 has 3 zeros ✓","wrong":{"1/3":"Flips the relationship.","30":"Doesn't match: 10<sup>30<\/sup> is enormous.","100":"That's 10<sup>2<\/sup>."}},"b53":{"ask":"Rewrite 5<sup>3<\/sup> = 125 as a logarithm.","idea":"<i>b<\/i><sup><i>y<\/i><\/sup> = <i>x<\/i> is the same statement as log<sub><i>b<\/i><\/sub> <i>x<\/i> = <i>y<\/i>. The base stays the base, and the logarithm equals the exponent.","steps":["Base 5, exponent 3, result 125.","log<sub>5<\/sub> 125 = 3."],"check":"Read it back: 5 to the power 3 is 125 ✓","wrong":{"log₁₂₅ 5 = 3":"Swaps the base and the result.","log₃ 125 = 5":"Swaps the base and the exponent.","log₅ 3 = 125":"Swaps the result and the exponent."}},"b54":{"ask":"Solve 2<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup> = 32.","idea":"Write both sides as powers of the same base. When the bases match, the exponents must match.","steps":["32 = 2<sup>5<\/sup> (2, 4, 8, 16, 32).","<i>x<\/i> = 5."],"check":"2<sup>5<\/sup> = 32 ✓","wrong":{"4":"2<sup>4<\/sup> = 16.","6":"2<sup>6<\/sup> = 64.","16":"That's 32 ÷ 2."}},"b55":{"ask":"Combine log 2 + log 5.","idea":"Product rule: log <i>a<\/i> + log <i>b<\/i> = log(<i>ab<\/i>). Logs turn multiplication into addition, because exponents add when you multiply powers.","steps":["log 2 + log 5 = log(2 × 5) = log 10.","log 10 = 1."],"check":"log 2 ≈ 0.301 and log 5 ≈ 0.699; they add to 1 ✓","wrong":{"0.7":"That's about log 5 alone.","log 7":"Adds the inputs; the rule multiplies them.","10":"That's the input, 2 × 5; the log of 10 is 1."}},"b56":{"ask":"Solve log<sub>3<\/sub>(<i>x<\/i> + 1) = 4.","idea":"Rewrite the logarithm as an exponent: log<sub>3<\/sub>(stuff) = 4 means 3<sup>4<\/sup> = stuff.","steps":["<i>x<\/i> + 1 = 3<sup>4<\/sup> = 81.","<i>x<\/i> = 80."],"check":"log<sub>3<\/sub> 81 = 4 because 3<sup>4<\/sup> = 81 ✓","wrong":{"11":"Multiplies 3 × 4 and subtracts 1.","63":"Uses 4<sup>3<\/sup> = 64 instead of 3<sup>4<\/sup>.","81":"That's <i>x<\/i> + 1; subtract 1."}},"b57":{"ask":"Solve 3<sup><i>x<\/i> + 1<\/sup> = 27<sup><i>x<\/i> − 1<\/sup>.","idea":"Write both sides with the same base, 3. Since 27 = 3<sup>3<\/sup>, the right side is (3<sup>3<\/sup>)<sup><i>x<\/i> − 1<\/sup> = 3<sup>3x − 3<\/sup>. Then set the exponents equal.","steps":["3<sup><i>x<\/i> + 1<\/sup> = 3<sup>3x − 3<\/sup>.","<i>x<\/i> + 1 = 3<i>x<\/i> − 3.","4 = 2<i>x<\/i>, so <i>x<\/i> = 2."],"check":"3<sup>3<\/sup> = 27 and 27<sup>1<\/sup> = 27 ✓","wrong":{"1":"Doesn't work: 3<sup>2<\/sup> = 9, but 27<sup>0<\/sup> = 1.","3":"Doesn't work: 3<sup>4<\/sup> = 81, but 27<sup>2<\/sup> = 729.","4":"That's 2<i>x<\/i>; divide by 2."}},"b58":{"ask":"What is $1,000 worth after 10 years with continuous compounding at 5%?","idea":"Continuous compounding uses <i>A<\/i> = <i>P<\/i><i>e<\/i><sup><i>rt<\/i><\/sup>. Multiply the rate by the time for the exponent, then use the given value of <i>e<\/i><sup>0<\/sup>.5.","steps":["<i>rt<\/i> = 0.05 × 10 = 0.5.","<i>A<\/i> = 1,000 × <i>e<\/i><sup>0<\/sup>.5 ≈ 1,000 × 1.64872 = $1,648.72."],"check":"Continuous compounding earns a little more than yearly compounding ($1,628.89) ✓","wrong":{"$1,500.00":"That's simple interest: $50 a year for 10 years.","$1,628.89":"That's compounding once a year: 1,000(1.05)<sup>10<\/sup>.","$2,718.28":"Uses <i>e<\/i><sup>1<\/sup> instead of <i>e<\/i><sup>0<\/sup>.5."}},"b59":{"ask":"Solve 5<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup> = 20.","idea":"20 isn't a whole-number power of 5, so use logarithms: take the log of both sides, and the exponent comes down. <i>x<\/i> = log 20 ÷ log 5.","steps":["log(5<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup>) = log 20, so <i>x<\/i> log 5 = log 20.","<i>x<\/i> = 1.3010 ÷ 0.6990 ≈ 1.86."],"check":"5<sup>1<\/sup> = 5 and 5<sup>2<\/sup> = 25, so <i>x<\/i> should be between 1 and 2, closer to 2 ✓","wrong":{"About 0.54":"Divides the other way: log 5 ÷ log 20.","About 3.00":"Doesn't match: 5<sup>3<\/sup> = 125.","4":"That's 20 ÷ 5."}},"b60":{"ask":"When does 100 g decay to 25 g?","idea":"The formula halves the amount every 8 hours (the half-life). 25 is one-quarter of 100, which takes two halvings.","steps":["25 = 100(1/2)<sup><i>t<\/i>/8<\/sup>, so (1/2)<sup><i>t<\/i>/8<\/sup> = 1/4 = (1/2)<sup>2<\/sup>.","<i>t<\/i>/8 = 2, so <i>t<\/i> = 16 hours."],"check":"100 → 50 (8 h) → 25 (16 h) ✓","wrong":{"32 hours":"That's 4 halvings, leaving 6.25 g.","8 hours":"That's 1 halving, leaving 50 g.","4 hours":"Less than one half-life; about 71 g would remain."}},"b61":{"ask":"What comes after 4, 7, 10?","idea":"An arithmetic sequence adds the same number (the common difference) each time.","steps":["Difference: 7 − 4 = 3.","Next: 10 + 3 = 13."],"check":"4, 7, 10, 13: each step is +3 ✓","wrong":{"3":"That's the common difference, not the next term.","12":"Adds 2 instead of 3.","14":"Adds 4 instead of 3."}},"b62":{"ask":"What comes after 3, 6, 12?","idea":"A geometric sequence multiplies by the same number (the common ratio) each time. Check: 6 ÷ 3 = 2 and 12 ÷ 6 = 2.","steps":["Ratio: 2.","Next: 12 × 2 = 24."],"check":"3, 6, 12, 24: each is doubled ✓","wrong":{"15":"Adds 3.","18":"Adds 6, treating it as arithmetic; the differences (3, then 6) aren't constant.","36":"Multiplies by 3."}},"b63":{"ask":"What is added each step in 20, 15, 10?","idea":"The common difference is next term − previous term. When the terms go down, it's negative.","steps":["15 − 20 = −5."],"check":"20 + (−5) = 15 and 15 + (−5) = 10 ✓","wrong":{"−10":"That's two steps.","5":"The terms decrease, so the difference is negative.","10":"Not a difference between consecutive terms."}},"b64":{"ask":"Find the 10th term of 2, 5, 8, …","idea":"The <i>n<\/i>th term is <i>a<\/i><sub>1<\/sub> + (<i>n<\/i> − 1)<i>d<\/i>. From term 1 to term 10 there are only 9 steps.","steps":["<i>a<\/i><sub>10<\/sub> = 2 + 9 × 3 = 29."],"check":"Terms: 2, 5, 8, 11, 14, 17, 20, 23, 26, 29 ✓","wrong":{"27":"Uses 8 steps.","30":"Multiplies 10 × 3 without starting from 2.","32":"Uses 10 steps instead of 9."}},"b65":{"ask":"Find the 6th term of 2, 6, 18, …","idea":"The <i>n<\/i>th term of a geometric sequence is <i>a<\/i><sub>1<\/sub> × <i>r<\/i><sup><i>n<\/i> − 1<\/sup>. From term 1 to term 6 there are 5 multiplications.","steps":["<i>a<\/i><sub>6<\/sub> = 2 × 3<sup>5<\/sup> = 2 × 243 = 486."],"check":"2, 6, 18, 54, 162, 486 ✓","wrong":{"36":"Doesn't match the pattern: the terms are 2, 6, 18, 54, 162, 486.","162":"That's the 5th term.","1,458":"That's the 7th term."}},"b66":{"ask":"Add 1 + 2 + 3 + … + 20.","idea":"Pair the numbers from the ends: 1 + 20, 2 + 19, 3 + 18… Each pair adds to 21, and there are 10 pairs. The formula is <i>n<\/i>(<i>n<\/i> + 1)/2.","steps":["20 × 21 ÷ 2 = 210."],"check":"10 pairs × 21 = 210 ✓","wrong":{"190":"That's the sum to 19.","200":"Multiplies 20 × 10.","420":"Forgets to divide by 2."}},"b67":{"ask":"Add the first 10 terms of 5, 9, 13, …","idea":"The sum of an arithmetic sequence is (number of terms) × (average of first and last term). Find the last term first.","steps":["10th term: 5 + 9 × 4 = 41.","Sum: 10 × (5 + 41) ÷ 2 = 10 × 23 = 230."],"check":"The terms average 23, and 10 of them add to 230 ✓","wrong":{"41":"That's the 10th term, not the sum.","250":"Uses a last term of 45 (10 steps instead of 9).","460":"Forgets to divide by 2."}},"b68":{"ask":"Add the first 6 terms of 3 + 6 + 12 + …","idea":"For a geometric series, <i>S<\/i> = <i>a<\/i><sub>1<\/sub>(<i>r<\/i><sup><i>n<\/i><\/sup> − 1)/(<i>r<\/i> − 1). With only 6 terms, you can also just list them and add.","steps":["Terms: 3, 6, 12, 24, 48, 96.","Sum: 189. (Formula: 3(2<sup>6<\/sup> − 1)/1 = 3 × 63 = 189.)"],"check":"Both methods agree ✓","wrong":{"93":"That's the first 5 terms.","96":"That's the 6th term.","192":"Uses 2<sup>6<\/sup> instead of 2<sup>6<\/sup> − 1."}},"b69":{"ask":"What does 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 + … add up to forever?","idea":"An infinite geometric series has a finite sum when the ratio is between −1 and 1, because the terms shrink toward 0. The sum is <i>a<\/i><sub>1<\/sub>/(1 − <i>r<\/i>).","steps":["Ratio: 1/2.","<i>S<\/i> = 8/(1 − 1/2) = 8/(1/2) = 16."],"check":"Partial sums 8, 12, 14, 15, 15.5, … get closer and closer to 16 ✓","wrong":{"8":"That's only the first term.","14":"That's the first 3 terms.","It has no finite sum":"The ratio 1/2 is less than 1, so the sum is finite."}},"b70":{"ask":"The 3rd term is 11 and the 7th is 27. Find the 20th.","idea":"Between two terms of an arithmetic sequence, the difference in value equals (number of steps) × <i>d<\/i>. Find <i>d<\/i>, then the first term, then the 20th.","steps":["From term 3 to term 7: 4 steps, increase 16, so <i>d<\/i> = 4.","<i>a<\/i><sub>1<\/sub> = 11 − 2 × 4 = 3.","<i>a<\/i><sub>20<\/sub> = 3 + 19 × 4 = 79."],"check":"<i>a<\/i><sub>7<\/sub> = 3 + 6 × 4 = 27 ✓","wrong":{"75":"Uses 18 steps from the first term.","80":"Multiplies 20 × 4 without the first term.","83":"Uses 20 steps instead of 19."}},"b71":{"ask":"Convert 180° to radians.","idea":"Radians measure angles by arc length on a circle of radius 1. Half a turn (180°) is half the circumference 2π, which is π.","steps":["180° = π radians."],"check":"360° = 2π, so half of that is π ✓","wrong":{"2π":"That's 360°.","π/2":"That's 90°.","180π":"Multiplies instead of converting."}},"b72":{"ask":"What is sin 90°?","idea":"On the unit circle (radius 1), the point at angle θ is (cos θ, sin θ). At 90°, the point is straight up: (0, 1).","steps":["At 90°: (0, 1).","sin 90° = the <i>y<\/i>-coordinate = 1."],"check":"The sine graph peaks at 90° ✓","wrong":{"−1":"That's sin 270°.","0":"That's cos 90° (the <i>x<\/i>-coordinate).","90":"That's the angle, not its sine."}},"b73":{"ask":"Convert 60° to radians.","idea":"Degrees to radians: multiply by π/180, because 180° = π.","steps":["60 × π/180 = π/3."],"check":"Three 60° angles make 180° = π ✓","wrong":{"3π":"Divides 180 by 60 and puts it on the wrong side.","π/6":"That's 30°.","60π":"Multiplies by π without dividing by 180."}},"b74":{"ask":"What is cos(π/3)?","idea":"Convert to degrees: π/3 = 60°. On the unit circle, the 60° point is (1/2, √3/2), and cosine is the <i>x<\/i>-coordinate.","steps":["π/3 = 60°.","cos 60° = 1/2."],"check":"In a 30-60-90 triangle with hypotenuse 2, the side next to 60° is 1: cos 60° = 1/2 ✓","wrong":{"√3/2":"That's sin 60° (or cos 30°).","√2/2":"That's cos 45°.","1":"That's cos 0°."}},"b75":{"ask":"Convert 5π/6 radians to degrees.","idea":"Radians to degrees: replace π with 180°.","steps":["5 × 180 ÷ 6 = 900 ÷ 6 = 150°."],"check":"π/6 = 30°, and 5 × 30 = 150 ✓","wrong":{"75°":"Uses 90° for π.","216°":"Uses 6 × 180 ÷ 5.","300°":"Uses 360° for π."}},"b76":{"ask":"What is sin 210°?","idea":"Find the reference angle (the acute angle to the <i>x<\/i>-axis) and the quadrant. 210° is 30° past 180°, in Quadrant III, where both sine and cosine are negative.","steps":["Reference angle: 210 − 180 = 30°. sin 30° = 1/2.","Quadrant III: sine is negative.","sin 210° = −1/2."],"check":"The unit-circle point at 210° is (−√3/2, −1/2) ✓","wrong":{"−√3/2":"That's cos 210°.","√3/2":"That's cos 30°, with the wrong sign too.","1/2":"Right size, but sine is negative in Quadrant III."}},"b77":{"ask":"sin θ = 3/5 in Quadrant II. Find cos θ.","idea":"Use sin<sup>2<\/sup> θ + cos<sup>2<\/sup> θ = 1 (the Pythagorean identity) to find the size of cos θ, then use the quadrant for its sign. In Quadrant II, <i>x<\/i> is negative, so cosine is negative.","steps":["cos<sup>2<\/sup> θ = 1 − 9/25 = 16/25, so cos θ = ±4/5.","Quadrant II: negative. cos θ = −4/5."],"check":"3-4-5 right triangle, flipped to the left side ✓","wrong":{"−3/5":"That's −sin θ.","3/4":"That's the size of tan θ, and its sign is wrong.","4/5":"Right size, but cosine is negative in Quadrant II."}},"b78":{"ask":"How long does <i>y<\/i> = sin(2<i>x<\/i>) take to repeat?","idea":"sin <i>x<\/i> repeats every 2π. Multiplying <i>x<\/i> by <i>b<\/i> speeds it up, so it repeats <i>b<\/i> times as often: period = 2π/<i>b<\/i>.","steps":["<i>b<\/i> = 2.","Period: 2π/2 = π."],"check":"sin(2<i>x<\/i>) completes a full cycle as <i>x<\/i> goes from 0 to π ✓","wrong":{"π/2":"Divides by 4.","4π":"Multiplies by 2 instead of dividing.","2π":"That's the period of sin <i>x<\/i>, without the 2."}},"b79":{"ask":"How tall are the waves of <i>y<\/i> = −3 cos <i>x<\/i> + 1?","idea":"The amplitude is the distance from the middle line to a peak. It's the absolute value of the number in front of cos: always positive. The negative only flips the graph, and the + 1 only moves it up.","steps":["Amplitude = |−3| = 3."],"check":"The graph goes from 1 − 3 = −2 to 1 + 3 = 4, which is 3 above and below the middle line <i>y<\/i> = 1 ✓","wrong":{"−3":"Amplitude is a distance, so it's never negative.","1":"That's the vertical shift.","4":"That's the maximum value, 3 + 1."}},"b80":{"ask":"Find the arc length for a 3π/4 radian angle on a circle with radius 4.","idea":"With radians, arc length = radius × angle (<i>s<\/i> = <i>r<\/i>θ). That's why radians are useful: no fraction of 360 needed.","steps":["<i>s<\/i> = 4 × 3π/4 = 3π."],"check":"In degrees: 135° is 3/8 of the circle, and 3/8 × 2π(4) = 3π ✓","wrong":{"12π":"Forgets to divide by 4.","3π/16":"Divides by 4 instead of multiplying.","16π/3":"Doesn't match: <i>s<\/i> = <i>r<\/i>θ = 4 × 3π/4 = 3π."}},"sa1":{"ask":"Count the students with 2, 3 or 4 pets.","idea":"In a dot plot, each dot stands for one data value, here one student. “2 or more” means 2, 3 and 4 all count.","steps":["There are 3 dots above 2.","There is 1 dot above 3 and 1 above 4: 2 in all.","3 + 2 = 5 students have 2 or more pets."],"check":"The 12 students split into 7 with fewer than 2 pets and 5 with 2 or more: 7 + 5 = 12 ✓","wrong":{"3":"Counts only the students with exactly 2 pets.","4":"That's the number of students with 1 pet.","7":"That's the number with fewer than 2 pets (0 or 1)."}},"sa2":{"ask":"Compare two bars by subtracting.","idea":"Read each bar's height on the vertical scale. “How many more” means subtract the smaller from the larger.","steps":["The grape bar reaches 12.","The banana bar reaches 5.","12 − 5 = 7 more students chose grapes."],"check":"5 + 7 = 12 ✓","wrong":{"12":"That's the grape total, not the difference.","5":"That's the banana total.","17":"Adds the two bars instead of subtracting."}},"sa3":{"ask":"Find the total number of students in a frequency table.","idea":"A frequency is a count: how many times a value appears. Adding all the frequencies gives the total number of data values, here students.","steps":["Each number counts the students who gave that answer.","4 + 9 + 12 + 5 = 30 students."],"check":"No student is counted twice, and every student is in one column ✓","wrong":{"12":"That's the largest frequency, the most common answer.","4":"That's only the 6-hour count.","34":"Adds the hours too, or miscounts. Check: 4 + 9 + 12 + 5 = 30."}},"sa4":{"ask":"Find which bar of a histogram contains the middle score.","idea":"The median is the middle value when data are in order. In a histogram, count up the bars from the left until you reach that position.","steps":["3 + 7 + 10 + 5 = 25 scores.","The median is the 13th score.","Scores 11 to 20 are in 80–89, so the 13th is there."],"check":"12 scores are below the 13th and 12 are above it ✓","wrong":{"70–79":"That bar only reaches the 10th score.","90–99":"That's the last bar; it holds the 21st to 25th scores.","60–69":"That's the lowest interval, with the 1st to 3rd scores."}},"sa5":{"ask":"Read a stem-and-leaf plot and find the most common value.","idea":"Each leaf joins its stem to make one value. The mode is the value that appears most often, so look for a repeated leaf.","steps":["It stands for 30, 31, 34, 34 and 37.","34 appears twice, more than any other age."],"check":"No other age repeats ✓","wrong":{"33":"That's the mean: 330 ÷ 10.","32.5":"That's the median, the average of 31 and 34.","4":"That's a leaf digit, not an age."}},"sa6":{"ask":"Turn a count into a percent of the whole group.","idea":"A relative frequency is part ÷ whole. Multiply by 100 to make it a percent.","steps":["14 ÷ 40 = 0.35.","0.35 = 35%."],"check":"35% of 40 = 0.35 × 40 = 14 ✓","wrong":{"14%":"Uses the count as a percent; it's 14 out of 40, not out of 100.","26%":"26 is the number who don't walk, and it's not out of 100 either.","40%":"That's the number surveyed."}},"sa7":{"ask":"Describe the shape of a distribution.","idea":"Skew is named for the direction of the long tail, not the pile of data. A pile on the left with a tail to the right is skewed right.","steps":["Most dots are at 1 and 2 hours, on the left.","The tail stretches right, to 5 and 7 hours."],"check":"The single 7 sits far to the right of the pile ✓","wrong":{"Skewed left":"Skewed left would have the tail stretching toward small values.","Symmetric":"The two sides don't mirror each other.","Uniform":"Uniform data have about equal stacks everywhere."}},"sa8":{"ask":"Decide what a histogram does and doesn't tell you.","idea":"A histogram shows how many values fall in each interval, not the values themselves. Statements about exact values can't be confirmed from it.","steps":["10 + 5 = 15 students scored 80 or higher.","No: a bar only counts scores in an interval, so exact values are unknown."],"check":"Only the statement built from bar counts is guaranteed ✓","wrong":{"The highest score was 99.":"The top score is somewhere from 90 to 99; the histogram doesn't say which.","The mean score was 85.":"You can't compute an exact mean without the exact scores.","Exactly 7 students scored 75.":"7 students scored somewhere from 70 to 79, not necessarily 75."}},"sa9":{"ask":"Read past a misleading graph to the actual numbers.","idea":"When a graph's axis doesn't start at 0, bar heights exaggerate differences. Compare the actual values, not the bar sizes.","steps":["96 − 92 = 4.","4 ÷ 92 ≈ 0.043, about 4%."],"check":"92 × 1.04 ≈ 95.7, close to 96 ✓","wrong":{"Brand B's value is about 3 times Brand A's.":"That's how the bars look, not the values: 96 isn't 3 times 92.","Brand B's value is about 30% greater than Brand A's.":"30% more than 92 would be about 120.","Brand A's value is about half of Brand B's.":"Half of 96 is 48, not 92."}},"sa10":{"ask":"Find the percent of a group that meets a condition.","idea":"“At least 2” includes 2 itself and everything above it. Count those students, then divide by the total.","steps":["7 + 2 + 1 = 10 students read at least 2 books.","10 ÷ 20 = 0.5, which is 50%."],"check":"The other 10 students read 0 or 1 book: 4 + 6 = 10 ✓","wrong":{"35%":"Counts only the 7 students who read exactly 2.","45%":"Leaves out one group; at least 2 includes 2, 3 and 4.","60%":"Doesn't match: 60% of 20 is 12 students."}},"sa11":{"ask":"Find the average of four numbers.","idea":"The mean shares the total equally among all the values: add them up, then divide by how many there are.","steps":["6 + 9 + 10 + 15 = 40.","40 ÷ 4 = 10."],"check":"4 × 10 = 40 ✓","wrong":{"9.5":"That's the median, the average of 9 and 10.","15":"That's the largest value.","40":"That's the total; divide by 4."}},"sa12":{"ask":"Find the middle value of a list.","idea":"The median is the middle value after putting the data in order. Skipping the ordering step is the most common mistake.","steps":["In order: 3, 5, 7, 8, 14.","The 3rd value is 7."],"check":"Two values are below 7 and two are above ✓","wrong":{"7.4":"That's the mean: 37 ÷ 5.","5":"That's the middle of the list before ordering.","14":"That's the largest value."}},"sa13":{"ask":"Find the most common value.","idea":"The mode is the value that appears most often. A data set can have one mode, several, or none.","steps":["9 appears 3 times.","9 is the mode."],"check":"6 appears twice; every other value once ✓","wrong":{"6":"6 appears twice, but 9 appears three times.","8":"That's the median, the average of 7 and 9.","7.5":"That's the mean: 60 ÷ 8."}},"sa14":{"ask":"Find a missing value from the mean.","idea":"Work with totals: total = mean × number of values. The missing value is whatever brings the total up to that amount.","steps":["84 × 5 = 420.","80 + 88 + 79 + 90 = 337.","420 − 337 = 83."],"check":"(337 + 83) ÷ 5 = 420 ÷ 5 = 84 ✓","wrong":{"84":"That's the mean; the fifth score needn't equal it.","85":"Doesn't work: (337 + 85) ÷ 5 = 84.4.","337":"That's the total of the first four scores."}},"sa15":{"ask":"Find a mean from a frequency table.","idea":"Each value counts as many times as its frequency. Multiply each value by its frequency, add, then divide by the total number of students (not the number of columns).","steps":["2 × 8 = 16 siblings.","0 + 6 + 16 + 9 = 31 siblings.","31 ÷ 20 = 1.55."],"check":"1.55 is between 1 and 2, where most students are ✓","wrong":{"1.5":"Averages the values 0, 1, 2, 3 without weighting them by frequency.","2":"That's the mode, the most common answer.","7.75":"Divides by 4 columns instead of 20 students."}},"sa16":{"ask":"Choose the best measure of a typical value when there's an outlier.","idea":"The mean uses every value, so one extreme value can pull it far from the rest. The median depends only on the middle, so it resists outliers.","steps":["The median is $35,000.","333 ÷ 5 = 66.6, or $66,600.","The median; the outlier distorts the mean."],"check":"4 of the 5 salaries are within $5,000 of the median ✓","wrong":{"The mean, $66,600":"The outlier pulls the mean above 4 of the 5 salaries.","The highest salary, $200,000":"That's the outlier itself.","The range, $170,000":"Range measures spread, not a typical value."}},"sa17":{"ask":"Update a mean when a value is added.","idea":"Means don't combine by averaging the old mean with the new value. Use totals: new mean = (old total + new value) ÷ new count.","steps":["4 × 12 = 48.","48 + 22 = 70.","70 ÷ 5 = 14."],"check":"The 22 raises the mean, but only a little, because it's 1 of 5 values ✓","wrong":{"17":"Averages 12 and 22, treating the new value like half the data.","12":"The new value is larger than the mean, so the mean must go up.","13":"Doesn't match: 70 ÷ 5 = 14."}},"sa18":{"ask":"Combine the means of two groups of different sizes.","idea":"A bigger group counts more. Convert each mean to a total, add the totals, then divide by everyone.","steps":["20 × 75 = 1,500.","30 × 80 = 2,400.","3,900 ÷ 50 = 78."],"check":"78 is closer to 80 because Class B is bigger ✓","wrong":{"77.5":"Averages 75 and 80 as if the classes were the same size.","80":"That's Class B's mean alone.","75":"That's Class A's mean alone."}},"sa19":{"ask":"Choose a new value that sets the median.","idea":"The median is decided by position. Think about how many values must sit below and above the target, and where the new value has to go.","steps":["The median is the 3rd number.","x must be 9 or more, so that 9 stays in the middle.","12: the list 2, 5, 9, 11, 12 has median 9."],"check":"x = 7 gives 2, 5, 7, 9, 11, with median 7, which shows why x must be at least 9 ✓","wrong":{"5":"Gives 2, 5, 5, 9, 11, with median 5.","7":"Gives 2, 5, 7, 9, 11, with median 7.","3":"Gives 2, 3, 5, 9, 11, with median 5."}},"sa20":{"ask":"Track how changing every value changes the mean.","idea":"Whatever you do to every value happens to the mean too: multiply all values by 2 and the mean doubles; add 3 to all and the mean goes up 3.","steps":["The mean doubles to 20.","The mean rises by 3 to 23."],"check":"Try 8, 10, 12 (mean 10): they become 19, 23, 27, with mean 23 ✓","wrong":{"20":"Doubles but forgets to add 3.","13":"Adds 3 but forgets to double.","26":"Adds 3 first, then doubles: (10 + 3) × 2."}},"sa21":{"ask":"Find how spread out the data are from end to end.","idea":"The range measures spread: the distance from the smallest value to the largest.","steps":["The largest is 20.","The smallest is 7.","20 − 7 = 13."],"check":"Every value lies between 7 and 20 ✓","wrong":{"20":"That's the largest value, not the range.","12.6":"That's the mean, a measure of center.","5":"Subtracts two neighboring values instead of the largest and smallest."}},"sa22":{"ask":"Read the median from a box plot.","idea":"A box plot shows five numbers: minimum, first quartile (Q1, left edge of the box), median (line inside), third quartile (Q3, right edge) and maximum.","steps":["The line inside the box is the median.","The median is 25 minutes."],"check":"Half the students took 25 minutes or less ✓","wrong":{"18 minutes":"That's Q1, the left edge of the box.","32 minutes":"That's Q3, the right edge of the box.","30 minutes":"That's the range, 40 − 10."}},"sa23":{"ask":"Find the spread of the middle half of the data.","idea":"The interquartile range is the width of the box: Q3 − Q1. Unlike the range, it isn't affected by extreme values at the ends.","steps":["Q3 = 26.","Q1 = 12.","26 − 12 = 14."],"check":"The middle 50% of games had between 12 and 26 points ✓","wrong":{"30":"That's the range, 35 − 5.","20":"That's the median.","23":"Subtracts Q1 from the maximum: 35 − 12."}},"sa24":{"ask":"Find quartiles from a list, then the IQR.","idea":"Q1 is the median of the lower half and Q3 is the median of the upper half. Then IQR = Q3 − Q1.","steps":["Q1 = 4.5.","Q3 = 11.","11 − 4.5 = 6.5."],"check":"Half the values (5, 7, 8, 10) lie between 4.5 and 11 ✓","wrong":{"13":"That's the range, 15 − 2.","11":"That's Q3 alone.","4.5":"That's Q1 alone."}},"sa25":{"ask":"Use the 1.5 × IQR rule to spot an outlier.","idea":"The rule builds “fences”: Q1 − 1.5 × IQR and Q3 + 1.5 × IQR. Values outside the fences are outliers.","steps":["IQR = 10.","1.5 × 10 = 15.","30 + 15 = 45, so values above 45 are outliers."],"check":"The lower fence is 20 − 15 = 5, so 12 is fine ✓","wrong":{"44":"Just inside the upper fence of 45.","31":"Barely above Q3, well inside the fence.","12":"Above the lower fence of 5, so not an outlier."}},"sa26":{"ask":"Compare the spread of two box plots.","idea":"A narrower box means the middle half of the data is closer together, so the data are more consistent. Compare medians for center and box widths for spread.","steps":["Class A: 80 − 72 = 8.","Class B: 85 − 65 = 20.","Class A, with an IQR of 8."],"check":"Class A's range is 30 and Class B's is 40 ✓","wrong":{"Class B has a higher median score than Class A.":"Both medians are 76.","Class A has a greater range than Class B.":"Class A's range is 30; Class B's is 40.","Class B's scores are more consistent than Class A's.":"Class B's box is wider, so its scores are less consistent."}},"sa27":{"ask":"Find the third quartile of a list with an odd number of values.","idea":"Q3 is the median of the upper half. With an odd number of values, the overall median is left out of both halves.","steps":["The median is 9.","The upper half is 11, 14, 18.","Q3 = 14."],"check":"Q1 is 6, so the middle half runs from 6 to 14 ✓","wrong":{"11":"That's the first value of the upper half, not its median.","12.5":"Includes the median 9 in the upper half, then averages 11 and 14.","18":"That's the maximum."}},"sa28":{"ask":"Compare spread without calculating every standard deviation.","idea":"Standard deviation grows when values sit far from the mean. Look for the set whose values stray farthest from the center.","steps":["It measures typical distance from the mean.","19 is 9 away from the mean, and so is 1.","No: 4 and 16 are only 6 away."],"check":"The standard deviations are about 6.4, 0, 2.2 and 4.5 ✓","wrong":{"{10, 10, 10, 10}":"Every value equals the mean, so its standard deviation is 0, the smallest possible.","{7, 9, 11, 13}":"Its values are at most 3 from the mean.","{4, 8, 12, 16}":"Its values are at most 6 from the mean, closer than the 9 in the correct set."}},"sa29":{"ask":"Understand how shifting data affects center and spread.","idea":"Adding a constant slides the data along the number line: center measures move, but spread measures (range, IQR, standard deviation) stay the same.","steps":["The mean goes up by 5.","Both ends move up by 5, so the range is unchanged."],"check":"2, 4, 9 (mean 5, range 7) become 7, 9, 14 (mean 10, range 7) ✓","wrong":{"Both the mean and the range increase by 5.":"Both ends of the data rise by 5, so the range doesn't change.","The mean stays the same, and the range increases by 5.":"This reverses the two effects.","Neither the mean nor the range changes.":"Every value is larger, so the mean must be larger."}},"sa30":{"ask":"Use a box plot's quartiles to estimate a percent.","idea":"The five numbers of a box plot split the data into four parts with about 25% each, no matter how long or short each part looks.","steps":["55 is Q1.","Each section holds about 25% of the data.","3 sections × 25% = 75%."],"check":"The remaining 25% are from 40 to 55 ✓","wrong":{"50%":"That's the percent between Q1 and Q3, the box alone.","25%":"That's a single section, like 40 to 55.","100%":"The 25% of scores below 55 aren't included."}},"sa31":{"ask":"Read one cell of a two-way table.","idea":"A two-way table sorts people by two categories at once. Each inner cell is the count for one row and one column together.","steps":["Use the 10th grade row.","That cell is 50."],"check":"45 + 50 = 95, matching the column total ✓","wrong":{"45":"That's 9th graders who play a sport.","70":"That's all 10th graders.","95":"That's everyone who plays a sport, in both grades."}},"sa32":{"ask":"Find the grand total of a two-way table.","idea":"In a two-way table, the row totals and the column totals both add up to the same grand total, which is a good way to check your reading.","steps":["The bottom-right cell.","150 students."],"check":"95 + 55 = 150 ✓","wrong":{"95":"That's the number who play a sport.","80":"That's the number of 9th graders.","145":"Doesn't match: 80 + 70 = 150."}},"sa33":{"ask":"Find a fraction of the whole group.","idea":"“Of all students” means the denominator is the grand total. The numerator is the column total for “Doesn't play.”","steps":["55 students.","55/150 = 11/30."],"check":"11/30 of 150 = 55 ✓","wrong":{"7/16":"That's 35/80, the fraction of 9th graders who don't play.","2/7":"That's 20/70, the fraction of 10th graders who don't play.","11/19":"That's 55/95, which divides by the wrong total."}},"sa34":{"ask":"Find a percent within one row of a two-way table.","idea":"“Of 9th graders” means only the 9th grade row counts, so divide by the 9th grade total. Choosing the right denominator is the key skill.","steps":["80 ninth graders.","45.","45 ÷ 80 = 56.25%."],"check":"35 ÷ 80 = 43.75% don't play, and 56.25 + 43.75 = 100 ✓","wrong":{"30%":"That's 45/150, dividing by all students.","47.4%":"That's 45/95, dividing by all sport players.","64.3%":"That's 45/70, dividing by the 10th grade total."}},"sa35":{"ask":"Find a fraction within one column of a two-way table.","idea":"“Of the students who play a sport” makes the sport column the whole. Compare with “What fraction of 10th graders play a sport,” which uses the 10th grade row instead.","steps":["95 play a sport.","50.","50/95 = 10/19."],"check":"9/19 are 9th graders, and 10/19 + 9/19 = 1 ✓","wrong":{"5/7":"That's 50/70: the fraction of 10th graders who play, the reverse question.","1/3":"That's 50/150, dividing by everyone.","9/19":"That's the fraction in 9th grade."}},"sa36":{"ask":"Fill in a missing cell of a two-way table.","idea":"Every row and column must add to its total. Sketch the table, fill in what you know, and subtract to find the gaps.","steps":["60 − 28 = 32 non-adults.","36 − 20 = 16.","32 − 16 = 16."],"check":"Adults: 20 summer + 8 winter = 28; non-adults: 16 + 16 = 32; summer: 20 + 16 = 36 ✓","wrong":{"8":"That's the number of adults who prefer winter.","12":"Doesn't fit the totals: non-adults would add to 28, not 32.","24":"That's everyone who prefers winter (60 − 36)."}},"sa37":{"ask":"Compare two groups of different sizes fairly.","idea":"When groups differ in size, compare percents within each group, not raw counts.","steps":["50 ÷ 70 ≈ 71%.","45 ÷ 80 ≈ 56%.","Compare percents: 71% > 56%."],"check":"71% − 56% = a 15-point difference ✓","wrong":{"Yes, because 50 is greater than 45.":"The right conclusion for the wrong reason: comparing counts ignores that the groups are different sizes.","No, because more 9th graders were surveyed.":"Group size doesn't decide likelihood; percents do.","No, because the percents are about the same.":"71% and 56% differ by about 15 points."}},"sa38":{"ask":"Decide whether two categorical variables are associated.","idea":"If the percent in a category is about the same in every row, the variables aren't associated. If it changes noticeably from row to row, they are.","steps":["Compare row percents: about 56% vs. 71%.","They differ, so there is an association."],"check":"If there were no association, both grades would have about the same 63% (95/150) playing ✓","wrong":{"No, because both grades have more players than non-players.":"Both having more players doesn't make the rates equal.","No, because the grades have different numbers of students.":"Different group sizes are handled by comparing percents.","Yes, because more students play a sport than don't.":"That describes one variable alone, not a relationship between two."}},"sa39":{"ask":"Build a two-way table from percents.","idea":"Each percent applies to a specific group. Turn percents into counts group by group, then add the counts.","steps":["0.6 × 200 = 120.","0.25 × 120 = 30.","0.5 × 80 = 40.","30 + 40 = 70."],"check":"70 of 200 is 35% overall, between the two group rates of 25% and 50% ✓","wrong":{"30":"Counts only the car owners with bikes.","40":"Counts only the non-owners with bikes.","75":"Averages 25% and 50% (37.5%) for everyone, but the groups are different sizes."}},"sa40":{"ask":"Find a joint percent: one cell compared with the grand total.","idea":"“Of all students” means the denominator is 150, even though the count comes from one row and one column.","steps":["35 students.","35 ÷ 150 ≈ 23.3%."],"check":"The four inner cells as percents add to 100%: 30 + 23.3 + 33.3 + 13.3 ✓","wrong":{"43.8%":"That's 35/80, the percent of 9th graders, not of all students.","63.6%":"That's 35/55, the percent of non-players.","53.3%":"That's 80/150, all 9th graders."}},"sa41":{"ask":"Describe the direction of a scatterplot.","idea":"Positive: as one variable increases, the other tends to increase. Negative: as one increases, the other tends to decrease. No association: no clear pattern.","steps":["They tend to go up.","Both increase together: positive."],"check":"Points don't fall exactly on a line, but the trend is clearly upward ✓","wrong":{"Negative":"Negative would slope down from left to right.","No association":"There's a clear upward pattern.","Perfectly linear":"The points scatter around a line rather than lying exactly on it."}},"sa42":{"ask":"Identify a negative association from a description.","idea":"When two quantities move in opposite directions, the association is negative.","steps":["Sales go down.","Opposite directions: negative."],"check":"On a scatterplot, the points would slope down from left to right ✓","wrong":{"Positive":"Positive means both go up together.","No association":"There is a clear pattern: warmer days, fewer sales.","Positive, because both change every day":"Changing every day doesn't make it positive; what matters is that they move in opposite directions."}},"sa43":{"ask":"Make a prediction from a line of best fit.","idea":"To predict, substitute the <i>x<\/i>-value into the equation of the line. The result is the predicted <i>y<\/i>.","steps":["3 × 10 = 30.","30 + 50 = 80."],"check":"At <i>x<\/i> = 0 the line predicts 50, and each step adds 3: 50 + 10 × 3 = 80 ✓","wrong":{"53":"Uses <i>x<\/i> = 1 instead of 10.","60":"Adds 10 to 50 instead of 3 × 10.","350":"Doesn't match: 3(10) + 50 = 30 + 50 = 80."}},"sa44":{"ask":"Interpret the slope of a line of best fit.","idea":"In <i>y<\/i> = <i>mx<\/i> + <i>b<\/i>, the slope <i>m<\/i> is the predicted change in <i>y<\/i> per one unit of <i>x<\/i>, and <i>b<\/i> is the prediction at <i>x<\/i> = 0. Always state units.","steps":["1.5 multiplies x, so it's the slope.","1.5 cm of growth per week."],"check":"Week 0: 20 cm. Week 1: 21.5 cm. Week 2: 23 cm ✓","wrong":{"The plant was 1.5 cm tall at the start.":"The starting height is 20 cm, the y-intercept.","The plant grows 20 cm each week.":"20 is the starting height, not the weekly growth.","The plant will be 1.5 cm tall after 20 weeks.":"The model gives 1.5(20) + 20 = 50 cm at 20 weeks."}},"sa45":{"ask":"Match a scatterplot to its correlation coefficient.","idea":"The sign of <i>r<\/i> gives the direction, and its size gives the strength: near ±1 is strong, near 0 is weak or none.","steps":["Downward: r is negative.","Strong: r is close to −1."],"check":"−0.95 is negative and close to −1 ✓","wrong":{"−0.2":"Negative, but −0.2 would mean a weak pattern with lots of scatter.","0.95":"Strong, but positive; the points slope downward.","0":"0 means no linear pattern."}},"sa46":{"ask":"Find how far a point is from the line of best fit.","idea":"A residual is actual − predicted. Positive: the point is above the line. Negative: below.","steps":["2(5) + 10 = 20.","23 − 20 = 3."],"check":"The point is 3 units above the line ✓","wrong":{"−3":"Subtracts in the wrong order (predicted − actual).","20":"That's the predicted value.","23":"That's the actual value."}},"sa47":{"ask":"Find the slope of a line of best fit from two points.","idea":"Slope = rise ÷ run between two points on the line. In context, it's the predicted change in <i>y<\/i> per one unit of <i>x<\/i>.","steps":["90 − 60 = 30.","8 − 2 = 6.","30 ÷ 6 = 5."],"check":"From (2, 60), adding 6 hours × 5 points = 30 gives (8, 90) ✓","wrong":{"30":"That's the change in y; divide by the change in x.","6":"That's the change in x.","15":"Divides by 2 instead of 6."}},"sa48":{"ask":"Tell association apart from cause and effect.","idea":"Correlation is not causation. Two things can rise together because a third factor (a lurking variable) drives both.","steps":["Yes, they're associated.","Hot weather is a likely third factor."],"check":"On cold days, both ice cream sales and swimmers would drop together ✓","wrong":{"Eating ice cream causes people to have trouble swimming.":"An association alone can't show cause.","Lifeguard rescues cause people to buy ice cream.":"Same problem, in the other direction.","There is no relationship between the two.":"The data do show a relationship: they rise together."}},"sa49":{"ask":"Recognize when a prediction goes too far beyond the data.","idea":"A line of best fit describes the trend within the data. Predicting far outside that range (extrapolation) can give absurd results.","steps":["0.5 × 120 + 2 = 62 feet.","No: 120 is far outside the data.","No: extrapolating that far is unreliable."],"check":"At 20 months the model gives 12 feet, already unrealistic for a child ✓","wrong":{"The line must be wrong, because it gives a negative value.":"The prediction is 62, a positive value.","The prediction is reliable because it comes from the line of best fit.":"A model is only reliable near the data it was built from.","Children grow 62 feet every 120 months.":"That misreads the prediction as a growth rate."}},"sa50":{"ask":"Predict how an outlier affects correlation.","idea":"Points far from the overall pattern pull <i>r<\/i> toward 0. Removing such a point makes the remaining pattern look stronger.","steps":["No, it breaks the pattern.","Yes: r moves closer to 1."],"check":"The pattern is positive, so a stronger correlation means closer to +1 ✓","wrong":{"It will move closer to 0.":"That would happen if the removed point fit the pattern well.","It will become negative.":"The remaining points still trend upward.","It will not change.":"An outlier far from the pattern affects r noticeably."}},"sb1":{"ask":"Find the probability of an event on one roll.","idea":"When outcomes are equally likely, probability = (number of favorable outcomes) ÷ (total number of outcomes).","steps":["Two outcomes: 5 and 6.","Six outcomes.","2/6 = 1/3."],"check":"The probability of 4 or less is 4/6 = 2/3, and 1/3 + 2/3 = 1 ✓","wrong":{"1/2":"That's numbers greater than 3 (4, 5, 6).","2/3":"That's the probability of 4 or less.","1/6":"That's rolling one specific number."}},"sb2":{"ask":"Find the probability of drawing one color.","idea":"Every marble is equally likely, so divide the number of blue marbles by the total number of marbles, including every color.","steps":["4 + 6 + 10 = 20.","6/20 = 3/10."],"check":"Red 4/20 + blue 6/20 + green 10/20 = 1 ✓","wrong":{"1/3":"Treats the three colors as equally likely, but there are different numbers of each.","3/7":"That's 6/14, which leaves the green marbles out of the total.","3/5":"That's 6/10, comparing blue with green instead of with all marbles."}},"sb3":{"ask":"Use the complement of an event.","idea":"The complement of an event is everything else that can happen. P(not A) = 1 − P(A).","steps":["They add to 1.","1 − 0.35 = 0.65."],"check":"0.35 + 0.65 = 1 ✓","wrong":{"0.35":"That's the probability of rain.","1.35":"Adds 1 instead of subtracting; a probability can't exceed 1.","0":"It may well not rain; that has a 65% chance."}},"sb4":{"ask":"List a sample space to find a probability.","idea":"Write out every equally likely outcome (the sample space). HT and TH are different outcomes, even though both have one head.","steps":["Four outcomes.","Two: HT and TH.","2/4 = 1/2."],"check":"P(0 heads) + P(1 head) + P(2 heads) = 1/4 + 1/2 + 1/4 = 1 ✓","wrong":{"1/4":"Counts only HT or only TH, not both.","1/3":"Treats “0, 1 or 2 heads” as equally likely, but they aren't.","3/4":"That's the probability of at least one head."}},"sb5":{"ask":"Count outcomes for two dice.","idea":"Each cube's result counts separately, so (2, 5) and (5, 2) are different outcomes. Sums are not equally likely; 7 has the most ways.","steps":["6 × 6 = 36.","Six pairs.","6/36 = 1/6."],"check":"A sum of 2 has only 1 way, (1,1), with probability 1/36, much less likely ✓","wrong":{"1/12":"Counts only 3 pairs, missing that (2,5) and (5,2) differ.","7/36":"Uses the sum 7 as the number of ways.","1/11":"Treats the 11 possible sums as equally likely."}},"sb6":{"ask":"Tell experimental and theoretical probability apart.","idea":"Theoretical probability comes from the setup (1 of 4 equal sections = 1/4). Experimental probability comes from actual results (18 of 60). They're often close but not equal.","steps":["Experimental probability.","18/60 = 3/10."],"check":"3/10 = 0.30, close to the theoretical 0.25 ✓","wrong":{"1/4":"That's the theoretical probability, from the spinner's design.","3/7":"That's 18/42, red compared with not red.","1/3":"Doesn't match: 18/60 = 0.3."}},"sb7":{"ask":"Predict how many times an event will happen.","idea":"If an event has probability <i>p<\/i>, then in <i>n<\/i> trials you expect about <i>p<\/i> × <i>n<\/i> occurrences.","steps":["2% = 0.02.","0.02 × 1,500 = 30."],"check":"1% of 1,500 is 15, so 2% is 30 ✓","wrong":{"3":"Uses 0.2% instead of 2%.","300":"Uses 20% instead of 2%.","75":"Uses 5% instead of 2%."}},"sb8":{"ask":"Find a probability using areas.","idea":"When a point lands at random in a region, probability = (favorable area) ÷ (total area).","steps":["10 × 10 = 100.","π × 3<sup>2<\/sup> = 9π.","9π/100 ≈ 0.28."],"check":"The circle covers about 28% of the board, which looks reasonable ✓","wrong":{"3/10":"Compares lengths (radius 3 to side 10), not areas.","6π/100":"Uses the circumference formula, 2πr.","9/100":"Forgets the π in the circle's area."}},"sb9":{"ask":"Convert odds to a probability.","idea":"Odds compare favorable to unfavorable outcomes. Probability compares favorable outcomes to all outcomes, so add both parts to get the total.","steps":["Winning to losing.","3 + 5 = 8.","3/8."],"check":"P(lose) = 5/8, and 3/8 + 5/8 = 1 ✓","wrong":{"3/5":"Treats odds as a probability: it divides by the losses, not the total.","5/8":"That's the probability of losing.","2/5":"Doesn't match: 3 favorable out of 8 total is 3/8."}},"sb10":{"ask":"Use the complement for “at least one.”","idea":"“At least one” includes many cases. It's usually easier to find the one case it excludes (none at all) and subtract from 1.","steps":["8 outcomes.","Just 1: HHH.","1 − 1/8 = 7/8."],"check":"Listing confirms it: every outcome except HHH has a tail ✓","wrong":{"3/8":"That's exactly one tail.","1/2":"That's the chance of a tail on one coin.","1/8":"That's the chance of no tails."}},"sb11":{"ask":"Find the probability of two independent events both happening.","idea":"Events are independent when one doesn't change the chances of the other. Then P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B).","steps":["P(heads) = 1/2.","P(6) = 1/6.","1/2 × 1/6 = 1/12."],"check":"There are 2 × 6 = 12 equally likely outcomes, and only one is heads-6 ✓","wrong":{"2/3":"Adds the probabilities; “and” for independent events means multiply.","1/8":"Doesn't match: 1/2 × 1/6 = 1/12.","7/12":"Adds and then adjusts; for “and,” multiply."}},"sb12":{"ask":"Recognize independent events.","idea":"Two events are independent if one happening doesn't change the probability of the other. Picking without replacement creates dependence.","steps":["No, so the flips are independent.","No, so those draws are dependent."],"check":"The second flip is 1/2 heads whatever the first flip was ✓","wrong":{"Drawing two cards from a deck without putting the first back":"The deck changes after the first draw, so the draws are dependent.","Choosing two students for two roles from the same class, one after the other":"The first student can't be chosen again, so the picks are dependent.","Picking two marbles from a bag without replacement":"Without replacement, the first marble changes the bag."}},"sb13":{"ask":"Find the probability of “or” for events that can't overlap.","idea":"For mutually exclusive events, P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B), because there's no overlap to double-count.","steps":["No: the events are mutually exclusive.","0.2 + 0.3 = 0.5."],"check":"The other colors make up the remaining 0.5 ✓","wrong":{"0.06":"Multiplies; that's for “and” with independent events.","0.1":"Subtracts the probabilities.","0.25":"Averages the two probabilities."}},"sb14":{"ask":"Find a probability for draws without replacement.","idea":"Without replacement, the second draw depends on the first: one marble is gone. Update both the count and the total before multiplying.","steps":["5/8.","4/7.","20/56 = 5/14."],"check":"With replacement it would be (5/8)<sup>2<\/sup> = 25/64, a bit higher ✓","wrong":{"25/64":"Uses 5/8 twice, as if the first marble were put back.","5/8":"That's only the first draw.","1/2":"Doesn't match: 5/8 × 4/7 = 5/14 ≈ 0.36."}},"sb15":{"ask":"Use the addition rule when events overlap.","idea":"P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). Subtracting the overlap avoids counting it twice.","steps":["13 hearts.","4 kings.","13 + 4 − 1 = 16, since the king of hearts was counted twice.","16/52 = 4/13."],"check":"Count directly: 13 hearts plus the 3 other kings = 16 ✓","wrong":{"17/52":"Counts the king of hearts twice.","1/52":"That's the king of hearts alone (“and,” not “or”).","1/4":"That's hearts alone."}},"sb16":{"ask":"Find “at least one” for repeated trials.","idea":"Use the complement: P(at least one) = 1 − P(none). Adding 1/6 + 1/6 double-counts rolling two 6s.","steps":["5/6.","25/36.","11/36."],"check":"Of 36 outcomes, 11 contain a 6: six with a 6 first, plus five more with a 6 only second ✓","wrong":{"1/3":"Adds 1/6 + 1/6, which counts double-6 twice.","1/36":"That's two 6s.","25/36":"That's no 6s."}},"sb17":{"ask":"Combine complements and independence.","idea":"First find each event's complement (works = 1 − breaks), then multiply because the machines are independent.","steps":["0.9.","0.81."],"check":"P(at least one breaks) = 1 − 0.81 = 0.19 ✓","wrong":{"0.8":"Subtracts 0.1 + 0.1 from 1, which isn't how independent events combine.","0.99":"That's 1 − 0.01, the probability that not both break.","0.9":"That's for one machine only."}},"sb18":{"ask":"Test whether two events are independent.","idea":"A and B are independent exactly when P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). Mutually exclusive means P(A and B) = 0, which is different.","steps":["0.2.","0.2.","Independent."],"check":"0.5 + 0.4 − 0.2 = 0.7 ✓","wrong":{"P(A and B) = 0.2, so A and B are mutually exclusive.":"Mutually exclusive events have P(A and B) = 0.","P(A and B) = 0.9, so A and B are independent.":"0.9 is P(A) + P(B); the overlap is 0.2.","P(A and B) = 0.1, so A and B are not independent.":"Doesn't match: 0.5 + 0.4 − 0.7 = 0.2."}},"sb19":{"ask":"Find a probability for three draws without replacement.","idea":"Each pick removes one person, so update the counts each time and multiply.","steps":["6/10.","5/9.","4/8.","120/720 = 1/6."],"check":"Using combinations: C(6,3)/C(10,3) = 20/120 = 1/6 ✓","wrong":{"27/125":"Uses 3/5 each time, as if people could be chosen twice.","1/2":"Doesn't match: the chance shrinks with each pick.","3/10":"Doesn't match: 6/10 × 5/9 × 4/8 = 1/6."}},"sb20":{"ask":"Find the probability of exactly one of two events.","idea":"Split “exactly one” into separate cases that can't overlap, find each, then add.","steps":["0.3 × 0.4 = 0.12.","0.6 × 0.7 = 0.42.","0.54."],"check":"Both: 0.18; neither: 0.28; exactly one: 0.54. Total: 0.18 + 0.28 + 0.54 = 1 ✓","wrong":{"0.18":"That's both happening.","0.9":"Adds P(A) + P(B), which counts the overlap twice.","0.72":"That's at least one (1 − 0.28), which includes both happening."}},"sb21":{"ask":"Find a conditional probability from a two-way table.","idea":"A condition (“given that,” “who eats breakfast”) shrinks the group you're choosing from. Its total becomes the denominator.","steps":["100.","60.","60/100 = 3/5."],"check":"P(under 8 | breakfast) = 40/100, and 60/100 + 40/100 = 1 ✓","wrong":{"3/10":"That's 60/200, dividing by everyone.","2/3":"That's 60/90: breakfast-eaters among good sleepers, the reverse question.","9/20":"That's 90/200, all students who sleep 8+ hours."}},"sb22":{"ask":"See how reversing the condition changes the answer.","idea":"P(breakfast | sleep 8+) and P(sleep 8+ | breakfast) use different groups. Read which group the question starts with.","steps":["90.","60.","60/90 = 2/3."],"check":"This is 2/3, while the reverse question gave 3/5: same 60 students, different denominators ✓","wrong":{"3/5":"That's P(sleeps 8+ | eats breakfast), the reverse question.","3/10":"That's 60/200, dividing by everyone.","1/2":"That's 100/200, the share who eat breakfast overall."}},"sb23":{"ask":"Read conditional probability notation.","idea":"P(A | B) = “the probability of A given B.” The event after the bar is what we know happened; it defines the group.","steps":["“Given.”","B."],"check":"In a table, B's row or column total is the denominator ✓","wrong":{"The probability of B, given that A has happened":"That's P(B | A), the reverse.","The probability that A and B both happen":"That's P(A and B).","The probability of A divided by the probability of B":"The bar means “given,” not division."}},"sb24":{"ask":"Pick the right group from a “given” phrase.","idea":"Whatever follows “given that” is the group; its total is the denominator.","steps":["The 110 students who sleep under 8.","70.","70/110 = 7/11."],"check":"7/11 ≈ 0.64 ✓","wrong":{"7/10":"That's 70/100: under-8 sleepers among breakfast-skippers, the reverse question.","7/20":"That's 70/200, dividing by everyone.","11/20":"That's 110/200, all students who sleep under 8 hours."}},"sb25":{"ask":"Use the conditional probability formula.","idea":"P(A | B) asks: of the times B happens, what fraction also have A? That's P(A and B) divided by P(B).","steps":["P(A | B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B).","0.12 ÷ 0.4 = 0.3."],"check":"0.3 × 0.4 = 0.12 ✓","wrong":{"0.048":"Multiplies instead of dividing.","0.52":"Adds the two probabilities.","0.28":"Subtracts the two probabilities."}},"sb26":{"ask":"Test independence with conditional probability.","idea":"A and B are independent if P(A | B) = P(A): knowing B doesn't change A's chances.","steps":["90/200 = 0.45.","60/100 = 0.6.","No: 0.6 ≠ 0.45."],"check":"Also: P(8+ and breakfast) = 0.3, but 0.45 × 0.5 = 0.225 ✓","wrong":{"Yes, because both groups have 100 students.":"Equal group sizes don't show independence.","Yes, because 60 + 30 = 90.":"That's just the column total; it says nothing about independence.","No, because more students sleep under 8 hours.":"The right answer for the wrong reason; independence is about comparing probabilities."}},"sb27":{"ask":"Combine the branches of a probability tree.","idea":"Draw a tree: first branch on bus or not, then late or not. Multiply along each branch, then add the branches that end in “late.”","steps":["0.06.","0.02.","0.08."],"check":"In a school of 1,000: 60 late bus riders + 20 others = 80, which is 8% ✓","wrong":{"0.15":"Adds 10% and 5% without weighting by group size.","0.075":"Averages 10% and 5% as if the groups were equal.","0.06":"Counts only the late bus riders."}},"sb28":{"ask":"Reverse a conditional probability.","idea":"To find P(bus | late), restrict to late students: of all late students, what share rode the bus?","steps":["0.06.","0.08.","0.75."],"check":"Of 80 late students in a school of 1,000, 60 rode the bus: 60/80 = 0.75 ✓","wrong":{"0.6":"That's the share of all students who ride the bus.","0.1":"That's P(late | bus), the reverse question.","0.06":"That's P(bus and late), not divided by P(late)."}},"sb29":{"ask":"Understand why a positive test can still mean a low chance of disease.","idea":"When a condition is rare, false positives from the large healthy group can outnumber true positives. Counting a large imaginary group (like 10,000 people) makes this clear.","steps":["100.","90.","495.","90 ÷ 585 ≈ 15%."],"check":"585 positives in total: 90 true, 495 false ✓","wrong":{"About 90%":"That's P(positive | sick), the reverse question.","About 1%":"That's the rate before testing; a positive result raises it.","About 5%":"That's the false-positive rate among healthy people."}},"sb30":{"ask":"Use “given” information to update a probability.","idea":"Once you know what happened first, update the counts to match the new situation and compute directly.","steps":["51.","3.","3/51 = 1/17."],"check":"P(both aces) = 4/52 × 3/51 = 1/221, and dividing by P(first ace) = 1/13 gives 1/17 ✓","wrong":{"1/13":"Ignores that one ace is already gone.","4/51":"That's the chance if the first card was not an ace.","1/221":"That's both cards being aces, without the “given.”"}},"sb31":{"ask":"Count combinations of choices.","idea":"The fundamental counting principle: if one choice has <i>m<\/i> options and another has <i>n<\/i>, together there are <i>m<\/i> × <i>n<\/i> combinations.","steps":["Multiply.","4 × 3 = 12."],"check":"Listing: each shirt makes 3 outfits, and 4 shirts × 3 = 12 ✓","wrong":{"7":"Adds 4 + 3 instead of multiplying.","24":"Doubles the correct count.","16":"Uses 4 × 4."}},"sb32":{"ask":"Count codes when repeats are allowed.","idea":"With repetition allowed, every position has the full set of options, so multiply the same number for each position.","steps":["10.","1,000."],"check":"The codes are 000 through 999, which is 1,000 codes ✓","wrong":{"720":"That's 10 × 9 × 8, which doesn't allow repeated digits.","30":"Adds 10 + 10 + 10.","27":"Uses 3 × 3 × 3."}},"sb33":{"ask":"Count the arrangements of all the items.","idea":"Arranging <i>n<\/i> different items in order gives <i>n<\/i>! (“<i>n<\/i> factorial”) = <i>n<\/i> × (<i>n<\/i> − 1) × … × 1, because each placed item leaves one fewer choice.","steps":["5.","120."],"check":"3 books: 3! = 6 orders (ABC, ACB, BAC, BCA, CAB, CBA), which matches the pattern ✓","wrong":{"25":"Uses 5 × 5.","15":"Adds 5 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 1.","3,125":"That's 5<sup>5<\/sup>, which allows a book to be used more than once."}},"sb34":{"ask":"Count arrangements when order matters (permutations).","idea":"When positions are different, each choice leaves one fewer person: <sub>8<\/sub>P<sub>3<\/sub> = 8 × 7 × 6.","steps":["Yes: it's a permutation.","336."],"check":"Each group of 3 people can fill the roles in 3! = 6 ways, and 56 groups × 6 = 336 ✓","wrong":{"56":"That's choosing 3 people when order doesn't matter (a committee).","512":"That's 8<sup>3<\/sup>, which lets one person hold several roles.","24":"Uses 4 × 3 × 2."}},"sb35":{"ask":"Count groups when order doesn't matter (combinations).","idea":"A combination counts groups, not orders: <sub>8<\/sub>C<sub>3<\/sub> = (8 × 7 × 6) ÷ (3 × 2 × 1).","steps":["No: it's a combination.","336.","56."],"check":"C(8,3) = 56 ✓","wrong":{"336":"That counts different orders as different committees.","24":"Doesn't match: (8 × 7 × 6) ÷ 6 = 56.","11":"Adds 8 + 3."}},"sb36":{"ask":"Count arrangements with repeated letters.","idea":"With repeated items, divide <i>n<\/i>! by the factorial of each repeat count, because swapping identical items doesn't create a new arrangement.","steps":["120.","2 × 2 = 4.","30."],"check":"Fewer than 120 makes sense, since some orders look identical ✓","wrong":{"120":"Treats the repeated letters as different.","60":"Divides by only one repeat.","20":"Doesn't match: 120 ÷ 4 = 30."}},"sb37":{"ask":"Count codes with no repeated digits.","idea":"Without repetition, each position has one fewer option than the one before. Order still matters in a PIN.","steps":["10.","9.","5,040."],"check":"Fewer than the 10,000 codes possible with repeats ✓","wrong":{"10,000":"That allows repeated digits.","210":"That's C(10,4), which ignores order; in a PIN, 1234 and 4321 are different.","24":"That's 4!, the orders of 4 given digits."}},"sb38":{"ask":"Use combinations to find a probability.","idea":"Probability = (favorable groups) ÷ (all possible groups). Count both with combinations when order doesn't matter.","steps":["84.","4.","4/84 = 1/21."],"check":"Step by step: 4/9 × 3/8 × 2/7 = 24/504 = 1/21 ✓","wrong":{"4/9":"That's the chance that just one chosen person is a girl.","64/729":"Uses (4/9)<sup>3<\/sup>, as if a person could be picked more than once.","1/3":"Doesn't match: only 4 of the 84 committees are all girls."}},"sb39":{"ask":"Count “at least one” using the complement.","idea":"Counting “at least one 7” directly means juggling overlapping cases. Count the numbers with no 7 and subtract from the total instead.","steps":["900.","648.","252."],"check":"Direct check: 100 have a 7 first; 80 more have a 7 second but not first (8 × 10); 72 more have a 7 only last (8 × 9). 100 + 80 + 72 = 252 ✓","wrong":{"243":"Doesn't match: 900 − 648 = 252.","271":"That's 1,000 − 729, which counts 000 to 999 instead of 100 to 999.","300":"Adds 100 + 100 + 100, counting numbers with several 7s more than once."}},"sb40":{"ask":"Count arrangements with a “must be together” rule.","idea":"Glue the people who must be together into a block, arrange the blocks, then multiply by the arrangements inside the block.","steps":["4 units.","24.","48."],"check":"Without the rule there are 120 line-ups; 48 have them together and 72 have them apart ✓","wrong":{"120":"Ignores the rule: that's all line-ups.","24":"Forgets that Ana and Ben can switch within the block.","72":"That's the line-ups where they're not together."}},"sb41":{"ask":"Identify a random sampling method.","idea":"Random sampling gives every member of the population the same chance of being picked, which avoids favoring one kind of person.","steps":["Everyone has an equal chance.","Yes, so it's random."],"check":"Early arrivals, volunteers and council members are all special groups ✓","wrong":{"Survey the first 50 students who arrive in the morning":"Early arrivers may differ from others (bus riders, early risers).","Ask for 50 volunteers during lunch":"Volunteers choose themselves, which is often biased.","Survey 50 members of the student council":"Council members aren't typical of all students."}},"sb42":{"ask":"Tell a population from a sample.","idea":"The population is everyone you want to know about. The sample is the smaller group you actually collect data from.","steps":["All 50,000 voters.","The sample."],"check":"A good sample represents the population ✓","wrong":{"The 300 voters surveyed":"That's the sample.","The new park":"That's the topic, not a group of people.","Everyone who visits the park":"The question is about the city's voters."}},"sb43":{"ask":"Spot bias in how a sample is chosen.","idea":"A sample is biased when the way it's chosen favors certain answers. Ask: does this group differ from the population in a way that matters for the question?","steps":["No, they likely exercise more.","Too high."],"check":"A random sample of town residents would avoid this ✓","wrong":{"The sample is too large.":"Size isn't the problem; a large biased sample is still biased.","Gyms are open every day.":"That doesn't affect who is surveyed.","Exercise is hard to measure.":"Possibly, but the main problem is who was chosen."}},"sb44":{"ask":"Tell an experiment from an observational study.","idea":"In an experiment, researchers impose a treatment, ideally by random assignment. In an observational study, they only observe what people already do.","steps":["Yes: they randomly assign it.","An experiment."],"check":"Random assignment makes the groups similar, so differences can be credited to the vitamin ✓","wrong":{"An observational study":"In those, researchers don't assign treatments.","A survey":"A survey only asks questions; nothing is assigned.","A census":"A census collects data from an entire population."}},"sb45":{"ask":"Decide what an observational study can conclude.","idea":"Without random assignment, other factors (confounding variables) could explain the difference. So observational studies support association, not causation.","steps":["No: it's an observational study.","No, only an association."],"check":"A randomized experiment would be needed to test cause ✓","wrong":{"Eating breakfast causes higher grades.":"Cause needs random assignment; students chose for themselves.","Higher grades cause students to eat breakfast.":"The study can't show cause in either direction.","There is no relationship between breakfast and grades.":"The data do show a relationship: an association."}},"sb46":{"ask":"Name a sampling method.","idea":"Systematic: every <i>k<\/i>th person. Stratified: split into groups and sample from each. Cluster: pick whole groups at random. Convenience: whoever is easiest to reach.","steps":["Yes: every 10th.","Systematic sampling."],"check":"If the first customer chosen is random, every customer has an equal chance ✓","wrong":{"Stratified sampling":"Stratified samples from each subgroup, such as each grade.","Cluster sampling":"Cluster picks whole groups, such as entire classrooms.","Convenience sampling":"Every 10th customer follows a fixed rule, not just whoever is easiest."}},"sb47":{"ask":"Use a margin of error to find a plausible range.","idea":"A sample estimate isn't exact. The margin of error gives a range, estimate − margin to estimate + margin, likely to contain the true value.","steps":["51%.","59%."],"check":"The range is 8 points wide, twice the margin of error ✓","wrong":{"55% to 59%":"Only adds the margin; it goes both ways.","53% to 57%":"Uses half the margin of error.","45% to 55%":"Starts from the wrong number; it should be centered on 55%."}},"sb48":{"ask":"Use the 68–95–99.7 rule.","idea":"In a normal (bell-shaped) distribution, about 68% of values are within 1 standard deviation of the mean, 95% within 2, and 99.7% within 3.","steps":["3 inches: one standard deviation.","About 68%."],"check":"Half of 68%, 34%, is on each side of 64 ✓","wrong":{"95%":"That's within 2 standard deviations (58 to 70).","50%":"That's the share above (or below) the mean.","34%":"That's only one side, from 64 to 67."}},"sb49":{"ask":"Find a tail percent in a normal distribution.","idea":"The normal curve is symmetric, so whatever lies outside a range is split equally between the two tails.","steps":["2.","5%.","2.5%."],"check":"About 2.5% are also shorter than 58 inches ✓","wrong":{"5%":"That's both tails together.","16%":"That's the share above 1 standard deviation (67 inches).","0.15%":"That's above 3 standard deviations (73 inches)."}},"sb50":{"ask":"Know what controls the margin of error.","idea":"For random samples, the margin of error gets smaller as the sample size gets larger. Non-random methods don't fix precision; they add bias.","steps":["More precise.","Increase the sample size."],"check":"Roughly, quadrupling the sample size halves the margin of error ✓","wrong":{"Decrease the sample size":"Smaller samples make estimates less precise.","Survey only people who volunteer":"That adds bias without making the estimate more precise.","Ask the questions in a different order":"Wording and order can matter, but they don't control the margin of error."}},"mx49":{"ask":"Solve a linear equation with parentheses on both sides.","idea":"Distribute carefully, especially a negative times a negative (−2 × −3 = +6), then collect x-terms on one side.","steps":["Right side: 7x − 2x + 6 = 5x + 6.","−25 = 3x, so x = −25/3."],"check":"Both sides equal −107/3 when x = −25/3 ✓","wrong":{"25/3":"Sign slip in the last step: 3x = −25, not 25.","−13/3":"Comes from writing −2(x − 3) as −2x − 6.","−5":"Comes from writing −4(x + 1) as −4x + 4."}},"mx50":{"ask":"Find the parameter that makes a linear equation impossible.","idea":"Get the equation into the form (coefficient)·x = constant. No solution means the coefficient is 0 but the constant isn't.","steps":["k = 3 makes the x-term disappear.","0 = 17 is impossible, so there's no solution."],"check":"With k = 3: 3x − 12 = 3x + 5, which is never true ✓","wrong":{"1/6":"That makes the constant 0, which gives exactly one solution, x = 0.","−3":"Sign slip: the coefficient is k − 3, not k + 3.","4":"Matches the 4 inside the parentheses, but that doesn't remove the x-term."}},"mx51":{"ask":"Build a linear cost model from two jobs.","idea":"A fixed fee plus a rate is a line. The rate is the change in cost over the change in hours; the fee is what's left after paying the hours.","steps":["225 ÷ 4.5 = $50 an hour.","255 − 150 = $105.","105 + 250 = $355."],"check":"7.5-hour job: 105 + 7.5(50) = 480 ✓","wrong":{"$425":"Treats cost as proportional to hours (255 ÷ 3 × 5), ignoring the fixed fee.","$305":"Adds just one more hour's rate to the 3-hour job.","$250":"Charges only 5 × $50 and leaves out the fixed fee."}},"mx52":{"ask":"Clear fractions from an equation, then answer what's asked.","idea":"Multiply every term by the least common denominator. Then check what the question wants: here, 10x, not x.","steps":["−x + 11.","x = 10, so 10x = 100."],"check":"x = 10: 23/5 − 9/2 = 46/10 − 45/10 = 1/10 ✓","wrong":{"10":"That's x; the question asks for 10x.","−100":"Comes from −5(x − 1) = −5x − 5, a sign slip.","120":"Comes from dropping the 1/10 term."}},"mx53":{"ask":"Find a linear function, then apply it twice.","idea":"Build f from two points (slope, then intercept). For f(f(3)), work from the inside out; here the inner result lands on a value you already know.","steps":["Slope −3, so f(x) = −3x + 13.","f(3) = 4.","f(f(3)) = f(4) = 1."],"check":"f(1) = −3 + 13 = 10 ✓","wrong":{"4":"That's f(3), only the inside step.","13":"That's the y-intercept, f(0).","−12":"Forgets the + 13 when evaluating f(4)."}},"mx54":{"ask":"Read the slope from a recurrence.","idea":"\"g(x + 2) = g(x) + 7\" says the output rises 7 when the input rises 2. That's a rate of 7/2 per unit.","steps":["Slope 3.5.","g(5) = −3 + 17.5 = 14.5."],"check":"g(2) = 4 and g(4) = 11: each step of 2 adds 7 ✓","wrong":{"32":"Uses 7 as the slope, as if x increased by 1.","17.5":"Forgets the starting value g(0) = −3.","11":"That's g(4), two steps of 2 from 0."}},"mx55":{"ask":"Interpret the change in a linear model.","idea":"A change in output depends only on the slope: change in cost ÷ cost per minute = change in minutes. The fixed $35 cancels out.","steps":["$15.60 more.","130 more minutes."],"check":"Month 1: 100 minutes. Month 2: 230 minutes. 230 − 100 = 130 ✓","wrong":{"230":"That's the total for the second month, not the increase.","100":"That's the total for the first month.","156":"Divides by 0.10 instead of 0.12."}},"mx56":{"ask":"Use two points to find where a line reaches a given value.","idea":"Find the equation first. \"h(x) = 2\" means set the output to 2 and solve for the input.","steps":["Slope −3/2.","x + 3 = 4, so x = 1."],"check":"h(1) = 8 − (3/2)(4) = 2 ✓","wrong":{"1/2":"That's h(2): it swaps the input and the output.","4":"That's x + 3; subtract 3.","−7":"Sign slip: x + 3 = 4, not −4."}},"mx57":{"ask":"Find a perpendicular bisector's equation.","idea":"A perpendicular bisector passes through the midpoint and has the negative reciprocal slope of the segment.","steps":["Midpoint (2, 3).","The segment's slope is 1/2, so ℓ's slope is −2.","b = 7."],"check":"(2, 3) is on y = −2x + 7, and slopes −2 × 1/2 = −1 ✓","wrong":{"2":"Uses the segment's own slope, 1/2, instead of the perpendicular slope.","−1":"Uses slope 2 instead of −2.","3":"That's the midpoint's y-coordinate."}},"mx58":{"ask":"Write a parallel line and find its x-intercept.","idea":"Parallel lines have the same slope. Use point-slope form, then set y = 0 for the x-intercept.","steps":["Slope 3/2; parallel lines share it.","x + 1 = −8/3, so x = −11/3."],"check":"y − 4 = (3/2)(−11/3 + 1) = (3/2)(−8/3) = −4, so y = 0 ✓","wrong":{"5/3":"Uses slope −3/2 instead of 3/2.","−5/3":"Adds 1 at the end instead of subtracting it: x = −8/3 + 1.","11/3":"Sign slip in the last step."}},"mx59":{"ask":"Use parallel lines to find a parameter.","idea":"\"Never intersect\" means parallel and distinct: the same slope, different y-intercepts.","steps":["Slope 2/3.","k = 5/3."],"check":"Intercepts 4 and −2 differ, so the lines are distinct ✓","wrong":{"2/3":"That's the slope itself; the slope is k − 1, not k.","−1/2":"Makes the lines perpendicular, not parallel.","1/3":"Solves k − 1 = −2/3, a sign slip."}},"mx60":{"ask":"Build a line from its intercepts and test points.","idea":"Two intercepts give two points. Find the equation, then substitute each choice; only one should work.","steps":["Slope 2/3, so y = (2/3)x − 4.","(2/3)(9) − 4 = 2 ✓"],"check":"(9, 2): (2/3)(9) − 4 = 2 ✓","wrong":{"(2, 9)":"Swaps the coordinates of the correct point.","(3, 2)":"(2/3)(3) − 4 = −2, not 2.","(−3, 2)":"(2/3)(−3) − 4 = −6, not 2."}},"mx61":{"ask":"Find a combination of variables without solving fully.","idea":"When the question asks for x + y, look for a way to combine the equations directly. Adding them here makes every coefficient 5.","steps":["5x + 5y = 30.","x + y = 6."],"check":"x = 5, y = 1: 15 + 2 = 17 ✓ and 10 + 3 = 13 ✓","wrong":{"30":"That's 5x + 5y; divide by 5.","4":"That's x − y.","5":"That's x alone."}},"mx62":{"ask":"Set up a system from two trips.","idea":"Going with the current, speeds add; going against it, they subtract. Two trips give two equations: b + c and b − c.","steps":["18 km/h = boat + current.","12 km/h = boat − current.","3 km/h."],"check":"Boat 15, current 3: 18 × 2 = 36 ✓ and 12 × 3 = 36 ✓","wrong":{"15":"That's the boat's speed in still water.","6":"That's the difference of the speeds; divide by 2.","18":"That's the downstream speed."}},"mx63":{"ask":"Solve a two-variable word problem and answer the exact question.","idea":"Write one equation for the count and one for the money. After solving, reread: the question asks for a difference.","steps":["5a = 600, so a = 120.","s = 100.","20 more."],"check":"120(14) + 100(9) = 1,680 + 900 = 2,580 ✓","wrong":{"120":"That's the number of adult tickets.","100":"That's the number of student tickets.","40":"Doubles the difference."}},"mx64":{"ask":"Solve a system with fractional coefficients.","idea":"Multiply each equation by its least common denominator first. Then the x-coefficients match, and subtracting removes x.","steps":["3x + 2y = 30.","3x − 2y = 12.","y = 9/2."],"check":"x = 7, y = 9/2: 7/2 + 3/2 = 5 ✓ and 7/4 − 3/4 = 1 ✓","wrong":{"7":"That's x, not y.","3/2":"That's y/3, a step inside the check, not y.","6":"Comes from adding the equations' right sides incorrectly."}},"mx65":{"ask":"Solve a compound inequality and count integer solutions.","idea":"Do the same operation to all three parts. Then watch the endpoints: < excludes, ≤ includes.","steps":["1 < x ≤ 6.","Five integers."],"check":"x = 1 gives −3, not more than −3 ✗; x = 6 gives 7, allowed ✓","wrong":{"6":"Includes x = 1, but 1 makes 2x − 5 = −3, which is excluded.","4":"Excludes 6, but ≤ includes it.","7":"Counts from 0, or includes values outside the bounds."}},"mx66":{"ask":"Write and solve an inequality with a whole-number answer.","idea":"Subtract what's already on board, then divide. Round down: you can't exceed the limit, and you can't carry part of a box.","steps":["1,270 pounds.","36 boxes; 37 would be 1,295 pounds, too heavy."],"check":"36 × 35 = 1,260 ≤ 1,270 ✓; 37 × 35 = 1,295 ✗","wrong":{"37":"Rounds 36.3 up, which goes over the limit.","46":"Forgets the 4 crates.","41":"Forgets the driver."}},"mx67":{"ask":"Test points in a system of inequalities.","idea":"A solution must satisfy every inequality. Watch strict signs: a point on a boundary line works only with ≤ or ≥.","steps":["2 < 3.5 ✓","2 ≥ 1 ✓, so (1, 2) is a solution."],"check":"(0, 4) sits exactly on y = −(1/2)x + 4, but that inequality is strict ✗","wrong":{"(2, 2)":"Fails y ≥ 2x − 1: 2 ≥ 3 is false.","(0, 4)":"On the boundary of a strict inequality: 4 < 4 is false.","(−2, 6)":"Fails y < −(1/2)x + 4: 6 < 5 is false."}},"mx68":{"ask":"Compare two pricing plans with an inequality.","idea":"Set up \"A's total < B's total\", solve, then pick the first whole number that truly satisfies the strict inequality.","steps":["m > 5.71.","6 months."],"check":"After 6 months: A $190, B $192 ✓. After 5: A $165, B $160 ✗","wrong":{"5":"Rounds 5.71 down; after 5 months, Gym A still costs more.","7":"Works, but 6 is the least.","2":"Compares only the fees, not the totals."}},"mx69":{"ask":"Use perimeter to find dimensions, then area.","idea":"Write the length in terms of the width, use the perimeter formula 2(l + w), then multiply for area.","steps":["w = 8 cm.","19 cm.","152 square cm."],"check":"Perimeter 2(8 + 19) = 54 ✓","wrong":{"629":"Forgets the 2 in the perimeter formula (w + l = 54).","190":"Uses a width of 10.","120":"Doesn't satisfy the perimeter of 54."}},"mx70":{"ask":"Pour volume from one shape into another.","idea":"The volume stays the same. Find it in the first shape, then divide by the new base area to get the height.","steps":["360π cubic inches.","180 square inches.","2π inches."],"check":"180 × 2π = 360π ✓","wrong":{"π/3":"Forgets to square the radius: π(6)(10) = 60π, then 60π ÷ 180.","8π":"Uses the diameter, 12, as the radius.","360π":"That's the volume; divide by the base area to get the height."}},"mx71":{"ask":"Scale volume between similar solids.","idea":"For similar figures with scale factor k: lengths × k, areas × k<sup>2<\/sup>, volumes × k<sup>3<\/sup>.","steps":["2.5.","15.625.","375π."],"check":"375π ÷ 24π = 15.625 = 2.5<sup>3<\/sup> ✓","wrong":{"60π":"Scales by k, as if volume were a length.","150π":"Scales by k<sup>2<\/sup>, the area factor.","600π":"Doesn't come from k, k<sup>2<\/sup> or k<sup>3<\/sup> of 2.5."}},"mx72":{"ask":"Use an isosceles triangle's equal angles.","idea":"Equal sides face equal angles. In triangle ABC with AB = AC, the angles at B and C match.","steps":["Angles B and C.","x = 17."],"check":"Angles: 68°, 56°, 56°; total 180° ✓","wrong":{"25":"Uses only one copy of angle B: 4x + 3x + 5 = 180.","35":"Sets angle A equal to angle B, the wrong pair.","12.5":"Arithmetic slip in solving 10x + 10 = 180."}},"mx73":{"ask":"Use the exterior angle theorem.","idea":"An exterior angle equals the sum of the two interior angles not next to it, and it's supplementary to the interior angle beside it.","steps":["The sum of the two remote interior angles.","x = 10, so the exterior angle is 66°.","114°."],"check":"Angles 30°, 36° and 114° add to 180° ✓","wrong":{"66":"That's the exterior angle, not the interior one.","10":"That's x.","30":"That's the angle at P."}},"mx74":{"ask":"Use similar triangles inside a right triangle.","idea":"The altitude to the hypotenuse is the geometric mean of the two pieces: h<sup>2<\/sup> = (piece 1)(piece 2).","steps":["Similar, so 4/h = h/9.","h = 6."],"check":"Legs: √(16 + 36) = √52 and √(81 + 36) = √117; 52 + 117 = 169 = 13<sup>2<\/sup> ✓","wrong":{"6.5":"Averages 4 and 9 instead of multiplying.","13":"That's the hypotenuse.","√97":"Treats 4 and 9 as legs of a right triangle."}},"mx75":{"ask":"Scale a 3-4-5 triangle to fit a perimeter.","idea":"A trig ratio gives the shape, not the size. Write the sides with a scale factor k, then use the perimeter to find k.","steps":["5k.","k = 3.","15."],"check":"Sides 9, 12, 15: perimeter 36 ✓ and tan A = 9/12 = 3/4 ✓","wrong":{"12":"That's the longer leg.","9":"That's the shorter leg.","20":"Uses k = 4."}},"mx76":{"ask":"Use the complementary-angle relationship between sine and cosine.","idea":"In a right triangle, the sine of one acute angle equals the cosine of the other, because they add to 90°.","steps":["When the angles are complementary.","x = 40."],"check":"sin 40° = cos 50° ✓","wrong":{"30":"Sets x = 2x − 30, as if sin a = cos a.","20":"Solves 3x − 30 = 30.","60":"Doesn't satisfy x + 2x − 30 = 90."}},"mx77":{"ask":"Use a 30-60-90 triangle.","idea":"In a 30-60-90 triangle the sides are x, x√3 and 2x. The longer leg is opposite the 60° angle.","steps":["Sine.","√3/2, so the height is 10√3."],"check":"Base 10, height 10√3: 100 + 300 = 400 = 20<sup>2<\/sup> ✓","wrong":{"10":"That's the distance along the ground (cosine).","20√3":"Doubles the correct height; 2x is the hypotenuse.","10√2":"Uses a 45-45-90 triangle."}},"mx78":{"ask":"Find a circle's radius by completing the square.","idea":"Group x-terms and y-terms, add (half the coefficient)<sup>2<\/sup> for each to both sides, and move the constant over.","steps":["9.","25.","r = 5."],"check":"Center (3, −5), radius 5 ✓","wrong":{"√43":"Adds 9 instead of subtracting it: 9 + 9 + 25.","25":"That's r<sup>2<\/sup>, not r.","3":"That's the x-coordinate of the center."}},"mx79":{"ask":"Relate a sector's area to its arc length.","idea":"Both come from the same fraction of the circle: arc = (θ/360)·2πr and area = (θ/360)·πr<sup>2<\/sup>. Dividing them gives area = (r/2) × arc.","steps":["Area = (1/2) r × arc.","r = 10."],"check":"r = 10: circumference 20π, so the arc is 1/5 of the circle; area (1/5)(100π) = 20π ✓","wrong":{"5":"Uses area = r × arc, dropping the 1/2.","20":"Divides incorrectly: 20π ÷ π.","2√5":"Uses r<sup>2<\/sup> = 20."}},"mx80":{"ask":"Find a radius from a chord and its distance to the center.","idea":"A perpendicular from the center bisects any chord. Half the chord, the distance and the radius form a right triangle.","steps":["8.","r = 10.","100π."],"check":"8, 6, 10 is a 3-4-5 triangle doubled ✓","wrong":{"64π":"Uses half the chord, 8, as the radius.","36π":"Uses the distance, 6, as the radius.","10π":"Uses πr instead of πr<sup>2<\/sup>."}},"mx81":{"ask":"Use vertex form to find x-intercepts.","idea":"x-intercepts of a parabola are symmetric about the axis of symmetry, so their sum is twice the vertex's x-coordinate.","steps":["(x − 3)<sup>2<\/sup> = 4.","6."],"check":"f(1) = 2(4) − 8 = 0 ✓ and f(5) = 0 ✓","wrong":{"3":"That's the vertex's x-coordinate, or one-half of the sum.","−6":"Sign slip: the vertex is at x = +3.","4":"That's the value of (x − 3)<sup>2<\/sup>."}},"mx82":{"ask":"Combine repeated percent decreases.","idea":"Each year multiplies by 0.85. Two years multiply by 0.85<sup>2<\/sup>; percent changes don't simply add.","steps":["0.7225: 72.25% remains.","27.75%."],"check":"2,400 → 2,040 → 1,734; 1,734 ÷ 2,400 = 0.7225 ✓","wrong":{"30%":"Adds 15% + 15%, but each decrease applies to a smaller amount.","15%":"That's the decrease for one year.","72.25%":"That's the percent remaining, not the decrease."}},"mx83":{"ask":"Solve a nonlinear system and combine the results.","idea":"Solve the quadratic, then plug each x into the line. Answer the question asked: here, the product of the y-values.","steps":["x = 5 or x = −3.","7 × (−1) = −7."],"check":"(5, 7): 25 − 10 = 15 ✓ and (−3, −1): 9 + 6 = 15 ✓","wrong":{"−15":"That's the product of the x-values.","6":"That's the sum of the y-values.","7":"That's one y-value."}},"mx84":{"ask":"Simplify rational expressions by factoring.","idea":"Factor each numerator to cancel with its denominator. Then subtract carefully: the minus applies to the whole second expression.","steps":["x − 3.","x − 2.","−1."],"check":"Try x = 7: 40/10 − 25/5 = 4 − 5 = −1 ✓","wrong":{"1":"Subtracts in the wrong order.","2<i>x<\/i> − 5":"Adds the two simplified expressions instead of subtracting.","−5":"Distributes the minus to x only: x − 3 − x − 2."}},"mx85":{"ask":"Undo an increase followed by an equal percent decrease.","idea":"An increase and a decrease of the same percent don't cancel: (1 + r)(1 − r) = 1 − r<sup>2<\/sup>, always a net loss.","steps":["r<sup>2<\/sup> = 0.09.","p = 30."],"check":"$100 → $130 → $91 ✓","wrong":{"9":"That's the net percent lost, r<sup>2<\/sup> = 0.09 written as 9%.","3":"Takes √9 without converting: r = 0.3 means 30%.","4.5":"Splits the 9% loss in half."}},"mx86":{"ask":"Track how transformations change spread.","idea":"Adding a constant moves the center but not the spread. Multiplying by a constant multiplies both the center and the spread.","steps":["No change.","It doubles, so B's is 2 times A's."],"check":"A's standard deviation is about 2.83; B is 28, 36, 36, 44, with about 5.66 ✓","wrong":{"It is 10 more.":"Adding 10 shifts the data but doesn't change its spread.","It is 2 times as large, plus 10.":"The +10 shift doesn't affect spread.","It is the same.":"Doubling the values doubles the spread."}},"mx87":{"ask":"Find a conditional probability from overlapping groups.","idea":"\"A student who plays soccer\" makes the soccer players the whole group. Subtract the overlap to find those who don't play chess.","steps":["14.","14/22 = 7/11."],"check":"Soccer-only 14 + both 8 = 22 ✓","wrong":{"7/25":"That's 14/50, dividing by the whole club.","4/11":"That's 8/22: soccer players who do play chess.","7/15":"That's 14/30, dividing by chess players."}},"mx88":{"ask":"Interpret a margin of error.","idea":"A random sample estimates a population value with uncertainty. The margin of error creates a range of plausible values around the estimate.","steps":["53%.","63%."],"check":"53% to 63% is centered on 58% ✓","wrong":{"Exactly 58% of all the school's students favor a later start time.":"A sample can't give the exact population value.","More than 63% of all the school's students favor a later start time.":"That's outside the plausible range.","Only 5% of the school's students were surveyed.":"The margin of error isn't the share of students surveyed."}},"m141":{"ask":"Solve a two-step equation, then answer what's asked.","idea":"Undo the addition first, then the multiplication. Read carefully: the question wants 2x, not x.","steps":["4x = 24.","2x = 12."],"check":"4(6) + 7 = 31 ✓","wrong":{"6":"That's x; the question asks for 2x.","24":"That's 4x.","19":"Comes from (31 + 7) ÷ 2, adding instead of subtracting."}},"m142":{"ask":"Evaluate a function.","idea":"f(4) means replace every x with 4, then follow the order of operations.","steps":["20.","f(4) = 17."],"check":"5(4) − 3 = 17 ✓","wrong":{"23":"Adds 3 instead of subtracting.","12":"Computes 5 + 4 + 3.","2":"Computes 5 − 3 = 2 and ignores the 4."}},"m143":{"ask":"Find a slope from an equation.","idea":"Solve for y. In y = mx + b, the slope is m.","steps":["y = 3x + 4.","Slope 3."],"check":"Points (0, 4) and (1, 7): rise 3, run 1 ✓","wrong":{"6":"Reads the slope before dividing by 2.","4":"That's the y-intercept.","8":"That's the constant before dividing."}},"m144":{"ask":"Solve a linear inequality and test values.","idea":"Solve like an equation. With \">\", the boundary value itself isn't a solution.","steps":["3x > 15.","6."],"check":"3(6) − 5 = 13 > 10 ✓; 3(5) − 5 = 10, not greater ✗","wrong":{"5":"Gives exactly 10, but the inequality is strict.","4":"Gives 7, which is less than 10.","0":"Gives −5."}},"m145":{"ask":"Solve a system by substitution.","idea":"When one equation gives a variable directly (y = 2x), substitute it into the other.","steps":["x = 4.","y = 8."],"check":"4 + 8 = 12 ✓ and 8 = 2(4) ✓","wrong":{"4":"That's x.","12":"That's x + y.","6":"Splits 12 in half, ignoring y = 2x."}},"m146":{"ask":"Write and solve an equation from a word problem.","idea":"Total = fixed fee + rate × months. Take away the fixed part, then divide by the rate.","steps":["$162.","9 months."],"check":"25 + 18(9) = 187 ✓","wrong":{"10":"Ignores the joining fee: 187 ÷ 18 ≈ 10.","8":"Arithmetic slip in 162 ÷ 18.","7":"Subtracts the fee twice."}},"m147":{"ask":"Extend a linear pattern from a table.","idea":"Find the rate of change per 1 unit of x, then build y = mx + b.","steps":["Slope 3.","b = 4.","34."],"check":"x = 5: 3(5) + 4 = 19 ✓","wrong":{"30":"Uses y = 3x without the intercept.","25":"Adds 6 to 19 just once.","37":"Adds 3 more steps of 6 after x = 5, but x = 10 is only 5 units (slope 3 × 5 = 15) past x = 5."}},"m148":{"ask":"Find a y-intercept from two points.","idea":"Get the slope first, then substitute one point into y = mx + b to find b.","steps":["Slope 2.","b = −3."],"check":"(6, 9): 2(6) − 3 = 9 ✓","wrong":{"3":"Sign slip: 1 − 4 = −3, not 3.","−1":"Uses a slope of 1.","5":"Adds instead of subtracting: 1 + 4."}},"m149":{"ask":"Use an inequality with an average.","idea":"Turn \"average at least 85\" into \"total at least 85 × 4\", then subtract what's already earned.","steps":["340.","248.","92."],"check":"(248 + 92) ÷ 4 = 85 ✓","wrong":{"85":"Assumes she just needs the target on the last test.","88":"Uses her best score so far.","90":"Arithmetic slip; 248 + 90 = 338 < 340."}},"m150":{"ask":"Solve an inequality with a negative coefficient.","idea":"Multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number reverses the inequality sign.","steps":["−2x ≤ −6.","x ≥ 3."],"check":"x = 4: −8 + 7 = −1 ≤ 1 ✓; x = 0: 7 ≤ 1 ✗","wrong":{"<i>x<\/i> ≤ 3":"Forgets to reverse the sign when dividing by −2.","<i>x<\/i> ≥ −3":"Sign slip: −6 ÷ −2 = 3.","<i>x<\/i> ≤ −3":"Makes both mistakes."}},"m151":{"ask":"Use a known solution to find a constant.","idea":"If x = 9 is a solution, substituting it must make both sides equal; that gives an equation for k.","steps":["46.","k = 1."],"check":"With k = 1: 6x − 8 = 5x + 1 gives x = 9 ✓","wrong":{"−1":"Sign slip: 46 − 45 = 1.","46":"That's the left side's value.","9":"That's x."}},"m152":{"ask":"Interpret parts of a linear model.","idea":"In a + b(x − h), a is the value at x = h and b is the change per unit after that.","steps":["C rises by $8.","Each gigabyte beyond 5."],"check":"C(5) = 30 and C(6) = 38 ✓","wrong":{"The cost of the first 5 gigabytes":"That's 30.","The number of gigabytes included in the plan":"That's 5.","The total monthly cost for 8 gigabytes":"C(8) = 30 + 24 = 54, not 8."}},"m153":{"ask":"Find an x-intercept of a parallel line.","idea":"Parallel lines have equal slopes. Use point-slope form, then set y = 0.","steps":["−2/5.","x − 10 = 2.5, so x = 12.5."],"check":"(12.5, 0): 0 − 1 = −(2/5)(2.5) = −1 ✓","wrong":{"7.5":"Sign slip: x − 10 = −2.5.","10":"That's the given point's x-coordinate.","2.5":"That's x − 10; add 10."}},"m154":{"ask":"Solve a system with symmetric coefficients.","idea":"When coefficients are swapped (3, 2 and 2, 3), adding and subtracting the equations gives L + M and L − M quickly.","steps":["L + M = 7.30.","L − M = 2.50.","$4.90."],"check":"3(4.90) + 2(2.40) = 19.50 ✓","wrong":{"$2.40":"That's the muffin.","$7.30":"That's a latte plus a muffin.","$5.50":"Doesn't satisfy either equation."}},"m155":{"ask":"Model a tolerance with absolute value.","idea":"\"Within t of a target value\" means |x − target| ≤ t.","steps":["The distance from x to 6.","|x − 6| ≤ 0.25."],"check":"x = 6.2 works (|0.2| ≤ 0.25); x = 6.3 doesn't ✓","wrong":{"|<i>x<\/i> − 0.25| ≤ 6":"Swaps the target and the tolerance.","|<i>x<\/i> + 6| ≤ 0.25":"Measures distance from −6.","<i>x<\/i> − 6 ≤ 0.25":"Only limits the length from above; 5 cm would pass."}},"m156":{"ask":"Simplify by distributing and combining like terms.","idea":"Multiply the number outside by every term inside, then combine like terms.","steps":["3x + 12.","x + 12."],"check":"x = 1: 3(5) − 2 = 13 and 1 + 12 = 13 ✓","wrong":{"<i>x<\/i> + 4":"Multiplies only the x by 3.","5<i>x<\/i> + 12":"Adds 2x instead of subtracting.","<i>x<\/i> + 10":"Subtracts 2 from 12 instead of 2x from 3x."}},"m157":{"ask":"Divide monomials.","idea":"Divide the coefficients and subtract the exponents of the same base.","steps":["3.","x<sup>2<\/sup>, so 3x<sup>2<\/sup>."],"check":"x = 2: 48 ÷ 4 = 12 and 3(4) = 12 ✓","wrong":{"3<i>x<\/i><sup>3<\/sup>":"Forgets to divide the x's.","4<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup>":"Subtracts 6 − 2 instead of dividing.","12<i>x<\/i><sup>4<\/sup>":"Multiplies instead of dividing."}},"m158":{"ask":"Evaluate a quadratic at a negative input.","idea":"Use parentheses when substituting a negative number, especially when squaring.","steps":["4.","6, so f(−2) = 10."],"check":"4 + 6 = 10 ✓","wrong":{"−2":"Computes 4 − 6.","2":"Computes −4 + 6.","−10":"Computes −4 − 6."}},"m159":{"ask":"Use the zero product property.","idea":"If a product equals zero, at least one factor equals zero. Set each factor to 0.","steps":["x = 3.","x = −5."],"check":"x = −5: (−8)(0) = 0 ✓","wrong":{"−3 and 5":"Uses the numbers in the factors with their signs flipped the wrong way.","3 and 5":"Sign slip on the second factor.","−3 and −5":"Sign slip on the first factor."}},"m160":{"ask":"Evaluate an exponential model.","idea":"In a(b)^t, apply the exponent first, then multiply by a.","steps":["8.","400."],"check":"50 → 100 → 200 → 400 ✓","wrong":{"300":"Uses 50 × 2 × 3, as if growth added the same amount.","200":"That's after 2 hours.","150":"Adds 50 each hour."}},"m161":{"ask":"Multiply two binomials.","idea":"Multiply every term in the first by every term in the second, then combine the middle terms.","steps":["1, so the middle term is x.","−15."],"check":"x = 1: (−3)(4) = −12 and 2 + 1 − 15 = −12 ✓","wrong":{"2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − 15":"Leaves out the middle terms.","2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> + 11<i>x<\/i> − 15":"Adds 6x and 5x instead of 6x − 5x.","2<i>x<\/i><sup>2<\/sup> − <i>x<\/i> − 15":"Sign slip in the middle term."}},"m162":{"ask":"Factor a perfect-square trinomial.","idea":"a<sup>2<\/sup> − 2ab + b<sup>2<\/sup> = (a − b)<sup>2<\/sup>. Check that the middle term is twice the product of the square roots.","steps":["(2x)<sup>2<\/sup>.","(2x − 3)<sup>2<\/sup>."],"check":"x = 2: 16 − 24 + 9 = 1 and (4 − 3)<sup>2<\/sup> = 1 ✓","wrong":{"(2<i>x<\/i> + 3)<sup>2<\/sup>":"Gives +12x in the middle.","(4<i>x<\/i> − 3)(<i>x<\/i> − 3)":"Expands to 4x<sup>2<\/sup> − 15x + 9.","(2<i>x<\/i> − 9)(2<i>x<\/i> − 1)":"Expands to 4x<sup>2<\/sup> − 20x + 9."}},"m163":{"ask":"Solve a quadratic by factoring.","idea":"Find two numbers that multiply to the constant and add to the x-coefficient.","steps":["(x − 1)(x − 5).","5."],"check":"25 − 30 + 5 = 0 ✓","wrong":{"1":"That's the smaller solution.","6":"That's the sum of the solutions.","−1":"Sign slip in the factors."}},"m164":{"ask":"Find the y-intercept of an exponential function.","idea":"The y-intercept is the output when x = 0, and b<sup>0<\/sup> = 1 for any nonzero b.","steps":["1.","4."],"check":"f(0) = 4 ✓","wrong":{"(0, 3)":"Treats 2<sup>0<\/sup> as 0.","(0, 5)":"Treats 2<sup>0<\/sup> as 2.","(0, 2)":"Treats it as 2<sup>0<\/sup> + 1."}},"m165":{"ask":"Read a vertex from vertex form.","idea":"y = (x − h)<sup>2<\/sup> + k has vertex (h, k). Watch the sign: x − 4 means h = +4.","steps":["h = 4.","k = 7, so the vertex is (4, 7)."],"check":"y is smallest (7) when x = 4 ✓","wrong":{"(−4, 7)":"Uses the opposite sign for h.","(4, −7)":"Uses the opposite sign for k.","(7, 4)":"Swaps the coordinates."}},"m166":{"ask":"Simplify a rational expression by factoring.","idea":"Factor the numerator, then cancel a factor that matches the denominator.","steps":["(x − 4)(x + 3).","x + 3."],"check":"x = 5: (25 − 5 − 12)/1 = 8 and 5 + 3 = 8 ✓","wrong":{"<i>x<\/i> − 3":"Factors as (x + 4)(x − 3), which multiplies to x<sup>2<\/sup> + x − 12.","<i>x<\/i> − 4":"That's the denominator.","<i>x<\/i> + 4":"Sign slip in the factor."}},"m167":{"ask":"Match coefficients in a perfect square.","idea":"(x + a)<sup>2<\/sup> expands to x<sup>2<\/sup> + 2ax + a<sup>2<\/sup>, so the constant is the square of half the x-coefficient.","steps":["a = 5.","c = 25."],"check":"(x + 5)<sup>2<\/sup> = x<sup>2<\/sup> + 10x + 25 ✓","wrong":{"10":"That's the x-coefficient.","5":"That's a, not c.","100":"Squares 10 instead of 5."}},"m168":{"ask":"Solve a system of a parabola and a line.","idea":"Set the expressions for y equal, rearrange to a quadratic, and solve.","steps":["x<sup>2<\/sup> − 3x + 2 = 0.","3."],"check":"(1, 2) and (2, 5) are on both graphs ✓","wrong":{"2":"That's the product of the solutions.","−3":"Sign slip.","1":"Counts only one intersection."}},"m169":{"ask":"Set up and solve a quadratic from a geometry context.","idea":"Write area = length × width with one variable, solve the quadratic and reject the negative answer.","steps":["w(w + 4) = 60.","w = 6."],"check":"6 × 10 = 60 ✓","wrong":{"10":"That's the length.","15":"Divides 60 by 4.","4":"That's the difference between length and width."}},"m170":{"ask":"Evaluate an exponential decay model.","idea":"Exponential decay multiplies by the same factor each year, so the losses shrink as the value shrinks.","steps":["0.64.","$15,360."],"check":"Year 1: 19,200. Year 2: 15,360 ✓","wrong":{"$14,400":"Subtracts 20% of the original twice: linear, not exponential.","$19,200":"That's after 1 year.","$16,000":"Doesn't follow from the model."}},"m171":{"ask":"Use a rate with a unit conversion.","idea":"Put time in one unit first (minutes), then divide by the minutes per mile.","steps":["80 minutes.","10 miles."],"check":"10 miles × 8 minutes = 80 minutes ✓","wrong":{"12":"Treats 1 hour 20 minutes as 96 minutes.","8":"That's the pace, not the distance.","6.5":"Treats 1 hour 20 minutes as 52 minutes, a conversion slip."}},"m172":{"ask":"Find a percent of a number.","idea":"Percent means per hundred: convert to a decimal, then multiply.","steps":["0.15.","12."],"check":"10% is 8 and 5% is 4: 8 + 4 = 12 ✓","wrong":{"15":"That's the percent itself.","65":"Subtracts 15 from 80.","5.33":"Divides 80 by 15."}},"m173":{"ask":"Find a mean when values repeat.","idea":"Multiply each score by how many students got it, add, then divide by the number of students, not the number of different scores.","steps":["850.","85."],"check":"Most scores are 80, so a mean a bit above 80 makes sense ✓","wrong":{"90":"Averages 80, 90 and 100 without weighting.","80":"That's the mode.","88":"Arithmetic slip."}},"m174":{"ask":"Find the probability of a complement.","idea":"P(not A) = 1 − P(A), or count the outcomes that aren't A.","steps":["5.","5/8."],"check":"3/8 + 5/8 = 1 ✓","wrong":{"3/8":"That's the probability of red.","3/5":"Compares red with not red.","1/8":"That's one section."}},"m175":{"ask":"Make a prediction from a line of best fit.","idea":"Substitute the x-value into the equation of the line.","steps":["−280.","120 cups."],"check":"Warmer days, fewer cups: the slope is negative ✓","wrong":{"280":"That's the drop from 400, not the prediction.","680":"Adds 280 instead of subtracting.","330":"Uses 400 − 70."}},"m176":{"ask":"Use a unit rate.","idea":"Find the rate per one unit, then scale it up.","steps":["25.","16."],"check":"16 × 25 = 400 ✓","wrong":{"25":"That's the miles per gallon.","10":"Arithmetic slip.","20":"Arithmetic slip."}},"m177":{"ask":"Work backward from a discounted price.","idea":"A 25% discount leaves 75% of the original. Divide by 0.75, not add 25% of the sale price.","steps":["75%.","$48."],"check":"25% of 48 is 12, and 48 − 12 = 36 ✓","wrong":{"$45":"Adds 25% of $36 to $36, using the wrong base.","$27":"Takes 25% off $36 again.","$61":"Adds 25 dollars."}},"m178":{"ask":"See how changing an extreme value affects mean and median.","idea":"The mean uses every value; the median depends only on the middle.","steps":["The mean rises.","The median stays 7."],"check":"Mean 9 → 11.4; median 7 → 7 ✓","wrong":{"The median increases, and the mean stays the same.":"This reverses the effects.","Both the mean and the median increase.":"The middle value doesn't move.","Neither the mean nor the median changes.":"The total, and so the mean, changes."}},"m179":{"ask":"Choose between linear and exponential models.","idea":"Constant difference → linear. Constant ratio (percent) → exponential.","steps":["The same percent.","Exponential growth."],"check":"1, 1.5, 2.25, 3.375: ratios of 1.5 ✓","wrong":{"Linear growth":"Linear growth adds the same amount each time.","Exponential decay":"Decay would shrink by a percent.","Linear decay":"Both the pattern and direction are wrong."}},"m180":{"ask":"Estimate a population count from a random sample.","idea":"A random sample's proportion estimates the population's proportion; multiply by the population size.","steps":["0.23.","About 1,840."],"check":"1,840 ÷ 8,000 = 46 ÷ 200 ✓","wrong":{"46":"That's the sample count.","3,680":"Doubles the estimate.","920":"Halves the estimate."}},"m181":{"ask":"Find a conditional probability from a table.","idea":"\"If a 10th grader is chosen\" makes the 10th grade row the whole group.","steps":["60.","27/60 = 9/20."],"check":"33/60 aren't in a club, and 27/60 + 33/60 = 1 ✓","wrong":{"3/5":"That's 27/45, dividing by everyone in a club.","9/40":"That's 27/120, dividing by all students.","3/8":"That's 45/120, the overall club rate."}},"m182":{"ask":"Know what affects the median.","idea":"The median depends on position. Changing values that stay on the same side of the middle doesn't move it.","steps":["The 4th.","No, so the median stays 20."],"check":"Example: 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35 → 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 35, 40: median 20 ✓","wrong":{"25":"Adds 5 to the median.","22.5":"Splits the change.","30":"Adds 10 to the median."}},"m183":{"ask":"Identify an exponential model from a table.","idea":"Check differences (linear) and ratios (exponential). A constant ratio less than 1 means exponential decay.","steps":["0.9.","Yes: exponential with factor 0.9."],"check":"200(0.9)<sup>3<\/sup> = 145.8 ✓","wrong":{"<i>y<\/i> = 200 − 20<i>x<\/i>":"Fits the first step, but gives 140 at x = 3.","<i>y<\/i> = 200(0.1)<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup>":"Uses the percent lost (10%) as the factor.","<i>y<\/i> = 200(1.1)<sup><i>x<\/i><\/sup>":"That's growth, not decay."}},"m184":{"ask":"Use a margin of error to judge plausible values.","idea":"Estimate ± margin of error gives the range of plausible values for the population.","steps":["58%.","66%, so 60% is plausible."],"check":"58% ≤ 60% ≤ 66% ✓","wrong":{"55%":"Below the range.","67%":"Just above the range.","70%":"Well above the range."}},"m185":{"ask":"Judge whether a claim follows from how data were collected.","idea":"Only a random sample supports generalizing to a population. Voluntary responses usually overrepresent people with strong opinions.","steps":["No, it's self-selected.","No."],"check":"A random sample of residents would be needed ✓","wrong":{"About 80% of all city residents support the stadium.":"The sample isn't representative of residents.","The poll proves most residents oppose the stadium.":"The poll shows nothing about opposition.","The results apply to everyone who visits sports websites.":"Respondents chose themselves, so they don't represent all visitors either."}},"m186":{"ask":"Go from perimeter to area.","idea":"A square's four sides are equal, so side = perimeter ÷ 4, then area = side<sup>2<\/sup>.","steps":["9 cm.","81."],"check":"Perimeter 4 × 9 = 36 ✓","wrong":{"36":"That's the perimeter.","144":"Uses 12 as the side.","9":"That's the side length."}},"m187":{"ask":"Find the base angles of an isosceles triangle.","idea":"An isosceles triangle's two base angles are equal; together with the vertex angle they make 180°.","steps":["140°.","70°."],"check":"40 + 70 + 70 = 180 ✓","wrong":{"140°":"That's both base angles together.","40°":"Copies the vertex angle.","50°":"Uses 90 − 40."}},"m188":{"ask":"Find a circumference.","idea":"Circumference C = 2πr; area A = πr<sup>2<\/sup>. Don't mix them up.","steps":["2πr.","10π."],"check":"Diameter 10, so C = π × 10 ✓","wrong":{"25π":"That's the area.","5π":"Uses πr.","20π":"Doubles twice."}},"m189":{"ask":"Write a sine ratio.","idea":"SOH: sine is opposite over hypotenuse, measured from the angle in question.","steps":["Opposite ÷ hypotenuse.","5/13."],"check":"(5/13)<sup>2<\/sup> + (12/13)<sup>2<\/sup> = 1 ✓","wrong":{"5/12":"That's the tangent (opposite ÷ adjacent).","12/13":"That's the cosine of this angle.","13/5":"Flips the ratio."}},"m190":{"ask":"Use vertical angles.","idea":"Angles across from each other where two lines cross are equal.","steps":["Equal.","x = 20."],"check":"3(20) + 10 = 70 ✓","wrong":{"60":"That's 3x.","25":"Arithmetic slip.","10":"Uses supplementary angles: 3x + 10 = 40."}},"m191":{"ask":"Find the surface area of a box.","idea":"A box has 6 faces in 3 matching pairs. Add the three different face areas, then double.","steps":["74.","148."],"check":"Faces: 20, 20, 24, 24, 30, 30 → 148 ✓","wrong":{"120":"That's the volume (4 × 5 × 6).","74":"Counts each pair once.","240":"Doubles the volume."}},"m192":{"ask":"Find a radius with the distance formula.","idea":"The radius is the distance from the center to any point on the circle: √(Δx<sup>2<\/sup> + Δy<sup>2<\/sup>).","steps":["3.","4.","5."],"check":"3, 4, 5 right triangle ✓","wrong":{"7":"Adds the distances 3 + 4.","25":"That's r<sup>2<\/sup>.","√5":"Uses 2 + (−2) = 0 for the vertical distance, a sign slip."}},"m193":{"ask":"Use cosine to find a side.","idea":"cos = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse. Multiply the hypotenuse by the cosine to get the adjacent side.","steps":["BC.","16."],"check":"The other leg is 30: 16<sup>2<\/sup> + 30<sup>2<\/sup> = 1,156 = 34<sup>2<\/sup> ✓","wrong":{"30":"That's AC, the side opposite B.","17":"Halves the hypotenuse.","72.25":"Divides 34 by 8/17 instead of multiplying."}},"m194":{"ask":"Use a scale factor between similar triangles.","idea":"Corresponding sides of similar triangles are proportional: match longest to longest, then scale.","steps":["3.","12."],"check":"Sides 12, 18, 27 are 3 times 4, 6, 9 ✓","wrong":{"13":"Adds 9 to 4 instead of multiplying by the scale factor.","22":"Adds 18 (27 − 9) to 4.","4":"That's the original side."}},"m195":{"ask":"Find a cone's volume.","idea":"A cone holds one-third as much as a cylinder with the same base and height: V = (1/3)πr<sup>2<\/sup>h.","steps":["180.","60π."],"check":"The matching cylinder holds 180π, and 180π ÷ 3 = 60π ✓","wrong":{"180π":"Forgets the 1/3: that's a cylinder.","30π":"Uses πrh: r instead of r<sup>2<\/sup>, and no 1/3.","10π":"Uses r instead of r<sup>2<\/sup>: (1/3)π(6)(5)."}},"m196":{"ask":"Use the tangent-radius right angle.","idea":"A tangent line is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency, which creates a right triangle.","steps":["90°.","12."],"check":"5, 12, 13 is a right triangle ✓","wrong":{"8":"Subtracts 13 − 5.","18":"Adds 13 + 5.","√194":"Adds the squares instead of subtracting."}},"m197":{"ask":"Find an arc length in radians.","idea":"In radians, s = rθ. (Sector area is ½r<sup>2<\/sup>θ, which is easy to mix up.)","steps":["s = rθ.","6π."],"check":"2π/3 is one-third of a full turn, and 18π ÷ 3 = 6π ✓","wrong":{"3π":"Uses π/3 instead of 2π/3.","18π":"That's the whole circumference.","27π":"That's the sector's area, ½r<sup>2<\/sup>θ."}},"m198":{"ask":"Find sine from cosine.","idea":"Build a right triangle from the ratio, find the missing side with the Pythagorean theorem, then form the new ratio.","steps":["12.","12/13."],"check":"(5/13)<sup>2<\/sup> + (12/13)<sup>2<\/sup> = 1 ✓","wrong":{"5/12":"That's the cotangent: adjacent ÷ opposite.","13/12":"Flips the sine.","8/13":"Subtracts 13 − 5 instead of using the Pythagorean theorem."}},"m199":{"ask":"Use alternate interior angles.","idea":"With parallel lines, alternate interior angles are equal, while same-side interior angles add to 180°.","steps":["Equal.","x = 25."],"check":"Both angles are 85° ✓","wrong":{"26.67":"Treats them as supplementary: 6x + 20 = 180.","15":"Arithmetic slip.","85":"That's the angle measure, not x."}},"m200":{"ask":"Relate a square inscribed in a circle to the radius.","idea":"The diagonal of an inscribed square is the circle's diameter. Side = d/√2, so area = d<sup>2<\/sup>/2.","steps":["10.","50."],"check":"Side 5√2: (5√2)<sup>2<\/sup> = 50 ✓","wrong":{"25":"Uses the radius as the side.","100":"Uses the diameter as the side.","25π":"That's the circle's area."}}};
